window.RULEBOOK_INDEX = [{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"colony-gauges","title":"The colony's four gauges","status":"In the game","text":"The TV's header and the Colony desk on every phone show four gauges. Everyone sees the same numbers. Gauge What it counts Good is Wealth Everything the colony owns, in credits: the colony fund plus every player's money, goods, land and installed MULEs, valued the way Worth values them (before office credit and any Collapse cut). The note under it shows the fund alone. High Environment The air gauge, 0 to 100 , with its bands: Clean from 0 , Smoggy from 30 , Toxic from 60 , Collapse from 80 . Higher is dirtier. Low Prestige Points from the colony's buildings that are standing, plus lasting points from events. The note shows how many buildings stand. High Happiness The colony's mood, 0 to 100 , with its band name: Thriving, Content, Restless, Unhappy or Unrest. On the TV it also shows how far it moved this month. High Each month end the game keeps a snapshot of the gauges, so the screens can show the change since the last month. The colony score at game over is built from the same four (see The colony score ). Why it is like this Heiko approved WS8's colony screens on 4 Nov (\"Yes, A\"): four gauges in the TV header, project cards by stage at month end, the Colony desk on the phone, and the colony score as a band under the winner screen at game over. What is changing Being built in the colony branch: the gauges count up and land at month end, and the mood's drift plays once on the TV (PR #365, CI green, waiting to merge into the colony branch). WS8 has also noted that the phone's air gauge keeps its own copy of the air bands and will drift if the engine's bands change; that is in the backlog. In the code: packages/engine/src/colony.ts::colonyGauges · ::colonyWealth · apps/server/src/colony-view.ts::colonyView · apps/web/src/components/Colony.tsx::ColonyGauges · config.ts::POLLUTION.bands Kept current by: WS7 (rules), WS8 (screens)."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"mood","title":"Mood: happiness and its bands","status":"In the game","text":"The colony starts every game at happiness 60 (Content). At each month end it moves toward a target set by how the month went, but by at most 5 points a month. One bad month dents the mood; a run of them sinks it. Events can also move it at once, by any amount, without that limit."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"mood-bands","title":"The bands","status":"","text":"Band Happiness Thriving 80 to 100 Content 60 to 79 Restless 40 to 59 Unhappy 20 to 39 Unrest 0 to 19"},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"mood-target","title":"What sets the month's target","status":"","text":"The target starts at 60 and each of these moves it. The result is kept between 0 and 100 . Input Effect on the target Goods the store ran short of this month −8 for each good Dirty air −0.4 for each air point above 30 A high sales tax −2 for each percent above 5 % The Governor broke their tax promise this month −10 Prestige standing +2 for each point, counting at most 15 points (so at most +30 ) The gap between the richest and the poorest player, by cash plus land ( 300 cr a plot) −2 for each 1,000 cr of gap, counting at most 10 (so at most −20 ) Example The mood is 60 . This month the store ran out of Food and Energy ( −16 ), the air is at 45 ( 15 points over, −6 ), the Governor set the tax at 7 % ( −4 ), the Hospital stands (prestige 3 , +6 ), and the richest player is 2,500 cr ahead of the poorest ( −5 ). The target is 60 − 16 − 6 − 4 + 6 − 5 = 35 . The mood can move only 5 a month, so it ends the month at 55 : Restless. If nothing changes, it keeps falling by 5 a month: 50, 45, 40, then 35, where it stops. The mood already changes how computer players vote: in an Unhappy or Unrest colony they will not back the sitting Governor if anyone else runs (see Computer players in politics ). Next it will change which events happen (see Social events ) and how fast the colony grows (see Mood and migration ). Why it is like this The mood is one gauge whose value shapes events, not a second event system (G-5, approved by Heiko 28 Oct). The monthly cap stops it flip-flopping. Welfare spending counts 0 until the budget lines exist (Heiko, G-5 card, 4 Nov: \"5. ok\"). The weights are WS1's placeholders, tuned at the end. In the code: packages/engine/src/colony.ts::moodTarget · ::moodStep · ::moodBand · ::wealthGap · config.ts::HAPPINESS · G-5 RULE-20 Kept current by: WS7 (the rule), WS1 (the numbers)."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"colony-fund","title":"The colony fund and its ledger","status":"In the game","text":"The colony fund is the colony's own money. It is separate from the store, which trades with its own capital. The fund never goes below 0 and never borrows: when it cannot pay a bill in full, it pays what it has. Every credit in or out is recorded under where it came from or what it paid for, and each month's report carries that ledger. Money in How much Sales tax On every sale a player makes, at the rate the Governor sets (it starts at 5 %). The seller pays it out of the sale. The Federation's grant 150 cr every month end. Flat for now; a rule that scales it is part of the final tuning. Law taxes Export tax, land tax and pollution tax, when those laws are in force (see The council ). Donations What players give toward a building (see Donations ). The Federation tax event When it fires, 10 % of each player's cash. Money out How much Office pay The Governor, 50 cr a month. Projects Credits and missing goods for the building being funded. Upkeep Each standing building's monthly upkeep. Repairs Mending a damaged building. Strike pay Paying off a general strike, 250 cr. Site bids A plot the colony wins at the land auction for a building."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"fund-month-end","title":"The month end, in order","status":"","text":"The land tax, then the pollution tax, from each player in seat order. The Federation's grant of 150 cr. The Governor's pay. The colony's buildings: upkeep is paid, buildings under construction tick on, and the building being funded gets money (see Commissioning ). The mood moves toward its target, and the gauges' month-end snapshot is taken."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"fund-reserve","title":"The reserve","status":"","text":"When the fund pays toward a building or bids for a site, it keeps 100 cr back, so next month's office pay is there. The reserve does not hold back a repair or strike pay: the Governor may empty the fund for those. Why it is like this Heiko ruled the income mix on 4 Nov (\"3. all three\"): the three law taxes, donations and the Federation's grant. One place in the engine writes to the fund, so the ledger can never miss a payment. In the code: packages/engine/src/colony.ts::fundIn · ::fundOut · ::payGrant · economy.ts::taxSale · game.ts (upkeep order) · config.ts::COLONY · ::TAX · G-5 RULE-27 Kept current by: WS7."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"prestige-objects","title":"Prestige objects: what the colony can build","status":"In the game","text":"The Governor can commission one of five buildings, called prestige objects. Each costs credits plus some goods, takes months to build, earns prestige while it stands, and costs upkeep every month. Building Credits Goods Build months Needs a plot Prestige Upkeep a month Repair Park 420 2 Alloy 1 Yes 2 30 150 Hospital 680 1 Electronics 2 No 3 45 225 Observatory 700 3 Crystite 2 No 3 50 250 Stadium 1,000 2 Alloy, 1 Electronics 3 Yes 4 70 350 Spaceport upgrade 1,380 2 Alloy, 2 Electronics 3 Yes 5 100 500 At the goods' reference prices (Alloy 90 , Electronics 220 , Crystite 100 ) the full costs are 600 , 900 , 1,000 , 1,400 and 2,000 cr; about 30% of each is goods."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"object-perks","title":"What each one does besides prestige","status":"","text":"The phone shows each building's perk. The perks belong to the social events and only work once those are in the game. Building Perk as the phone shows it Built? Park Calms the colony in a riot Waits for the riot (designed) Hospital Softens an epidemic Not built; no rule reads it yet Observatory Prestige only Nothing to build Stadium Festivals come more often Being built: each standing Stadium adds 5 points to the festival's chance (colony branch) Spaceport upgrade Prestige only Nothing to build"},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"object-stages","title":"A building's life","status":"","text":"Stage (phone label) What it means Counts prestige? Commissioned (\"Collecting\") Being paid for: credits, goods and, if it needs one, a plot. No Building Fully paid and sited; counts down its build months, one per month end. No Standing Built and open. Pays its upkeep at each month end. Yes Closed The fund could not pay its upkeep. It reopens at the first month end the fund can pay. No Damaged Damaged by a riot. Pays no upkeep. Open again once the Governor pays the repair. No Nobody ever spends prestige. The only way it falls is a building that closes or is damaged, and it counts again once reopened or repaired. Why it is like this Heiko, G-5 card, 4 Nov: \"6. zero\" (nobody spends prestige; a closed or damaged building counts 0 until it works again). The table's starting rows are WS1's affordability study; upkeep is about 5% of the credit cost a month and a repair about 25%. All are placeholders until the final tuning. In the code: packages/engine/src/config.ts::PRESTIGE · colony.ts::colonyPrestige · ::runColony · apps/web/src/game/colony.ts::PERK · G-5 RULE-21 Kept current by: WS7."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"commissioning","title":"Commissioning, funding and building","status":"In the game","text":"Only the Governor commissions, in planning, once any vote that month is over. The colony works on one building at a time: while one is being paid for or built, the Governor cannot start another. Each building can be built once per game, and there is no cancel. The phone's catalogue dims a building the Governor cannot start and says why, in the engine's words, for example \"The colony is already building the Hospital\". From the next month end on, the fund pays toward the commissioned building: It pays as many of the missing credits as it can, keeping its 100 cr reserve. For each missing good, it buys what it can from the store at the store's selling price: no more than is missing, no more than the store will sell, and no more than the fund can pay above its reserve. No sales tax is charged on these. Once every credit and every good is in, and the building has its plot if it needs one, building starts. It does not count down until the next month end. Players can speed this up by donating credits or the goods it still needs (see Donations ). Example: the Observatory needs 3 Crystite The Governor commissions the Observatory in month 4. At that month end, after the grant and the Governor's pay, the fund holds 900 cr. It pays the 700 cr at once and has 200 left, so only 100 above its reserve. The store sells Crystite at about 110 cr and starts the game with none on its shelves, so the fund buys nothing. The Observatory waits, fully paid in credits, for its 3 Crystite. In month 5 a player who mines Crystite donates 3 units: the plaque gets their name with 300 cr (3 × the reference price of 100 ), and building starts. It stands after two more month ends, at the end of month 7, and pays 50 cr upkeep from the end of month 8. Why it is like this \"One project open at a time\" keeps the colony's money from being spread thin. The fund buying missing goods from the store was a question put to Heiko on 4 Nov; the engine has it on behind a switch ( COLONY.fundBuysGoods ), so that only donors' goods count once it is turned off. What is changing Whether the fund should buy goods from the store at all is still listed as open with Heiko in the PM's log of 4 Nov (the switch is on today). Owner: WS7, decision Heiko. In the code: packages/engine/src/colony.ts::commission · ::commissionRefusal · ::stillNeeds · ::runColony · config.ts::COLONY.maxOpen · ::COLONY.reserve · G-5 RULE-21, RULE-22 Kept current by: WS7."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"donations","title":"Donations and plaques","status":"In the game","text":"Any player can donate in planning toward the building being funded: credits, or units of a good it still needs, or both. You cannot give more credits than it still needs, a good it does not need, more units than are missing, or more than you have. Each refusal says exactly why (\"The Observatory still needs 2 crystite\"). Every donation goes on the building's plaque under your name, valued at credits plus each good at its reference price. The same value counts as taxes you have paid, which can earn you votes (see Your votes ). At game over, each player's plaques are shown beside their Worth. Example Kai gives the Hospital 200 cr and 1 Electronics (reference price 220 ). His plaque reads 420 , and 420 is added to his taxes paid. That alone takes him past 300 , worth one extra vote. What is changing Computer players do not donate yet. WS9 has an approved plan for it (C28): placeholders per persona in the politics config, a decision of how much to give, and a test. Status: designed, not on any branch I could find. Open question for Heiko: his scoring decision of 28 Oct said \"colony donations count 1:1\" in Worth. The colony score rule approved by the PM on 5 Nov shows plaques beside Worth, never in it, and that is what the game does: a donation lowers your Worth by what you gave. Owner: WS7, decision Heiko. In the code: packages/engine/src/colony.ts::donate · game.ts::playerStanding · apps/web/src/components/ColonyActions.tsx (Donate) · G-5 RULE-23 Kept current by: WS7; WS9 for computer players' donations."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"site-bid","title":"The colony's site and its bid","status":"In the game","text":"The Park, the Stadium and the Spaceport upgrade need a plot. The colony gets one at the land auction, bidding from the fund like a player, up to a top bid the Governor sets on the phone (in steps of 25 cr). A newly commissioned building has no bid, so the colony does not bid until the Governor sets one; everyone's phone shows \"Waits for a plot: the Governor sets the colony's bid\" until then. The colony bids as a proxy , the way an auction site bids for you: on any player's plot that comes up, it leads at one credit over the best player's bid (or at the lot's minimum if nobody bids), as long as that stays under its cap. Its cap is the Governor's top bid or what the fund holds above its 100 cr reserve, whichever is lower. A player who bids up to the cap takes the lead. It never bids for MULEs or for the colony's own lots. If the colony wins, the fund pays the seller (who pays sales tax as on any sale), any MULE on the plot goes back to the corral, and the plot becomes the colony's, bare, as the building's site. A plot with a building on it produces nothing. Example The Park waits for a plot. The Governor sets the bid to 300 cr; the fund holds 350 , so the cap is 250 (350 minus the 100 reserve). Brix lists a plot with a minimum of 120 . Lena bids 180 : the colony leads at 181 . Lena walks to 250 , the cap: now she leads, and the colony stops. Had she stopped at 200, the colony would have won at 201 . Why it is like this Heiko, G-5 card, 4 Nov: \"4. it bids\". The proxy rule was approved by the PM on 5 Nov (G-5 AM-8). In the code: packages/engine/src/colony.ts::setSiteBid · ::siteProject · lots.ts::colonyBid · ::lotLeader · G-5 RULE-28 Kept current by: WS7 (the bid), WS3 (the land auction)."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"elections","title":"Offices and elections","status":"In the game","text":"The colony elects a Governor in months 1 , 4 , 7 and 10 . A term runs until the next election. The Governor is paid 50 cr a month from the fund (as far as it reaches), and every term served adds 1,200 cr to that player's Worth, about one good month's income."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"running","title":"Running for office","status":"","text":"You can declare in any month's planning, not only in election months: \"Throw your hat in the ring\" on the phone. Running for Governor carries a tax promise, one of three: Cap: the tax will be no higher than a percent you name ( 0 to 15 ). Lower: the tax will be below where it is when you take office. Hold: the tax will stay where it is when you take office. You can change your promise or withdraw until the field closes. It closes as an election month opens (in month 1, whose planning is the first, it stays open through the announcement). Nobody may serve more than 2 Governor terms in a row; after that someone else must hold it before they can run again."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"election-day","title":"Election day","status":"","text":"The announcement. Every phone and the TV show who runs and on what promise. Each player taps Ready to vote. The secret ballot. If two or more people run, each player picks one or abstains, then taps Cast my vote. Nobody, not even the TV, sees whom you chose. There is no clock: it closes once everyone has voted, or when the host closes it for someone who is away. The reveal. On the TV and every phone at once: the office's title, the candidates, then one block per vote growing beside each name, then the winner and the congratulations. One office with two candidates and six votes takes about 13 seconds. Votes are weighted (see Your votes ). Weights are taken as the ballot closes. The Governor then sets the sales tax."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"election-outcomes","title":"How the winner is found","status":"","text":"Situation Result Nobody runs The richest player (by cash) is appointed. An appointed Governor made no promise. One person runs Unopposed; no ballot. Most weighted votes Wins. A tie The Land Baron's ballot decides, if the Baron voted for one of the tied; otherwise the tied candidate with the most plots; otherwise a fair draw."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"sheriff-office","title":"The Sheriff and the Land Baron","status":"","text":"Only the Governor is elected today. The Sheriff comes back when it has powers (see Sabotage and the Sheriff ); its pay of 30 cr a month is in the config but nobody draws it. The Land Baron is not an office: it is a title, fixed at the start of each planning phase, for whoever owns strictly the most plots. On a tie for most, the current Baron keeps it if they are among the tied; otherwise nobody holds it. The Baron gets 1 extra vote and breaks tied elections. The Baron's 10% discount at the land auction and choosing which lot goes first (design 05) are not built. Why it is like this Heiko, 28 Oct: \"I should be able to run for office anytime. Even in no election months ... time for diplomacy and to adjust my promise\", and the ballot closes once everybody has voted, then a dramatic reveal, office by office. On the Sheriff, Heiko 28 Oct: \"Governor only for now\". The month-1 tie-break used to favour the host's seat; the PM approved the Baron-then-land-then-draw order on 28 Oct. Office credit of 1,200 a term was WS1's measurement of a good month's Worth gain, accepted the same day. In the code: packages/engine/src/politics.ts::runFor · ::canRun · ::stepElection · ::decide · ::appointee · ::landBaron · ::officeScore · config.ts::POLITICS (electionMonths, offices, maxGovernorTermsInRow, pay, termScore, ceremony) · apps/web/src/phone/hub/politics/ElectionDay.tsx · Reveal.tsx · Run.tsx Kept current by: WS7."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"vote-weight","title":"Your votes: vote weight","status":"In the game","text":"Everyone has 1 vote, plus extras worked out fresh every time a ballot closes. The same weights count in elections and law votes. The TV's election screen shows everyone's votes, and your phone shows your own on each ballot. Part Votes Base 1 Land +1 per 10 plots you own Reputation +1 at 70 or more, −1 below 30 (everyone starts at 50 ) Land Baron +1 Taxes and donations paid into the fund +1 per 300 cr paid over the game, at most +2 \"Taxes paid\" counts the sales tax you paid on your sales, the export, land and pollution taxes you paid, and the value of your donations. Example Lena owns 12 plots, the most, so she is the Land Baron. Her reputation is 50. She has paid 650 cr in taxes and donations. Her votes: 1 base + 1 land + 0 reputation + 1 Baron + 2 taxes (650 ÷ 300, rounded down) = 5 . In the code: packages/engine/src/politics.ts::voteWeight · config.ts::POLITICS.weight · ::START.reputation Kept current by: WS7."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"sales-tax","title":"The Governor's sales tax and promises","status":"In the game","text":"The sales tax starts at 5 %. Once the month's votes are over, the Governor can set it on the Governor desk, in whole percent from 0 to 15 , and at most 5 points up or down from where it stood when the month began. The desk shows the allowed range and the promise the Governor ran on. At the end of each planning phase the game checks the Governor's promise against the tax as it stands. A broken promise lowers that month's mood target by 10 . Nothing is checked in a planning phase whose election was still open, since the Governor could not set the tax yet. Promise Broken when the tax is Example Cap at N% above N cap 6, tax 7: broken Lower at or above the rate when the term began lower from 5, tax 4: kept Hold anything but the rate when the term began hold at 5, tax 5: kept In the code: packages/engine/src/politics.ts::setSalesTax · ::taxBounds · colony.ts::keepPromise · config.ts::POLITICS.tax · ::TAX.salesTax · apps/web/src/phone/hub/politics/Governor.tsx · G-5 RULE-24 Kept current by: WS7."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"council","title":"The council and its four laws","status":"In the game","text":"Once a month, after the votes are over, the Governor may propose one law, a new setting for a law in force, or the repeal of one. The proposal is voted on at the start of the next month's planning, after that month's election if there is one. If the proposer is no longer Governor by then, the proposal is dropped. Law Settings What it does When it is charged Export tax 10 , 20 or 30 % That share of each unit a player sells to the export ship goes into the fund, after the sales tax and out of the sale. Sales to the store or to another player pay none. At each sale to the ship Land tax 5 , 10 or 20 cr a plot Every owner pays per plot owned. Month end Pollution tax 10 , 20 or 40 cr a point Each player pays per point of colony pollution their plots caused that month. Month end Emission cap Air level 40 , 50 or 60 No MULE may be outfitted for Rare earth or Alloy (no new Rare earth mines or smelters) while the air is above the level. The refusal comes before you pay. When outfitting A player who cannot pay a land or pollution tax pays what they have; the rest is waived, not owed. Every law tax counts toward that player's taxes paid, and so toward votes."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"council-rules","title":"Rules for laws","status":"","text":"At most 3 laws are in force at once. A fourth must name the law it replaces. A proposal that fails cannot be put again for 2 months: one that fails in month 3 can come back in month 6. A law stays in force until it is replaced or repealed. A repeal is a normal proposal. The sales tax is not a law: the Governor sets it alone. Example A land tax of 10 is in force. At month end Ossa owns 7 plots and has 500 cr: she pays 70 . If she had only 40 she would pay 40 and the other 30 would be waived. With an export tax of 20 % and the sales tax at 5 %, a unit sold to the ship for 100 leaves the seller 75 . Why it is like this Heiko, G-5 card, 4 Nov: \"2. thous four now\": the first laws are the land tax, export tax, pollution tax and emission cap, the four that only move money or block an action. The sabotage ban and the Sheriff's fines come with the Sheriff. The other laws in design 05 (price cap, strategic reserve, anti-hoarding, windfall tax, anti-monopoly, eminent domain, green zone) wait for the systems they need. Slice 2a and the colony joined the MVP the same day (\"7. yes i want them in\"). In the code: packages/engine/src/politics.ts::proposeLaw · ::collectLawTaxes · ::chargeExportTax · ::emissionCapped · ::outfitBlocked · config.ts::POLITICS.laws · ::POLITICS.council · slice 2a RULE-41, RULE-43, RULE-44 Kept current by: WS7."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"law-ballot","title":"The law ballot on the phones and the TV council","status":"In the game","text":"All month, every phone except the Governor's carries the pending proposal under This month as \"Next month's vote\", with what it would cost that player (\"For you\"). When the ballot opens it takes the stage on every phone and on the TV. On your phone: the proposal, what it costs you a month (or saves you, for a repeal), Yes or No, then Done. You can change your vote until the last vote is in. There is no clock; the host can close the vote for someone who is away, and the votes as they stand are counted. On the TV: \"Vote on your phone. Nobody sees how.\" The Governor's proposal in large type, what it means, each player's cost, and who has voted, never how. The result: after everyone is Done, the weighted Yes and No totals and Passed or Failed show on every screen at once for 4 seconds. A passed law takes effect when that ends. The laws in force then sit as chips along the TV's foot and on the Council desk (\"Land tax 10 cr · since month 3\"). The Governor's own ballot starts at Yes, and the Governor's ballot breaks a tie: a tie passes if the Governor voted Yes."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"law-cost-preview","title":"What a law would cost you","status":"","text":"Law \"For you\", a month Land tax The setting × the plots you own. Pollution tax The setting × the pollution your plots caused last month (0 before the first production). Export tax The setting's share of what your stock of the goods the ship wants would fetch off-world, if you sold it to the ship. Emission cap Whether it would block outfits now, and how many of your plots are Rare earth mines or smelters. A repeal The same figure, as a saving. What is changing The TV's line under the vote count says \"Each vote weighs as it did at the election\". The engine actually weighs each vote as the law ballot closes, so a player who bought land since the election may have more votes than then. Owner: WS8 (the TV words), WS7 to confirm. In the code: packages/engine/src/politics.ts::castLawVote · ::stepVotes · ::tally · ::lawImpact · apps/web/src/phone/hub/politics/LawBallot.tsx · LawPicker.tsx · slots.ts · apps/web/src/table/TvCouncil.tsx · slice 2a RULE-40, RULE-42, RULE-45, RULE-47 Kept current by: WS7 (rules), WS8 (the picker, the ballot and the TV council)."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"colony-desks","title":"The colony desks on your phone","status":"In the game","text":"In planning, politics lives on three desks of the phone's hub. All three appear only in a politics game. Desk Who sees it What is on it Colony Everyone The colony fund: how much is in it, what fills it (the sales tax and who sets it) and the Governor's pay. For the Governor, \"Commission a project\": the five buildings with their cost, build months, plot need and upkeep, one the colony cannot start dimmed with the reason. The colony card: the four gauges, then the build site, each building at its stage with its buttons: Donate, the site bid (the Governor sets it; others see it), Repair (the Governor). Then the Land Baron and the air. Council Everyone The laws in force, this month's result once the election is over, the pending proposal with your cost, the last election's result with each candidate's votes and how the office was won, running for office with your promise, and what the Governor does. Governor The Governor only; it opens first The sales tax within this month's range, with the promise in view; then next month's law: all four laws with their settings as chips, \"This replaces:\" when three are in force, each player's cost before Propose, and Change or Withdraw while it is pending. The hub's This month list also carries \"Throw your hat in the ring\" (while an election is still to come and you may run), \"Next month's vote\" and, for the Governor, \"Governor's desk\". Every button sends one request; the server decides, and a refusal comes back in the engine's own words. Why it is like this The desks are grafted from WS8's approved desk mock (C35 and C46 share one mock). The colony screens follow the mock Heiko approved on 4 Nov. What is changing Being built in the colony branch: the general strike on the phones and the TV, with the Governor's pay-off choice in the phone's Now card (PR #368, in review; see The general strike ), and the colony's mood events landing on the Happiness gauge (PR #364, ready). In the code: apps/web/src/phone/hub/politics/Colony.tsx::ColonyWithFacts · Council.tsx · Governor.tsx · slots.ts · apps/web/src/components/ColonyActions.tsx · apps/web/src/components/Colony.tsx::ColonyDeskCard Kept current by: WS7 (desks), WS8 (colony card), WS6 (hub)."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"ai-governor","title":"Computer players in politics","status":"In the game","text":"Every computer seat, Classic or Smart, plays politics with the same built-in rules; Claude does not take politics decisions yet. What a computer player does depends on its persona: Trader, Cautious, Aggressive or Saboteur. On election day it waits a human's pause ( 0.8 to 2.5 seconds) before it acts and presses Done; on a law ballot it votes and presses Done at once, so no ballot waits for a computer seat."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"ai-running","title":"Running and voting","status":"","text":"Persona Runs for Governor Tax it steers toward Proposes its best law Trader 50% 5% 50% Cautious 30% 4% 30% Aggressive 80% 8% 70% Saboteur 60% 10% 60% When it runs: it decides once per election, in the planning phase one month before (for month 1, at month 1's announcement). Its promise follows its tax target: a cap at its target if that is above today's tax, \"lower\" if below, \"hold\" if equal. (There are chances for running for Sheriff too, but no Sheriff is elected.) How it votes for Governor: for itself if it runs; otherwise for the candidate with the fewest plots, so the table leans against the leader (a tie is a fair draw; a Rookie picks at random). In an Unhappy or Unrest colony it will not vote for the sitting Governor if anyone else runs. How it votes on a law: the Governor always votes Yes. The Land Baron always votes No to a land tax. A Rookie flips a coin. Otherwise it votes Yes to a new law if the law costs it no more than the middle player's cost, and Yes to a repeal if the law costs it more than the middle player."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"ai-as-governor","title":"What a computer Governor does","status":"","text":"Each planning phase, once the votes are over, a computer Governor acts in this order: Sets the tax. One step toward its persona's target, at most 5 points, plus 2 points when the fund is low (from month 2, under three months of the Governor's pay, so under 150 cr), always inside the month's allowed range. Proposes a law. It looks at every law: each law not in force at its middle setting (export tax 20%, land tax 10, pollution tax 20, emission cap 50) and the repeal of each law in force. It picks the one that costs the leader (the other player with the highest Worth) most compared with itself. It proposes only if that gap is at least 20 cr a month, and then only with its persona's chance. With three laws in force, the new one replaces the law that costs it most. Pays a general strike if its persona does and the fund holds the 250 cr. Repairs damaged buildings, the most prestige first, while the fund covers the cost, if its persona does. Bids for sites: gives every building waiting for a plot its persona's top bid. Commissions the building with the most prestige whose credits the fund holds now (the cheaper one on a tie), if its persona commissions and the colony is free to start one. It picks a building that needs a plot only if its persona bids for sites, and sets that bid at once. Persona Commissions Pays strikers Repairs Top site bid Trader Yes Yes Yes 400 Cautious Yes Yes Yes 300 Aggressive Yes No Yes 500 Saboteur No No No Never bids Example An Aggressive computer is Governor in month 5. The tax stands at 5% and the fund at 1,100 cr. Its target is 8%, so it sets 8% (a step of 3). The fund is not low, so there is no bump. Nothing is damaged and there is no strike. The Spaceport upgrade's 1,380 cr is more than the fund holds; the Stadium's 1,000 is not, and it has the most prestige of the rest (4). It commissions the Stadium and bids up to 500 cr for its plot. Had a Saboteur been Governor, it would have raised the tax toward 10% and built nothing. Why it is like this The PM approved the computer Governor's colony steps on 5 Nov under Heiko's design authority: pay the strike, then repair the most prestige first, then commission the most prestige it can afford (plot-needing only with a site budget), then set the site bid. The council's own draws use a separate seeded stream per seat and month, so adding the council moved no other dice in a replay. All persona numbers are placeholders until the sim tunes them. What is changing Computer players donating (C28, WS9, designed). Claude taking politics decisions for Smart seats, and making deals, waits for the deal cards (see Reputation and deal cards ). In the code: packages/ai/src/hands/vote.ts::VoteHand · ::nextTax · config.ts::POLITICS.ai (runGovernor, taxTarget, taxFundBump, fundMonths, council, colony) · slice 2a RULE-46 · G-5 C25 Kept current by: WS7 (rules), WS1 (the hands and the numbers)."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"general-strike","title":"The general strike","status":"Being built","text":"A general strike is a colony event announced in planning. The colonists who work one good stop: this month every working plot of that good makes only 20 % of its output, unless the Governor pays the strikers 250 cr from the fund. Paid, the plots produce in full. The strike hits the good that the most working plots make (a tie is settled on the event's dice). With no working plots there is no strike. Only the Governor can pay, in that month's planning, once the votes are over. The reserve does not hold back: paying may empty the fund. If the fund holds less than 250 , the Governor cannot pay and the strike holds. With no Governor, nobody can pay. Unpaid, production makes 20 % (rounded down) of each struck plot's output, after every other effect on it. Its chance rises with a bad mood: +2 points a month when Restless, +5 Unhappy, +10 in Unrest, +3 more when the store's Food costs more than 1.5 times its usual 30 cr. It never happens in a Thriving colony, and never in the same month as a festival. Example The colony is Unhappy and Food sells at 55 . The strike's chance this planning is 5 + 3 = 8 points. It fires on Food. Lena, the Governor, has 400 in the fund and pays: the fund drops to 150 , the news says \"Lena pays the food strikers 250 cr\", and every Food plot produces in full. Had she not, a plot that would make 5 Food makes 1."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"strike-status","title":"What is on trunk and what is not","status":"","text":"On trunk today: the pay-off rule, the server's pay request and the computer Governor paying by persona. But no event starts a strike on trunk yet, and the phone has no button to pay one, so a strike cannot happen in a trunk game. Why it is like this The catalogue Heiko approved (rev 22) says a strike is announced now and this month every plot of one good makes 20% unless the Governor pays. The share has one number, COLONY.strike.output , beside the cost (PM ruling 5 Nov). On how it looks, the PM approved WS8's option A on 5 Nov: the Governor's choice sits in the phone's Now card, so the map stays in view and the decision cannot drift. What is changing The event and its production effect (C26 PR-A, PR #370) merged into the colony branch on 5 Nov; it reaches trunk with the next colony batch. The strike on the TV and phones, with the Governor's Now card, a STRIKE badge in the TV header and \"Waiting for Kai, the Governor\" on the TV, is PR #368 (in review, changes requested 5 Nov and since fixed). In the code: packages/engine/src/colony.ts::callStrike · ::strikeHolds · ::payStrike · config.ts::COLONY.strike (trunk: cost ; colony branch adds output: 0.2 ) · colony branch events.ts::strikeEvent , config.ts::SOCIAL_EVENTS.colony.generalStrike · G-5 RULE-26 Kept current by: WS7 (the pay-off), WS1 (the event), WS8 (screens)."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"social-events","title":"Social events: the mood shapes what happens","status":"Being built","text":"The colony's mood tilts the odds of the colony's social events. It does not make more events in total: it changes which ones come. Each event rolls on its own dice like every other event. A mood band can add to an event's chance, and some events can never happen in some bands. None of these happen with politics off."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"planning-social-events","title":"The planning events (PR-A, in the colony branch)","status":"","text":"Event Chance a month Never in What it does Festival 1 %; +5 Thriving, +1 Content, +5 for each standing Stadium Unhappy, Unrest; never with a strike Mood +10 at once; +2 prestige for good; the colonists eat 25 % more Food this month General strike 0; +2 Restless, +5 Unhappy, +10 Unrest, +3 when Food is dear Thriving See The general strike Emigration wave 0; +1 Restless, +4 Unhappy, +8 Unrest, +3 when the air is Toxic or in Collapse none 10 % of the colonists leave for good Immigration wave (its mood part) its 2 %; +6 Thriving, +1 Content Unhappy, Unrest The colony grows by 10 % (as today) Blamed for the unrest (each player's own roll) 0; +2 Unhappy, +5 Unrest, +2 for the richest player none yet (see below) That player loses 1 reputation Every chance is capped at 90 %. Chances are in percentage points per month, and all are placeholders."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"riot","title":"Riots and the other events (PR-B, designed)","status":"","text":"PR-B adds the events that roll in development, production and the month end: the store strike, MULE theft's mood part (development); the walkout and the loyal crew (production, personal); the riot and the volunteer clean-up (month end). Ruled so far: The riot: for each good, the store loses a third of its stock, rounded down, so a shelf of 1 or 2 loses nothing. The share is a named config fraction, 1/3 . A riot can also damage a colony building (which then counts no prestige until repaired; see Prestige objects ); the event picks the building on its own dice. A standing Park calms the colony in a riot. Blamed for the unrest will never happen in a Thriving or Content colony. The immigration wave gets air parts: more likely in clean air, never when the air is Toxic. Example: a riot at the store The store holds 7 Food, 2 Water and 12 Smithore. A riot takes 2 Food (7 ÷ 3, rounded down), no Water (2 ÷ 3 rounds to 0) and 4 Smithore. The event's line shows exactly what was lost. Why it is like this G-5 (Heiko, 28 Oct) asks that unhappy months draw at least twice the strikes and thefts of thriving months, and that a strike cuts the struck good's plots to 20%. The rows come from the event catalogue Heiko approved (rev 22). The PM ruled the riot's store loss (floor of stock × 1/3, catalogue 04:149), \"blamed\" never in Thriving or Content, and the immigration wave's air parts on 5 Nov. What is changing PR-A (#370) merged into the colony branch on 5 Nov and reaches trunk with the next colony batch. PR-B is designed, due from WS1 next. Not yet settled in what I could read: the riot's chance by mood band, which building it damages and by how much the Park calms it; and the immigration wave's exact clean-air figure (the ruling reads \"Clean air +1\"). Owner: WS1. Balance question: \"blamed for the unrest\" costs 1 reputation, and reputation only changes votes below 30 , from a start of 50 . As built it would take 21 blamings to lose a vote. Owner: WS1 and WS7, at the final tuning. The colony's mood events landing on the TV's Happiness gauge (PR #364) and the event shows for all of these (WS8) are being built. In the code (colony branch): packages/engine/src/config.ts::SOCIAL_EVENTS · events.ts (mood, neverIn, objects, foodPrice in the chance) · colony.ts::addPrestige · ::moodShift · politics.ts::reputationShift · G-5 C26, AM-11 Kept current by: WS1 (events), WS7 (the colony's writers), WS8 (event shows)."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"migration","title":"Mood and migration","status":"Designed","text":"Today the colony grows by 4 % in a month its colonists were fed and shrinks by 5 % when they went hungry, whatever the mood. The design says the mood will scale that growth and shrinkage: a happy colony attracts settlers, an unhappy one loses them. How much is not set yet; the numbers will live with the mood's. What is changing C27, after C26's PR-B, on WS1's queue (it was planned for the evening of 5 Nov). Owner: WS1. In the code: packages/engine/src/upkeep.ts::runUpkeep · config.ts::UPKEEP.colonistGrowth · G-5 C27 Kept current by: WS1."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"colony-score","title":"The colony score at game over","status":"In the game","text":"At game over the colony gets its own score, beside the players' Worth. The winner is still the player with the highest Worth; the colony score says how the colony did. It is worked out from the final state alone, so a replay of the same seed and moves gives the same score. Term How it is counted Wealth The Wealth gauge: the fund plus everything every player owns, in credits. Environment 100 cr for each point the air is cleaner than 100 . Prestige 1,000 cr for each point of prestige standing. Happiness factor From 0.8 at happiness 0 to 1.2 at happiness 100, in a straight line. Colony score = (Wealth + Environment + Prestige) × the happiness factor , rounded once. Each player's standing at the end is their Worth, with the plaques they earned shown beside it, not added to it. The TV shows the colony score as a band under the winner screen, with the sum, and who gave most. Example The colony ends with Wealth 26,400 , air at 34 , the Park standing (prestige 2 ) and happiness 64 . Environment is 100 × (100 − 34) = 6,600 . Prestige is 1,000 × 2 = 2,000 . The factor is 0.8 + 0.4 × 0.64 = 1.056 . The score is (26,400 + 6,600 + 2,000) × 1.056 = 36,960 . The same colony at happiness 0 would score 28,000; at 100, 42,000. A colony whose air ends in Collapse still cuts every player's Worth by the Collapse rule (× 0.8 ); that cut does not touch the colony score's terms. Why it is like this Heiko's scoring model, 28 Oct: one Worth number decides the winner; the colony score is separate, \"colony wealth + air + prestige, × mood (~0.8 unrest .. 1.2 thriving)\". The PM approved the exact formula on 5 Nov (RULE-25). The rates are placeholders, tuned at the end. What is changing The end screen's verdict line has placeholder thresholds: 100 or more reads \"A colony to be proud of\", 50 or more \"A colony that held together\", below that \"A colony in ruins\". Real scores are in the tens of thousands, so today every colony that is not in Collapse reads \"A colony to be proud of\". Owner: WS8, with WS7 for the scale. Heiko's 28 Oct model also said the colony score is \"shown every month next to the league\". Today it is worked out only at game over; during the game the four gauges stand in for it. Owner: WS7, decision Heiko. In the code: packages/engine/src/game.ts::colonyScoreOf · ::colonyScore · ::playerStanding · config.ts::COLONY_SCORE · apps/web/src/game/colony.ts::scoreVerdict · apps/web/src/components/Colony.tsx · G-5 RULE-25 Kept current by: WS7 (the rule), WS3 (the score in the engine and the replay), WS8 (the end screen)."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"reputation-deals","title":"Reputation and deal cards","status":"Backlog","text":"Every player has a reputation from 0 to 100 , starting at 50 , and it already counts in vote weight ( +1 at 70 or more, −1 below 30). On trunk nothing changes it yet, so it is 50 for everyone all game. In the colony branch, \"blamed for the unrest\" takes 1 point. The design (politics slice 3) makes reputation move: a deal card attached to a whisper turns a promise into something one tap accepts (\"a non-aggression pact\", \"my vote for your law\"), and breaking a signed card costs reputation, so it costs votes next time. Deal cards carry pacts, votes and promises only; nothing of value ever changes hands outside an auction. Borrowed votes (another player's votes for one month, by deal card) are part of the same design. Computer players may bluff about their own intentions, never about facts (Heiko, 28 Oct). What is changing Slice 3 is outside the MVP and runs in parallel after it (Heiko, 4 Nov); no lane is on it now. Claude seats winning allies through deal cards (WS2's social play) waits for it. Owner: WS7. In the code: config.ts::START.reputation · ::POLITICS.weight · design docs/design/05-colony-politics.md · docs/specs/2026-10-22-ws7-politics.md (P-2, slice 3) Kept current by: WS7."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"sabotage-sheriff","title":"Sabotage and the Sheriff","status":"Backlog","text":"There is no sabotage order and no Sheriff in the game today. The design (politics slice 4): players order sabotage in secret; the elected Sheriff guards plots (a sabotage against a guarded plot fails and reveals the saboteur), investigates one past sabotage, theft or broken deal a month, fines a revealed culprit (half to the victim, half to the fund), inspects one player a month for a broken law, and can open an inquiry into the Governor. The sabotage ban law (fines doubled or tripled) comes with it. The Sheriff is paid 30 cr a month; nobody may hold Governor and Sheriff at once. Computer personas already carry a spite weight and a per-level sabotage switch (off for Rookie), used today only to bid up the leader in the auction. Why it is like this Heiko, 28 Oct: \"Governor only for now\": the Sheriff is elected only once slice 4 gives it powers. Heiko, 4 Nov: the sabotage ban and the Sheriff's fines go with slice 4, outside the MVP. In the code: config.ts::POLITICS.offices · ::POLITICS.pay.sheriff · ::AI_PERSONAS (spite) · ::AI_LEVELS (sabotage) · design 05 \"The Sheriff\" Kept current by: WS7."},{"page":"colony-and-politics.html","id":"budget-overrule","title":"The budget lines, overrule and inquiry","status":"Backlog","text":"Today the Governor spends the fund only on buildings, repairs, site bids and strike pay. The design (politics slice 5) has the Governor split the fund each month across five budget lines (Reserve for emergency imports, Security for the Sheriff's guards, Environment for clean-up squads, Welfare for the last-placed player, Projects for building and upkeep), with a protective kind of colony project (reservoir, levee, meteor shield and others) on the same pipeline as the prestige objects. Any player could call an overrule vote against the Governor's tax or budget once a month; twice overruled in a term means a snap election. The Sheriff's inquiry could lead to a no-confidence vote. What is changing Outside the MVP; no lane is on it. Welfare will then feed the mood (it counts 0 until then). Owner: WS7. In the code: design docs/design/05-colony-politics.md \"Taxes and the budget\", \"Colony projects\" · G-5 non-goals Kept current by: WS7."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"how-it-is-built","title":"How a computer player is built","status":"In the game","text":"A computer player is a seat at the table like yours. It sees only what a person in that seat would see (its own goods and money, the public board, the plots it has analysed) and it plays only the moves a phone can send. It never reads another player's stock, the game's dice or ground it has not assayed. Every move it makes is checked by the same rules as yours. Inside, it has layers. The thinking happens once per decision; the hands then carry it out in real time, tick by tick, at a person's speed. Layer What it does When it runs Strategist Lists every option for the decision (which plan for land, which MULE on which plot, buy or sell how much), scores each with named reasons, throws out the ones a hard rule forbids, and hands the best few to the decider. At each decision Value model Says what each unit of each good, each plot and each MULE is worth to this seat, in credits. Inside the strategist Outlook Plays the rest of the month forward on a private copy of what the seat can see, to forecast next month's prices. Inside the value model Utility scoring Turns each option's reasons into one score from 0 to 1, shaped by the seat's persona. Inside the strategist Decider Picks one option from the shortlist and says a line at the table. The built-in decider (Classic) picks by weighted chance among the near-best; Claude can pick instead (Smart). At each decision Hands Carry the pick out: press Claim when a good plot lights up, walk the floor to the corral and the plots, walk the auction board up to a price limit and never past it, bid on lots, vote. Every server tick Playbook A whole month's plan in one document: the strategy, what to list, which plots to want, the development route, and per good how to trade. The engine always drafts one; Claude may edit it. At the start of each phase Why it is like this Heiko decided on 21 October 2026 that real strength comes from a strategist written in code that uses the game's own engine to look ahead, with a small language model making the final call in character. Code does the arithmetic well and costs nothing; the model makes the seat feel like a person. The game must never wait for a model, so if it is slow, broken or out of budget, the strategist's own top pick plays. Also decided that day: light look-ahead only (no deep search), and the model picks from 3 to 5 near-best options. In the code: packages/ai/src/seat.ts::AiSeat · packages/ai/src/strategist.ts · packages/ai/src/brain.ts::Brain · docs/design/06-ai-players.md Kept current by: WS1 (strategist), WS9 (playbooks, utility), WS2 (Claude)."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"seats-and-levels","title":"Seats: Classic and Smart, levels and names","status":"In the game","text":"Every empty seat at a table can be filled by a computer player. Each one has a name, which fixes its persona, and a level."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"seat-names","title":"Names and personas","status":"","text":"Name at the table Persona The Accountant Cautious (“The careful one”) The Baron Aggressive (“The baron”) The Grudge Saboteur (“The grudge”) The Gambler Trader (“The trader”) The Good Neighbour Cautious A seat with any other name gets a persona by its seat number: trader, aggressive, cautious, saboteur, in turn."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"classic-and-smart","title":"Classic and Smart","status":"","text":"Classic : the strategist and the built-in decider. It costs nothing, always answers at once, and its chat lines come from short templates per persona. Smart : the same strategist, but Claude makes the pick and writes the chat line in character (see Claude as a player ). Today the lobby has no Smart or Classic switch. If the server holds an API key, every computer seat at the table is Smart; without a key, every seat is Classic. A Smart seat that runs out of budget, or whose call fails or is late, plays that decision as Classic, so the game never stalls."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"levels","title":"Levels","status":"","text":"There are three levels. They differ in how complete the scoring is and how quick the hands are, never in what the seat may see. Real tables play at Trader level today; the lobby does not offer a choice yet. The simulator can run any level. Setting Rookie Trader Tycoon Forecasts next month's prices (look-ahead) no, uses today's yes yes Counts spoilage, the depot limit and the air (full scoring) no yes yes Options on the shortlist, at most 3 4 5 Shortlist takes options within this share of the best 0.35 0.2 0.12 The built-in decider picks within this share of the best 0.25 0.1 0.04 Reaction time, ms 320–550 250–400 200–320 Development pace (times a quick human's step time) 1.4 1.1 1 MULEs it leads out a month, at most 1 2 3 Chance it misses a plot it wanted in the land grant 0.25 0.05 0 May corner a good no yes yes May act on spite against the leader no yes yes What is changing A lobby picker for Smart and Classic seats and for the level was a non-goal of the first Claude build; it is not designed yet. Until then the server's key decides Smart for every computer seat, and DEFAULT_AI_LEVEL is Trader. Owner: WS2 (Smart) and WS4 (lobby). In the code: packages/engine/src/config.ts::AI_LEVELS · apps/server/src/mule-game.ts::AI_NAMES, DEFAULT_AI_LEVEL · packages/ai/src/personas.ts::personaFor · apps/server/src/brain/index.ts::createBrain Kept current by: WS1, with WS2 for Smart seats."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"personas","title":"Personas","status":"In the game","text":"A persona is a set of weights that tilt every decision. A weight of 1 is neutral; above 1 that reason matters more, below 1 less, 0 ignores it. The same weights also drive a few direct numbers (how much cash to keep back, how much to pay for a corner). Weight What it changes Cautious Aggressive Trader Saboteur risk Dislike of guesses: unanalysed ground, forecasts, buying to resell 1.6 0.6 1.0 0.9 greed How much the gain in credits counts 0.8 1.4 1.1 0.9 reserve Cash kept back (it multiplies the cash reserve) and how much feeding itself counts 1.6 0.7 1.0 1.0 spite Fencing in and bidding against the leader 0 0.3 0 1.5 speculate Willingness to buy now to sell next month 0.3 1.0 1.4 0.6 corner Buying up a scarce good to sell it dear 0 1.0 0.4 0.6 patience How long it waits in an auction before it moves 1.4 0.6 1.0 0.9 Each persona also has a short character card (who it is, how it talks), which Claude reads, and two template lines per kind of decision for Classic seats. For example the cautious seat says “Better safe than sorry: …” when it builds, and the saboteur “Not so fast, leader.” in the auction. In the code: packages/engine/src/config.ts::AI_PERSONAS · packages/ai/src/personas.ts::PERSONAS Kept current by: WS1 (weights), WS2 (cards and lines)."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"utility","title":"How it scores a choice","status":"In the game","text":"Every option gets a handful of considerations : one fact about the game turned into a score from 0 to 1, with a reason a person can read (“makes about 40 cr a month, costs 175 cr”, “ground not analysed”). The persona's weight raises each score to a power. The scores are multiplied together, with a correction so an option with many considerations is not punished for having them. The result is the option's utility . Some options are not scored at all because a hard rule forbids them: a MULE the seat cannot pay for without dipping into its cash reserve, a factory it cannot feed, a fifth MULE when its level allows two a month, selling Food it needs. A forbidden option never reaches the shortlist, and the log says why (“Ruled out: it would leave 40 cr, under the 210 cr kept for upkeep”)."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"gain-curve","title":"The gain curve","status":"","text":"Gains in credits are scored on an S-shaped curve: no gain scores 0.5, a gain of one “scale” scores 0.75, a loss of one scale 0.25. The scale differs per decision, because a plot is worth much more over a game than one auction trade. Decision Gain that scores 0.75 A plot (land plans) 1,200 cr A MULE (development) 300 cr A trade (auction) 80 cr"},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"shortlist","title":"The shortlist and the pick","status":"","text":"The options are sorted by utility. The shortlist keeps the best one and any others within the level's tolerance of it, up to the level's maximum (see Levels ). The decider picks one. The built-in decider keeps only those within its own, tighter tolerance and draws one at random, each with a chance in proportion to its utility. The draw is seeded from the game, so a replay picks the same, yet the seat has no fixed habit a person could learn and exploit. Example Say a Trader-level seat in development has three options left after the hard rules (the numbers are made up to show the arithmetic): a Food MULE on its river plot (utility 0.62), an Energy MULE on the same plot (0.58) and keep the cash (0.41). The shortlist takes options within 0.2 of the best (above 0.496): Food and Energy. The built-in decider takes options within 0.1 of the best (above 0.558): still both. It draws Food with chance 0.62 / 1.20, about 52%, and Energy about 48%. A Tycoon would have kept only Food on its decider list (within 0.04: above 0.595). In the code: packages/ai/src/utility.ts · packages/ai/src/decider.ts::TopPickDecider · packages/engine/src/config.ts::STRATEGIST.gainScale Kept current by: WS1."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"outlook","title":"Looking ahead: the outlook","status":"In the game","text":"To value anything the seat needs next month's prices. The outlook takes a copy of what the seat can see and plays the rest of the month as if nobody builds or trades any more: this month's production, the auction, upkeep (eating, spoilage, the depot limit, the ship), and next month's production. Then it asks the store's own pricing rule what it would post, with or without a few more units of a good. One run of the status quo, no sampling. For the months after next, it plays the same status quo forward 2 more months (and on to the 6 -month horizon to look for shortages). Where that run shows colonists going short of Food or Water, or the corral unable to build its MULEs for want of Smithore, it raises the later price even if today's price has not caught up. A seat at Rookie level does not look ahead: it values everything at today's posted price, which is its built-in mistake. In the code: packages/ai/src/outlook.ts::outlook, rollForward · packages/engine/src/config.ts::STRATEGIST.outlookMonths, horizon Kept current by: WS1."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"value-of-goods","title":"What a good is worth to it","status":"In the game","text":"For each good, the seat builds a list: what each unit it could hold after the auction is worth to it, most valuable first. It keeps two versions: what a unit it already holds is worth (its keep value, the least it will sell for) and what one more unit is worth (its buy value, the most it will pay)."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"value-needed-units","title":"Units it needs","status":"","text":"The units next month's needs will use are worth what going short would cost the seat: Good What going short costs it How many units count as needed Food Walking slower in development. A colonist without Food walks at 0.6 of its pace, so the month's walking takes longer; each second lost is priced at what a second of development is worth to the seat. This month's eating, plus next month's eating grossed up for spoilage Water Only with a Food farm: an unwatered farm makes half. The half lost, at the Food price, spread over the units. As Food, only while it owns a farm Energy Every plot short of Energy makes half: the half of all its other plots' output, at their prices, spread over the need. Its plots' need minus what its own Energy plots make, grossed up for spoilage Medicine Only when the air makes people sick: the chance of falling sick, times the walking lost at half pace. The month's need Factory inputs A factory's run is worth its output; that worth is shared over its inputs. One run's inputs, grossed up for spoilage A second of development time is worth 10 cr while the seat has idle land and the money for a MULE, otherwise 3 cr, and never less than what the pub would pay for it."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"value-spare-units","title":"Spare units","status":"","text":"A unit beyond the need is worth what it would fetch next month: the forecast price, less the sales tax, less what spoils by then, less a safety cut of 0.05 per point of the persona's risk weight because the forecast may be wrong. To buy a spare unit just to resell it the seat also needs a reason to speculate: the resale value is multiplied by its speculate weight (at most 1) and cut a further 0.1 per point of risk. Past what the depot keeps ( 10 units, more for a good that spoils) a unit is worth nothing. A Rookie ignores spoilage, the depot and the air. Food and Energy the seat needs are never sold at any price. When the store cannot feed the colonists, spare Food and Water held for next month's price are capped at what the store pays now, so the seat sells to the hungry colony instead of hoarding."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"cash-reserve","title":"The cash reserve","status":"","text":"The seat always keeps cash back: 60 cr, plus what next month's Food, Water and Energy shortfall would cost at the store's selling price (but no more than 3 times the reference price per unit, so a price spike does not freeze all its cash), all times its persona's reserve weight. It will not spend into this reserve, except to buy the Food and Energy it needs. Example: how a computer player values one unit of Food The Gambler (trader persona, Trader level) in month 3 owns a Food plot and an Energy plot and holds no Food. It has idle land and the money for a MULE, so a second of development is worth 10 cr to it. Its month's walking takes about 20 seconds (WS1 measured about this). It eats 2 + 1 for its crew of 2 = 3 Food a month. The needed block is 3 now plus 3 / 0.5 = 6 for next month (Food spoils by half) = 9 units. Going hungry: 20 s × (1 / 0.6 − 1) = 13.3 s lost, × 10 cr = 133 cr, over 9 units = about 15 cr a unit . Resale: the outlook says Food posts 45 next month. After the 5% tax 42.75, half spoils: 21.4, the risk cut (1 − 0.05) leaves about 20 cr (its keep value). For buying it is cut again (× 1 for speculate, × 0.9 for risk): about 18 cr . Its buy value for each of the 9 needed units is the higher of the two: 18 cr . The store sells Food at its posted price plus 10%: at 45 posted, about 50. The seat's limit is 18, so it does not buy, and goes hungry with money in the bank (WS9 measured hungry seats holding about 1,200 cr). This is exactly what Heiko saw at the table on 5 November. See Buying what it is short of for the fix being built. Why it is like this The design (21 October) asked each unit to be valued by what it does for the seat, not by its market price, so that the seat sells what it does not need and keeps what it does. For Food the only cost of hunger the engine had was slower walking, so that is all the value model counted. Measured on 5 November (WS9, 40 games on each of 3 seeds): a seat came to the Food auction short in 86–87% of its months; in 76–85% it refused on value (its median value 19 cr against store asks of 49–56), although in 99% it could have paid for a unit. In the code: packages/ai/src/value.ts::unitValues, cashReserve, secondValue · packages/engine/src/config.ts::STRATEGIST.forecastHaircut, resaleRisk, cashFloor, reservePriceCap, secondValue · DEV_WALK.hungerPaceMin Kept current by: WS1 (value.ts), WS9 (the utility design)."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"auction","title":"Buying and selling in the goods auction","status":"In the game","text":"When a good comes up, the seat makes a plan for it on the status screen and decides there whether to be a buyer, a seller or stay out, and how far to go. Then its hands walk the board."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"auction-decision","title":"The decision","status":"","text":"Sell : sell down to the last unit worth less to it than the sale would bring after tax. A second option keeps a buffer of half next month's need (at least 1 unit) on top. Buy : buy up to the last unit worth more to it than the store's selling price, within its budget for the good. If some of those units are only for resale, a second option buys just what it needs. Pass . The budget for one good is half its money, and never so much that it dips into the cash reserve. A sale counts as fairly sure (0.9), a buy of what it needs too (0.9), a buy partly to resell less so (0.6), and the persona's risk weight sharpens that. It expects a sale to fetch at least 0.85 of the posted price, or what the store or the ship pays if more."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"auction-hands","title":"On the board: the buyer's limit","status":"","text":"A buyer's limit for its next unit is limit = the lower of (what one more unit is worth to it) and (the money it may spend) where the money it may spend is all its money for Food or Energy it needs, and otherwise what is left of the good's budget. A seller's floor is the keep value of the unit it would give up, raised to cover the sales tax. Both follow its stock as units trade. Then, on every tick: If the best price on the other side is inside its limit, it walks there and trades. If a rival on its own side is ahead and the limit leaves room, it overtakes by one credit and a little more. If the gap to the other side is small ( 3–8% of the board, times its patience), it waits for them to close it. Otherwise it edges toward them, never past its limit. It re-aims only after its reaction time, starts after a delay of 300–1,500 ms times its patience, and wanders its stopping point by up to 3% of the board inside its limit, so its stops are hard to read. At the store's or the ship's line, units trade by themselves; it stays while it still wants units there and steps off after the last."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"auction-corner-spite","title":"Cornering and spite","status":"","text":"At Trader and Tycoon level, a seat with a corner weight may try to corner a good: when the store holds no more than the other players are short of, and the good spoils less than half a month, it adds 0.5 of next month's price times its corner weight to the value of up to 6 units the others lack. A seat with spite, while the leader is short of the good, adds 0.1 of next month's price times its spite to the next 2 units, to keep them from the leader. Unclear in the code Cornering needs a good that spoils less than half a month ( STRATEGIST.cornerMaxSpoilage 0.5), and Food spoils exactly half ( GOOD_CONFIG.food.spoilage 0.5). So Food is never cornered, although the monopoly plan's list ( AI_PLANS.monopoly.goods ) names it. Whether that is meant is for WS1 (strategist) and WS9 (playbooks) to say. What is changing The buyer's limit uses the value model above, which values needed Food far below the store's price. The stopgap in the next section is being built. Heiko's 5 November table notes on the auction itself (slower selling, auto-stop at equilibrium, rest zones) are WS3's; the store's pricing rule is being redesigned by WS5. Both will need the hands checked again. Owner: WS1 for the hands. In the code: packages/ai/src/strategist.ts::goodPlan, tradeDecision, expectedPrices · packages/ai/src/hands/auction.ts::AuctionHand · packages/engine/src/config.ts::AI_HANDS.auction, STRATEGIST.budgetShare, trade, cornerPremium, spiteShare Kept current by: WS1."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"shortfall-stopgap","title":"Buying what it is short of","status":"Being built","text":"Heiko, 5 November, at the table: all the computer players were short of Food while he and the store were selling, and none of them bought. Later two were short of Energy, “which would most likely lead to them not producing anything next month, the worst case”, and stood still in the auction. The PM ruled the same morning: a computer player short of Food or Energy must buy at the store's price when it can afford it. Water was added to the same fix."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"shortfall-cause","title":"What was measured","status":"","text":"WS1 measured the Energy case over 5 seeds: 167 seat-months short of Energy. In 47 of them the store was selling and the seat could pay, but did not buy: Why it did not buy Seat-months It counted its own plots' forecast output as already held 30 Its cash reserve blocked the buy (and the reserve counts that same Energy shortfall) 7 The decider chose Pass 5 It valued a unit below the ask 5 For Food the value itself is the problem: median 19 cr against asks of 49 to 56 (see the worked example )."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"shortfall-rule","title":"The stopgap","status":"","text":"For Food, Energy and Water, while the seat holds less than next month's need as the status screen shows it and the store is selling the good: it counts what it holds , not what its plots may make; each unit of the shortfall is worth at least the store's asking price, so it buys there, or cheaper from a player, never dearer; it buys those units as far as its money goes: the cash reserve and the per-good budget do not stop it; it never sells below the need; Pass is ruled out while it is short and can pay. Measured on 5 games: the payable no-buys fell from 47 to 0 for Food, 54 to 0 for Energy and 6 to 0 for Water. A hand bug that pressed Done before a trade at its limit was fixed on the way. The cost: with the seats now buying from the store, the colonists go short more often (52% to 60% of months on one measured cell) and Food stands above 3 times its usual price in more games (78% to 92%). The PM ruled to ship it anyway and balance at the end; Food supply is recorded for Heiko as a balancing finding. What is changing Built and measured on the WS1 branch, not yet on trunk (WS1, about 17:00Z, 5 November). An approved correction follows: the target becomes next month's whole need (Food: eating plus what spoils; Energy: need grossed up for spoilage; Water only with a farm). The Energy turn-off rule (AM-17, plots switched off biggest user first, an off plot makes 20%) changes what an Energy shortage costs; the AI's valuation of it comes in the same WS1 PR. The stopgap is replaced by the resource-utility model once that is designed and built. In the code: branch ws1-food-short · packages/ai/src/strategist.ts::goodPlan (mustHold), tradeDecision · packages/engine/src/config.ts::STRATEGIST.buyShortfall (on the branch) Kept current by: WS1."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"land","title":"Claiming land","status":"In the game","text":""},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"land-plans","title":"Land plans","status":"","text":"At planning the seat weighs up to six land plans and ranks every free plot under each: Plan Which outfits count Take the most valuable land Any Farms and power first: feed myself Food, Water, Energy Mines for the export trade Smithore, Crystite, Rare earth Ground for a factory Alloy, Electronics, Medicine Grow my own patch Any, with plots next to its own counting double Fence in the leader Any, with plots next to the leader's counting (only with spite, Trader level up) A plot is worth the best gain the seat sees from a MULE on it over the game (the MULE's monthly earnings for up to 6 months, each month counting 0.9 of the one before, less the MULE's and outfit's price, plus 0.6 of the MULE price for what the developed plot adds to the score) plus the 300 the plot itself adds to the score. From Trader level, a Food, Water or Energy MULE that covers the seat's own shortfall counts each such unit at 3 times the reference price at least. Unanalysed ground scores lower (0.8), weighted by risk. The plans themselves are scored by what their best plots are worth (weighted by greed), whether they feed the seat (weighted by reserve) and whether it has the cash to develop them."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"land-grant","title":"The land grant","status":"","text":"The grant is one pass of the light, and each seat may claim 1 plot. When a plot lights up, the seat presses Claim only if, among the free plots still ahead of the light, the lit one is within its claims left and its utility is at least 0.75 of the best one ahead. Early in the pass better plots lie ahead and it waits; late it takes the best that is left. It never presses while it already holds idle plots it cannot pay to develop. It presses after its reaction time plus 150–300 ms for the choice, and a Rookie misses a wanted plot one time in four. When it wants nothing still ahead, it is done with land."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"land-auction","title":"The land auction","status":"","text":"For a plot up for auction, the seat bids up to the lowest of: what the plot is worth to it, its money minus the cash reserve, and its money minus the price of a MULE for this plot and each idle plot it already holds. For a MULE lot, up to what a MULE of that outfit gains on its best idle plot. Against a player it overtakes by 10–35% of the gap to its limit; against the colony's proxy bid it walks straight to the colony's cap if that is within its limit, otherwise leaves the lot to the colony. When its money is under its cash reserve it lists one lot a month itself (an idle plot or a MULE, the one worth least to it, never its last Food or Energy MULE) at a minimum of 0.8 of its worth, sold to the store if nobody bids. What is changing Since the grant gives one claim (Heiko, 5 November), the seats' rankings nearly match, so four to six seats queue for the same few plots and a loser could end month 1 with no land (75 cases in 400 games). The fix (WS1, PR #366, being built) makes a seat count rivals still claiming when it judges which plots are within reach; measured 75 to 0 plotless seats. A MULE won at the land auction is installed first at development (fixed on trunk since the 5 November floor fixes). In the code: packages/ai/src/strategist.ts::landDecision, wantsPlot, plotValue, plotRoom, muleValue, sellWanted · packages/ai/src/value.ts::installGain · packages/ai/src/hands/lot.ts::LotHand · packages/engine/src/config.ts::STRATEGIST.land, grantShare, LOTS.aiOvershoot, AI_PLANS.sell Kept current by: WS1, with WS3 for the grant and auction rules."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"development","title":"Developing land","status":"In the game","text":"In development the seat works through a to-do list: analyse the plots worth it, then for each MULE the strategist picks, buy it, outfit it and install it; then fit filters and go to the pub. A MULE it led out of the land auction goes in first. Which MULE : for each idle plot and each outfit, the gain over the game (as for land) minus the walk there at what its time is worth; plus “keep the cash”. Hard rules: the corral has a MULE, the level's MULEs a month are not used up, the walk fits the clock, it can pay without dipping into the reserve, and a factory has inputs for 2 months. A farm for a good it is short of may use the cash kept back for that good. After each MULE it decides again. Analyses : an unknown plot of its own is analysed when its best and second-best uses are within 25% of each other; at most 2 a month (Rookie 1 ), never with fewer than 20 seconds left. Filters : only once the air is within 10 points of Smoggy, on its own plots putting out more than 1 air point a month, worst first, as far as its Alloy goes. Air : at full scoring each air point a plot puts out costs it 10 cr, rising as the air gets dirtier (doubled by Smoggy). In the code: packages/ai/src/strategist.ts::developDecision, analysesWanted, filtersWanted · packages/ai/src/hands/develop.ts::DevelopHand · packages/engine/src/config.ts::STRATEGIST, AI_HANDS.develop Kept current by: WS1."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"floor-walker","title":"On the floor: walking, wolves and glints","status":"In the game","text":"Development is walked on the arcade floor, and computer players walk it like you: one step of the to-do list at a time, by stick and button, at the same speed ( 1 times a person's), along the quickest path on the map and through the store's doors. They stand 0.5 s at a counter before pressing. A step fails, and what depends on it is dropped, when its place cannot be reached, the counter offers something else (the corral sold out, money short) or the colonist pushes without moving for 20 ticks. Moment What a computer player does Time is running out Drops its errand for the pub once the clock shows its walk there plus 2 s; buys a MULE only if its errand, 10% longer than estimated, still leaves that margin Wolves Half of its legs without a MULE go by wolf land (never while leading a MULE); half of the colonists fight, half run; it stands 0.6 s before choosing. Rookies fight as rookies, Traders as regulars, Tycoons as veterans A glint lights 75% of computer colonists race for it if it is within 8 tiles and their errand still fits The corral scramble 90% of those still needing a MULE race, from within 10 tiles of the store On the floor the development pick is made inside the server's tick, so a replay can compute it again. That means a Smart seat's development choice on the floor is the built-in pick, not Claude's (see Claude writes the plan for how this changes). In the code: packages/ai/src/hands/floor-walker.ts · packages/ai/src/hands/develop.ts · packages/engine/src/config.ts::DEV_STEER.bot*, WOLVES.bot*, GLINT.botShare, SCRAMBLE.botShare Kept current by: WS1."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"playbook","title":"The playbook: a month's plan","status":"In the game","text":"A playbook is one seat's plan for a month, as data: its strategy, what it lists in planning and where it flies the drone, which plots it wants in the grant, its development route, and per good its role, how many units to end with, its walk-away price, its opening price and how fast it concedes. The engine always drafts a complete, legal one; every value is checked against bounds; the final playbook goes into the game record, so a replay plays it without calling any model."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"strategies","title":"Strategies","status":"","text":"Each month the seat drafts a strategy of one of six kinds. It takes the kind its persona leans to most among those it can play now: Kind Plays when Cautious Aggressive Trader Saboteur Builder: farms and power first Always 1 0.3 0.3 0.2 Specialist: one money-maker good A good earns on its best plots 0.5 0.5 1 0.4 Monopolist: corner a good Trader level up; Energy or Smithore with at most 1 rival plot making it, and at least 2 free or idle plots where it is the best use 0.2 0.6 1 0.5 Land baron: grab land At least 30% of plots free 0.3 1 0.3 0.4 Politician: run for office Politics on 0.4 0.2 0.2 0.3 Spoiler: bring down the leader Spite, Trader level up 0 0.4 0 1 The strategy also names its money-maker good (scored over its best 3 plots, cut 15% per rival plot making it), a cash floor (its reserve, at most 2,000 ), a land target for the next 3 months ( 2 plots a month for a land baron and 1 otherwise, but never more than the grant's 1 claim, so today it is 1 a month for every kind) and a stance toward each rival. A Trader seat thus usually plays Specialist or Monopolist, the Baron plays Land baron while land is free."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"who-plays-the-playbook","title":"Who plays it","status":"","text":"A Classic seat plays its own draft for planning (listings, the drone). A Smart seat plays today's asks unless it has adopted a playbook. For development and the auction its own draft waits behind a switch that is off: measured on 5 November, the drafted route and trades were weaker than today's hands (on seed 1: developed 74% to 68%, Food above 3 times its price in 28% to 45% of games), because the draft plans the month's MULEs once where the hand re-decides after each. So today the hands still decide those phases as described above. In the land grant the hands claim by the land plan. A playbook that Claude has edited and the seat has adopted plays for every part it carries, switch or not. Why it is like this Heiko's two layers (PM ruling, 28 October): Claude writes the seat's strategy and its next phase's playbook, starting from the engine's draft and setting only fields within the validator's bounds; the hands play it in real time with no model call. The switch for the seat's own development and auction drafts (PM ruling, 5 November, on WS9's measurement) is a feature switch, not a balance value. What is changing PR #379 (WS9, the playbook follow-ups) goes to trunk in batch 32: a route plays no filter while the seat still leads a MULE, a rival counts as “declared but idle” only for a declared Sell that traded nothing, more tests (among them a bluffing seller never selling under value) and spec amendments that tick the switch's preconditions. Turning the switch on waits for the draft to re-decide after each MULE. Owner: WS9. In the code: packages/engine/src/playbook.ts::Playbook, Strategy · packages/ai/src/playbook/draft.ts, validate.ts, path.ts · packages/engine/src/config.ts::AI_PLANS ( ownPhases: false ) Kept current by: WS9 (playbooks), WS1 (hands)."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"ai-politics","title":"In politics","status":"In the game","text":"Computer players run for office, vote and, as Governor, set the tax, propose laws and spend the colony fund. In short: a persona runs for Governor with a set chance per election (the aggressive seat most often); it votes for itself if it runs, otherwise for the smallest landowner, so the table leans against the leader; in an unhappy colony it will not back the sitting Governor if anyone else runs. As Governor it steers the sales tax toward its persona's target, proposes the law that costs the leader most against itself, pays a general strike and repairs damage when its persona does, and commissions the most prestige the fund can pay for. On a law ballot it votes for a new law when it costs it no more than the table's median, and for a repeal when the law costs it more (a Governor always votes yes, a Rookie flips a coin). The full rules and numbers are in Colony and politics: the computer Governor . In the code: packages/ai/src/hands/vote.ts::VoteHand · packages/engine/src/config.ts::POLITICS.ai Kept current by: WS7, with WS1 for the hands."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"claude-decider","title":"Claude as a player: the decider","status":"In the game","text":"A Smart seat asks Claude (the model claude-haiku-5-5 ) to make three kinds of decision: which land plan to play, which MULE to build, and buy, sell or pass in the auction. Development is walked on the floor at every table today, where the pick stays the built-in one (see On the floor ), so in practice Claude decides land and the auction. For each, Claude receives: a fixed primer on how a month goes and how to reply, plus the persona's character card (the same for every call of that persona, so it is cached and cheap); a few lines on the seat's situation: month, money, plots, goods and needs, the MULE price, who looks like the richest rival; the shortlist of up to 5 options, lettered A to E, each with its label, its reasons and its score; the last 6 lines of table talk, each cut to 200 characters and marked as quoted speech. Claude answers with one letter and one line to say at the table (at most 140 characters, or nothing). It may pick a weaker option when the character would. It can never name an option off the list, never invent a move, and never act on what someone says in chat: table talk is quoted, never an instruction. A reply that is late (the hands wait 2.5 s in development and the auction, 5 s for land), unreadable or off the list is dropped, and the built-in pick plays."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"claude-chat","title":"Chat","status":"","text":"A computer seat says at most 3 lines a game month and at most one a phase. A Smart seat's line is Claude's; a Classic seat's is its persona's template. What is changing PM ruling (a), 5 November: a Smart seat's built-in picks say no template line, so the phase's one chat slot waits for Claude's own line. Classic seats are unchanged. Being built in the brain branch (WS2). Why it is like this Heiko, 21 October: a hosted small model called by our server, with the cost of every call tracked and built into pricing. The safety rules (quoted table talk, only the moves a human has, the engine checks everything) are so that “a clever kid cannot talk an AI into handing over its Crystite”. In the code: packages/ai/src/llm/prompt.ts::buildPrompt, PRIMER · packages/ai/src/llm/decider.ts::LlmDecider, WAIT_MS · packages/ai/src/llm/reply.ts::parseReply · apps/server/src/brain/chat.ts · packages/engine/src/config.ts::BRAIN Kept current by: WS2."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"claude-cost","title":"What Claude costs, and the cap","status":"In the game","text":"Every call is written to a cost ledger: game, seat, persona, level, model, decision kind, tokens in, cached and out, dollars, time taken and how it ended (answered, refused, timed out, error, unreadable, off the list, out of budget). Each game also gets a total line. Price per million tokens (Haiku 5.5) US dollars Input 0.10 Cached input read 0.01 Cache write 0.125 Output 0.50 Before each call the server works out the worst case it could cost (the whole prompt at the dearer input rate and a full 200 -token reply). On trunk, each Smart seat may spend $0.06 a game. A call that would go past it is refused, and the seat plays Classic for the rest of the game, so it never flips back and forth. A call whose bill is unknown (a timeout, a lost connection) counts at its worst case. What is changing Approved by the PM within Heiko's limit (28 October): the per-seat budget is replaced by $1.00 a game for all Smart seats together, each holding an equal share (half a dollar each at 2 Smart seats). It is in the brain branch, with Sonnet 5.5 prices for the playbook writer. The paid proof, inside Heiko's $10 cap: step 1 is cleared (at most about $1.10); a game with 2 Smart seats costs about $0.85; the full 20-game proof (about $20) needs Heiko. Owner: WS2. Why it is like this Heiko asked (21 October) that adding a computer player is priced so the game never loses money on model costs. The design proposes Smart seats at about $0.10 a seat a game, about three times the estimated cost, with the budget as the no-loss guarantee. On 28 October Heiko noted that with players bringing their own key “we can be more generous with the AI budgets”; that is parked until a host can bring a key, so the cap stays. In the code: apps/server/src/brain/budget.ts::worstCaseUsd, createBudget · apps/server/src/brain/ledger.ts · packages/engine/src/config.ts::BRAIN.seatBudgetUsd, pricesPerMTok Kept current by: WS2."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"claude-playbook-writer","title":"Claude writes the plan","status":"Being built","text":"The next step lets Claude do more than pick a letter: it writes the seat's strategy and edits its playbook, which the hands then play in real time. This is built in the brain branch and not yet on trunk. The manual : every phase, how Worth is counted, every move and when it is smart, generated from the game's own move list and config so a rule change cannot leave it stale. It is the same for every seat, so it is cached. The brief : the seat's moment, from its own view only: who owns what land, this month's prices with the last 3 months', the export ship's wishlist, up to 5 land plans with their best plots, the engine's draft, how its last phase went against its plan, what the rules refused of its last reply, and the table talk. Tools it may call while it thinks: legalMoves (the moves of a phase and what they cost now), worth (its Worth line by line), standings (every seat's Worth) and whatIf (plays its playbook with proposed edits over one month on a copy and returns its Worth after). At most 3 tool rounds and 4 whatIf months per call. The playbook writer : Claude (Sonnet 5.5 by default, Haiku 5.5 as the cheaper fallback, up to 30 s a call) replies with strategy fields and edits, each with a reason of at most 140 characters. The validator clamps or refuses each edit; a refused edit keeps the draft's value. The playbook that plays is the draft unless at least one edit is accepted. When : the land-grant playbook is asked for at game start and as each upkeep opens, without waiting, and taken when the grant starts from the reply that came in time, else from the draft. PR #377 (PR-4b) adjusts the auction just before it; PR #378 (PR-4c) adjusts development as it starts, which is how a Smart seat's own choices reach the floor. What is changing PR #377 and PR #378 are open into the brain branch (WS2, 5 November), each with one review. The paid proof needs Heiko for its full 20-game run. A lobby choice of brain, players' own keys and MCP seats remain ideas in the design, not planned. In the code: branch ws2-brain · packages/ai/src/llm/manual.ts, brief.ts, tools.ts, writer.ts, system.ts · apps/server/src/brain/playbooks.ts · BRAIN.writeCalls, toolRounds, whatIfsPerWrite, gameBudgetUsd (on the branch) Kept current by: WS2."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"resource-utility","title":"Next: a resource-utility model","status":"Being designed","text":"Heiko, 5 November: the seats need “a more clear algorithm”. A seat should look at its whole shortage, understand the overall price level, know every resource it needs, the store's prices and how much is available, and get utility from having enough or even a surplus, which can make sense when prices are cheap and it can store. Later they might speculate: “they might believe prices go up and buy cheap to sell more expensive later. This is what would make them clever.” This is the base, which Claude can later overrule by defining strategies."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"resource-utility-goals","title":"Proposed goals (awaiting Heiko's confirmation)","status":"","text":"Goal What it means at the table How it is measured G-1 Keep yourself supplied A computer player is not caught short of Food or Energy when the store had stock and it could pay Short (or plots halved for want of Energy) while the store had stock and it could pay, in under 5% of months G-2 Value having enough and a surplus It holds a buffer, larger when the good is cheap By price band G-3 Speculate (phase 2) It buys cheap under a belief that the price will rise, and sells dear; persona-shaped and settable by Claude The margin it makes WS9 measured today's play for the design (40 games on each of 3 seeds): Energy shortages cost 40–43% of seat-months some production, a median of 2 of 5 plots halved, about 265 cr of output; 44–47% of seats that wanted to buy still ended short. The store's own supply and pricing are not part of this design (WS5 is designing store pricing separately). What is changing Three approaches are drafted, due 20:00Z on 5 November: A, thin needs-first; B, the value of each extra unit set by what running short costs against what holding costs, with a price on cash and a price belief (WS9 recommends B); C, a monthly planner. The goals await Heiko. Owner: WS9 designs; WS1 owns value.ts and builds with it. Kept current by: WS9."},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"simulator","title":"The simulator","status":"In the game","text":"The simulator plays whole games with only computer seats (Classic, no model) and checks them against the balance targets. Every balance change is checked with it. Command What it does pnpm sim 100 games, 4 seats, seed 1, Trader level, personas rotating by seat and seed, politics on --games 40 --players 6 --seed 101 How many games, how many seats, the first seed --level rookie , --persona cautious One level for all; one persona for every seat --plays playbook | today | pair Every seat on its playbook, every seat on today's hands, or even seats on the playbook and odd on today's; adds the plan lines to the report --set STRATEGIST.cashFloor=80 Any config number or switch for this run; repeatable; the report's header lists them --politics off , --walking off , --air smoggy No offices or Governor's tax; development as before walking cost time; count events as if the air were in a band"},{"page":"computer-players.html","id":"sim-targets","title":"What it checks","status":"","text":"Check Target MULE price median 150 to 300 cr MULE price, 90th percentile under 500 cr Corral sold out in under 10% of months Food above 3 times its reference price in 10% to 50% of games Colonists short of Food or Water in under 15% of months Owned plots developed at the end 70% or more No computer player broke (under 100 cr with idle land for 3 months in a row) in no game Ended in Collapse 10% of games or fewer For Smart seats there is a separate trial: pnpm --filter @mule/server brain:trial -- --games 20 --seed 101 . Without the key every seat plays Classic and nothing is paid; with it, Smart seats play against strategist seats, a spending cap is required, and only one paid run a day is allowed. What is changing Heiko, 4 November: first make the functional parts work, tune balance at the end. Several checks miss today (on 5 November's start values roughly half of games have colonists short, and the stopgap raises that); these are recorded for the balancing round, not fixed now. In the code: packages/ai/src/sim.ts · packages/ai/src/lineup.ts::simSeats · packages/engine/src/config.ts::SIM_TARGETS · apps/server/src/brain/trial.ts Kept current by: WS1 (sim), WS2 (trial)."},{"page":"development.html","id":"development-phase","title":"The development phase and its clock","status":"In the game","text":"Development comes after the land grant and the land auction, and before production. Every player's colonist is on the map at the same time, steered live from their phone (or by the computer for a computer player), and everyone watches it all on the TV. One clock for everyone: 60 seconds, the same for every player, every month. Hunger and sickness never shorten it; they slow your walk instead (see Pace ). Your turn ends when you go to the pub or when the clock runs out. The phase ends early once every player is at the pub, out of time, or has dropped off the table. Everything costs time: walking, standing still for an analysis, waiting at the wolves. The time you have left when you reach the pub is paid out in cash. At the start, each colonist stands just outside one of the store's two doors, the seats taking the left and right doors in turn. A player who won a MULE at the land auction starts with it on the lead. The game steps every colonist together in small ticks of 50 ms, on the server and in the simulator alike, so a replay of the same seed and the same moves plays the same. Why it is like this Heiko, 28 Oct: \"if we just steer it via the walking then managing the time bars also gets easier. Then we only have one global time bar for all users, which is much better.\" From that the PM ruled that every per-player condition acts through walking pace, never the clock. The length, 60 seconds, is Heiko's of 4 Nov. In the code: config.ts::DEVELOPMENT.fullSeconds · config.ts::DEV_STEER.tickMs · packages/engine/src/game.ts::startDevelopment · packages/engine/src/floor/floor.ts::buildFloor · apps/server/src/mule-game.ts::startClock Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3) for the clock and server, the Strategy team (WS1) for the floor."},{"page":"development.html","id":"month-one","title":"Month 1: the clock counts from the start","status":"In the game","text":"Month 1 is no different from any other month: the 60 -second clock starts counting as development opens. Why it is like this For a week, month 1 worked differently. On 28 Oct Heiko approved that month 1's clock should wait until every player's first MULE was installed. A review then found that a player whose MULE was in could keep buying, installing and analysing for free while waiting. On 29 Oct Heiko ruled \"Once in, you wait\" : a player whose first MULE was in could only go to the pub, press Ready or walk home until the clock started. On 5 Nov Heiko was asked whether month 1's clock should simply count down like every other month and answered \"Yes, always count\" . The wait, the host's Continue for it and the \"Once in, you wait\" rule were all removed; none of them is in the game today. In the code: apps/server/src/mule-game.ts::startDevelopment Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3), with the UX team (WS6) for the words."},{"page":"development.html","id":"walking","title":"Walking the map","status":"In the game","text":"You steer your colonist with the stick (a thumb pad on the phone, arrow keys on a computer) and do things with one action button. You walk freely, in any direction, diagonals included (a diagonal is no faster). At full pace on plains a colonist covers 2.5 plots a second. Other land is slower: Land Share of full speed Plots a second at full pace Wolves? Plains 1 2.5 no Desert 0.9 2.25 no Tundra 0.85 2.13 yes Crater 0.8 2.0 no Forest 0.75 1.88 yes Ice 0.75 1.88 no Hills 0.7 1.75 yes Volcanic 0.6 1.5 no River 0.6 1.5 no Swamp 0.5 1.25 no Mountains 0.4 1.0 no Lake 0 (blocked) 0 no Lakes block. A lake plot is worked from its shore: stand on the land beside it, within 0.45 of a plot of its edge, and the button offers the lake plot (the words add \"from the shore\"). Other people's land may be walked across freely. The store's own plot cannot be walked on; you go in through a door. Leading a MULE slows you to 0.9 of your speed. The MULE walks a little behind you. Example Your plot is four plots east of the store across plains, then one plot of swamp. Fed and well, you walk the plains at 2.5 plots a second ( 1.6 s) and the swamp at 1.25 ( 0.8 s): about 2.4 seconds. Leading a MULE it takes 2.4 ÷ 0.9 = about 2.7 seconds. Why it is like this Heiko's walking rule R1 (28 Oct) set 0.4 s a plot for the first, tap-to-walk version. When steering arrived, Heiko set the steered pace to the same, 2.5 plots a second, on 4 Nov (\"1.25 was half\"). The land speeds are Heiko's starting values from the arcade walking design. In the code: config.ts::DEV_STEER ( mapTilesPerSecond , terrain , leadPace , lakeMode , shoreReachTiles , trespass ) · packages/engine/src/floor/speed.ts::speedOf · packages/engine/src/floor/step.ts::stepFloor Kept current by: the Strategy team (WS1) for the floor, the UX team (WS6) for the phone controls."},{"page":"development.html","id":"pace","title":"Pace: hunger, sickness and species","status":"In the game","text":"Each colonist walks at a pace , a share of a fed, healthy colonist's speed. It is worked out once, when development starts, and applies on the map and in the store alike. Three things multiply together: What Pace factor Fed (holding the month's Food) 1 Hungry falls in a straight line from 1 (all the Food you need) to 0.6 (none) Sick, with no Medicine 0.5 Zipwing (species perk) 1.25"},{"page":"development.html","id":"hunger","title":"Hunger","status":"","text":"The Food you need this month is 2 , plus 1 for every 4 crew or part of four (a working raw plot has a crew of 1 , a factory 2 , a green belt none). At the start of development, if you hold less Food than that, you are hungry. The screens name how hungry by the share of Food missing: Share of the month's Food missing Shown as none fed under 30% peckish 30% to under 70% hungry 70% or more starving The words are bands only; the pace itself slides smoothly with the Food you hold. Hunger and sickness are public: everyone can see who is hungry or sick."},{"page":"development.html","id":"sickness","title":"Sickness","status":"","text":"At the start of each development every player rolls for sickness: 3 % at base, more in dirty air ( +15 % from Smoggy, +30 % from Toxic, +50 % in Collapse) and +3 % for every air point your own plots caused last month (at most +40 %; a filter halves your points), never above 90 %. A rare epidemic ( 0.5 % a month) makes everyone sick this development and the next. If you are sick and hold a Medicine, you take one and are well; otherwise you walk at half pace this turn. Example Brix has five working raw plots: a crew of 5 , so he needs 2 + 2 = 4 Food. He holds 1 : three quarters missing, \"starving\". His hunger factor is 1 − 0.4 × 0.75 = 0.7 . He is also sick with no Medicine: 0.7 × 0.5 = 0.35 . On plains he walks 2.5 × 0.35 = under 0.9 plots a second. A Zipwing in the same state would walk at 0.35 × 1.25 = about 0.44 of full pace. Why it is like this The slowest hunger pace, 0.6 , is Heiko's of 4 Nov (\"Yes, 60%\"). It is the strongest slowdown with which a starving colony can still recover: at 0.2 a starving colonist could not reach Food and the pub inside the turn, and the colony went short in 58% of months; from 0.6 , under 15%. Sick pace 0.5 and the sickness odds are Heiko's catalogue numbers (28 Oct). The Zipwing perk is 1.25 because at 1.5 Zipwings won 37% of four-player games instead of a fair 25%. In the code: packages/engine/src/dev-walk.ts::pace · dev-walk.ts::hungerPace · config.ts::DEV_WALK ( hungerPaceMin , sickPace , hungerLevels ) · config.ts::SPECIES_PERK.zipwingSpeed · config.ts::EVENTS.player.sickness · packages/engine/src/production.ts::needsOf Kept current by: the Strategy team (WS1), with the Events team (WS1/WS8) for sickness."},{"page":"development.html","id":"store-floor","title":"The store floor","status":"In the game","text":"The store is one hall, entered by an airlock on its left or right side. Walk up to a door from outside and press ENTER STORE ; inside, stand in a door and press LEAVE (\"to the colony\", or \"with your MULE\"). The hall has two rows of rooms off one walkway: Row Rooms, left to right Back wall (outfitters) Food, Water, Energy, Smithore, Crystite, Rare earth Front row The works (three bays: Alloy, Electronics, Medicine), Green belt, the corral, the pub At full pace the walk from the left door to the corral takes 3 seconds. Hunger, sickness, the Zipwing perk and a MULE on the lead change your speed in the hall too. Standing in a room is being at it: the action button changes to what that room offers. You can also sell wolf pelts at either door (see Wolves ). Why it is like this Heiko's store layout \"One hall, two rows\" (21 Oct): six outfitters along the top, the corral in the middle of the bottom row, the works and green belt to one side, the pub by the door, all on one screen. In the code: packages/engine/src/floor/hall.ts::HALL · config.ts::DEV_STEER.floorSeconds · config.ts::DEV_STEER.doorHalfTiles Kept current by: the Strategy team (WS1) for the hall plan, the Table team (WS4) for its drawing."},{"page":"development.html","id":"buying-a-mule","title":"Buying a MULE","status":"In the game","text":"Stand in the corral, not leading a MULE, and press BUY MULE · price . You pay at once and lead an empty MULE. You can lead only one MULE at a time. If you are short of money the button is dim and says by how much (\"170 cr · 30 short\"); if the corral is empty it reads \"NO MULES LEFT · the pub pays now\"."},{"page":"development.html","id":"mule-price","title":"The MULE price","status":"","text":"A MULE costs its labour, 120 cr, plus the Smithore it is built from ( 1 unit at the store's posted Smithore price), times a scarcity factor: price = (120 + Smithore price) × scarcity factor , rounded. The factor starts at 1 . Each month the corral sells out, it rises by 15 % for the next month. Each month at least half of the MULEs that were in the corral when development began are left over, it falls by 10 %, but never below 0.6 . A Gearling pays 95 % of the price (rounded), for MULEs and outfits alike. Example Smithore is posted at 50 : a MULE costs 120 + 50 = 170 . Last month the corral sold out, so the factor is 1.15 : 170 × 1.15 = 195.5 , so 196 . A Gearling pays 186 ."},{"page":"development.html","id":"corral","title":"The corral","status":"","text":"The store starts every game with 10 MULEs. At each month's end it builds new ones, up to 2 per player plus 2 spare ( 10 in a four-player game, 14 with six), using 1 unit of its own Smithore for each. Short of Smithore, it builds fewer. MULEs come back to the corral when the store buys one at the land auction, when the colony wins a plot with a MULE on it, and (soon) when you end your turn still leading a swapped-out MULE (see the swap ). In the code: game.ts::buyMule · game.ts::mulePriceFor · packages/engine/src/economy.ts::mulePrice · economy.ts::corralTarget · packages/engine/src/upkeep.ts · config.ts::MULE · config.ts::START.storeMules · config.ts::SPECIES_PERK.gearlingPrice Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3) for the rule, the Strategy team (WS1) for the price and corral numbers."},{"page":"development.html","id":"outfitting","title":"Outfitting","status":"In the game","text":"An empty MULE does nothing. Lead it into an outfitter's room (or a bay of the works, or the Green belt room) and press OUTFIT · GOOD · price . A MULE bought this turn is outfitted once; to change its good later you take it off its plot and re-outfit it (below). Outfit Where Price (cr) Food Food outfitter 25 Water Water outfitter 25 Energy Energy outfitter 50 Smithore Smithore outfitter 75 Crystite Crystite outfitter 100 Rare earth Rare earth outfitter 100 Alloy (factory) Works, first bay 150 Electronics (factory) Works, second bay 200 Medicine (factory) Works, third bay 150 Green belt Green belt room 20 When politics is on and the colony's emission cap is in force and the air is above its level, Rare earth and Alloy outfits are refused; the button shows the cap and the air (\"cap 50, air 57\"). What each outfit makes on which ground is in Production . In the code: game.ts::outfitMule · game.ts::outfitCostFor · config.ts::MULE.outfitCost · packages/engine/src/politics.ts::outfitBlocked Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3)."},{"page":"development.html","id":"installing","title":"Installing a MULE","status":"In the game","text":"Lead an outfitted MULE onto one of your own plots (or to the shore of your lake plot) and press INSTALL · GOOD . The MULE stays there and works that plot every month from now on, until it is taken off, sold, stolen or struck by a meteor. A plot makes nothing without a MULE. A plot remembers how many months in a row its outfit has run (factories gain from experience). Installing a different outfit starts that count again from zero; putting the same outfit back on a plot it was taken off this turn keeps the old count. Important for the button: while you lead an outfitted MULE and stand on your own plot, the button offers INSTALL before anything else. So analyse a plot before you bring the MULE to it. In the code: game.ts::installMule · packages/engine/src/floor/reach.ts (row install ) Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3) for the rule, the Strategy team (WS1) for the button."},{"page":"development.html","id":"mule-swap","title":"Installing over a MULE: the swap","status":"Being built","text":""},{"page":"development.html","id":"swap-today","title":"Today","status":"","text":"If the plot already has a MULE, the button reads SWAP and warns \"your Food MULE runs away\" (or \"same outfit, nothing gained\" if the outfits match). Pressing it puts the new MULE on the plot, and the old MULE simply vanishes. It is lost, with no refund."},{"page":"development.html","id":"swap-ruling","title":"What Heiko ruled","status":"","text":"Heiko met this on 5 Nov: he led a Water MULE to his Food plot, pressed install, and the Food MULE vanished. He expected to lead the Food MULE away. His ruling, built now: Installing over a plot's MULE swaps : the new MULE goes on the plot and you now lead the old one, with its outfit, as if you had taken it off. If you still lead that old MULE when your turn ends, it goes back to the store's corral. You get no money for it. Installing the same outfit over itself is refused: \"Same outfit: nothing to swap\". A button to hand a MULE back to the corral mid-turn for a refund is in the backlog, for Heiko to decide. Example Lena's plains plot has a Food MULE. She leads in a new Energy MULE and presses SWAP. The Energy MULE now works the plains plot; Lena leads the Food MULE. She walks it to her bare river plot and installs it there: two working plots for the price of one MULE. Had she gone to the pub still leading it, it would have gone back to the corral. What is changing Ruled by Heiko on 5 Nov; the details (corral at turn end, no refund, same outfit refused) are the PM's ruling AM-12 the same day. Being built by the Auctions team (WS3) in the game rules, with named edits by the Strategy team (WS1) for the button and the computer players; the first failing test is on branch ws3-install-swap. In the code: game.ts::installMule · packages/engine/src/floor/reach.ts::reachWords (the SWAP words) Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3)."},{"page":"development.html","id":"take-and-reoutfit","title":"Taking a MULE off and re-outfitting","status":"In the game","text":"Stand on your own plot that has a MULE, not leading one, and the button can offer TAKE MULE : the MULE comes onto your lead and the plot stops working until a MULE is installed there again. With a MULE you took off this turn, go to another outfitter and press RE-OUTFIT · GOOD · price . It costs the full outfit price for the new good. The outfitter for the good it already has is dim (\"already fitted\"), and a MULE bought this turn cannot be re-outfitted. Then install it wherever you like. If your turn ends while you still lead a MULE you took off a plot (even one that bolted from wolves), it goes back to that plot with the outfit and run of months it had. Nothing is lost. The button offers one thing at a time on your own plot, in this order: install (when leading an outfitted MULE), analyse (if you have not analysed the plot), fit a filter (if you hold Alloy and the plot has none), take the MULE. So on a plot you have not analysed, TAKE MULE appears only after you analyse it. Why it is like this Heiko, 28 Oct, approved re-outfitting a MULE at the store for the outfit price, as a rule change (AM-2). Only taken-off MULEs re-outfit (AM-14) so an outfit is chosen once at purchase. The button order (AM-9, WS6, approved by Heiko with the arcade mocks on 29 Oct) puts TAKE after ANALYSE and FIT FILTER so those two are never hidden on a plot with a MULE. In the code: game.ts::takeMule · game.ts::reoutfitMule · game.ts::returnTakenMule · config.ts::DEV_STEER.reoutfitCost · floor/reach.ts::ROWS Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3), with the Strategy team (WS1) for the button order."},{"page":"development.html","id":"mule-runs-off","title":"When a MULE runs off","status":"In the game","text":"A MULE you bought (or won at the land auction) and did not install by the end of your turn runs off and is lost, with no refund. That happens when the clock runs out and when you walk into the pub leading it. The pub pays nothing back for it. Still leading at turn end What happens A MULE bought this turn, empty or outfitted Runs off, lost A MULE won at the land auction Runs off, lost A MULE taken off one of your plots this turn Goes back to that plot, unchanged A MULE you led away in a swap Today: it already vanished at the swap. Being built: back to the corral, no refund Two other ways to lose a MULE are events: a MULE theft (a 1 % chance in development and again at the month end) takes one installed MULE off one of your plots, and a meteor strike destroys the MULE on the plot it hits. See Events . In the code: game.ts::endLead · game.ts::visitPub · config.ts::DEV_STEER.pubLeadRefund · config.ts::EVENTS.player.muleTheft Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3)."},{"page":"development.html","id":"wolves","title":"Wolves","status":"In the game","text":"On forest, hills and tundra, a pack of 3 wolves may come for your colonist: a 10 % chance each second you are on that land, and not again for 20 seconds after a pack. You have 3 seconds to choose; doing nothing is a fight. Choice How it goes Win Lose Run (push the stick) The wolves chase at 0.88 of full walking speed. They give up after 5 seconds, 4 plots, or when you reach the store. You got away Bitten: half speed until you reach the store or one of your own plots Fight (press) Up to 10 seconds in place. A wolf crouches ( 0.8 s to dodge) then lunges; dodge with the stick, strike with the button at a wolf left open. 3 strikes: 2 pelts, which the store buys at either door for 20 cr each 3 bites: the MULE you lead bolts ; with no MULE, you are stunned for 4 seconds A bolted MULE runs 4 plots in a random direction and stands. It is still yours. Walk within 0.6 of a plot of it and press CATCH MULE ; until then you cannot outfit or install it. Why it is like this Heiko, 28 Oct, asked for more arcade in the walk: \"when you are walking you are being attacked by wolves and you need to run away or spend 10 seconds fighting them\". He approved wolves, the glint race and the corral scramble (\"Yes, those three\") the same day. In the code: config.ts::WOLVES · packages/engine/src/floor/wolves.ts · game.ts::boltMule · game.ts::catchMule · game.ts::sellPelts Kept current by: the Strategy team (WS1)."},{"page":"development.html","id":"glint","title":"The glint race","status":"In the game","text":"Now and then a Crystite sparkle lights one plot on every screen at once: \"A Crystite glint at 7·3! First there wins\". The first colonist to step onto that plot, or to press GRAB THE GLINT within 1.2 plots of its middle, wins 1 Crystite. Rule Value No glint in a turn's first seconds 6 s Average wait between glints while none is lit 20 s A glint stays lit 8 s Pause after one is won or fades 5 s Prize 1 Crystite A glint can light any plot a colonist can stand on, owned or free, but never a lake, the store, or a plot someone is standing on as it lights. A player already at the pub cannot win it. Nobody there in time: \"The glint faded\". The prize goes straight into your stock. What is changing Being built: a computer player that pressed GRAB on the very tick a glint faded had its press fall through to the next thing the button offered, which could be ANALYSE: it then stood still for a 4 -second assay it never meant to do (measured 2 of 201 grabs before, 0 of 199 after). The fix is in the Strategy team's PR #366 (WS1), together with the land-grant fix in Land . In the code: config.ts::GLINT · packages/engine/src/floor/glint.ts · game.ts::awardGlint Kept current by: the Strategy team (WS1)."},{"page":"development.html","id":"scramble","title":"The corral scramble","status":"In the game","text":"When a sale leaves the corral with 2 MULEs or fewer and somebody still needs one, every screen calls a race: \"2 MULEs left! Race to the corral\". It is first there, first served at the normal BUY MULE. Each sale is told (\"Ossa got one: 1 left\"), and the last one ends it: \"Brix got the last one: the corral is empty\", then who went without. Otherwise it ends after 15 seconds, or once everyone who needed a MULE has one. There is at most one scramble a turn. A player \"needs a MULE\" when they are not at the pub, not leading one, own a plot with no MULE, and can afford the price. In the code: config.ts::SCRAMBLE · packages/engine/src/floor/scramble.ts Kept current by: the Strategy team (WS1)."},{"page":"development.html","id":"analysing","title":"Analysing a plot","status":"In the game","text":"Stand on any plot you have not analysed yet, owned or free, with or without a MULE, and press ANALYSE . Your colonist stands still while the button reads ASSAYING: 8 seconds, or 4 for a Mollox. Then you know the plot's rating for all six raw goods and its traits, privately and for the rest of the game. It costs no money, only time, which also lowers your pub payout. A lake plot is analysed from its shore. Ratings, rings and what the drone does are in Land and geology . In the code: game.ts::analysePlot · game.ts::analysisSeconds · config.ts::DEVELOPMENT.analysisSeconds · config.ts::SPECIES_PERK.molloxAnalysis Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3), with the UX team (WS6) for the ground card."},{"page":"development.html","id":"filters","title":"Filters","status":"In the game","text":"On your own plot with no filter, holding at least 1 Alloy, the button offers FIT FILTER · 1 Alloy . The Alloy comes from your own stock. A filter halves the air pollution the plot's MULE causes and stops its pollution spreading to the plots around it. It stays on the plot (a meteor strike destroys it). See Production and Colony and politics for what pollution does. In the code: game.ts::fitFilter · config.ts::DEVELOPMENT.filterAlloy · config.ts::POLLUTION.filterFactor Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3)."},{"page":"development.html","id":"pub","title":"The pub","status":"In the game","text":"Walk into the pub, in the store's front row, and press PUB · END TURN . The button shows what you will win. Your turn is over: you cannot do anything else this month. The earlier you go, and the later in the game, the more it pays: payout = 20 + (60 + 20 × month) × (seconds left ÷ 60) , rounded. Month 60 s left 30 s left 10 s left 0 s left 1 100 60 33 20 4 160 90 43 20 8 240 130 57 20 12 320 170 70 20 Example Month 4. Ossa buys, outfits and installs a MULE and reaches the pub with 45 seconds left: 20 + (60 + 80) × 45/60 = 20 + 105 = 125 cr. Had she stopped for an 8 -second analysis on the way, she would have arrived with 37 seconds and won 106 : the analysis cost her 19 cr. A MULE you lead into the pub runs off (unless you took it off your own plot this turn, which goes home). If the clock runs out before you reach the pub, you get nothing. In the code: game.ts::pubPayoutFor · game.ts::visitPub · config.ts::DEVELOPMENT.pub Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3)."},{"page":"development.html","id":"computer-players-floor","title":"Computer players on the floor","status":"In the game","text":"Computer players walk the same floor under the same rules: the same speeds, the same pace from hunger and sickness, the same button. They differ only in how they choose. They stand 0.5 seconds at a counter before pressing, like a person reading the button. They head for the pub when the clock shows no more than their walk there plus 2 seconds, and buy a MULE only if the errand, allowed 10 % longer than planned, still leaves that margin. At the default level (Trader) they lead at most 2 MULEs out of the store a month (Rookie 1 , Tycoon 3 ). They analyse an unknown plot of their own when its two best uses look close, at most 2 a month (Rookie 1 ), never with under 20 seconds left. Glint: three in four race for a glint within 8 plots, only if their errand still fits afterwards. Scramble: nine in ten who need a MULE race, from within 10 plots of the store. Wolves: half of them fight rather than run; they never lead a MULE across wolf land, and take wolf land on about half their other walks, so the table sees them meet packs. They know only what a phone in their seat would show; see What the computer players know and Computer players . In the code: config.ts::DEV_STEER ( bot… values) · config.ts::WOLVES.botFightShare · config.ts::GLINT.botShare · config.ts::SCRAMBLE.botShare · config.ts::AI_LEVELS · packages/ai/src/hands/floor-walker.ts Kept current by: the Strategy team (WS1)."},{"page":"development.html","id":"tap-walk-removed","title":"The old tap walk and development screen","status":"Being built","text":"Before steering, development worked by tapping: you tapped a destination and an action (the corral, an outfitter, a plot, the pub), and the game walked your colonist there in grid steps, never diagonally, at 0.4 seconds a plot, plus 1 second across the store floor to the corral, divided by your pace. The move happened on arrival. Today every game at the table steers; the server refuses a tap move (\"Development is steered: walk there and press the button\"). The tap walk survives only inside the code: the old phone screen is still shown for a game started without steering, which only tests do. What is changing PR #369 (the Auctions team, WS3, arcade PR-12) deletes the tap walk from the server and the old development screen from the phone, so every game steers and there is one development screen. All owners have approved it; it is waiting to be brought up to date with trunk and merged. Afterwards the Strategy team (WS1) and the Brain team (WS2) retire what is left behind: the tap walk's time formula, the on/off switch for steering, and the computer players' tap branches. In the code: config.ts::DEV_WALK.tileSeconds · config.ts::DEV_WALK.floorSeconds · config.ts::DEV_STEER.on · packages/engine/src/dev-walk.ts::walkSeconds · apps/web/src/phone/Develop.tsx · apps/web/src/phone/arcade/Develop.tsx Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3), with the UX team (WS6) for the phone screen."},{"page":"events.html","id":"event-model","title":"How events work","status":"In the game","text":"Heiko's model, in his words (28 October): \"We just throw a dice for each event and see whether it fires. So we can have multiple events happening in the same turn.\" Every event has its own row in the game's settings and its own dice. Colony events and player events. A colony event rolls once for the whole colony. A player event rolls once for each player, and hits only that player's land, cash or stock. Colony events roll first, in table order; then each player's events, seat by seat. Each event belongs to a phase. It rolls at the start of that phase: planning, development, production, the goods auction or the month end. The land grant and the land auction have no events today. MULE theft is the one event that rolls in two phases. Several can fire at once. Each row rolls on its own, so a month can bring none, one or several events, even in the same phase. No events in month 1. The rolled events start in month EVENTS.firstMonth = 2 : in month 1 there is nothing developed to hit. Sickness is the exception and rolls from month 1. Some pairs never fire together. Drought and flood, and dust storm and solar flare: if both roll in one phase, the pair's own dice keep one of them. An event needs something to hit. A personal event starts on that player's land; if they have no plot it could hit (a pest swarm with no farm), it does not fire. Rats and the crooked accountant fire only when they would take at least 1. Events that roll after production's output. A meteor strike rolls once, after every plot has produced. A cavern collapse rolls once for each crystal cavern that was mined for Crystite this month, also after the output. Replays. Every roll is seeded from the game's seed, the month, the event, the phase and whose roll it is, so a game replays exactly, and retuning one event never moves another's dice. A computer player's look-ahead never sees events. Why it is like this Heiko, 28 October: \"We distinguish player events from colony events. each event has a likelihood\", then \"or we have events per phase\". Before this, at most one event fired a month, from one dice, always in production, and events came in about 45% of months. One row per event means a new event or a retune is a line in the settings, not code. Heiko's four rulings of 28 October on the catalogue: keep the catalogue's amount of events; count a player's own pollution per air point; build wave 1, then wave 2, then the colony's mood events, then the rest; and lean bad luck toward the richest and good luck toward the poorest. In the code: packages/engine/src/events.ts::rollPhase , ::rowDice , ::keepOneOfEachPair , ::rollAfterOutput , ::firePhase · config.ts::EVENTS ( firstMonth , colony , player , excludes ) Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1), with the Events team (WS8) for the shows."},{"page":"events.html","id":"event-chance","title":"How likely an event is","status":"In the game","text":"Each row has a base chance for its phase. Things that make it likelier or less likely are added as points (a point is one percent), and the result is held between 0 and the row's cap, 90% for every event today. chance = base + air band + own pollution + wealth + follows, never below 0, never above 90% Influence What it reads Used by Air band The colony's air gauge band: Clean, Smoggy, Toxic or Collapse. Sickness, drought, acid rain Own pollution The air points the player's own plots caused last month (a filter halves them), so many points each, up to a cap. Sickness Wealth Whether the player is the richest or the poorest, by cash plus 300 per plot owned. A tie goes to the first seat. Pest swarm, wildfire, bumper harvest Follows Whether another event fired earlier in this phase, or last month. It counts once even if both. Flood, wildfire Example: how likely is Kai to fall sick? The air is Smoggy (gauge 34). Last month Kai's Rare earth mine and his unfiltered smelter caused 7 air points. Sickness: base 3 + Smoggy 15 + 7 × 3 = 21 = 39% . Had he fitted filters, his points would have been 3.5, worth 10.5: about 29%. In Clean air with no industry he would be at 3%. Example: a fire after a drought Lena is the richest player. Last month a drought hit the colony. Wildfire for Lena: base 2.4 + richest 1 + follows a drought 3 = 6.4% . For the other players it is 2.4% + 3 = 5.4%. In the code: packages/engine/src/events.ts::eventChance , ::influences , ::wealthShare · config.ts::EVENTS , GAME.plotScore Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1)."},{"page":"events.html","id":"event-phases","title":"When each phase rolls","status":"In the game","text":"Phase When it rolls Events Does the phase wait for the show? Planning As the month opens, before the hub and any election Federation tax, Federation grant, immigration wave, inheritance No: events play while people act Development At the start, after each player's sickness roll Sickness, epidemic, MULE theft Yes Production At the start; meteor and cavern after the output Dust storm, drought, acid rain, solar flare, comet pass, flood, volcanic ash, cold snap, pest swarm, wildfire, bumper harvest; then cavern collapse and meteor strike The show is part of production, which grows to fit it Goods auction Before the store posts its prices Pirate raid, store fire Yes: nobody can trade until the show ends Month end Before anyone eats and before goods spoil MULE theft, rats in the silo, crooked accountant, package from home No, today (the month end has no clock) The order inside the month end matters: rats eat before your household does, and the Food from a package from home is then eaten or half of it spoils like any other Food. In the code: packages/engine/src/game.ts::advancePhase , ::startDevelopment · packages/engine/src/production.ts::runProduction · apps/server/src/mule-game.ts::holdsForEvents Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1); the phase hooks with the Auctions team (WS3)."},{"page":"events.html","id":"catalogue-planning","title":"Planning's events","status":"In the game","text":"Planning's events move cash and colonists, never land. Event Who Chance What it does On the TV Federation tax Colony 2% The Federation takes 10% of every player's cash (rounded down) into the colony fund. Headline Federation grant Colony 2% The Federation pays the 2 poorest players (cash plus land) 150 cr each. New money, from no one. Headline Immigration wave Colony 2% A ship brings 10% more colonists, for good (rounded). The store then has more mouths to feed. Headline Inheritance Each player 1.5% From 100 cr for the richest to 300 cr for the poorest, in a straight line by wealth rank. Players tied on wealth share the average of their ranks' amounts, rounded down. Ranks are read once, before any of the phase's payouts. A player alone gets 300. Bulletin Example: an inheritance in a four-player game The ranks from richest to poorest get 100, 166, 233 and 300 cr (100 + 200 × rank ÷ 3, rounded down). If the two middle players are tied, each gets the amount at rank 1.5: 200 cr. In the code: packages/engine/src/events.ts::applyEvents , ::grantees , ::inheritanceAmount · config.ts::EVENTS.colony.federationTax , .federationGrant , .immigrationWave , EVENTS.player.inheritance Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1)."},{"page":"events.html","id":"catalogue-development","title":"Development's events","status":"In the game","text":"Event Who Chance What it does On the TV Sickness Each player 3% + air + own pollution Your colonist walks at half pace ( DEV_WALK.sickPace 0.5 ) this turn, unless you hold a Medicine, which is then used up and cures it. Bulletin, with the reason Epidemic Colony 0.5% Every player who holds a Medicine uses one. Everyone else is sick this development and the next (unless they then hold a Medicine, which cures it). Headline MULE theft Each player 1% One installed MULE is taken off one of your plots at random. The plot stands idle until you outfit it again; its run in a row and any filter go with it. Rolls again at the month end. Spotlight"},{"page":"events.html","id":"sickness-numbers","title":"Sickness in numbers","status":"","text":"Part of the chance Points Base 3 Clean air 0 Smoggy air +15 Toxic air +30 Collapse +50 Each air point your plots caused last month +3, at most +40 Cap 90 The sickness bulletin says why: \"fell sick: the air is bad\", \"their plots foul the air\", \"bad luck\" or \"the epidemic\", whichever raised the chance most. Hunger and sickness are public: everyone sees who is hungry and who is sick. Why it is like this Heiko, 28 October: \"is there a general event that a player can get sick? if not we should add it. we should make this more likely if the general air pollution is high, and we should make this much more likely when he is a big polluter.\" His catalogue numbers: base 3%, +3 per own air point last month up to +40. On pace: \"sickness half your walking speed\". Measured at these odds (WS1, 28 Oct): sick in 4 to 5% of player-months; last month's top polluter 7 to 11%. In the code: packages/engine/src/game.ts::startDevelopment · packages/engine/src/events.ts::rollPlayerEvent , ::playerEventReason · apps/server/src/mule-game.ts::sickView · config.ts::EVENTS.player.sickness , EVENTS.colony.epidemic , EVENTS.player.muleTheft , DEV_WALK.sickPace Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1)."},{"page":"events.html","id":"catalogue-production","title":"Production's events","status":"In the game","text":"Production's events change what plots make. They act last on each plot's line, after the ground, the bonuses, pollution and any shortage; several can hit the same plot, each on what the ones before it left, never below 0. They only reach farms, wells, Energy plots and mines, never factories (a meteor strike is the exception). See Production ."},{"page":"events.html","id":"production-colony-events","title":"Colony events","status":"","text":"Event Chance Hits What it does On the TV Dust storm 8% Every Energy plot on the map, sweeping in from one edge Takes 50% of each plot's output (it keeps half, rounded down). Headline Drought 8% (+2 Toxic, +4 Collapse) Every Food and Water plot, sweeping in Each makes 1 less. Headline Acid rain 1.6% (+4 Smoggy, +8 Toxic, +12 Collapse) Every Energy and Food plot, sweeping in Each makes 1 less. Headline Solar flare 8% Every Energy plot, sweeping in Each makes 2 more. Headline Comet pass 8% Every Water plot, sweeping in Each makes 2 more. Headline Flood season 8% (+10 after a drought last month) Every Food plot on river ground They make nothing this month. Headline Volcanic ash 6.4% Every farm, well, Energy plot and mine within 2 steps (diagonals count) of one volcanic or mountain plot Each makes 1 less. Headline Cold snap 3.2% Every plot Every plot needs 2 times its Energy this month. Output is not touched directly; plots left short of Energy make half (factories nothing). Headline Cavern collapse 6% per cavern Each crystal cavern mined for Crystite this month, after the output The cavern caves in and becomes a plain plot: it loses its +4 Crystite. The mine and this month's output stay. Spotlight Meteor strike 3.2% One tile at random, owned or not, never the store; after the output The plot made its output and counted its pollution; then its MULE, run count and filter are destroyed, any special field gives way, and it becomes a meteor crater with Crystite rated 4 . The owner keeps the plot. Headline The sweeping events (dust storm, drought, acid rain, solar flare, comet pass) come in from a random edge of the map and reach the plots nearest that edge first; they hit every player's plots of their goods. Flood and volcanic ash spread from one source, nearest plots first. A colony event with nothing to hit (no Energy plot anywhere for a dust storm) does not fire."},{"page":"events.html","id":"production-player-events","title":"Player events","status":"","text":"Event Chance Hits What it does On the TV Pest swarm 2.4% (richest +1, poorest −1) One of the player's Food plots, then their touching Food plots, up to 4 plots Pests eat 50% : each plot keeps half, rounded down. Spotlight Wildfire 2.4% (richest +1; +3 after a drought this phase or last month) One of the player's Food plots and every Food plot of theirs joined to it The burnt plot makes nothing; every other farm in that group makes 1 less. Spotlight Bumper harvest 2.4% (poorest +1.5, richest −1) One of the player's farms, wells, Energy plots or mines It makes 2 times its output (after any shortage). Spotlight Example: two events on one plot A farm makes 3 before events. A drought takes 1 (2 left), then a pest swarm eats half (1 left). The order is the order the events fired: colony events first, then each player's. Why it is like this Heiko, 29 October, chose option \"b\": every production chance at 1.6 times the catalogue's, keeping the catalogue's relative odds, for the liveliness he asked for. Measured with these odds (40 games a cell): about 1 event a month in Clean air (0.93 to 1.01), about 2 in Smoggy air (2.02 to 2.15), about 2.8 in Toxic air (2.71 to 2.83). The cold snap and the meteor strike take the same 1.6 (2% × 1.6 = 3.2%), as the PM picked and Heiko accepted on 4 November. The meteor strikes after the output so the MULE produces this month, then is destroyed (Heiko, 4 November). What is changing Unclear in the code: the cavern collapse's roll is not held back in month 1 (the other rolled events are), because it is rolled from the special fields' settlement, which does not read EVENTS.firstMonth . In practice a cavern is rarely mined in month 1. Owner of the answer: the Balance team (WS1). In the code: packages/engine/src/events.ts::buildEvent , ::eventOutput , ::spread , ::plotEvent · packages/engine/src/specials.ts::settleSpecials , ::formMeteorCrater · config.ts::EVENTS ( dustStorm , drought , acidRain , solarFlare , cometPass , volcanicAsh , pestSwarm , wildfire , bumperHarvest , colony , player ), MAP.meteorCrater Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1); craters and caverns with the Maps team (WS5)."},{"page":"events.html","id":"catalogue-auction","title":"The goods auction's events","status":"In the game","text":"Both roll before the store posts its prices, so the prices already follow the loss. Event Who Chance What it does On the TV Pirate raid Colony 1% Every player's Crystite and the store's Crystite go to 0. Headline Store fire Colony 1% The store's stock of every good goes to 0, and the MULEs in its corral too. At the month end the store rebuilds the corral only from the Smithore it holds by then (what players sold it at that auction), so the next month may be short of MULEs. Headline Why it is like this Heiko, 28 October: \"pirates take all crystite\", the players' and the store's; and \"store fire burns all store storage, not only food, all the storage that the store had\". The PM ruled the corral's MULEs burn too. In the code: packages/engine/src/events.ts::applyEvents ( pirateRaid , storeFire ) · packages/engine/src/game.ts::advancePhase (auction branch) · config.ts::EVENTS.colony.pirateRaid , .storeFire Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1)."},{"page":"events.html","id":"catalogue-upkeep","title":"The month end's events","status":"In the game","text":"Event Who Chance What it does On the TV MULE theft Each player 1% As in development: one installed MULE taken, the plot idle. Spotlight Rats in the silo Each player 1.5% Rats eat 50% of your stored Food, rounded down. Only when that is at least 1 (you hold 2 Food or more). Bulletin Crooked accountant Each player 1% Takes 10% of your cash, rounded down. Only when that is at least 1 (you hold 10 cr or more). Bulletin Package from home Each player 1.5% +3 Food. Bulletin Nothing an event takes ever goes below 0. In the code: packages/engine/src/events.ts::applyEvents , OFF_MAP · config.ts::EVENTS.player ( muleTheft , ratsInSilo , crookedAccountant , packageFromHome ) Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1)."},{"page":"events.html","id":"event-show","title":"How the TV and the phone show events","status":"In the game","text":"Every event plays its own story where it happens: a dust wall sweeps in from its edge, the river swells and spills, a pirate ship lands at the store and carries the Crystite up its ramp, the store burns shelf by shelf. The show is a pure function of the server's clock, so a phone that reconnects lands in the same place as everyone else."},{"page":"events.html","id":"event-show-order","title":"What plays, and for how long","status":"","text":"When a phase fires events, its show opens with a short card of what fired, then plays the events in the order they fired. Each event has a weight that sets how big its story is. Weight How it plays Length Events Intro The phase's opening card of what fired. None in a quiet phase. 1.8 s Every phase with an event Headline The full story over the whole map. 7.0 s Dust storm, drought, acid rain, solar flare, comet pass, flood season, volcanic ash, Federation tax, Federation grant, immigration wave, epidemic, cold snap, meteor strike, pirate raid, store fire. Any event the table forgets plays as a headline. Spotlight A shorter story on the plots it hits. 5.2 s Pest swarm, wildfire, bumper harvest, cavern collapse, MULE theft Bulletin One line in a roll call that every bulletin of the phase shares. 4.0 s shared Inheritance, rats in the silo, crooked accountant, package from home, sickness A phase's events play for at most 30 s in all. Past that, the intro keeps its length and every event's share is cut in the same proportion. Example: a busy development An epidemic fires, and two players fall sick. Show: intro 1.8 s + epidemic 7.0 s + one roll call 4.0 s naming both sick players = 12.8 s. Development's clock starts when the show ends."},{"page":"events.html","id":"event-show-hold","title":"Who waits","status":"","text":"Land grant, land auction, development, goods auction: every clock of the phase starts when the show ends. Until then nothing can be done; a press is refused with \"The event is still playing\". Planning: not held. The events play over the hub while people act. Production: its events play in the production show's \"This month on Irata\" beat, and production grows by their length (see the production show ). The month end: not held today, because it has no clock."},{"page":"events.html","id":"event-show-tv","title":"On the TV","status":"","text":"The TV plays every story on the big map. A row of badges, one per event of the phase, lights the one playing and ticks off the ones done. A log beside the map keeps what is playing now and what already happened, in words. Changes off the map (cash, Crystite, colonists, the store's shelves) show as chips on each player's slot and the colony bar."},{"page":"events.html","id":"event-show-phone","title":"On the phone","status":"","text":"When What your phone does A TV is in the room A light card for each event as it plays, your own plots marked, a short buzz as each is announced, and \"what it did to you\". Alone, no TV The phone plays each event on its own map at its own pace, then the result. Alone, and you tap It skips to the result: the card of what every event did to you. Alone, with reduced motion The same show without camera moves or shaking. Outside production the phone draws the event over whatever phase screen is up. While a phase is held, it covers the screen until the show ends; in planning the screen stays usable, with the light card floating over it. Why it is like this Heiko: \"I would like to see the event and the event ux and the cool event animations\"; \"make this really entertaining and all should get their animations\". And for a player alone: \"it could be that a player is playing alone on his phone. how would that look then for him\". Heiko approved the \"Living weather\" gallery on 29 October as the design to build, and the server owns every length so the clock and the animation cannot disagree. In the code: config.ts::EVENT_SHOW · apps/server/src/mule-game.ts::eventShow , ::weightOf , ::holdsForEvents · packages/ui/src/events/ ( tv.tsx , phone.tsx , stories.tsx , stories-ledger.tsx ) · apps/web/src/events/TvEventLayer.tsx , PhoneEventOverlay.tsx Kept current by: the Events team (WS8), with the Table team (WS4) for the TV and the UX team (WS6) for the phone."},{"page":"events.html","id":"event-waves","title":"Wave 1 and wave 2","status":"In the game","text":"Events reached the game in steps, in the order Heiko ruled on 28 October. Step What it brought Status Wave 1 (28 and 29 Oct) Every event on its own dice; several a month; phases; personal events per player with the lean toward the richest and poorest; sickness as the first player event; cavern collapse as an event. The ten production events kept their effects, with every chance at 1.6 times the catalogue's. In the game Wave 2 (4 Nov) Thirteen more events across the month: Federation tax and grant, immigration wave, inheritance, epidemic, MULE theft, cold snap, meteor strike, pirate raid, store fire, rats in the silo, crooked accountant, package from home. Every phase can now surprise you. Each plays its own story. In the game The colony's mood events (G-5) Festival, general strike, emigration, blamed for the unrest, then riot, store strike, walkout, loyal crew, volunteer clean-up, and the mood parts of immigration and MULE theft. Being built; see below Later Warnings, forecasts, hedges, insurance, chains. Backlog Why it is like this Heiko's \"steer to completion, a functional playable game first\" (4 November) put wave 2 into the first milestone. Wave 2 brought no new balancing: its chances are the catalogue's, apart from the 1.6 on the two production rows. Heiko's six picks on wave 2 (4 November, \"I go with your picks\"): the meteor after the output; cold snap and meteor at 3.2%; MULE theft can fire twice a month; the epidemic makes you sick next development only if you then still hold no Medicine; the Federation grant is new money; inheritance by wealth rank, 300 for the poorest to 100 for the richest. In the code: config.ts::EVENTS · specs docs/specs/2026-10-28-ws1-event-model.md , docs/specs/2026-11-04-ws8-wave2-events.md Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1)."},{"page":"events.html","id":"colony-social-events","title":"The colony's social events in planning","status":"Being built","text":"When politics is on, the colony has a mood, from 0 to 100 , starting at 60 . Its band is Thriving (80 and up), Content (60 to 79), Restless (40 to 59), Unhappy (20 to 39) or Unrest (below 20); see Colony and politics . The first set of social events lets the mood tilt the odds: a happy colony throws festivals, an unhappy one strikes and leaves. They roll in planning, use the same dice and influences as every other event, and add two more: the mood band, and a hard \"never in\" list of bands where the event cannot fire whatever raises it. With politics off they never fire. Event Who Chance (points) Never in What it does Festival Colony 1 base; Thriving +5, Content +1; +5 with a stadium standing Unhappy, Unrest The mood jumps +10 at once (beyond the month's usual step), the colony gains 2 prestige for good, and the colonists eat 25% more Food this month. Never in the same month as a general strike. General strike Colony 0 base; Restless +2, Unhappy +5, Unrest +10; +3 when the store's Food price is above 1.5 times its usual price Thriving Announced in planning on the good the most plots in the colony make (a tie on the dice). Unless the Governor pays the strikers, every plot of that good makes 20% of its output in production, rounded down. Emigration wave Colony 0 base; Restless +1, Unhappy +4, Unrest +8; Toxic air +3, Collapse +3 none 10% of the colonists leave, for good (rounded). Immigration wave (its mood part) Colony 2 base as today; Thriving +6, Content +1 Unhappy, Unrest As today: 10% more colonists, for good. Blamed for the unrest Each player 0 base; Unhappy +2, Unrest +5; the richest +2 in every band none in this step (see below) The player loses 1 reputation, so fewer votes."},{"page":"events.html","id":"general-strike","title":"The general strike, step by step","status":"","text":"Planning: the strike is announced on one good. Everyone sees it. Still in planning, once the votes are over, the Governor may pay the strikers COLONY.strike.cost = 250 cr from the colony fund. The fund's reserve does not hold this back: paying may empty the fund. Nobody else can pay; with no Governor the strike holds. Production: if unpaid, every plot of the struck good makes COLONY.strike.output = 0.2 of its output, rounded down, and the production show plays a \"General strike\" event on those plots. If paid, everything runs in full and only a news line says so. Example: a Food strike The colony is Unhappy and Food sells at 50 (above 1.5 × 30 = 45). Strike chance: 0 + 5 + 3 = 8% . It fires on Food, the good most plots make. Lena governs and the fund holds 400 cr. If she pays, the fund falls to 150 and every farm works. If not, a farm that would have made 4 makes 0.8, rounded down to 0 , and one that would have made 6 makes 1. Why it is like this From the colony design (G-5): running the colony badly has visible consequences (strikes, theft, emigration), and happiness tilts the odds of social events, not the overall event rate. The Governor's choice to pay is G-5's rule 26. Measured in the build (40 games on each of 3 seeds, politics on): strikes fired 10 to 16 times in Restless months and 5 to 7 in Unhappy ones, and no game reached Thriving or Unrest, so festivals were rare (1 to 4). The tuning belongs to the colony's balance pass at the end. What is changing Being built: C26 PR-A, PR #370 into the colony branch ( ws7-politics ), by the Balance team (WS1); reviewed and approved, merging when CI is green. It adds SOCIAL_EVENTS in config.ts , COLONY.strike.output 0.2 beside cost , and the four event keys. Not on trunk yet: today the strike's pay-off rule ( colony.ts::payStrike , strikeHolds ) exists but no event calls a strike, and production does not read it. In the code (branch ws1-c26a ): packages/engine/src/events.ts ( festival , generalStrike , emigrationWave , blamedForUnrest ) · config.ts::SOCIAL_EVENTS , COLONY.strike · on trunk: packages/engine/src/colony.ts::callStrike , ::strikeHolds , ::payStrike , ::moodShift , ::moodBand , config.ts::HAPPINESS Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1), with the Politics team (WS7) for the colony."},{"page":"events.html","id":"colony-social-events-next","title":"More social events: riot, strikes, walkout, loyal crew, clean-up","status":"Designed","text":"The second set of the colony's social events rolls in development, production and the month end, and two rulings of 5 November change the first set. Event Phase Who What it does Store strike Development Colony The store sells no MULEs this turn. Walkout Production Each player The player's plots of one good make 20% this month. Loyal crew Production Each player One of the player's plots makes 1 more this month. Riot Month end Colony For each good, the store loses its stock × 1/3, rounded down, a named fraction in the settings (so a shelf of 1 or 2 loses nothing). One prestige object is damaged and earns no prestige until it is repaired. Volunteer clean-up Month end Colony The air gauge drops. MULE theft (its mood part) Development, month end Each player Its chance follows the mood. Blamed for the unrest Planning Each player PM ruling, 5 Nov: it never fires in a Thriving or Content colony. Immigration wave Planning Colony PM ruling, 5 Nov: its air parts, +1 point in Clean air and never when the air is Toxic. Why it is like this The riot rule is the PM's ruling of 5 November, from the catalogue's \"the store loses a third of its stock\": per good, so the loss is exact and a small shelf is spared. Heiko asked on 28 October for the riot to be seen: \"also we need something for riots, maybe an angry mob walking on the map?\"; the gallery's riot is a mob of every species that gathers, loots the store and smashes a prestige object. What is changing Designed; built next as C26 PR-B by the Balance team (WS1), with damageObject for the riot's object (the Politics team, WS7). The chances, the walkout's and loyal crew's details and the clean-up's size come from the approved catalogue and land with PR-B; this page gives them once they are in the settings. Not yet clear from the ruling: whether \"never when Toxic\" for the immigration wave also covers Collapse. Specs: docs/specs/2026-10-28-g5-colony.md (C26, RULE-26) · the gallery design/mocks/ws8-events Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1), with the Politics team (WS7)."},{"page":"events.html","id":"colony-event-shows","title":"Strikes, mood events and gauges on screen","status":"Being built","text":"Three pull requests by the Events team bring the colony's events to the TV and the phones. They are built against the frozen event contract, so the stories go live when the events themselves land."},{"page":"events.html","id":"strike-on-screen","title":"The general strike","status":"","text":"TV header: a badge beside the month: \"STRIKE · Food 20%\" while unpaid, \"PAID · −250 cr\" once paid. TV planning map and the phone's map: a striker with a placard on every plot of the struck good, each with a \"STRIKE · 20%\" chip, gone once paid. The Governor's phone: a strike card with the fund before and after, \"Pay the strikers\" and \"Let them strike\" (which keeps the way to pay open until planning ends). While a vote is open both read \"After the votes\". Everyone else's phone: \"General strike on Food: Kai decides whether the fund pays 250 cr\", then \"Kai pays the Food strikers 250 cr: the strike is off.\""},{"page":"events.html","id":"mood-events-on-screen","title":"The mood events' stories","status":"","text":"Festival: bunting over the colony, lanterns, a crowd at the store; the changes card reads \"Mood +10 · Prestige +2\". General strike (planning): strikers walk out to every plot of the struck good; the log reads, for example, \"Food at 20% on 4 farms unless the Governor pays\". Riot: a third of each shelf goes; a damaged object gets the map's hit mark (or, for the hospital, which has no plot, a line in the log). Any event that moves a gauge (mood, prestige, air, reputation) says so in words, for example \"Lena's Food farm stands idle · Mood −2\"."},{"page":"events.html","id":"gauges-month-end","title":"The Happiness gauge at the month end","status":"","text":"At the month end the TV's Happiness gauge opens on last month's number and counts to this month's; as it crosses a band the label flips (Restless to Unhappy, say) and the gauge pulses. It plays once; a TV that reconnects, or reduced motion, shows the end state at once. With politics off there is no gauge. What is changing Being built in the colony branch ( ws7-politics ) by the Events team (WS8): PR #368 (the strike on the TV and phones, and the Governor paying it), PR #364 (the mood events in the show; approved, CI green), PR #365 (the gauges count and land at the month end). The scaffolds are design/mocks/ws8-colony-events . In the code (branches ws8-cev-strike , ws8-cev-stories , ws8-cev-gauges ): packages/ui/src/events/ ( stories-colony.tsx , copy.ts , glyphs.tsx ) · apps/web/src/table/TableApp.tsx · apps/web/src/phone/hub/today.tsx Kept current by: the Events team (WS8)."},{"page":"events.html","id":"events-later","title":"Warnings, forecasts, hedges and chains","status":"Backlog","text":"The event design goes further than the game. None of this is built, and nobody is on it. Warnings and forecasts. Most events announced before they hit: dust on the horizon, a pirate sighting, or an observatory's forecast. The month report already has a place for warnings, but it is always empty. Hedges. Insurance, silos, vaults against pirates, levees against floods, storm shutters, firebreaks, Medicine reserves. Lasting effects. A flood's fertile silt, an eruption's new mountain, a comet's falling Water price. Chains. Storylines over several months: the Long Drought, Pirate Season, the Crystite Rush, the Plague Ship, the Long Winter, Colonist Unrest, the Federation Audit, the Alien Signal. More events from the design catalogue: planetquake, ice melt, meteor shower, derelict ship crash, off-world recession, Crystite crash, land speculator and others. Plot traits that change event risks. Bedrock, sheltered, unstable, infested and flood plain are on the map but no event reads them (see Production ). Design: docs/design/04-event-catalogue.md · packages/engine/src/types.ts::EventWarning (unused) Kept current by: the PM, with the Balance team (WS1)."},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"players-and-seats","title":"Players and seats","status":"In the game","text":"A game seats 2 to 6 players. Each seat is either a person with a phone (or a laptop) or a computer player. The table needs at least one person, because only a person can host it, so a game has at most 5 computer players. People join from their own phone with the room's four-letter code or its QR. Each picks a name (up to 16 characters), a colour and a species. The host is the first person at the table who is connected. The host adds computer players, removes seats, closes the room and presses Start. If the host's phone drops, the next connected person hosts until it comes back. Computer players are added by the host: The Accountant, The Baron, The Grudge, The Gambler and The Good Neighbour, in that order, each with its own temperament. All play at the Trader skill level. With a Claude key on the server they can think and chat with Claude on a small budget. See Computer players . The big screen (a TV or laptop) is not a seat. It shows the public view and has no controls. See The table and the phones . Once the host presses Start the seats are fixed. Nobody new can join a running game; a player whose phone reloads gets their own seat back, and a player can move their seat to another device with a one-time code. In the code: packages/engine/src/config.ts::GAME (minPlayers, maxPlayers) · apps/server/src/mule-game.ts::AI_NAMES, DEFAULT_AI_LEVEL · packages/ai/src/personas.ts::personaFor · packages/tabletop/src/room.ts Kept current by: the PM, with the Table team (WS4) for seats and the lobby."},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"species","title":"Species","status":"In the game","text":"Every player is drawn as a small creature of their species in their colour. Two players may pick the same species. A player who does not pick gets the species of their seat number, in the order below. Each species has one perk; all perk numbers are placeholders until the balance work sets them. Species Perk, as the game applies it today Value Zipwing Walks faster in development × 1.25 Bramblet Extra Food on every farm +1 Gearling Pays less for MULEs and outfits (the share of the normal price) 0.95 Mollox Extra output on every working mine; a geo analysis takes this share of the normal time +1 · 0.5 Glimmer Meant to see next month's event a month early: not built none Quibbit First in line when the store hands out units in turns at the goods auction first What is changing Two facts do not match. (1) The Glimmer has no perk in the rules: nothing in the engine, the server or the computer players reads it, so a Glimmer plays with no advantage. (2) The lobby's perk text promises more than the rules give: it says a Zipwing \"moves twice as fast\" (the rules give × 1.25) and a Gearling pays \"a quarter less\" (the rules give 5 % less). The numbers were cut on purpose (at × 1.5 Zipwings won 37 % of games, at 0.75 Gearlings 44 %); the words were not updated. Owners: the Balance team (WS1) for the Glimmer's perk and the numbers, the Table team (WS4) for the lobby's words. In the code: packages/engine/src/config.ts::SPECIES_PERK, DEFAULT_SPECIES · packages/engine/src/auction.ts (Quibbit) · packages/ui/src/components/species.ts::SPECIES_PERK (the lobby's words) Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1) for perks; the Table team (WS4) for the lobby."},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"setup","title":"Setting up a game","status":"In the game","text":"When the host presses Start, the server draws a seed and makes the map from it. The same seed and the same moves replay the same game, which is how games are recorded and checked. The map's size depends on how many players sit at the table, and the store sits on its own tile on it. Players Map Width × height 2 or 3 Small 13 × 7 4 Mid 15 × 9 5 or 6 Large 19 × 11"},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"start-values","title":"What everyone starts with","status":"","text":"Who Start value Amount Each player Money 1,000 cr Each player Food · Water · Energy in the home depot 2 · 6 · 2 Each player Reputation, out of 100 50 Each player Land none The colony Colonists 100 The colony Air (0 clean, 100 worst) 0 The colony Sales tax on every sale, into the colony fund 5 % The colony Happiness (with the council on), out of 100 60 The store MULEs in the corral 10 The store Capital 4,000 cr The store Food, for each seat 4 The store Energy, for each seat 5 The store Water · Smithore · Crystite 20 · 20 · 0 The store Rare earth · Alloy · Electronics · Medicine 4 · 8 · 0 · 4 The store's first prices are set where supply and demand put them at the start, not at the goods' reference prices. Month 1 opens in planning. Example A four-seat game starts with a 15 × 9 map, four players holding 1,000 cr, 2 Food, 6 Water and 2 Energy each, and a store holding 4 × 4 = 16 Food and 4 × 5 = 20 Energy beside its fixed 20 Water and 20 Smithore. Why it is like this Heiko set the start on 5 Nov after playing the walking build: players start with only 2 Energy and 2 Food, and the store holds 5 Energy and 4 Food for each seat. Asked whether to tune the food shortage this causes, he chose to \"judge it at the table\" rather than run a balance sweep first. In the code: packages/engine/src/config.ts::START, storeStockFor, mapSize, TAX, HAPPINESS · packages/engine/src/game.ts::createGame Kept current by: the PM, with the Balance team (WS1) for the numbers."},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"the-month","title":"The month, phase by phase","status":"In the game","text":"A game runs month by month. Every month walks through the same phases in the same order. Events can strike at the start of planning, development and the goods auction, during production and at the month end, from month 2 on. With the council on, an election or a law vote opens planning in some months. Phase What happens Who acts How long Planning Election day in months 1, 4, 7 and 10, then any law vote; then everyone plans: order a geo drone, list a plot or MULE for the land auction, read the market, chat. The Governor sets the tax and runs the colony. Everyone at once No clock: waits for every person's Ready, or the host's Continue Land grant Free plots light up one at a time, top row first, left to right. The first to press Claim takes the lit plot. Everyone at once, one claim each One pass of the light, 0.65 s a plot; ends as soon as everyone has claimed or is done Land auction Plots and MULEs listed in planning, and sometimes a free plot from the colony, are sold one lot at a time. Skipped in a month with nothing listed. Every bidder walks up; the seller watches Per lot: 3 s intro, 12 s walk (late leader changes add up to 6 s), 2.5 s result Development Every colonist walks the colony at once: buy a MULE, outfit it, install it on your plot, analyse ground, fit filters, or go to the pub for cash. Everyone at once, computer players included One 60 s clock for everyone; ends early once everyone is at the pub or out of time Production Every working plot makes its good. The TV plays the harvest good by good, then the bonuses, the losses, the month's events, and the goods fly into each player's silo. Nobody: watch As long as its show, about 14 s in a quiet month, at most about 51 s; can be sped up × 3 Goods auction The nine goods come up one at a time. Each player picks a side, then walks up and down the board to buy and sell with each other, the store and, in its month, the export ship. Everyone at once Per good: side pick with no clock, a 20 s walk, 2.5 s receipt Month end Players and colonists eat, goods spoil, the export ship leaves, the store builds MULEs, the colony collects taxes and pays its bills, the mood moves. Everyone reads the league table by Worth. Everyone taps \"On to month N\" No clock: waits for every person, or the host's Continue After the month end of the last month, the game is over and the final table shows instead. In the code: packages/engine/src/game.ts::advancePhase (the order) · packages/engine/src/config.ts::PHASES, LOTS, WALK, DEVELOPMENT, PRODUCTION_SHOW, EVENTS.firstMonth · apps/server/src/mule-game.ts::startPhase Kept current by: the PM, with the Auctions team (WS3) for phases and the clock."},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"planning","title":"Planning","status":"In the game","text":"Planning is the month's calm moment, with no clock. Everyone uses their phone's hub at once: Order a geo drone for 60 cr. It surveys up to 2 free or listed plots from orbit, and its results arrive as the land grant starts, in time to choose. Sell a plot or a MULE : list it for the land auction with a minimum price. Each player may list up to 2 lots a month, and the land auction takes at most 4 lots a month from everyone together. Talk and read the market : chat and whispers, the store's prices, the export ship's next call."},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"planning-colony","title":"Colony and council steps in planning","status":"","text":"With the council on (the server's default), planning carries the colony's politics, in this order: Election day in months 1 , 4 , 7 and 10 : candidates announce with a tax promise, everyone votes in secret, and the TV and every phone play the reveal. Today one office is elected, the Governor. A Governor who has served 2 terms in a row may not run again until someone else has held it. The law vote , when the Governor proposed a law the month before: yes or no, in secret, then the reveal. Planning proper : the Governor sets the sales tax (0 to 15 %, at most 5 points from the month's starting rate), may propose a law for next month, commission a building, pay off strikers; anyone may donate to the building being funded. As planning closes, the game records whether the Governor kept their tax promise. Every stage of the votes waits for everyone's Done, with no clock; only the reveals are timed shows. See Colony and politics for the rules of each. In the code: packages/engine/src/game.ts::orderGeoDrone · packages/engine/src/config.ts::DEVELOPMENT.droneFee, LOTS.perPlayer, POLITICS · packages/engine/src/politics.ts::openPlanning Kept current by: the PM; the Politics team (WS7) for the council steps."},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"land-grant","title":"The land grant","status":"In the game","text":"Every free plot lights up once, in reading order: the top row from left to right, then the next row. Each lit plot stays lit for 0.65 s. The first player to press Claim while a plot is lit takes it. Each seat may claim 1 plot a month. A player who wants nothing presses \"I'm ready\" (done with land) and claims no more this month. The grant ends as soon as every seat has claimed or is done, when no free land is left, or when the light has passed every free plot once. Once every person is done, the light races on: the computer players' presses resolve at once on each lit plot, and the light moves every 0.08 s, so the TV shows the rest of the pass in a few seconds. A phone that drops in the grant does not end it for anyone; it just does not claim. A press counts for the plot the player saw lit. Computer players press after a human reaction time, so a quick person can beat them. Example In month 1 of a four-seat game, the 15 × 9 map has 134 free plots. One full pass of the light would take 134 × 0.65 s = 87.1 s. The pass is usually much shorter: as soon as all four seats have claimed their one plot, the grant ends. Why it is like this One claim a month: Heiko, 5 Nov (\"we should only have 1\"). The store's MULE supply is sized separately, so cutting the claims did not shrink the corral. One pass with no clock: Heiko approved on 28 Oct that the grant always completes one full pass over the free plots instead of running on a fixed 60 s clock, so a player who wants no land does not hold up the table. In the code: packages/engine/src/config.ts::GAME.claimsPerGrant, GAME.grantSweeps, GAME.grantTickMs, PHASES.grantFastForwardMs · packages/engine/src/game.ts::claimPlot, grantDone · apps/server/src/mule-game.ts::scheduleGrantTick Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3). Details in Land and geology ."},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"land-auction","title":"The land auction","status":"In the game","text":"After the grant, each month there is a 10 % chance that the colony puts one free plot up for sale, at a minimum of two thirds of a plot's Worth. With nothing listed by players or the colony, the land auction is skipped and development starts at once. Otherwise the lots come up one at a time: a 3 s intro, a 12 s walk in which bidders hold Up, and a 2.5 s result. A change of leader in the last 2 s puts the clock back to 2 s, at most 6 s more per lot. The highest bid at the bell wins; the store buys what does not sell. In the code: packages/engine/src/config.ts::LOTS · packages/engine/src/lots.ts Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3). Details in Land and geology ."},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"development","title":"Development and its clock","status":"In the game","text":"Development is the arcade part of the month. Every colonist starts at the store, and everyone steers theirs live at once: buy a MULE at the corral, fit it at an outfitter, lead it out and install it on your plot. You can also analyse ground ( 8 s on the plot), fit a filter ( 1 Alloy), re-outfit a MULE, fight wolves, race for a glint, or go to the pub."},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"one-clock","title":"One clock for everyone","status":"","text":"There is one development clock of 60 s, the same for every player, shown on the TV and on every phone. It counts down from the start in every month, month 1 included. Hunger and sickness never take time off the clock; they slow your walk instead: Hunger : a player who starts development short of this month's Food walks slower, from 60 % of normal pace with no Food at all, rising evenly to full pace with all of it. Sickness : each development a player may fall sick (likelier in dirty air and for a big polluter). A sick player walks at 50 % pace. One Medicine held when the turn starts cures it and is used up. The two multiply: no Food and sick is 0.6 × 0.5 = 30 % pace. A Zipwing's × 1.25 comes on top. Development ends when the clock runs out, or earlier once every player, computer players included, is at the pub or out of time (or has dropped), and no colonist is still walking. A MULE still on your lead when your turn ends runs away; one you took off your own plot goes back to it."},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"the-pub","title":"The pub","status":"","text":"Walking into the pub ends your turn and pays cash: more the more time you have left, and more later in the game. The pub pays 20 + ( 60 + 20 × month) × the share of the clock left. Example In month 3, Lena installs her MULE and walks into the pub with 45 s of the 60 s left. Her share is 45 / 60 = 0.75, so the pub pays 20 + (60 + 3 × 20) × 0.75 = 20 + 120 × 0.75 = 110 cr. A player who walks straight to the pub in month 1 with the full clock gets 20 + 80 = 100 cr; in month 12, 20 + 300 = 320 cr. Why it is like this Heiko, 28 Oct: \"if we just steer it via the walking then managing the time bars also gets easier. Then we only have one global time bar for all users, which is much better.\" The PM then ruled that every per-player condition acts through walking pace, never the clock. The clock became 60 s on 4 Nov (Heiko: \"lets just generally increase the time to 60 seconds\"). The hunger floor is Heiko's (\"Yes, 60%\", 4 Nov). Month 1 counts down like any month since 5 Nov (Heiko: \"Yes, always count\"); it used to wait for everyone's first MULE. What is changing The old tap-to-walk development screen is being removed so every game steers (PR #369, the Auctions team, being built). Steered walking is already the default in every game the server hosts. In the code: packages/engine/src/config.ts::DEVELOPMENT, DEV_WALK, DEV_STEER · packages/engine/src/game.ts::startDevelopment, pubPayoutFor, visitPub, endLead · apps/server/src/mule-game.ts::startDevelopment, checkDevelopmentOver Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3) for the clock; details in Development ."},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"production","title":"Production","status":"In the game","text":"Every plot with a MULE makes its good; nobody acts, everyone watches. The phase lasts exactly as long as its show: a 1 s intro, a 1.1 s beat for each good made (every player's plots of that good together), all bonuses for 1.6 s, all losses for 1.6 s, the month's production events (at most 30 s), then the flight into the silos and a 2 s hold. Once every person at the table has pressed Speed up, the rest of the show plays 3 times faster. Computer players always agree; a dropped phone is not waited for. After the show, each player can press \"Ready for the auction\". Example A quiet month in which plots made Food, Energy and Smithore: 1 s intro + 3 × 1.1 s + 0.5 s gap + 1.6 s + 1.6 s + 0.5 s gap + 2.4 s of flights + 0.75 s for the last flight + 2 s hold = 13.65 s. The longest possible month, all nine goods and events at their 30 s cap, comes to 50.75 s. Why it is like this Heiko, 5 Nov, on the production mock: harvest \"resource by resource for all players in parallel\", then the bonuses for everyone, then the losses, \"and the way that the resources fly to the silos is super\". The PM ruled the same day that Ready waits for the end of the show and Speed up is the only shortcut, following his \"more dramatic\" ask. In the code: packages/engine/src/config.ts::PRODUCTION_SHOW · packages/engine/src/production-show.ts::productionShow · apps/server/src/mule-game.ts::startPhaseTimer, hurryIfAllAsked Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1) for the rules; details in Production ."},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"goods-auction","title":"The goods auction","status":"In the game","text":"Auction events strike first (a pirate raid or a store fire), the store posts its prices, and the goods come up one at a time: Food, Water, Energy, Smithore, Crystite, Rare earth, Alloy, Electronics, Medicine. A good nobody can trade is skipped. For each good: Side pick on a status screen of what everyone holds and needs. No clock: it waits for everyone, or the host moves on. The walk : 20 s. Where a buyer and a seller meet, units change hands, and the clock runs at 25 % speed meanwhile. Close : 2.5 s in which each phone shows its receipt for the good. Every sale pays the sales tax into the colony fund. In the code: packages/engine/src/config.ts::WALK · packages/engine/src/auction.ts · packages/engine/src/game.ts::advancePhase (auction entry) Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3); details in The goods auction ."},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"month-end","title":"The month end","status":"In the game","text":"The month end (the game calls it upkeep) settles the month in this order: Any month-end events strike. Players eat and drink : each player needs 2 Food and 1 Water for themselves, plus 1 Food for every 4 crew or part of 4, and 1 Water for every 6 crew or part of 6. A plot with a MULE making a raw good has a crew of 1, a factory 2, a green belt none. Energy for the plots was already paid in production. Colonists buy 1 Food and 1 Water for every 20 colonists from the store, at its selling price, as far as their wages reach. Fed, the colony grows by 4 %; short, it shrinks by 5 %. Goods spoil , and anything above 10 units of a good in a player's home depot is lost. The store's goods spoil at a quarter of the normal rate. The export ship , in its month, takes the store's surplus and leaves. The store builds MULEs from Smithore, until its corral holds 2 for each seat plus 2 spare. The colony (with the council on): law taxes are collected, the Federation pays 150 cr into the colony fund, the Governor is paid 50 cr from the fund, buildings pay their upkeep and go up, the building being funded takes what the fund can spare, and the colony's mood moves toward its target by at most 5 points. Then the screens show the league table by Worth, with each player's change since last month, and how the colony is doing. The month end has no clock: it waits for every person to tap \"On to month N\" (computer players are on at once), or for the host's Continue. A dropped phone is waited for here too. After the last month's end the game goes straight to the final table. What is changing In the colony branch, not yet on trunk: at the month end the TV's Happiness gauge will open on last month's value, count to this month's, and pulse when it crosses into another mood band (PR #365, the Events team). A general strike will show as a badge on the TV, with the table waiting for the Governor's choice and strikers standing on the struck plots (PR #368, the Events team). See The table and the phones . In the code: packages/engine/src/upkeep.ts::runUpkeep · packages/engine/src/game.ts::advancePhase (upkeep) · packages/engine/src/colony.ts::payGrant, runColony · packages/engine/src/config.ts::UPKEEP, MULE, COLONY, POLITICS.pay, HAPPINESS Kept current by: the PM; the Balance team (WS1) for upkeep, the Politics team (WS7) for the colony."},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"clocks-and-waits","title":"Clocks, Ready and who the table waits for","status":"In the game","text":"Some moments run on a clock; others wait for people. The rule of thumb: decisions you need to read for have no clock; action has one. Moment Clock Ends when A dropped phone Host can go on Planning None Every person pressed \"I'm ready\" Waited for Yes, after one confirming tap Election: announce, ballot None Every person is Done Waited for Yes; they abstain Election reveal, law reveal A timed show The show ends Not relevant No Law vote None Every person is Done Waited for Yes; they do not vote Land grant The light, 0.65 s a plot All claimed or done, or the pass is over Just does not claim No need Land auction lot Intro, walk, result Each lot's clocks Stops bidding where it stands No Development 60 s, one for all Clock out, or everyone at the pub or out of time Colonist stands still; not waited for No Production Its show The show ends, or every person is Ready after it Not waited for No (Speed up instead) Goods auction side pick None Every seat is Done Waited for Yes: \"move on\" for them Goods auction walk 20 s The clock Sits the walk out No Month end None Every person tapped \"On to month N\" Waited for Yes, after one confirming tap When the host goes on without someone, the news line names who was left out."},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"event-holds","title":"Events hold the clocks","status":"","text":"When an event strikes at the start of development or the goods auction, that phase's clocks, the computer players' included, wait until the event's show has played: 7 s for a headline, 5.2 s for a spotlight, one shared 4 s roll call for all small bulletins, after a 1.8 s intro, and never more than 30 s in all. Planning is never held; production plays its events inside its own show. Players who fall sick are told in development's roll call, so development often opens with a short show. Why it is like this Heiko, 28 Oct (\"Yes, all as recommended\", to the UX audit's seven questions): the month-end table \"holds until everyone taps On to month N\", with the host's Continue for anyone absent. The goods auction's side pick lost its clock on 29 Oct (the Auctions team's change A-7, built on that audit); the simulator measured computer players picking within seconds, so only a person who never picks can hold the table, and the host can move on for them. In the code: packages/engine/src/config.ts::PHASES, WALK.statusMs, EVENT_SHOW · apps/server/src/mule-game.ts::checkAllReady, continuePlanning, holdMs Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3)."},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"export-ship","title":"The export ship's calendar","status":"In the game","text":"The export ship calls every 3 months: in months 3 , 6 , 9 and 12 . It is announced a month before with its wishlist: 3 goods drawn from the seed, out of the six below. Food, Water and Energy do not travel, and nothing is ever imported. Its hold for each listed good is the off-world demand per player per month × the players × 3 months. Good Off-world demand per player per month Hold in a four-seat game Crystite 2 24 Smithore 1 12 Alloy 1 12 Electronics 1 12 Rare earth 0.5 6 Medicine 0.5 6 In its month the ship buys at the goods auction: it pays the off-world price for its first unit of a good and 2 % less for each further unit, and 10 % more while the colony's air is clean. At that month's end it takes the store's surplus of its goods (what the store holds above two months of demand) while it still has room, leaves, and its next call is planned three months on. In the code: packages/engine/src/config.ts::GAME.exportShipEvery, EXPORT_SHIP, EXPORT_DEMAND, POLLUTION.cleanExportBonus · packages/engine/src/economy.ts::planShip, shipAnnounced, shipInPort, shipPrice · packages/engine/src/upkeep.ts Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1); details in The store and prices ."},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"worth","title":"Worth and scoring","status":"In the game","text":"A player's score is their Worth , in credits. It is shown every month, on the TV and on every phone, as one total per player; only your own phone shows your Worth line by line. Worth is: Line How it counts Value Cash Your money as held Goods Every unit you hold, at the store's posted price today varies Land Each plot you own 300 cr MULEs Each MULE installed on your plots, at this month's MULE price varies Office Each term you have served as Governor (council on) 1,200 cr Collapse cut While the air is in Collapse, the whole total is multiplied by this × 0.8 Example Lena holds 1,450 cr, 3 Food at the store's price of 32 and 2 Smithore at 55, owns 4 plots with 3 MULEs installed, the MULE price this month is 175 cr, and she has served one term as Governor. Her Worth is 1,450 + (96 + 110) + 4 × 300 + 3 × 175 + 1,200 = 1,450 + 206 + 1,200 + 525 + 1,200 = 4,581 cr. Were the air in Collapse, it would be 4,581 × 0.8 = 3,665 cr (rounded)."},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"colony-score","title":"The colony's score","status":"","text":"With the council on, the colony gets a score of its own at the end, beside the players' Worth and never added to it: (the colony's wealth + 100 cr for each point the air is cleaner than 100 + 1,000 cr for each point of prestige) × a happiness factor that runs from 0.8 at happiness 0 to 1.2 at happiness 100. The colony's wealth is the fund plus every player's Worth before the office line and any Collapse cut. Each player's donations to colony buildings are shown on plaques beside their Worth, never in it. Example The colony ends with 18,000 cr of wealth, air at 25 and 5 points of prestige, at happiness 70. Environment: 100 × (100 − 25) = 7,500. Prestige: 5 × 1,000 = 5,000. Factor: 0.8 + 0.4 × 0.70 = 1.08. Score: (18,000 + 7,500 + 5,000) × 1.08 = 32,940. What is changing The game-over verdict on the colony's score (\"A colony to be proud of\" from 100, \"A colony that held together\" from 50, \"A colony in ruins\" below) uses placeholder thresholds written before the score's scale was set. The score is now in credits, in the tens of thousands, so every colony reads \"proud\". The colony-score numbers themselves are placeholders too, to be tuned at the end. Owner: the Politics team (WS7), with the Auctions team (WS3) for the score rule. Why it is like this Heiko agreed the scoring model on 28 Oct (\"Yes, as recommended\"): one Worth number in credits, the same every month and at the end; highest Worth wins; others see totals only; and the Collapse cut applies mid-game too, so Worth is \"your score if the game ended now\". The same day he ruled the cut takes the office line too (\"Yes, cut it too\"). A term is worth about one good month's income for a top-half player. Donations sit beside Worth on plaques under the colony spec's end rule (RULE-25). In the code: packages/engine/src/game.ts::scoreParts, colonyScoreOf, playerStanding · packages/engine/src/config.ts::GAME.plotScore, POLITICS.termScore, POLLUTION.collapseScore, COLONY_SCORE · apps/web/src/game/colony.ts::scoreVerdict Kept current by: the PM, with the Table team (WS4) for the score's screens."},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"game-end","title":"How long a game lasts and who wins","status":"In the game","text":"A game lasts 12 months. It ends earlier when the land runs out: once the last free plot is claimed, the game goes on for 3 more months. The check is made at each month end, so the game ends at the month end of whichever comes first. Example The last free plot is claimed in month 6's land grant. The game ends at the end of month 9 (6 + 3), three months short of the full 12. The winner is the player with the highest Worth after the last month end. On the screens, players level at the top share the win (\"You share the win!\"). The final screens show the winner, the final table by Worth and, with the council on, the colony's score and plaques. Your phone shows your place, your Worth line by line, and the winner. What is changing On a tie at the top, the screens call it a shared win, but the server's closing news line (\"Game over. … wins the colony!\") names only the first of the tied players, by seat order. Owner: the Auctions team (WS3) for the server's news, the Table team (WS4) for the screens. In the code: packages/engine/src/game.ts::isGameOver · packages/engine/src/config.ts::GAME.maxMonths, GAME.landRushMonths · apps/server/src/mule-game.ts::enterPhase (game over) · apps/web/src/components/MonthEnd.tsx::WinnerCard Kept current by: the PM."},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"collapse","title":"Colony collapse","status":"In the game","text":"The colony's air runs from 0 (clean) to 100. Mines and smelters foul it; nature cleans 2 points a month for each player, and green belts and filters help. The air sits in one of four bands: Band From What it does Clean 0 The export ship pays 10 % more Smoggy 30 The dirty air can make a player sick in development; the colonists need 1.5 × the Medicine Toxic 60 As smoggy, and every farm and well makes 1 less Collapse 80 As toxic, and 5 % of colonists leave every month whatever they eat; every Worth is cut to 0.8 Collapse does not end the game. The game plays on to its normal end; if the air is in Collapse when the game ends, the Federation judges the colony a failure and every player's final Worth is cut by a fifth. While the air is in Collapse, the month-end screens show the cut already, with a banner saying why every Worth is lower. The colony's own score is counted before any cut. In the code: packages/engine/src/config.ts::POLLUTION · packages/engine/src/game.ts::colonyFailed, scoreParts · packages/engine/src/upkeep.ts (colonists leaving) Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1); details in Production and Colony and politics ."},{"page":"game-flow.html","id":"host-options","title":"Host and server options","status":"In the game","text":"These belong to the server, not the lobby; the host cannot change them today: The council (elections, laws, the colony fund, buildings, mood) is on in every game. Without it there are no offices, no moving tax and no colony score. The game length is 12 months unless the server is started with another. Steered walking in development is on for every game. The computer players' skill level is Trader for every computer seat. In the code: apps/server/src/server.ts::createMuleServer (politics on) · apps/server/src/index.ts · apps/server/src/mule-game.ts::DEFAULT_AI_LEVEL Kept current by: the PM, with the Table team (WS4) for the server."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"overview","title":"The auction in one minute","status":"In the game","text":"The goods auction is the sixth phase of every month: planning, land grant, land auction, development, production, auction , upkeep. It comes right after production, so everyone knows what they made, and right before upkeep, when everyone eats and goods spoil. It is played live, as in the original M.U.L.E. For each good, in a fixed order: The status screen. A table shows what every player had, used, made and holds now, what they need next month and what would go bad. Everyone starts as a buyer; tap Sell to sell instead, then Ready . There is no clock on this screen. The walk. Every player's creature stands on a tall board whose height is a price. Buyers start at the bottom and walk up to bid more; sellers start at the top and walk down to ask less. The store's selling line and buying line are drawn across the board, and so is the export ship's line when it is in port. The walk has a clock of 20 seconds, which runs at a quarter of its speed while any goods are changing hands. The close. The board stands still for 2.5 seconds while each phone shows a receipt for that good: what you bought or sold, at what prices, the tax, and where you now stand against your need. Then the next good comes up. Nothing is asked and nothing is confirmed while you trade: standing where the lines meet is the trade. To stop, step one price off the line. To sit a good out, stand still at your starting edge and press Done . In the code: packages/engine/src/auction.ts · packages/engine/src/walk.ts · packages/engine/src/game.ts::advancePhase · packages/engine/src/config.ts::WALK Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3) for the rules, WS6 for the phone screens, WS4 for the TV."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"order-of-goods","title":"Which goods come up, and in what order","status":"In the game","text":"All nine goods are auctioned every month, raw goods first and processed goods after, in this order: # Good Kind Who needs it in the colony 1 Food Grown Every player's household and crew; colonists 2 Water Grown Every player's household and crew; Food farms for irrigation; colonists 3 Energy Grown Every working plot and factory; colonists 4 Smithore Mined Alloy smelters; the store, which builds MULEs from it 5 Crystite Mined Nobody in the colony: only the export ship buys it (see Crystite ) 6 Rare earth Mined Electronics works 7 Alloy Processed Electronics works 8 Electronics Processed The export ship 9 Medicine Processed Colonists; players in dirty air"},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"skipped-goods","title":"Goods that are skipped","status":"","text":"A good with nothing to trade gets no status screen of its own. It is skipped, and the next screen shows one line such as Skipped: Crystite, Medicine: nobody trading (the TV names the goods only). There are three reasons: Reason on screen When nobody has any No player holds any of the good, and the store has none for sale. nobody buys The store does not want any more, the ship (if in port) does not want it, and no player who is short of it can afford the store's buying price. nobody trading The status screen ended, but no seller has a possible buyer and no buyer a possible seller, so there is nothing to walk for. A skipped good still teaches the market: its reference price drifts a little toward the off-world price, as described in reference and off-world prices . Example In month 2 the export ship has announced it wants Smithore, Alloy and Medicine, but not Crystite. The store then wants no Crystite either (it only stocks Crystite for a ship that will take it), and no player needs Crystite. Even if you hold 6 Crystite, the good is skipped with \"nobody buys\". You can sell it in a month when the store is buying it again, or to the ship when Crystite is on its wishlist. In the code: packages/engine/src/config.ts::WALK.order · packages/engine/src/auction.ts::skipBefore · auction.ts::nextGood Kept current by: WS3."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"status-screen","title":"Picking a side: the status screen","status":"In the game","text":"When a good comes up, every screen shows its status table, as in the original. Your own row is highlighted and named \"You\". Column What it says Had What you held before production this month. Used What production burnt: Energy for your plots, and the inputs your factories took. Made What your plots and factories made this month. Now What you hold now, in bold. Needs What you need for next month: Food and Water for your household and crew, Energy for your plots, factory inputs, and 1 Medicine when the air makes people sick. Goes bad What would be lost at month end if you kept everything above your need: the share that spoils, plus anything over the 10 units your home depot holds. Spare Now minus Needs: green +3 when you have more than you need, red short 2 when you have less. Beside the table your phone says, in one line, whether you are short, even or have a surplus (\"You need 2 more Food this month.\" or \"You have 3 more than you need.\"), what the store charges and pays (\"You can buy from the store at 33 · sell to it at 27 · it has 12 for sale\"), and, when the export ship is in port, what it still wants (\"The ship wants 24 Crystite this month\")."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"sides","title":"Buy or Sell: everyone takes part","status":"","text":"There are two buttons, Buy and Sell , and no Pass . Everyone starts every good as a buyer , standing at the bottom of the board. Tap Sell to become a seller; you can only sell a good you hold, so Sell is greyed out when you have none. Tapping your own side again changes nothing. A buyer who never walks up never buys anything, so standing still and pressing Done is how you sit a good out. You can change your side as often as you like until you press Ready , and also during the walk (see switching sides )."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"no-clock","title":"No clock on the status screen","status":"","text":"The status screen waits until every player has pressed Ready . A bar shows how many have picked (\"3 / 4 PICKED\", \"No clock: the walk starts when everyone has picked.\"). Computer players pick on their own within a few seconds. If someone is slow or their phone has dropped, the host can tap Move on for Kai ; the phone first says, in words, what each waiting player will do (\"Kai buys Food: 1 of 3, short 2.\"), and moving on starts the walk with everyone on the side they stand on. A player whose phone has dropped sits the walk out. Why it is like this Heiko, 5 Nov, after playing: \"in an auction always all players should participate; a player is a buyer by default.\" The PM approved WS3's amendment the same day (C2: everyone starts as a buyer, no Pass, sitting out is standing still plus Done). The status screen lost its clock in the UX audit (A-7, G-7) so that nobody is rushed into a side. In the code: packages/engine/src/auction.ts::statusRows · auction.ts::seedSides · auction.ts::declareRole · config.ts::WALK.statusMs (null: no clock) · apps/web/src/phone/Auction.tsx · apps/web/src/components/AuctionParts.tsx::StatusTable · apps/server/src/mule-game.ts (host Continue) Kept current by: WS3 (rules), WS6 (phone)."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"walk-board","title":"The walk board","status":"In the game","text":"The board is a tall lane. Its height is a price in credits: higher means dearer. Every player's creature stands on it at its own height. A buyer's height is their bid , a seller's height is their ask . The phone and the TV draw the same board, live."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"lines","title":"The lines on the board","status":"","text":"Line Colour What it is Who it stops Sellers' asks Red The lowest ask any seller is making. Buyers walking up meet it. Buyers' bids Green The highest bid any buyer is making. Sellers walking down meet it. Store sells Dashed, near the top What the store charges. Shown only while it has some of the good for sale. Buyers can't walk above it while the store sells. Store buys Dashed, near the bottom What the store pays. Shown only while it still wants the good. Sellers can't walk below it. Ship buys Amber What the export ship pays for its next unit, only in a month it is in port and wants this good. Sellers who reach it sell to the ship. Above the board your phone says the store's prices from your side (\"You can buy from the store at 33 · sell to it at 27 · the ship buys at 104\") and what the colours mean: \"Walk your bid up to a red line and you buy\", or for a seller \"Walk your ask down to a green line and you sell\"."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"board-size","title":"How tall the board is","status":"","text":"When a good comes up, the board is sized around its prices. It takes the store's buying and selling prices, the store's posted price and the ship's price, and adds a margin on each side: 30% of the distance between the outermost lines ( WALK.boardMargin ), plus 10% of the posted price ( WALK.boardPostedMargin ). Sellers start at the top edge, buyers at the bottom edge. Because the bottom edge is always below the store's buying line, a buyer who stands still at the start never trades. The board on screen stretches further when someone walks beyond it, so every creature is always visible."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"walking-speed","title":"How fast you walk","status":"","text":"Hold ▲ or ▼ to walk; let go to stand. A quick tap (shorter than 0.18 seconds) moves exactly one credit. You can also drag on the board. Walking speed is set per good so that crossing the whole board always takes the same time, whatever the good costs: the board's height divided by WALK.crossSeconds , today 6 seconds, but never slower than WALK.minSpeed , 2 credits a second. A cheap good's board is short and you walk slowly in credits; an expensive good's board is tall and you walk fast in credits, but both take 6 seconds end to end. Example: the Food board Food's posted price is 30 . The store sells at 33 (10% above) and buys at 27 (10% below). The outermost lines are 27 and 33, so they are 6 apart. Margin: 30% of 6 = 1.8, plus 10% of 30 = 3, so 4.8 on each side. The board runs from 22 (27 − 4.8, rounded down) to 38 (33 + 4.8, rounded up): 16 credits tall. Speed: 16 ÷ 6 = 2.7 credits a second (above the minimum of 2). A buyer starting at 22 reaches the store's selling line at 33 in 11 ÷ 2.7 = about 4 seconds of holding ▲. After the slower walk being built ( below ), the same board takes 7.5 seconds to cross: 16 ÷ 7.5 = 2.1 credits a second, about 5 seconds from 22 to 33."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"what-stops-you","title":"What stops you","status":"","text":"A buyer walking up stops at the store's selling line (while the store sells) and at the lowest ask of any seller who still has units. Meeting that seller is a trade. A seller walking down stops at the store's buying line (while the store buys) and at the highest bid of any buyer who can pay it. Meeting that buyer is a trade. When no house is buying at all, a seller can walk down to one credit above the board's bottom edge, so a buyer still standing at the start is never traded with. A buyer can walk down to 0; a seller can walk up without limit. Neither trades up there. (Heiko's rest zones, being designed , would put hard edges here.) A buyer who can no longer pay their own bid, or a seller with nothing left, no longer blocks anyone: the other side walks through them. In the code: packages/engine/src/walk.ts::stepBoard , stepWalker , takeSide , sellerFloor · auction.ts::nextGood (board range and speed) · config.ts::WALK.crossSeconds , minSpeed , boardMargin , boardPostedMargin · apps/web/src/phone/Auction.tsx::WalkHold (TAP_MS) · AuctionParts.tsx::BoardKey Kept current by: WS3 (rules), WS6 (phone board), WS4 (TV board)."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"how-a-trade-happens","title":"How a trade happens","status":"In the game","text":"A trade happens wherever a bid reaches an ask. Nobody types an amount and nobody confirms: while you stand where the lines meet, units change hands one at a time, by themselves , until one of you steps off, runs out of goods or money, or the clock ends."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"the-price","title":"The price","status":"","text":"Between two players , a unit trades at the seller's ask , in whole credits. Because the buyer can't walk past the seller, the two stand at the same price when they meet. With the store or the ship , a unit trades at the house's line: the store's selling price when you buy from it, the store's buying price or the ship's price when you sell to them."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"who-trades-first","title":"Who trades with whom","status":"","text":"Only one pair of players trades at a time: the seller with the lowest ask, and the highest bidder who reaches that ask and can pay it. On a tie, whoever got to that price first goes first, then seat order. At the same time, each house line can be trading: the store selling, the store buying and the ship buying. So up to four deals can run at once. A seller standing at or under several buying lines sells to the one that pays most . With the ship in port that is usually the ship. When several players stand at the same house line, the house serves them in turns , one unit each per round. Within a round a Quibbit is served first (that species' perk at the goods auction), then whoever got to the line first, then seat order."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"trade-speed","title":"Slowly at first, then faster and faster","status":"","text":"The first unit of a deal takes WALK.tradeStartMs , today 400 ms. Each further unit takes WALK.tradeSpeedUp = 0.8 times as long as the one before, down to a floor of WALK.tradeFloorMs , today 60 ms. If the pair separates, the next meeting starts slow again. A house line keeps one running deal and keeps its speed while it serves players in turns. Unit Wait today (ms) Wait after #376 (ms) 1st 400 1,000 2nd 320 800 3rd 256 640 4th 205 512 5th 164 410 6th 131 328 7th 105 262 8th 84 210 9th 67 200 10th and after 60 200 Standing at the line for Units traded today Units after #376 1 second 3 1 2 seconds 13 2 5 seconds 63 12 10 seconds 146 37 The server steps the auction every WALK.tickMs = 100 ms, so at today's top speed up to two units can move in one step. While units move, the board shows chevrons that light up as the deal speeds up, your phone says \"Selling Water to the store at 22 cr, faster and faster.\" with \"Tap ▲ to stop.\", and each phone clicks once per unit."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"stopping","title":"Stopping","status":"","text":"One tap away from the line stops the trade at once: the next unit does not move. A trade also stops when the seller runs out, the buyer can't pay the next unit, the house stops trading (the store has sold all it will sell, or bought all it wants) or the clock ends. Example: two players meet Water is posted at 25: the store buys at 22 and sells at 28. Ana has 6 spare Water and sells; Ben is short 3 and buys. Ana walks down from the top; Ben walks up from the bottom. Ana stops walking at 26; Ben walks up until he meets her at 26. They trade at Ana's ask of 26. The first unit moves after 0.4 s, the second 0.32 s later, the third 0.26 s after that. Ben, now even, taps ▼ and the trade stops. Ben paid 3 × 26 = 78; the store would have charged him 3 × 28 = 84. Ana received 78 less the sales tax of 1 per unit (5% of 26, rounded) = 75; the store would have paid her 3 × 22 = 66, less 1 tax each = 63. Why it is like this Heiko, 5 Nov, after playing the walking rig: \"the selling and buying process should start automatically when the buy and sell lanes meet. They should start slowly and then get faster and faster so that even large quantities can be sold. The question (How much do you want to sell to the store) is unnecessary, we shouldn't have this.\" The PM approved WS3's amendment as written (C1: no amount and no confirm at a house's line; C3: the same speed-up curve governs house lines). Heiko picked variant A for the display: the speed shown on the board and the words in the rail. In the code: packages/engine/src/walk.ts::stepBoard , tradeInterval , houseLineAt · auction.ts::goodsMarket (Quibbit first in line) · config.ts::WALK.tradeStartMs , tradeSpeedUp , tradeFloorMs , tickMs · AuctionParts.tsx::lineHint , dealSpeed Kept current by: WS3."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"deals-between-players","title":"Deals between players","status":"In the game","text":"Trading with another player is where the money is: a buyer usually pays less than the store's selling price and a seller usually gets more than the store's buying price, because the store keeps a margin of 10% on each side (see the spread ). The store's lines are the outer limits; deals between players happen in the space between them, and beyond them when the store has sold out."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"what-the-market-learns","title":"What the colony learns from your deals","status":"","text":"Only deals between players teach the market. When a good closes, the average price players paid each other for it nudges that good's reference price , the price the store aims at when supply is normal. The store's and the ship's trades teach nothing, because they trade at their own prices. How far the nudge goes is explained in reference and off-world prices ."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"sales-tax-at-auction","title":"Sales tax","status":"","text":"Every unit a player sells, to another player, the store or the ship, pays the sales tax: 5% at the start of the game ( TAX.salesTax ), set later by the Governor between 0% and 15%. It is worked out per unit, rounded to whole credits, and goes into the colony fund. Prices on the board are what the buyer pays; the seller keeps the price less the tax. Units sold to the ship also pay the export tax when that law is in force."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"news-line","title":"The news line","status":"","text":"When a good's walk ends, the news ticker reports the biggest deal between players in that good, for example \"Mia sold 4 food to Kai at 41.\" Trades with the store or the ship are not reported there. In the code: packages/engine/src/auction.ts::learnFromGood · economy.ts::driftReference , taxSale · politics.ts::chargeExportTax · apps/server/src/mule-game.ts::reportGood Kept current by: WS3, with WS1 for the price learning."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"trading-with-the-store","title":"Trading with the store and the ship","status":"In the game","text":"The store is always on the board as two lines, so there is always someone to trade with while it has stock and wants more. Its prices for the good are fixed when the good comes up and do not move during the walk."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"buying-from-the-store","title":"Buying from the store","status":"","text":"The store sells at its posted price plus 10%, rounded up. Walk up to that line and you buy, one unit at a time. It sells only what it has for sale . For Food and Water it holds back what its colonists will buy at month end, one unit per 20 colonists: the colony's own people come first, so in a shortage the shelf runs empty for the players first. When the store has nothing more for sale, its selling line disappears (\"STORE SELLS out\" on the TV) and buyers can walk higher, to tempt a player to sell."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"selling-to-the-store","title":"Selling to the store","status":"","text":"The store buys at its posted price less 10%, rounded down. Walk down to that line and you sell. It buys only as many as it wants this month: enough to fill its shelves to 3 months of demand, plus a full ship's load of a good the export ship buys. When it has bought that many, its buying line disappears. The store never runs out of money; only shelf room limits it. Restocking a short shelf. When the store's Food or Water would not feed its colonists for a month, it bids up to 1.5 times the posted price to restock, and sells at least one credit above that, so nobody can buy from it and sell straight back. The numbers are in The store and prices ."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"selling-to-the-ship","title":"Selling to the export ship","status":"","text":"Every third month the export ship is in port (months 3, 6, 9 and 12). For each good on its wishlist it stands on the board as an amber line at what it pays for the next unit: the off-world price for the first, 2% less for each further unit as its hold fills, and 10% more from a colony with clean air. Sellers who reach it sell to it. Your phone's status screen tells you how many it still wants. The store sells the ship its own surplus later, at upkeep, so players get first go. The details are in the export ship . Why it is like this Heiko, 22 Oct: \"the store should have unlimited amount of money\". The store's buying is limited only by what it wants, never by its capital. The short-shelf bid (pricing B: PRICE.shelfCover 1, restockPremium 1.5) was approved by Heiko the same day, with WS3's fix that the store always sells above what it pays. Heiko, 28 Oct: \"the store is the always available market buying what the settlers can offer to a calculated price, so the store will adjust the buying price based on scarcity.\" In the code: packages/engine/src/auction.ts::comeUp , storeSellLine , storeBuyLine , shipLine · economy.ts::storeForSale , storeWants , shelfShort , shipPrice · config.ts::PRICE , UPKEEP.colonistsPerUnit , UPKEEP.colonistBudget Kept current by: WS3 (the lines), WS1 (the store's prices)."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"the-clock","title":"The clock and how it slows","status":"In the game","text":"Stage Clock Ends when Status screen None ( WALK.statusMs is empty) Every player pressed Ready, or the host moved on. Walk 20 seconds ( WALK.goodMs ) The clock runs out, every player on the board pressed Done, or no trade is possible any more. Close 2.5 seconds ( WALK.closeMs ) Always runs out; nothing trades in it. The clock slows while goods change hands. While any deal is running, the walk's clock runs at WALK.tradeClockRate = 25% of its speed, as in the original. The time bar says so (\"Trading · clock slowed\" on the TV). A good whose walk is trading the whole time can therefore last up to 80 seconds. Done. Press Done with Food when you have finished with a good. The button is greyed out while you are trading at a line. Walking again takes your Done back. When everyone still on the board is done, the walk ends early. It also ends as soon as nobody could trade any more: no seller with a possible buyer and no buyer with a possible seller. The phone ticks with the clock and rings a bell when a good's walk ends. The whole auction has no fixed length; it runs good by good on these clocks. The simulator measures about 54 seconds of auction a month today. In the code: packages/engine/src/auction.ts::stepAuction , closeGood , auctionDone · walk.ts::stepBoard (clock rate), tradePossible · config.ts::WALK.goodMs , closeMs , tradeClockRate · apps/web/src/game/sound.ts::useAuctionSound Kept current by: WS3."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"needs-and-surplus","title":"Your needs and surplus on the phone","status":"In the game","text":"Throughout a good your phone keeps three numbers in view: HAVE , NEED and SPARE (or SHORT ), green when you have more than you need and red when you have less. Under them a hint tells you where to walk: \"Walk up to the store's line at 33: it's the only seller.\", \"Walk up to Mia's ask at 30: Kai asks 32.\", \"Walk down to the ship's line at 104: it pays the most. The store pays 81.\" and how: \"Hold ▲ to walk up; a tap moves 1 cr.\" When you stand at a line where nothing moves, the phone says why: \"You can't pay for one at 33 cr\" or \"You have no Water to sell\"."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"selling-into-shortage","title":"Selling below your need","status":"","text":"You may sell goods you need next month. The phone does not stop you or ask: it shows an amber note, \"You keep 3 of the 5 Food you need next month.\", and your HAVE turns amber. A shortage next month has real costs: a hungry colonist walks slower in development, a plot without its Energy makes only part of its output, and a Food farm without Water makes half (see Production and Development )."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"the-receipt","title":"The receipt","status":"","text":"When a good closes, each phone shows a receipt over the still board: \"Water: you sold 5 for 117 cr\", with each unit's price and who bought (\"26 + 26 + 26 + 22 + 22 cr to Ben and the store\"), on a sale, the tax spelled out: \"122 cr, less 5 cr sales tax: 117. The tax goes to the colony fund.\" (and the export tax beside it when it applies), where you stand: \"You have 4 Water, you need 2 ✓\", or \"short 1\" in red, what comes next (\"Next: Energy, buy or sell?\") and your running total for the month's auction (\"This month: +212 cr\"). If you bought and sold the same good, there is a line for each. If you did not trade, it says \"Water: you didn't trade\". Why it is like this The PM ruled WS3's open question \"show, don't stop\" on 5 Nov: selling below next month's need is allowed and shown, never asked, matching Heiko's words that day (\"no question\"). Heiko later asked for a stronger guard, an automatic stop at your need; that is being designed . The receipt comes from the UX audit (A-5, G-5). In the code: apps/web/src/phone/Auction.tsx (rail, ReceiptCard , MonthTally ) · apps/web/src/components/AuctionParts.tsx::walkHint , keepWords , lineMoment · packages/engine/src/auction.ts::noteUnit (receipts and tally) Kept current by: WS6 (phone), WS3 (receipt data)."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"switching-sides","title":"Switching sides during the walk","status":"In the game","text":"During the walk your phone keeps the Buyer and Seller buttons under your numbers, with a caption: \"Sell puts you at the top, asking.\" or \"Buy puts you at the bottom, bidding.\" Switching sides moves your creature to the new side's starting edge, standing still, so a switch never trades at once. It also takes back your Done. You can't switch to Seller without any of the good. What is changing Heiko asked on 5 Nov to be able to flip sides within the auction (rule C, below). The engine and the phone on trunk already allow it; WS3's draft amendment (AM-1, RULE-5) writes the rule down and adds that a switch stops any trade at once. WS3's draft was grounded on an older trunk that showed the buttons only on the status screen; the PM should confirm whether anything is still to build. In the code: packages/engine/src/auction.ts::declareRole · walk.ts::takeSide · apps/web/src/phone/Auction.tsx ( sides ) Kept current by: WS3 (rules), WS6 (phone)."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"computer-players","title":"The computer players at the auction","status":"In the game","text":"Computer players walk the same board by the same rules, through the same moves a phone sends. At the status screen each one values every unit of the good: what one more unit is worth to it as a buyer, and what the unit it would give up is worth as a seller. It then picks buy or sell and how many, and presses Ready. If its thinking takes more than 2.5 seconds it plays its own top choice. It switches to Seller only to sell; one that wants nothing stays a buyer at the bottom edge and presses Done. Its limit. A buyer never bids above what the next unit is worth to it or what it can spend; a seller never asks below what the unit is worth to it. Both follow its stock as units trade. It spends at most half its money on a good ( STRATEGIST.budgetShare ) and keeps a cash reserve, except that it may spend everything on Food and Energy it truly needs. Its walk. If the best price on offer is inside its limit, it walks there and trades. If a rival on its side is ahead and its limit leaves room, it overtakes by one. If the gap to the other side is small (a few percent of the board, more for a patient temperament), it waits for them to close it. Otherwise it edges toward them, never past its limit. Human timing. It starts 0.3 to 1.5 seconds after the walk begins (longer for a patient one), re-aims only after a reaction time that depends on its level (0.32 to 0.55 s for a rookie, 0.25 to 0.4 s for a trader, 0.2 to 0.32 s for a tycoon), and wanders its stopping point by up to 3% of the board, so there is no fixed habit to read. At a house line it stands while units move, counts again after every unit, and steps off within one step of its last wanted unit. Tricks. Higher levels may corner a good that other players are short of and that keeps well, bidding up to half above the expected price for up to 6 units, and may bid a little extra to keep the leader short (spite). How the computer players think in general is in Computer players . In the code: packages/ai/src/hands/auction.ts::AuctionHand · packages/ai/src/strategist.ts::goodPlan , tradeDecision · config.ts::AI_HANDS.auction , AI_LEVELS , STRATEGIST Kept current by: WS1 (the AI), WS9 (playbooks)."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"ai-shortage-fix","title":"Computer players buy what they are short of","status":"Being built","text":"Today a computer player short of Food sometimes stands still at the bottom of the board while the store has Food for sale and it has the money. It values Food mainly by how much a hungry walk costs it in development, which often comes out below the store's price, so it refuses. The fix being built: a computer player short of Food, Energy or Water values its shortfall at least at the store's selling price, and does not sell below its own need. It buys enough to cover next month as a whole: Food it will eat plus what will spoil, Energy its plots need allowing for spoilage, and Water only when it has an irrigated farm. Why it is like this Heiko, 5 Nov, watching a game: \"two ai players had a shortage of energy (-3) which would most likely lead to that they won't produce anything next month ... i had a surplus and the store had stock but they didn't buy? they were just standing there not even bidding\". WS9 measured that a seat comes to the Food auction short in 86–87% of seat-months, and in 76–85% it refuses on value (a median of 19 cr against a store ask of 49–56), though 99% could pay for a unit. WS1's first runs of the fix: payable no-buys fell to zero for Food, Energy and Water. The cost: colonists go short of Food or Water in more months (for example 52% to 60%), because players now buy the store's stock. The PM ruled to ship it and balance at the end. What is changing WS1 is building this (branch ws1-food-short ), due about 17:00Z on 5 Nov. The longer-term answer is WS9's resource-utility design, which values goods by what they do for the seat. In the code: packages/ai/src/strategist.ts::tradeDecision (where the fix lands) Kept current by: WS1."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"tv-board","title":"The TV board","status":"In the game","text":"On the big screen everyone sees the whole auction, as in the original. The title reads \"Auction · Food\"."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"tv-status","title":"During the status screen","status":"","text":"The status table, large, for every player. The store box: STORE SELLS , STORE BUYS and IN STOCK (red when 0); \"out\" when the store has none to sell. The ship panel when the export ship is in port and wants this good or one still to come. Every player with their side: BUY ✓ , SELL ✓ , or PICKING ( AWAY · PICKING for a dropped phone), with \"No clock: the walk starts when everyone has picked.\" and, while someone is still picking, which player is the host and can move on for them."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"tv-walk","title":"During the walk","status":"","text":"The board, large, with every creature walking live and the store and ship lines; the time bar shows when the clock is slowed. A panel with the good, the stage ( Walk , Trading · clock slowed , Closed ) and the units traded so far. The readout: LOWEST ASK and who holds it, HIGHEST BID and who holds it (the store and the ship count), and the GAP (\"4 apart\", \"they meet\", or \"MET: TRADING\"). The store's Sells , Buys and Stock , and \"The ship buys Smithore at 50\" when it is in port. Each running deal on a row (\"Mia → Kai · 4 units @ 41\") with its speed shown as chevrons, \"faster and faster\" until \"full speed\". When nothing trades, a reminder: \"Sellers push the red line down, buyers push the green line up. When the two lines meet, the goods change hands.\" The room code and the goods still to come (\"NEXT WATER, ENERGY, …\"). During the close stage the board dims for 2.5 seconds while the phones show their receipts. The TV never shows what a player holds, except in this status table. In the code: apps/web/src/table/TableApp.tsx::TvAuction · apps/web/src/components/AuctionParts.tsx::TvReadout , StoreBox , ShipWants , StatusTable · packages/ui WalkBoard Kept current by: WS4 (TV), with WS6 for the shared auction parts."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"slower-walk","title":"Slower walking and slower trading","status":"Being built","text":"Walking up and down will be about a quarter slower, and trading at a meeting will start at about one unit a second, speeding up to at most five a second. Value Today After #376 What it changes WALK.crossSeconds 6 7.5 Seconds to cross a whole board WALK.minSpeed 2 1.6 Slowest walk, credits a second LOTS.walkMs 12 s 15 s The land auction's walk clock per lot, stretched the same 25%, because the land auction walks at the same speed WALK.tradeStartMs 400 ms 1,000 ms The first unit of a deal: about one a second WALK.tradeFloorMs 60 ms 200 ms The fastest a deal goes: five a second WALK.tradeSpeedUp 0.8 0.8 Unchanged With the new values a deal reaches full speed at its 9th unit, about 4.4 seconds in, and moves 12 units in its first 5 seconds instead of 63. Why it is like this Heiko, 5 Nov: \"the movement speed at the auction needs to be slightly reduced, walking up and down must be a bit slower.\" And, selling Water at the table: it sold too fast; start at about one unit a second and ramp up to at most five a second, \"guess numbers\", to be tested. The PM set the values as a value ruling and ruled that the land auction slows too, with its clock stretched to match (Heiko was told and did not object). What is changing WS3's PR #376 carries all five values. The simulator, before and after: auction time 54 to 59 seconds a month; units sold to the store 29.1 to 26.5; land sold 13 to 16; colonists short of Food or Water 50% to 58% (already a miss before this change; balance is done at the end, not now). Status on 5 Nov 07:13Z: one test still expected the old speed and was sent back to WS3. In the code: packages/engine/src/config.ts::WALK , LOTS.walkMs Kept current by: WS3."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"headline-glyph","title":"The good's symbol in the headline","status":"Being built","text":"Today the good being auctioned is named in small type: in the phone's title (\"Food · buy or sell?\", \"Food auction\") and in the corner of the TV. On every goods-auction screen, status, walk and close, on phone, laptop and TV, the headline will lead with the good's symbol and name, large. Why it is like this Heiko, 5 Nov, with a screenshot of the status screen: \"we need to make it more prominent what the auction is currently about. currently it is a text at the top right corner. we should have it in the headline together with the resource symbol\". The PM ruled it for every auction screen. What is changing PR #374 (WS6, with named edits in WS3's phone auction screen and WS4's TV) is green and reviewed, waiting for WS3's and WS4's OK before it merges. In the code: apps/web/src/phone/Auction.tsx · apps/web/src/table/TableApp.tsx::TvAuction Kept current by: WS6, with WS3 and WS4."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"heiko-auction-rules","title":"Heiko's auction rules of 5 November","status":"Being designed","text":"Selling a Water surplus at the table on 5 Nov, Heiko sold too much and ended up short. He asked for four changes, in his words (shortened): \"When I am selling it should auto-stop when I reached my equilibrium, it should get me up the store lane. If I still want to sell (in shortage) it must be a conscious decision … It should be possible to flip sides (buy / sell) within the auction. Below the store buying lane and above the store selling lane there should be safe or rest areas … so that I am not participating in the auction accidentally. When the store has no more goods to sell, buyers can walk up and drive the prices up further, trying to convince a seller to sell. The sell lane of the store is representing the latest bid price of the buyers.\" WS3 has drafted the rules (an amendment to its goods-auction spec) and WS6 is drawing the screens. Both are due at 20:00Z on 5 Nov for Heiko's approval; nothing is built until he approves. The draft's proposals:"},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"rule-b-stop-at-need","title":"B · You stop at your need","status":"","text":"A seller who holds more than their need sells down to their need and then stops: the unit that would take them below need does not sell, and they are moved one step up off the line. To sell on into a shortage, you press ▼ again. That second press is the conscious decision; from then on you sell for the rest of that good, starting slow again. It applies to sales to the store, the ship and other players. A good you don't need (Smithore, Crystite and the like, when no factory of yours uses them) has no stop. Computer players press down again only if they had already decided to sell past their need."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"rule-c-flip-sides","title":"C · Flip sides during the auction","status":"","text":"Already possible on trunk (see switching sides ). The draft writes it down: a switch stops any trade at once, takes back your Done, and keeps your stop-at-need for this good. Computer players do not switch mid-walk."},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"rule-d-rest-zones","title":"D · Rest zones with hard edges","status":"","text":"Below the best buying line (the store's, or the ship's if higher) is the buyers' rest zone ; above the store's selling line is the sellers' rest zone . Standing in your own rest zone you can't trade, because the other side can't reach you. Buyers can't walk below the board's bottom edge; sellers can't walk above the top edge. Sellers always stop at the old buying line, even after the store has bought all it wants and no ship is in. (Today they can then walk down into the buyers' area.)"},{"page":"goods-auction.html","id":"rule-e-sold-out","title":"E · Bidding past a sold-out store","status":"","text":"When the store has none of the good left to sell, buyers may walk above its old selling line, up to one step below the top. Walking up never trades with a seller resting in the sellers' zone: the buyer stops one step below them. A seller who wants the bid walks down onto it, and then they trade. The store's selling line then shows the highest bid on the board (\"Highest bid N\"), so every seller sees what walking down would fetch. The store itself sells nothing. What is changing Owners: WS3 the rules (draft amendment AM-1 to the goods-auction spec), WS6 the mock, WS1 the computer players' part. Two questions are open for Heiko in the draft: Does the stop at your need apply to sales between players too, or only at the store's line? WS3 recommends both, since either can leave you short. Should a buyer have the mirror stop, no more buying once they have enough? Not asked for; WS3 recommends not now. How the store sets its prices after it sells out, and a wider gap between its buying and selling prices, are part of WS5's store-pricing design , not this one. In the code (where it would land): packages/engine/src/walk.ts::stepBoard , sellerFloor · auction.ts::declareRole · packages/ai/src/hands/auction.ts · draft: docs/specs/2026-11-05-ws3-auction-auto-trade.md §10 (WS3's branch, not on trunk) Kept current by: WS3 (rules), WS6 (screens), WS1 (AI)."},{"page":"index.html","id":"how-to-read","title":"How to read this book","status":"In the game","text":"Each chapter follows one part of a month at the table. Every topic starts with how it works in the game today, in plain words, with its real numbers. Where a rule is about to change, a box says what is changing, who decided it and who is building it. Each topic carries one label, so you can see at a glance how settled it is: In the game on the main build today Being built a change is in a pull request Designed ruled or approved, not built yet Being designed a design or a question is open Backlog wanted, nobody on it yet Search finds any word in any chapter: type in the box at the top, or press / . Press Enter to jump to the first result."},{"page":"index.html","id":"chapters","title":"Chapters","status":"In the game","text":""},{"page":"index.html","id":"sources","title":"Where the facts come from","status":"In the game","text":"The game's code on the main build is the source of truth. Every number in this book is the value in the game's settings file, and the Every number page is generated from that file, so it can't drift. Rulings and their reasons come from the programme's decision log: what Heiko decided, and what the programme manager ruled under his authority, each with its date. Where this book and the game disagree, the game is right and the book has a bug. Tell the programme manager."},{"page":"index.html","id":"keeping-current","title":"How the book stays current","status":"In the game","text":"The rule for every team: a pull request that changes a rule, a number, a screen or a behaviour updates the chapter that describes it, in the same pull request. When the change merges, its \"Being built\" label becomes \"In the game\". A new design gets its own section the day its goals are agreed. The programme manager publishes the book again after every batch that reaches the main build. Kept current by: the programme manager. How to write a section: docs/rulebook/WRITING.md ."},{"page":"land.html","id":"the-map","title":"The map","status":"In the game","text":"Every game is played on one grid of square plots, made fresh from the game's seed. The same seed always makes the same map, so a game can be replayed. The size depends on how many players sit at the table. Players Size band Columns Rows Plots (with the store) Special fields 2 or 3 small 13 7 91 3 4 mid 15 9 135 4 5 or 6 large 19 11 209 5 The store sits on the plot at the very centre of the map. It is not land: nobody owns it, claims it or walks across it, and colonists go in and out through the doors on its left and right sides (see the store floor ). A river runs from the top row to the bottom row, always passing right beside the store. It bends from row to row: at least 2 bends on a small map, 3 on a mid map and 4 on a large one, plus 0 or 1 more by chance. It never comes closer than 2 columns to the left or right edge. Where it bends, an extra river plot joins the two rows, so the river is always one connected stream. The rest of the land is laid out in regions of one terrain, each at least 3 plots big and none larger than 12 % of the land beside the river. Each terrain aims at a share of that land, allowed to stray by a third of its target plus one plot, and no terrain may cover more than a third. Terrain Target share of land Plains 24% Forest 11% Hills 10% Desert 9% Tundra 8% Mountains 7% Swamp 7% Lake 6% Volcanic 6% Crater 6% Ice 6%"},{"page":"land.html","id":"map-promises","title":"What every map promises","status":"","text":"The map maker checks each map against a set of rules before play, and repairs the ground until it meets them. So no table is dealt a map without Food, Water, Energy or Smithore. Enough good land. A plot counts as good for Food, Water or Energy at a rating of 1 or more, and for Smithore, Crystite or Rare earth at 3 or more. Every map has at least 10 good Food, 10 good Water, 10 good Energy and 3 good Smithore plots per player. Usually more. Normally the map has at least 12 Food, 13 Water, 13 Energy and 4 Smithore plots per player. With a chance of 25 %, one of these four goods is the map's scarce good and only gets the floor above. Nothing out of reach. From any plot, a good Food, Water and Energy plot lies within 3 steps ( 4 on a large map), and a good Smithore plot within 5 steps ( 6 on a large map). Smithore comes in seams. At least 70 % of the good Smithore plots touch another good Smithore plot. In the code: packages/engine/src/config.ts::mapSize · config.ts::MAP · packages/engine/src/map-layout.ts::placeRiver · packages/engine/src/map-rules.ts Kept current by: the Maps team (WS5)."},{"page":"land.html","id":"terrain","title":"Terrain and what it hints","status":"In the game","text":"Everyone sees every plot's terrain. Terrain does not decide what a plot makes; it only sets the odds for its hidden ground (next section). For each grown good (Food, Water, Energy) a terrain is either often good , neutral or poor ; for Smithore it has an odds level from none to very high . Terrain Often good for Poor for Smithore odds Hint the phone shows River Food, Water nothing low Often good for Food and Water. Little Smithore. Lake Water, Food nothing none Often good for Water and Food. No Smithore. Plains Food, Energy nothing low Often good for Food and Energy. Little Smithore. Forest Food Energy low Often good for Food. Poor for Energy. Little Smithore. Swamp Water Food low Often good for Water. Poor for Food. Little Smithore. Hills nothing Food high Good odds of Smithore. Poor for Food. Mountains nothing Food, Energy very high Very good odds of Smithore. Poor for Food and Energy. Desert Energy Water, Food low Often good for Energy. Poor for Water and Food. Little Smithore. Tundra Energy, Water Food medium Often good for Energy and Water. Poor for Food. Some Smithore. Ice Water Food, Energy none Often good for Water. Poor for Food and Energy. No Smithore. Volcanic Energy Food medium Often good for Energy. Poor for Food. Some Smithore. Crater nothing Food low Poor for Food. Little Smithore. No hint ever names Crystite or Rare earth: they turn up anywhere (see the rings ). Terrain also decides how fast a colonist walks across a plot and whether wolves live there; that is in Development . Why it is like this The design rule is \"what you see is not what you get\": terrain tells you the odds, only an analysis tells you the truth. Two hills side by side can be a rich Smithore seam and an empty rock. The hints are kept honest: over many maps, each terrain's average rating must sit within 0.3 of the hint, and no part of the map (an edge row or column, or the middle) may run more than 0.1 poorer or richer than the hints say. In the code: config.ts::TERRAIN_ODDS · config.ts::MAP.honesty · config.ts::MAP.placeHonesty · apps/web/src/game/derive.ts::terrainHint Kept current by: the Maps team (WS5), with the phone team (WS6) for the hint words."},{"page":"land.html","id":"hidden-ground","title":"Hidden ground ratings","status":"In the game","text":"When the map is made, every plot gets a hidden rating for each of the six raw goods. The rating, not the terrain, is what a MULE on that plot works with. Nobody sees it until they analyse the plot or fly a drone over it. Goods Rating scale What a MULE makes from it Food, Water, Energy (grown) -2 to +3 2 plus the rating, but never less than 1 : a poor plot still works, badly. Smithore, Crystite, Rare earth (mined) 0 to 4 Exactly the rating. A 0 means nothing to mine: a MULE there makes nothing. That is the plot's base. Traits, species, special fields, neighbours and pollution then add or take away; see Production ."},{"page":"land.html","id":"grown-odds","title":"How grown ratings are dealt","status":"","text":"For Food, Water and Energy, each plot's rating is drawn from its terrain's odds row: Terrain suits the good -2 -1 0 +1 +2 +3 Average Often good 0% 5% 15% 30% 30% 20% +1.45 Neutral 5% 20% 40% 25% 10% 0% +0.15 Poor 30% 35% 25% 10% 0% 0% -0.85 The draw is not plot by plot at random. A smooth random \"field\" is laid over the map for each good, and within each terrain the best ratings go where the field is highest. So good farmland comes in patches rather than speckles. Smithore uses a narrower field, which gathers its rich plots into seams."},{"page":"land.html","id":"smithore-odds","title":"How Smithore is dealt","status":"","text":"Smithore odds Terrains 0 1 2 3 4 Average none Lake, Ice 100% 0% 0% 0% 0% 0 low River, Plains, Forest, Swamp, Desert, Crater 70% 20% 10% 0% 0% 0.4 medium Tundra, Volcanic 40% 30% 20% 10% 0% 1.0 high Hills 5% 15% 30% 30% 20% 2.45 very high Mountains 2% 8% 15% 40% 35% 2.98"},{"page":"land.html","id":"floors","title":"Floors and repairs","status":"","text":"After the first deal the map maker repairs the ground until the promises in The map hold: it lifts single plots to exactly the \"good\" threshold, swaps ratings within a terrain to join Smithore seams, and lowers the scarce good on a few plots. It never rerolls the map. Crystite and Rare earth have no floors and no scarcity: their rings replace them."},{"page":"land.html","id":"rings","title":"Crystite and Rare earth: the rings","status":"","text":"Crystite and Rare earth ignore terrain completely. For each of the two, the map maker picks 3 or 4 peak plots at random anywhere on the land, then rates every plot by how many steps (across the grid, never diagonally) it lies from the nearest peak: Steps from the nearest peak Rating Plots in one ring 0 (the peak) 4 1 1 3 up to 4 2 1 or 2, by chance up to 8 3 or more 0 the rest Rings may overlap each other and the other mineral's rings, and a plot takes its rating from the nearest peak. One full ring holds 5 good plots (rating 3 or more), so a map has roughly 15 to 20 good plots of each, whatever its size. On a small map that is plenty per player; on a six-player map it is about 3 each. Because no terrain says where the rings are, every plot shows the same hint for both: the map-wide average rating, 0.6 (measured over maps of 2 to 6 players: about 0.82 on small maps, 0.60 on mid and 0.41 on large). The phone's terrain line simply leaves them out. Example A Crystite peak lands on a plains plot at column 5, row 3. That plot is Crystite 4 . The four plots touching it are Crystite 3 , whether they are hills, swamp or river. The eight plots two steps away are each 1 or 2 . Everything further out is 0 , unless another peak is nearer. A player who analyses one of the 3 s and finds Crystite knows a richer plot is next door, and the race is on. Why it is like this Heiko, 21 Oct: Crystite and Rare earth come in rings, as in the original, at 3 to 4 random places per map with no tie to terrain or river side: \"the whole idea is that you need to analyse the ground and you can be lucky\". On hints he chose \"Nothing, test to find\", and \"Rings replace them\" for floors and scarcity. In the code: config.ts::GROWN_ODDS · config.ts::MINERAL_ODDS · config.ts::MAP.peak · config.ts::MAP.unknownMineral · packages/engine/src/map-ground.ts::placePeaks · map-ground.ts::ringRating · packages/engine/src/production.ts::rawOutputParts Kept current by: the Maps team (WS5)."},{"page":"land.html","id":"traits","title":"Hidden hazard traits","status":"In the game","text":"Each land plot also carries 0 , 1 or 2 hidden traits. The count is drawn evenly from the list 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 2 , so half of all plots have none, a third have one and a sixth have two (a repeated draw adds nothing, so some \"two\" plots end with one). Trait What it does today Hot spring Energy +2 , Water -1 on that plot Toxic Food -2 on that plot Thin vein A mine there makes 1 less for every 3 months it has run Bedrock, Sheltered, Unstable, Infested, Flood plain Nothing yet (see below) You see a plot's traits once you have analysed it, and you always see the traits of plots you own. What is changing The design says the other five traits change event risks (floods, quakes and so on). Today no rule reads them: a search of the engine finds them only in the list of trait names. Whether they should do something, and what, is open with the Events team (WS1) and the Maps team (WS5). In the code: config.ts::MAP.traits · config.ts::TRAIT_EFFECT · config.ts::THIN_VEIN_MONTHS · packages/engine/src/map.ts::generateMap Kept current by: the Maps team (WS5)."},{"page":"land.html","id":"special-fields","title":"Special fields","status":"In the game","text":"A few plots carry a special field, drawn on the map for everyone to see, the TV included. Unlike the ground, a special field is public. A map has 3 (small), 4 (mid) or 5 (large) of them, at most 2 of any kind and at least 3 different kinds. They sit at least 4 steps apart, both sides of the river get at least one, and none sits on the river, a lake, the store or a plot touching the store. Field Where it can be What it does Crystal cavern Crater, mountains, hills, and only where the ground has Crystite 1 or more +4 Crystite when mined for Crystite. Each month it is mined, a 6 % chance it caves in and becomes a plain plot. Ruins Any dry land Pays its first owner 2 Alloy or 1 Electronics (fixed when the map is made), once, at that month's production. Then it is a plain plot. Alien relic Any dry land +1 to every working plot touching it (raw or factory, whoever owns it), per relic. Geyser Volcanic, tundra, ice +2 when outfitted for Water or Energy. Oasis Desert with at least 2 desert neighbours +3 Food from a farm there. Meteor crater Never placed; formed in play No bonus, but its Crystite rating becomes 4 ."},{"page":"land.html","id":"meteor-craters","title":"Meteor craters","status":"","text":"A meteor strike is a production event with a 3.2 % chance each month. After the month's goods are made it hits one plot (never the store). The owner keeps the plot, but its MULE, its run of months and its filter are destroyed, any special field there is replaced, and the plot becomes a meteor crater with Crystite 4 ."},{"page":"land.html","id":"colony-sites","title":"Colony sites","status":"","text":"When politics is on, the colony can win a plot at the land auction for a building that needs one (a Park, a Stadium or the Spaceport upgrade). That plot becomes the colony's: it is no player's, cannot be claimed or flown over by the drone, and stands bare. See the colony's bid below and Colony and politics . In the code: config.ts::MAP.special · config.ts::MAP.specialEffect · config.ts::EVENTS.colony.cavernCollapse · config.ts::EVENTS.colony.meteorStrike · packages/engine/src/specials.ts · packages/engine/src/colony.ts::siteProject Kept current by: the Maps team (WS5), with the Events team (WS1) for meteor strikes and cavern collapses."},{"page":"land.html","id":"land-grant","title":"The land grant","status":"In the game","text":"Every month starts with planning, then the land grant : the colony gives away free land, one plot to each player who wants one. The free plots light up one at a time in reading order : the top row first, left to right, then the next row. Each stays lit for 650 ms. While a plot is lit, the first player to press CLAIM gets it. A press counts only for the plot the player saw lit, so a late thumb never grabs the next one by mistake (\"That plot is not lit\"). Each player has one claim per grant. The button shows \"1 OF 1 CLAIMS LEFT\" and turns to \"Done\" once used. A player who wants nothing more presses I'm ready (\"You're done with land\") and claims nothing more this month. The light passes over the free plots once . There is no clock: the grant ends when every player has claimed or is done, when no free land is left, or when the light has passed the last free plot. Once every person at the table is done, the computer players' presses resolve at once and the light races to the end at an eighth of the step (about 81 ms a plot), so nobody waits. The \"Lit now\" panel names the lit plot's terrain, any special field, and its terrain hint; for a plot you have analysed or flown your drone over, it shows the real ratings instead (see the geo drone ). Each plot you own adds 300 to your worth, before any MULE on it. When the last free plot is taken, the game ends 3 months later, or at month 12 , whichever comes first (see The game at a glance ). Example A four-player game, month 1: the map has 134 free plots, so a full pass can take up to 134 × 0.65 = about 87 seconds. Lena wants the river plot in row 2; it lights, she presses, it is hers. Once every human has claimed or pressed I'm ready, the computer players' choices settle in a flash."},{"page":"land.html","id":"grant-ai","title":"Computer players in the grant","status":"","text":"A computer player presses for a lit plot only if it is worth at least 75 % of the best free plot still ahead of the light, and only with a human-like reaction time, so a quick human can beat it. At the default level (Trader) it reacts in 250 to 400 ms plus 150 to 300 ms to decide, and misses a plot it wanted 5 % of the time (Rookie 25 %, Tycoon never). Why it is like this One pass with no clock (WS3, 28 Oct) means a player who wants no land does not hold the table up, and nobody is rushed. One claim per grant is Heiko's ruling of 5 Nov (it was two); the store still builds two MULEs per player a month, so the corral did not shrink. What is changing Being built: with one claim each, computer players all chased the same few best plots and a slow one could end the grant with no plot at all (measured: 25 plotless seats in 200 four-player games, 50 at six players). The fix makes them spread their presses; in 400 test games no seat ended plotless. It is in the Strategy team's PR #366 (WS1), which also fixes the glint grab described in Development . In the code: packages/engine/src/game.ts::claimPlot · game.ts::grantDone · game.ts::advanceGrant · config.ts::GAME.claimsPerGrant · config.ts::GAME.grantTickMs · config.ts::PHASES.grantFastForwardMs · config.ts::STRATEGIST.grantShare · apps/server/src/mule-game.ts::maybeFastForward Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3) for the grant, the Strategy team (WS1) for the computer players."},{"page":"land.html","id":"land-auction","title":"The land auction","status":"In the game","text":"After the grant comes the land auction , where plots and MULEs change hands between players. It happens only in a month where something is up for sale; otherwise the game goes straight to development."},{"page":"land.html","id":"listing","title":"Putting a lot up","status":"","text":"In planning, a player can list a plot they own, or the MULE standing on it, from the Sell tool. The listing says: the minimum bid, any whole number of 1 or more (the form starts at 250 ); what happens if nobody bids that much: Store buys (the store pays a fixed price, shown on the form) or Keep it . Limit Value Lots one player may list in a month 2 Lots the auction takes in a month, from everyone 4 Times one plot may be listed in a month (as plot or as MULE) 1 A seller can take a listing back at any time in planning. Listings are made only in planning (\"Listings are made in planning\")."},{"page":"land.html","id":"lot-clock","title":"One lot at a time","status":"","text":"Lots come up one after another. Each has three stages: Stage Length What happens Intro 3 s The plot lights on the map with its seller and minimum. Ends early once every buyer presses Ready to bid. Walk 12 s Buyers walk their bids up. Ends early once every buyer presses Done bidding. Result 2.5 s \"Yours!\", \"Sold to …\", \"The store bought it\" or \"Unsold\"."},{"page":"land.html","id":"who-bids","title":"Who may bid","status":"","text":"Everyone but the seller is a buyer, except a player whose money is below the minimum (shown as passing), and, on a MULE lot, a player already leading a MULE. Each buyer starts at the bottom of the board, below the minimum."},{"page":"land.html","id":"bidding","title":"Bidding","status":"","text":"Hold up to raise your bid, hold down to lower it, let go to stand. Your bid can never go above the money you hold. The board runs from 0.7 times the minimum to 2 times the minimum, and crossing it takes 6 seconds. Once the leading bid passes the top of the board, bids climb twice as fast for the rest of the lot, so a hard-fought lot can still reach its price before the bell. Soft close: when the lead changes hands with less than 2 seconds left, the clock goes back to 2 seconds, so the bell never decides who wins. These top-ups add at most 6 seconds to one lot. A lot's first leader does not count as a change. At the bell the highest bid at or over the minimum wins; on a tie, whoever got there first, then seat order. Sold plot: the winner pays their bid and owns the plot at once. Any MULE, filter and run of months on it go with it. Sold MULE: the winner pays and leads the MULE, already outfitted, into development, where they must install it on one of their plots before their turn ends or it runs off (see Development ). The seller's plot is left without a MULE. The seller receives the bid less sales tax ( 5 % at the start of a game; the Governor may change it), which goes to the colony fund. Unsold, Keep it: nothing moves. Unsold, Store buys: see Selling to the store . Example Brix lists a hills plot at a minimum of 250 . The board runs from 175 to 500 , so bids climb at about 54 cr a second. Ossa reaches 310 , Lena stops at 290 . At the bell Ossa pays 310 ; the tax is 16 (5% of 310, rounded), so Brix gets 294 ."},{"page":"land.html","id":"capped-mule-lot","title":"A MULE under the emission cap","status":"","text":"When the colony's emission cap stops new Rare earth or Alloy outfits, a MULE already outfitted for them may still be sold at a lot and installed by its buyer. The cap refuses new outfitting, not moving or installing a MULE that already exists, and the air is no worse for the sale. Why it is like this The sale rule is the original's land auction: buyers walk up, the highest bid at the bell wins (WS3 spec, 21 Oct). The soft close and the faster climb were added (AM-6) so that a lot is not decided by who happens to be leading when time runs out. The capped MULE is a PM ruling of 5 Nov."},{"page":"land.html","id":"lot-ai","title":"Computer players at the auction","status":"","text":"A computer player never bids past the lowest of its money less its cash reserve, what the lot is worth to it, and (for a plot) its room to develop more land. Overtaking, it jumps a random 10 to 35 % of the gap to that limit, so its stops are hard to read. Short of cash, it lists one plot or MULE a month itself, at 80 % of what the lot is worth to it, with \"Store buys\" as its fallback. In the code: packages/engine/src/lots.ts ( list , standing , lotLeader , stepLandAuction , resolve ) · config.ts::LOTS · config.ts::WALK.crossSeconds · config.ts::TAX.salesTax · config.ts::STRATEGIST.sell · packages/engine/src/politics.ts::outfitBlocked Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3), with the Strategy team (WS1) for computer players' bids."},{"page":"land.html","id":"selling","title":"Selling to the store","status":"In the game","text":"There is no \"sell\" button for land outside the land auction. The way to get rid of a plot or a MULE for sure is to list it with Store buys : if no bid reaches your minimum, the store pays a fixed price, fixed when you list. Lot The store pays Then Plot 2/3 of a plot's worth, 300 : 200 cr The plot goes back to free land. A MULE on it goes back to the store's corral. MULE half this month's MULE price, rounded The MULE goes back to the corral; the plot stays yours, bare. The store always pays, even when its own cash runs below zero, so \"Store buys\" is a sure exit. The store price is set below a plot's worth on purpose, so selling to the store can never beat simply holding the land at the end. Example The MULE price this month is 170 . Lena lists the Smithore MULE on her plains plot at a minimum of 120 , Store buys. Nobody bids 120 . The store pays her 85 (half of 170), the MULE returns to the corral, and her plains plot is bare, ready for a better outfit. In the code: lots.ts::storePaysAt · lots.ts::resolve · config.ts::LOTS.storePlotShare · config.ts::LOTS.storeMuleShare · config.ts::GAME.plotScore Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3)."},{"page":"land.html","id":"colony-lot","title":"The colony's plot sale","status":"In the game","text":"Each month, as the land grant ends, there is a 10 % chance that the colony itself puts one free plot up for auction (Heiko asked for a chance between 1 in 10 and 1 in 8). The plot is picked at random among the free plots, and its lot comes up first. Its minimum is 2/3 of a plot's worth: 200 . Sold, the money goes to the store's cash, with no sales tax (the colony is not a player). Unsold, it simply stays free land. It never pushes out a player's listing: if 4 lots are already listed, or no land is free, there is no colony lot. The roll comes from the game's seed and the month alone, so it is the same in a replay."},{"page":"land.html","id":"colony-bids","title":"The colony bids for a site","status":"","text":"With politics on, the colony can also buy . When a commissioned building needs a plot and the Governor has set a top bid, the colony bids on every player's plot that comes up: one credit over the best player's bid, up to the lower of the Governor's top bid and what the fund holds above its reserve. A player who reaches that cap takes the lot. If the colony wins, the fund pays the seller (less tax), any MULE on the plot goes back to the corral, and the plot becomes the colony's bare building site. In the code: lots.ts::colonyLot · lots.ts::colonyBid · config.ts::LOTS.colonyPlotChance · config.ts::LOTS.colonyPlotShare · colony.ts::siteProject Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3), with the Politics team (WS7) for the colony's bid."},{"page":"land.html","id":"geo-drone","title":"The geo drone","status":"In the game","text":"In planning you can send a geo drone to read the ground of plots before the land grant, from orbit. Rule Value Fee, paid to the store at once 60 cr Plots per drone 1 or 2 (same fee) Drones per player per month 1 Which plots: on the phone, only free plots (not the store, nobody's land, not a colony site). The game's rules also allow a plot listed for this month's land auction, but the phone does not offer it; today only a computer player's plan can choose one. How: open Geo drone in planning, tap up to two free plots (\"Tap up to 2 free plots\"; tapping a third drops the oldest), then press \"Send the drone · 2 of 2 plots · 60 cr\". If you picked one, the screen reminds you a second costs nothing extra. Once sent, it cannot be changed: \"Your drone is on its way\". When the results arrive: silently, as the land grant starts. From then on those plots count as analysed for you, for the rest of the game, exactly as if you had stood on them. What you see: nothing announces the results. While one of your drone's plots is lit in the grant, the \"Lit now\" panel shows its ground card (pips for every good) instead of the terrain hint, with a line such as \"Your drone: 12·5 Desert, good for Energy\", naming the best goods if they reach 2 pips or more. Afterwards the same card shows whenever you look at that plot. Who sees it: only you. The TV and the other players never see what your drone found, nor that you sent one; computer players cannot see it either. Example Ossa has her eye on two crater plots near the river. In planning she sends the drone over both for 60 cr. At the grant, the first lights and her phone says \"Your drone: 7·2 Crater, good for Crystite\": she has found a ring. She claims it. The other crater was empty; she lets it pass."},{"page":"land.html","id":"drone-ai","title":"Computer players and the drone","status":"","text":"A computer player's own plan never sends a drone. Only a plan written by Claude (when Claude plays a seat) can name drone plots, and the rules check them the same way. In the code: game.ts::orderGeoDrone · game.ts::advancePhase (the results at the land grant) · config.ts::DEVELOPMENT.droneFee · config.ts::DEVELOPMENT.dronePlots · apps/web/src/phone/hub/tools.tsx::DroneMap · apps/web/src/phone/LandGrant.tsx::droneFoundLine · packages/ai/src/seat.ts Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3) for the rule, the UX team (WS6) for the drone screens."},{"page":"land.html","id":"analysing","title":"Analysing a plot on foot","status":"In the game","text":"During development your colonist can stand on any plot (owned or free, with or without a MULE) and press ANALYSE . The colonist stays put, the button reads ASSAYING, and when it is done you know that plot's rating for all six raw goods and its traits. Who Time standing still Cost in money Any colonist 8 s 0 Mollox (dig faster) 4 s 0 The time comes out of the one 60 -second development clock, so it also lowers what the pub pays you. A plot analysed once stays known all game; a lake plot is analysed from its shore. See Development for when the button offers it. In the code: game.ts::analysePlot · game.ts::analysisSeconds · config.ts::DEVELOPMENT.analysisSeconds · config.ts::SPECIES_PERK.molloxAnalysis · packages/engine/src/floor/reach.ts Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3) for the rule, the Strategy team (WS1) for the floor, the UX team (WS6) for the screens."},{"page":"land.html","id":"who-knows-what","title":"Who knows what","status":"In the game","text":"Information Who sees it Terrain, owners, MULEs and their outfits, filters, pollution, special fields Everyone, the TV too A plot's real ratings Only a player who analysed it or flew a drone over it A plot's traits A player who analysed it, and its owner Your drone order and what it found Only you Who analysed which plot Nobody else (the colonist standing still is visible, the result is not) Owning a plot does not show you its ratings. A plot you claimed without analysing shows only its terrain hint until you analyse it."},{"page":"land.html","id":"ai-knowledge","title":"What the computer players know","status":"","text":"Computer players read the game only through the same filter as a phone. For any plot they have not analysed, they see the terrain's average rating (and 0.6 for Crystite and Rare earth on every plot), never the real one, and no traits except on their own plots. They never see another player's analyses, drone orders or drone finds. To learn the truth they must analyse on foot, like a human: they analyse an unknown plot of their own when its best and second-best uses are within 25 % of each other on the averages; at most 2 analyses a month (Rookie 1 ), and never with less than 20 seconds left; when choosing land, an unknown plot counts for less than a known one (they score \"known ground\" lower when it is a guess from the terrain). In the code: packages/engine/src/observe.ts::observe · packages/engine/src/map.ts::expectedGround · apps/server/src/mule-game.ts::viewFor · config.ts::STRATEGIST.analyseWhenClose · config.ts::STRATEGIST.analysesPerMonth Kept current by: the Auctions team (WS3) for the views, the Strategy team (WS1) for the computer players."},{"page":"land.html","id":"drone-geology-redesign","title":"Drone and geology redesign","status":"Being designed","text":"On 5 Nov Heiko played a game, could not get the drone to work as he expected, and asked for a redesign of how players learn about the ground. In his words, condensed: Pick, then scan. Mark the plots first, press Scan, and get a detailed report. Always see your own land. An easy way to look at your plots and their resources at any time, in planning and in development. Pay to know. Resources show only after you paid for them, by drone or by the assay in development. A clear marker on plots that hold something special, like Crystite or Rare earth. One scale. Every resource rated 0 to 3 per plot."},{"page":"land.html","id":"redesign-today","title":"How today differs","status":"","text":"Ask Today Pick, then scan, then a report You pick and pay in planning, but the results arrive silently when the land grant starts. You meet them only when a drone plot is lit (its ground card and one line) or when you look the plot up later. There is no Scan button and no report screen. Always see your own land The Look tool shows a ground card only for plots you analysed; a plot you own but never analysed shows its terrain hint and its traits, not its ratings. Pay to know Already so: ratings show only after a drone or an analysis. The analysis costs time ( 8 s), not money. A marker for Crystite or Rare earth None. An analysed plot shows pips for each good on its card, nothing on the map itself marks a rich deposit. Every good 0 to 3 Grown goods are rated -2 to +3 and mined goods 0 to 4 ; the phone draws both on 0 to 4 pips (a grown rating plus one, so -2 and -1 both show as no pips). What is changing Routed by the PM on 5 Nov to the UX team (WS6), which writes a plain \"how it works today\" with screenshots from the real build and the design goals, due 5 Nov 23:00Z, for Heiko to read before any approaches. Changing every rating to 0 to 3 moves production and needs a balance measurement from the Strategy team (WS1) first. The engine and server side (drone, analysis, the rating type) is the Auctions team's (WS3); the rings and the map maker are the Maps team's (WS5); the computer players' use of ratings is WS1's and WS9's; the TV is WS4's. Nothing here is decided yet. In the code: game.ts::orderGeoDrone · game.ts::analysePlot · packages/engine/src/types.ts::Ground · apps/web/src/game/derive.ts::groundPips · apps/web/src/phone/ground.tsx::GroundCard Kept current by: the UX team (WS6), with WS3 and WS5."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"good-config","title":"GOOD_CONFIG","status":"In the game","text":"Setting Value What it does GOOD_CONFIG.food.refPrice 30 No note in the settings file. GOOD_CONFIG.food.sensitivity 1.5 How strongly price reacts to low cover (k in the price formula). GOOD_CONFIG.food.spoilage 0.5 Share lost each month without suitable storage. GOOD_CONFIG.water.refPrice 25 No note in the settings file. GOOD_CONFIG.water.sensitivity 1.5 How strongly price reacts to low cover (k in the price formula). GOOD_CONFIG.water.spoilage 0.05 Share lost each month without suitable storage. GOOD_CONFIG.energy.refPrice 25 No note in the settings file. GOOD_CONFIG.energy.sensitivity 1.2 How strongly price reacts to low cover (k in the price formula). GOOD_CONFIG.energy.spoilage 0.25 Share lost each month without suitable storage. GOOD_CONFIG.smithore.refPrice 50 No note in the settings file. GOOD_CONFIG.smithore.sensitivity 1.2 How strongly price reacts to low cover (k in the price formula). GOOD_CONFIG.smithore.spoilage 0 Share lost each month without suitable storage. GOOD_CONFIG.crystite.refPrice 100 No note in the settings file. GOOD_CONFIG.crystite.sensitivity 0.5 How strongly price reacts to low cover (k in the price formula). GOOD_CONFIG.crystite.spoilage 0 Share lost each month without suitable storage. GOOD_CONFIG.rareEarth.refPrice 60 No note in the settings file. GOOD_CONFIG.rareEarth.sensitivity 0.8 How strongly price reacts to low cover (k in the price formula). GOOD_CONFIG.rareEarth.spoilage 0 Share lost each month without suitable storage. GOOD_CONFIG.alloy.refPrice 90 No note in the settings file. GOOD_CONFIG.alloy.sensitivity 0.8 How strongly price reacts to low cover (k in the price formula). GOOD_CONFIG.alloy.spoilage 0 Share lost each month without suitable storage. GOOD_CONFIG.electronics.refPrice 220 No note in the settings file. GOOD_CONFIG.electronics.sensitivity 0.5 How strongly price reacts to low cover (k in the price formula). GOOD_CONFIG.electronics.spoilage 0 Share lost each month without suitable storage. GOOD_CONFIG.medicine.refPrice 120 No note in the settings file. GOOD_CONFIG.medicine.sensitivity 1 How strongly price reacts to low cover (k in the price formula). GOOD_CONFIG.medicine.spoilage 0.2 Share lost each month without suitable storage."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"price","title":"PRICE","status":"In the game","text":"Setting Value What it does PRICE.targetCover 2 Months of demand the store aims to hold. PRICE.minCover 0.25 Cover below this counts as this, so an empty shelf still gives a finite target. PRICE.maxRise 0.5 Momentum: the posted price moves toward its target by at most this share up or down a month. PRICE.maxFall 0.4 No note of its own; the note on PRICE.maxRise , just before it in the file, may cover it. PRICE.storeSpread 0.1 The store sells this much above and buys this much below its posted price. PRICE.storeMaxCover 3 The store fills its shelves up to this many months of demand. PRICE.shelfCover 1 Food and Water count as short when the store's shelf holds under this many months of the colonists' need, so the price climbs before they go hungry. PRICE.restockPremium 1.5 With that shelf short, the store bids up to this times the posted price for Food and Water, capped so it can still sell to colonists within their budget. PRICE.storeSpoilage 0.25 Share of normal spoilage the store suffers, thanks to its cold store and silos. PRICE.refDrift 0.25 How fast the reference price follows what players pay each other (0..1). PRICE.worldPull 0.1 How fast the reference price drifts back toward the off-world price (0..1)."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"export-demand","title":"EXPORT_DEMAND","status":"In the game","text":"Off-world demand per player per month: what the export ship's buyers take. Food, Water and Energy don't travel well, so only the colony wants them. The colony is self-contained: nothing is imported. Setting Value What it does EXPORT_DEMAND.smithore 1 See the note on EXPORT_DEMAND . EXPORT_DEMAND.crystite 2 See the note on EXPORT_DEMAND . EXPORT_DEMAND.rareEarth 0.5 See the note on EXPORT_DEMAND . EXPORT_DEMAND.alloy 1 See the note on EXPORT_DEMAND . EXPORT_DEMAND.electronics 1 See the note on EXPORT_DEMAND . EXPORT_DEMAND.medicine 0.5 See the note on EXPORT_DEMAND ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"recipes","title":"RECIPES","status":"In the game","text":"Factory recipes: inputs consumed per unit of output. Setting Value What it does RECIPES.alloy.inputs.smithore 2 See the note on RECIPES . RECIPES.alloy.inputs.energy 2 See the note on RECIPES . RECIPES.alloy.output 2 See the note on RECIPES . RECIPES.electronics.inputs.rareEarth 1 See the note on RECIPES . RECIPES.electronics.inputs.alloy 1 See the note on RECIPES . RECIPES.electronics.inputs.energy 1 See the note on RECIPES . RECIPES.electronics.output 1 See the note on RECIPES . RECIPES.medicine.inputs.food 1 See the note on RECIPES . RECIPES.medicine.inputs.water 1 See the note on RECIPES . RECIPES.medicine.inputs.energy 1 See the note on RECIPES . RECIPES.medicine.output 2 See the note on RECIPES ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"grown-base","title":"GROWN_BASE","status":"In the game","text":"Production from the ground. A grown good makes GROWN_BASE plus the plot's rating, but never less than GROWN_MIN: a poor plot still works, just badly. A mined good makes exactly its deposit rating, so a 0 means nothing to mine. Setting Value What it does GROWN_BASE 2 See the note above."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"grown-min","title":"GROWN_MIN","status":"In the game","text":"Setting Value What it does GROWN_MIN 1 Explained in the note on GROWN_BASE ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"grown-odds","title":"GROWN_ODDS","status":"In the game","text":"Chance of each grown-good rating from GROWN_ODDS_MIN up (today -2 to +3), by how the terrain suits the good. Setting Value What it does GROWN_ODDS.good [0, 0.05, 0.15, 0.3, 0.3, 0.2] See the note on GROWN_ODDS . GROWN_ODDS.neutral [0.05, 0.2, 0.4, 0.25, 0.1, 0] See the note on GROWN_ODDS . GROWN_ODDS.poor [0.3, 0.35, 0.25, 0.1, 0, 0] See the note on GROWN_ODDS ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"grown-odds-min","title":"GROWN_ODDS_MIN","status":"In the game","text":"The grown-good rating GROWN_ODDS' first chance is for; each later chance is one rating higher. Setting Value What it does GROWN_ODDS_MIN -2 See the note above."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"mineral-odds","title":"MINERAL_ODDS","status":"In the game","text":"Chance of each mineral deposit rating from MINERAL_ODDS_MIN up (today 0 to 4). Setting Value What it does MINERAL_ODDS.none [1, 0, 0, 0, 0] See the note on MINERAL_ODDS . MINERAL_ODDS.low [0.7, 0.2, 0.1, 0, 0] See the note on MINERAL_ODDS . MINERAL_ODDS.medium [0.4, 0.3, 0.2, 0.1, 0] See the note on MINERAL_ODDS . MINERAL_ODDS.high [0.05, 0.15, 0.3, 0.3, 0.2] See the note on MINERAL_ODDS . MINERAL_ODDS.veryHigh [0.02, 0.08, 0.15, 0.4, 0.35] See the note on MINERAL_ODDS ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"mineral-odds-min","title":"MINERAL_ODDS_MIN","status":"In the game","text":"The deposit rating MINERAL_ODDS' first chance is for; each later chance is one rating higher. Setting Value What it does MINERAL_ODDS_MIN 0 See the note above."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"terrain-odds","title":"TERRAIN_ODDS","status":"In the game","text":"Terrain only sets the odds; the real rating is rolled once per plot when the map is made and stays hidden until a geo analysis. From the design doc's Terrain and geology tab. Goods not listed are neutral. Setting Value What it does TERRAIN_ODDS.river.grown.food \"good\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.river.grown.water \"good\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.river.mined.smithore \"low\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.lake.grown.water \"good\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.lake.grown.food \"good\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.lake.mined.smithore \"none\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.plains.grown.food \"good\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.plains.grown.energy \"good\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.plains.mined.smithore \"low\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.forest.grown.food \"good\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.forest.grown.energy \"poor\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.forest.mined.smithore \"low\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.swamp.grown.water \"good\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.swamp.grown.food \"poor\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.swamp.mined.smithore \"low\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.hills.grown.food \"poor\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.hills.mined.smithore \"high\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.mountains.grown.food \"poor\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.mountains.grown.energy \"poor\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.mountains.mined.smithore \"veryHigh\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.desert.grown.energy \"good\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.desert.grown.water \"poor\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.desert.grown.food \"poor\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.desert.mined.smithore \"low\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.tundra.grown.energy \"good\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.tundra.grown.water \"good\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.tundra.grown.food \"poor\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.tundra.mined.smithore \"medium\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.ice.grown.water \"good\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.ice.grown.food \"poor\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.ice.grown.energy \"poor\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.ice.mined.smithore \"none\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.volcanic.grown.energy \"good\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.volcanic.grown.food \"poor\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.volcanic.mined.smithore \"medium\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.crater.grown.food \"poor\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS . TERRAIN_ODDS.crater.mined.smithore \"low\" See the note on TERRAIN_ODDS ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"trait-effect","title":"TRAIT_EFFECT","status":"In the game","text":"How hazard traits change output. Traits not listed only change event risks. Setting Value What it does TRAIT_EFFECT.hotSpring.energy 2 See the note on TRAIT_EFFECT . TRAIT_EFFECT.hotSpring.water -1 See the note on TRAIT_EFFECT . TRAIT_EFFECT.toxic.food -2 See the note on TRAIT_EFFECT ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"thin-vein-months","title":"THIN_VEIN_MONTHS","status":"In the game","text":"A thin vein's mining output drops by 1 every this many months the mine runs. Setting Value What it does THIN_VEIN_MONTHS 3 See the note above."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"factory-fit","title":"FACTORY_FIT","status":"In the game","text":"Factories. A good place gives +1 output and a poor place -1, with the reason the phone shows before you install. Plains suit any factory. Setting Value What it does FACTORY_FIT.alloy.volcanic [1, \"Geothermal heat helps the furnace\"] See the note on FACTORY_FIT . FACTORY_FIT.alloy.hills [1, \"Firm rock under the furnace\"] See the note on FACTORY_FIT . FACTORY_FIT.alloy.mountains [1, \"Firm rock under the furnace\"] See the note on FACTORY_FIT . FACTORY_FIT.alloy.swamp [-1, \"Soft ground: the furnace sinks\"] See the note on FACTORY_FIT . FACTORY_FIT.alloy.ice [-1, \"Too cold for the furnace\"] See the note on FACTORY_FIT . FACTORY_FIT.alloy.lake [-1, \"Wet ground\"] See the note on FACTORY_FIT . FACTORY_FIT.electronics.tundra [1, \"Cold air cools the works\"] See the note on FACTORY_FIT . FACTORY_FIT.electronics.desert [1, \"Dry air, no damp\"] See the note on FACTORY_FIT . FACTORY_FIT.electronics.plains [1, \"Flat and dry\"] See the note on FACTORY_FIT . FACTORY_FIT.electronics.swamp [-1, \"Damp gets into the circuits\"] See the note on FACTORY_FIT . FACTORY_FIT.electronics.river [-1, \"Damp gets into the circuits\"] See the note on FACTORY_FIT . FACTORY_FIT.electronics.volcanic [-1, \"Quakes and ash\"] See the note on FACTORY_FIT . FACTORY_FIT.medicine.forest [1, \"Herbs nearby\"] See the note on FACTORY_FIT . FACTORY_FIT.medicine.swamp [1, \"Herbs nearby\"] See the note on FACTORY_FIT . FACTORY_FIT.medicine.river [1, \"Clean water\"] See the note on FACTORY_FIT . FACTORY_FIT.medicine.desert [-1, \"No herbs, little water\"] See the note on FACTORY_FIT . FACTORY_FIT.medicine.volcanic [-1, \"Ash in the air\"] See the note on FACTORY_FIT . FACTORY_FIT.medicine.crater [-1, \"Dust in the air\"] See the note on FACTORY_FIT ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"factory","title":"FACTORY","status":"In the game","text":"Setting Value What it does FACTORY.parkBonus 1 Two factories of the same kind side by side each make this much more (an industrial park). FACTORY.feederEnergySaving 1 A factory next to its owner's plot that makes one of its inputs saves this much Energy. FACTORY.experienceMonths 3 After running this many months in a row, a factory makes experienceBonus more. FACTORY.experienceBonus 1 Explained in the note on FACTORY.experienceMonths ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"species-perk","title":"SPECIES_PERK","status":"In the game","text":"Species perks. Numbers are placeholders until the balance sim has run. Setting Value What it does SPECIES_PERK.zipwingSpeed 1.25 Zipwing: walks this many times as fast in development (DEV_WALK). At 1.5 Zipwings won 37% of games, not 25%. SPECIES_PERK.brambletFood 1 Bramblet: extra Food on every farm. SPECIES_PERK.gearlingPrice 0.95 Gearling: share of the normal price paid for MULEs and outfits. At 0.75 Gearlings won 44% of games, not 25%. SPECIES_PERK.molloxMine 1 Mollox: extra output on every working mine, and share of the normal geo analysis time. SPECIES_PERK.molloxAnalysis 0.5 No note of its own; the note on SPECIES_PERK.molloxMine , just before it in the file, may cover it."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"default-species","title":"DEFAULT_SPECIES","status":"In the game","text":"Default species by seat, when a player hasn't picked one. Setting Value What it does DEFAULT_SPECIES [\"zipwing\", \"bramblet\", \"gearling\", \"mollox\", \"glimmer\", \"quibbit\"] See the note above."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"adjacency","title":"ADJACENCY","status":"In the game","text":"Setting Value What it does ADJACENCY.neighbour 1 Bonus when at least one own neighbour produces the same good. ADJACENCY.clusterSize 4 Bonus instead, when the connected cluster has this many plots or more. ADJACENCY.cluster 2 Explained in the note on ADJACENCY.clusterSize ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"upkeep","title":"UPKEEP","status":"In the game","text":"Setting Value What it does UPKEEP.baseFood 2 A player's crew eats and drinks: the player alone needs baseFood and baseWater, and every few crew add one more. A raw plot has a crew of 1, a factory a crew of 2, so a bigger estate costs more to feed. UPKEEP.baseWater 1 Explained in the note on UPKEEP.baseFood . UPKEEP.crewPerFood 4 No note of its own; the note on UPKEEP.baseFood , just before it in the file, may cover it. UPKEEP.crewPerWater 6 No note of its own; the note on UPKEEP.baseFood , just before it in the file, may cover it. UPKEEP.crew.raw 1 No note in the settings file. UPKEEP.crew.factory 2 No note in the settings file. UPKEEP.crew.greenBelt 0 No note in the settings file. UPKEEP.irrigated [\"food\"] Plots that need irrigation: when their owner holds less Water than the household and crew need, they make half (on top of any Energy shortfall). UPKEEP.shortOutputShare 0.5 Share of its output a plot makes without the Energy (or, for an irrigated plot, the Water) it needs. UPKEEP.colonistsPerUnit 20 Colonists need 1 Food and 1 Water a month per this many colonists. UPKEEP.colonistEnergyShare 0.5 Colonists' Energy demand, as a share of their Food and Water units. UPKEEP.colonistsPerMedicine 50 Colonists need 1 Medicine a month per this many colonists (more when the air makes them sick). UPKEEP.colonistGrowth The colony grows by this factor in a month its colonists were fed, and shrinks by this one when they went hungry. UPKEEP.colonistGrowth.fed 1.04 See the note on UPKEEP.colonistGrowth . UPKEEP.colonistGrowth.hungry 0.95 See the note on UPKEEP.colonistGrowth . UPKEEP.colonistBudget The most colonists pay per unit, from their off-world wages. Above it they buy less, so their demand falls as the price rises: this is the demand curve. UPKEEP.colonistBudget.food 150 See the note on UPKEEP.colonistBudget . UPKEEP.colonistBudget.water 120 See the note on UPKEEP.colonistBudget . UPKEEP.colonistBudget.energy 100 See the note on UPKEEP.colonistBudget . UPKEEP.colonistBudget.medicine 400 See the note on UPKEEP.colonistBudget . UPKEEP.depotCapacity 10 Units of each good a player's home depot holds."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"upkeep-energy-use","title":"UPKEEP.energyUse","status":"","text":"Energy a raw plot burns a month, by what it makes. Factories burn what their recipe says. Without it a raw plot makes half, a factory nothing. Setting Value What it does UPKEEP.energyUse.food 1 See the note on UPKEEP.energyUse . UPKEEP.energyUse.water 1 See the note on UPKEEP.energyUse . UPKEEP.energyUse.energy 0 See the note on UPKEEP.energyUse . UPKEEP.energyUse.smithore 2 See the note on UPKEEP.energyUse . UPKEEP.energyUse.crystite 2 See the note on UPKEEP.energyUse . UPKEEP.energyUse.rareEarth 2 See the note on UPKEEP.energyUse ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"start","title":"START","status":"In the game","text":"Setting Value What it does START.money 1000 No note in the settings file. START.playerStock Each player's depot at the start. Food 2 and Energy 2 (Heiko, 5 Nov). START.playerStock.food 2 See the note on START.playerStock . START.playerStock.water 6 See the note on START.playerStock . START.playerStock.energy 2 See the note on START.playerStock . START.reputation 50 Each player's reputation at the start, out of 100. START.colonists 100 No note of its own; the note on START.reputation , just before it in the file, may cover it. START.storeMules 10 No note of its own; the note on START.reputation , just before it in the file, may cover it. START.storeCapital 4000 No note of its own; the note on START.reputation , just before it in the file, may cover it. START.storeFoodPerPlayer 4 The store's Food at the start, for each seat: 4 (Heiko, 5 Nov), so 16 in a 4-seat game. START.storeEnergyPerPlayer 5 The store's Energy at the start, for each seat: 5 (Heiko, 5 Nov), so 20 in a 4-seat game."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"start-store-stock","title":"START.storeStock","status":"","text":"Little enough Water that the AI's outlook (STRATEGIST.horizon, outlook.ts) sees the store run dry from month 1 and prices Water at what colonists pay, so the seats build wells early. A few units more put the dry month past the outlook, and the colonists go short later in the game. When this, START.colonists, UPKEEP.colonistGrowth or the outlook changes, re-run pnpm sim -- --games 40 --seed N --politics on|off for seeds 1, 101 and 201 and read \"Colonists short of Food or Water\". Food and Energy are per seat (storeFoodPerPlayer, storeEnergyPerPlayer); storeStockFor adds them. Setting Value What it does START.storeStock.water 20 See the note on START.storeStock . START.storeStock.smithore 20 See the note on START.storeStock . START.storeStock.crystite 0 See the note on START.storeStock . START.storeStock.rareEarth 4 See the note on START.storeStock . START.storeStock.alloy 8 See the note on START.storeStock . START.storeStock.electronics 0 See the note on START.storeStock . START.storeStock.medicine 4 See the note on START.storeStock ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"mule","title":"MULE","status":"In the game","text":"Setting Value What it does MULE.labour 120 Labour in an empty MULE. Its price is this plus the Smithore it is built from. MULE.smithorePerMule 1 Smithore the store needs to build one MULE, as in the original. MULE.corralPerPlayer 2 MULEs the store builds for each seat a month: the land a seat gains in a month, by the land grant and the land auction, not by the grant alone. Sizes the corral (upkeep.ts, and its copy in the AI's outlook) and the MULEs' share of Smithore demand (economy.ts mulesWanted). 2 keeps the corral it had when a grant gave two claims, so GAME.claimsPerGrant 2 → 1 (Heiko, 5 Nov) shrinks neither. MULE.spareInCorral 2 The store builds MULEs for every seat's corralPerPlayer, plus this many. MULE.scarcityUp 0.15 If the corral sold out, MULEs cost this much more next month; with surplusShare of the month's MULEs or more left over, surplusDown less. MULE.surplusDown 0.1 Explained in the note on MULE.scarcityUp . MULE.surplusShare 0.5 Explained in the note on MULE.scarcityUp . MULE.minFactor 0.6 The scarcity factor never falls below this."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"mule-outfit-cost","title":"MULE.outfitCost","status":"","text":"Setting Value What it does MULE.outfitCost.food 25 No note in the settings file. MULE.outfitCost.water 25 No note in the settings file. MULE.outfitCost.energy 50 No note in the settings file. MULE.outfitCost.smithore 75 No note in the settings file. MULE.outfitCost.crystite 100 No note in the settings file. MULE.outfitCost.rareEarth 100 No note in the settings file. MULE.outfitCost.alloy 150 No note in the settings file. MULE.outfitCost.electronics 200 No note in the settings file. MULE.outfitCost.medicine 150 No note in the settings file. MULE.outfitCost.greenBelt 20 No note in the settings file."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"development","title":"DEVELOPMENT","status":"In the game","text":"Setting Value What it does DEVELOPMENT.fullSeconds 60 Seconds of development, the same for every player: hunger and sickness slow the walk instead (DEV_WALK). 60 (Heiko, 4 Nov). With walking on, the clock alone doesn't save a starving colonist: at a fifth of its pace even the nearest Food errand and the pub take longer than 60 s, which is why DEV_WALK.hungerPaceMin is 0.6. DEVELOPMENT.analysisSeconds 8 Seconds a colonist stands on a plot for a geo analysis. DEVELOPMENT.pub The pub pays base + (start + month * perMonth) * share of time left. DEVELOPMENT.pub.base 20 See the note on DEVELOPMENT.pub . DEVELOPMENT.pub.start 60 See the note on DEVELOPMENT.pub . DEVELOPMENT.pub.perMonth 20 See the note on DEVELOPMENT.pub . DEVELOPMENT.droneFee 60 A geo drone analyses this many free plots from orbit for this fee. DEVELOPMENT.dronePlots 2 No note of its own; the note on DEVELOPMENT.droneFee , just before it in the file, may cover it. DEVELOPMENT.filterAlloy 1 Alloy a filter costs, from the player's own stock."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"dev-walk","title":"DEV_WALK","status":"In the game","text":"Walking in development costs time off the one development clock (Heiko's R1, 0.4 s a tile). A walk goes in grid steps on the map, never diagonally; the store and its corral are on the store's tile. A hungry or sick colonist walks slower, and the two multiply: no Food and sick is 0.6 × 0.5 = 0.3 of normal pace (Heiko's numbers). Not WALK, the goods auction's walk. Setting Value What it does DEV_WALK.tileSeconds 0.4 Seconds for each step from one map tile to the next. DEV_WALK.floorSeconds 1 Seconds to cross the store floor to the corral, from the door or any other room. DEV_WALK.hungerPaceMin 0.6 Share of its pace a colonist with no Food walks at; it rises linearly to 1 with the share of the month's Food it holds. 0.6 (Heiko, 4 Nov, \"Yes, 60%\"): the strongest slowdown with which the colony escapes famine with walking on. At 0.2 a starving colonist couldn't reach Food and the pub inside the turn and stayed starving (measured: 58% of months short of Food or Water; from 0.6, under 15% in every measured cell). DEV_WALK.sickPace 0.5 Share of its pace a sick colonist walks at (EVENTS.player.sickness rolled, and no Medicine held). DEV_WALK.hungerLevels The hunger levels the screens show, as shares of the month's Food missing (0 fed, 1 none): peckish below hungryFrom, hungry from it, starving from starvingFrom. Display bands only: the pace itself is continuous. Player.hungry is any shortfall; these levels are only the words on screen. WS6's thresholds, in the variant-B mock Heiko approved 2026-10-28 (design/mocks/ws6-ux at bb4bc11). DEV_WALK.hungerLevels.hungryFrom 0.3 See the note on DEV_WALK.hungerLevels . DEV_WALK.hungerLevels.starvingFrom 0.7 See the note on DEV_WALK.hungerLevels ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"dev-steer","title":"DEV_STEER","status":"In the game","text":"Arcade walking in development: every colonist steered live and stepped at one fixed tick, everywhere the game runs. Heiko's starting values (arcade spec RULE-7); DEV_WALK's hunger, sickness and Zipwing pace still apply. Setting Value What it does DEV_STEER.on true Steered walking instead of DEV_WALK's tap-to-walk. On since Heiko's OK on the rig (arcade PR-11). DEV_STEER.mapTilesPerSecond 2.5 Tiles a second on plains at full pace: the tap walk's own pace, 1 / DEV_WALK.tileSeconds (Heiko, 4 Nov; 1.25 was half). DEV_STEER.floorSeconds 3 Seconds across the store's hall at full pace, door to corral. DEV_STEER.leadPace 0.9 Share of its speed a colonist keeps while it leads a MULE. DEV_STEER.lakeMode \"shore\" No note of its own; the note on DEV_STEER.terrain , just before it in the file, may cover it. DEV_STEER.wading 0.3 Share of full speed in a lake when lakeMode is 'wade'. DEV_STEER.shoreReachTiles 0.45 In shore mode, a colonist reaches a lake plot nearer than this to its edge, in tiles (RULE-3 row 10; the mock's). DEV_STEER.mountainsImpassable false Mountains block like a lake instead of slowing. DEV_STEER.trespass true Others' plots may be walked across; false makes them block like a lake. DEV_STEER.tickMs 50 Ms of game time a tick steps: the server, the sim and the phone's prediction all step at this. DEV_STEER.catchUpTicks 10 The most missed ticks a late server timer runs before it resyncs. DEV_STEER.steerMaxPerSecond 10 The most steering messages a phone sends a second, under the server's RATE_LIMIT. DEV_STEER.botSpeed 1 AI colonists walk at this times a person's speed. DEV_STEER.botCounterPauseSeconds 0.5 Seconds an AI colonist stands at a counter before it presses. DEV_STEER.botsSpareTime true An AI colonist joins a glint race or the scramble only if its errand still fits the time left after the detour. DEV_STEER.botPubMarginSeconds 2 An AI colonist drops its errand for the pub once the clock shows no more than its walk there and this many seconds. DEV_STEER.botPlanSlackShare 0.1 An AI colonist buys a MULE only while its errand, this share longer than estimated, still leaves the pub margin (botPubMarginSeconds, as the hurry for the pub counts it): a walk that runs a little long then costs the margin nothing, and the MULE is installed, not walked into the pub (FM-hurry-margin). DEV_STEER.botStuckTicks 20 Ticks an AI colonist may push its stick without moving before it gives that step of its errand up. DEV_STEER.botShoreTiles 0.3 An AI colonist installing on a lake plot from the shore stands this far from the shore tile's middle towards the lake, in tiles. DEV_STEER.pubLeadRefund 0 Share of what a MULE cost that the pub pays back when its owner walks in leading it. DEV_STEER.reoutfitCost 1 A re-outfit's price, as a share of the outfitter's price for the new good: 1 is the outfit price. DEV_STEER.bodyTiles 0.18 A colonist's clearance from walls, lakes and the map's edge, in tiles. DEV_STEER.doorHalfTiles 0.25 How far the door reaches above and below the middle of each side face of the store's tile, in tiles. DEV_STEER.leadTiles 0.55 How far behind its colonist a MULE on its lead walks: in tiles on the map, as a share of the hall's width inside. DEV_STEER.leadHallShare 0.075 No note of its own; the note on DEV_STEER.leadTiles , just before it in the file, may cover it. DEV_STEER.snapTiles 0.25 The phone's own colonist: within snapTiles of the server's it eases there over correctMs, beyond it snaps. DEV_STEER.correctMs 150 Explained in the note on DEV_STEER.snapTiles . DEV_STEER.interpolateMs 100 Other colonists draw this far behind the newest frame, interpolated between frames. DEV_STEER.staleMs 1000 No frame for this long and the phone shows \"reconnecting\" and stops predicting."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"dev-steer-terrain","title":"DEV_STEER.terrain","status":"","text":"Share of full speed on each land. 0 blocks it. Setting Value What it does DEV_STEER.terrain.plains 1 See the note on DEV_STEER.terrain . DEV_STEER.terrain.desert 0.9 See the note on DEV_STEER.terrain . DEV_STEER.terrain.tundra 0.85 See the note on DEV_STEER.terrain . DEV_STEER.terrain.forest 0.75 See the note on DEV_STEER.terrain . DEV_STEER.terrain.ice 0.75 See the note on DEV_STEER.terrain . DEV_STEER.terrain.crater 0.8 See the note on DEV_STEER.terrain . DEV_STEER.terrain.hills 0.7 See the note on DEV_STEER.terrain . DEV_STEER.terrain.volcanic 0.6 See the note on DEV_STEER.terrain . DEV_STEER.terrain.river 0.6 See the note on DEV_STEER.terrain . DEV_STEER.terrain.swamp 0.5 See the note on DEV_STEER.terrain . DEV_STEER.terrain.mountains 0.4 See the note on DEV_STEER.terrain . DEV_STEER.terrain.lake 0 See the note on DEV_STEER.terrain ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"wolves","title":"WOLVES","status":"In the game","text":"Wolves on forest, hills and tundra: run, or fight for pelts (arcade spec RULE-4). Setting Value What it does WOLVES.on true See the note on WOLVES . WOLVES.chancePerSecond 0.1 Chance a second, on wolf land, that a pack comes for a colonist. WOLVES.packSize 3 No note of its own; the note on WOLVES.chancePerSecond , just before it in the file, may cover it. WOLVES.cooldownSeconds 20 No second pack for the same colonist for this long. WOLVES.chooseSeconds 3 Time to choose run or fight before they pounce, which is a fight. WOLVES.chaseShare 0.88 A chasing wolf's speed, as a share of DEV_STEER.mapTilesPerSecond (AM-32). Relative, not tiles a second, so a change of walking pace cannot defang the pack: RULE-4 tuned 1.1 tiles a second against colonists at 1.25, and 0.88 is that. WOLVES.giveUpSeconds 5 A chase ends, got away, after this long, or this far from where it started. The distance stays in tiles at any pace (AM-32, corrected): the pack's distances (packRingTiles, chaseCatchTiles) are tiles and its speed is a share of the pace, so a give-up distance in tiles holds the chase's odds; scaled with the pace, it let the pack close twice the ground at 2.5 and escapes fell to 4 of 20. WOLVES.giveUpTiles 4 No note of its own; the note on WOLVES.giveUpSeconds , just before it in the file, may cover it. WOLVES.bittenPace 0.5 Share of its speed a bitten colonist keeps, till it reaches the store or its own plot. WOLVES.fightSeconds 10 No note of its own; the note on WOLVES.bittenPace , just before it in the file, may cover it. WOLVES.lungeEverySeconds 1.6 No note of its own; the note on WOLVES.bittenPace , just before it in the file, may cover it. WOLVES.warnSeconds 0.8 The crouch before a lunge: the time there is to dodge. WOLVES.strikesToWin 3 No note of its own; the note on WOLVES.warnSeconds , just before it in the file, may cover it. WOLVES.bitesToLose 3 No note of its own; the note on WOLVES.warnSeconds , just before it in the file, may cover it. WOLVES.pelts 2 Pelts a won fight gives, and what the store pays for each. WOLVES.peltPrice 20 No note of its own; the note on WOLVES.pelts , just before it in the file, may cover it. WOLVES.stunSeconds 4 A lost fight with no MULE on the lead freezes its colonist this long. WOLVES.boltTiles 4 A lost fight's MULE bolts this many tiles at this many tiles a second. WOLVES.boltSpeed 3.5 No note of its own; the note on WOLVES.boltTiles , just before it in the file, may cover it. WOLVES.lands [\"forest\", \"hills\", \"tundra\"] The lands a pack comes on. WOLVES.packRingTiles 1.6 Where a pack stands when it comes: this far from its colonist, spread over this arc from one side, in radians. WOLVES.packArcRadians 1.9 No note of its own; the note on WOLVES.packRingTiles , just before it in the file, may cover it. WOLVES.headStartSeconds 0.4 A chase: the wolves set off after this head start, and catch a colonist they come this close to. WOLVES.chaseCatchTiles 0.35 No note of its own; the note on WOLVES.headStartSeconds , just before it in the file, may cover it. WOLVES.fightRingTiles 1.3 A fight: the wolves circle this far from where it began, turning this fast; the colonist stays within arenaTiles of it. WOLVES.prowlRadiansPerSecond 0.7 No note of its own; the note on WOLVES.fightRingTiles , just before it in the file, may cover it. WOLVES.arenaTiles 1 Explained in the note on WOLVES.fightRingTiles . WOLVES.firstLungeSeconds 0.9 The fight's first lunge comes this long after it starts. WOLVES.lungeSeconds 0.22 A lunge takes this long to land, and bites a colonist still this close to the spot it aimed at. WOLVES.biteTiles 0.38 No note of its own; the note on WOLVES.lungeSeconds , just before it in the file, may cover it. WOLVES.openSeconds 0.9 A wolf that missed stands open this long; one struck, or that has bitten, is out of the fight this long."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"wolves-part-2","title":"WOLVES , continued","status":"","text":"Setting Value What it does WOLVES.hurtSeconds 0.7 No note of its own; the note on WOLVES.openSeconds , just before it in the file, may cover it. WOLVES.strikeTiles 0.8 A strike lands on a wolf this close; a strike at nothing leaves its colonist open this long. WOLVES.whiffSeconds 0.45 No note of its own; the note on WOLVES.strikeTiles , just before it in the file, may cover it. WOLVES.catchReachTiles 0.6 A bolted MULE is caught by its colonist this close to it (arcade RULE-3 row 4). WOLVES.botFightShare 0.5 Share of AI colonists that fight rather than run. WOLVES.botChooseSeconds 0.6 An AI colonist stands this long before it runs or fights, and dodges a lunge till it is this far beyond its bite. WOLVES.botDodgeMarginTiles 0.2 No note of its own; the note on WOLVES.botChooseSeconds , just before it in the file, may cover it. WOLVES.botWolfLandShare 0.5 Share of an AI colonist's legs, among those it walks with no MULE on the lead, that go by wolf land. A leg leading a MULE never does (a pack met then costs the install), so the share of all its legs is lower than this. WOLVES.simMonthsWithPack 0.5 The share of development months with at least one AI wolf encounter that pnpm sim 's report asks for (AM-27), on each of seeds 1, 101 and 201 (40 games each, AI-only): UBER-AC-3 needs the table to see an AI colonist meet wolves on the TV in an ordinary game. Under it the report prints MISS."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"wolves-levels","title":"WOLVES.levels","status":"","text":"Each level's chance to dodge a lunge, and its delay before it strikes at a wolf that stands open. Setting Value What it does WOLVES.levels.rookie.dodge 0.4 See the note on WOLVES.levels . WOLVES.levels.rookie.strikeDelaySeconds 0.5 See the note on WOLVES.levels . WOLVES.levels.regular.dodge 0.6 See the note on WOLVES.levels . WOLVES.levels.regular.strikeDelaySeconds 0.35 See the note on WOLVES.levels . WOLVES.levels.veteran.dodge 0.9 See the note on WOLVES.levels . WOLVES.levels.veteran.strikeDelaySeconds 0.15 See the note on WOLVES.levels ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"glint","title":"GLINT","status":"In the game","text":"The glint race: a Crystite sparkle on a tile, and the first colonist onto it wins (arcade spec RULE-5). Setting Value What it does GLINT.on true See the note on GLINT . GLINT.meanGapSeconds 20 Mean seconds between glints while none is lit. GLINT.showSeconds 8 No note of its own; the note on GLINT.meanGapSeconds , just before it in the file, may cover it. GLINT.firstAfterSeconds 6 None in a turn's first seconds, nor this soon after one ends. GLINT.pauseSeconds 5 No note of its own; the note on GLINT.firstAfterSeconds , just before it in the file, may cover it. GLINT.onOwnedPlots true A glint may light someone's plot (never a lake or the store). GLINT.prize \"crystite\" No note of its own; the note on GLINT.onOwnedPlots , just before it in the file, may cover it. GLINT.crystite 1 The prize: this much Crystite, or these credits when prize is 'credits'. GLINT.credits 60 Explained in the note on GLINT.crystite . GLINT.grabReach 1.2 GRAB THE GLINT reaches it from this far from its tile's middle, in tiles: about a step away (arcade RULE-3 row 3). GLINT.botShare 0.75 Share of AI colonists that race, and only for a glint within botCareTiles. GLINT.botCareTiles 8 Explained in the note on GLINT.botShare ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"scramble","title":"SCRAMBLE","status":"In the game","text":"The corral scramble: the race for the store's last MULEs (arcade spec RULE-6). Setting Value What it does SCRAMBLE.on true See the note on SCRAMBLE . SCRAMBLE.mulesLeft 2 It starts when a sale leaves the corral with this many MULEs or fewer. SCRAMBLE.maxSeconds 15 It ends after this long, if the corral has not emptied first. SCRAMBLE.botShare 0.9 Share of AI colonists still needing a MULE that race, and only from within botCareTiles of the store. SCRAMBLE.botCareTiles 10 Explained in the note on SCRAMBLE.botShare ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"pollution","title":"POLLUTION","status":"In the game","text":"What pollutes, and by how much. From the design doc's Pollution tab. Setting Value What it does POLLUTION.airMax 100 The air gauge runs from 0 (clean) to this. POLLUTION.maxPlot 3 The highest plot level (toxic). POLLUTION.natureCleansPerPlayer 2 Nature cleans this many air points a month per player (a bigger colony has a bigger map). POLLUTION.recovery 1 Plot levels a plot with no polluter beside it recovers each month; untouched forest and swamp recover faster. POLLUTION.wildRecovery 2 No note of its own; the note on POLLUTION.recovery , just before it in the file, may cover it. POLLUTION.filterFactor 0.5 A filter multiplies the source's air points by this and stops the local spread. POLLUTION.greenBeltCleans 1 A green belt cleans its plot and the four touching it by this much, and the air by greenBeltAir. POLLUTION.greenBeltAir 2 Explained in the note on POLLUTION.greenBeltCleans . POLLUTION.sensitive [\"food\", \"water\", \"medicine\"] Goods that lose 1 unit per plot pollution level. POLLUTION.cleanExportBonus 0.1 Clean colony: off-world buyers pay this much more for exports. POLLUTION.sickMedicineDemand 1.5 From Smoggy: colonists' Medicine demand is multiplied by this. POLLUTION.toxicFarmLoss 1 From Toxic: every farm and well makes this much less. POLLUTION.collapseLeave 0.05 In Collapse: this share of colonists leaves each month, and final scores are multiplied by collapseScore. POLLUTION.collapseScore 0.8 Explained in the note on POLLUTION.collapseLeave ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"pollution-sources","title":"POLLUTION.sources","status":"","text":"Colony air points a month, and whether the source also fouls the plots around it. Setting Value What it does POLLUTION.sources.rareEarth.air 4 See the note on POLLUTION.sources . POLLUTION.sources.rareEarth.spreads true See the note on POLLUTION.sources . POLLUTION.sources.alloy.air 3 See the note on POLLUTION.sources . POLLUTION.sources.alloy.spreads true See the note on POLLUTION.sources . POLLUTION.sources.electronics.air 1 See the note on POLLUTION.sources . POLLUTION.sources.electronics.spreads false See the note on POLLUTION.sources . POLLUTION.sources.smithore.air 1 See the note on POLLUTION.sources . POLLUTION.sources.smithore.spreads false See the note on POLLUTION.sources . POLLUTION.sources.crystite.air 1 See the note on POLLUTION.sources . POLLUTION.sources.crystite.spreads false See the note on POLLUTION.sources ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"pollution-bands","title":"POLLUTION.bands","status":"","text":"The colony gauge's bands: from a band that sickens, a colonist who takes no Medicine is sick (DEV_WALK.sickPace). Setting Value What it does POLLUTION.bands[0].name \"clean\" See the note on POLLUTION.bands . POLLUTION.bands[0].from 0 See the note on POLLUTION.bands . POLLUTION.bands[0].sickens false See the note on POLLUTION.bands . POLLUTION.bands[1].name \"smoggy\" See the note on POLLUTION.bands . POLLUTION.bands[1].from 30 See the note on POLLUTION.bands . POLLUTION.bands[1].sickens true See the note on POLLUTION.bands . POLLUTION.bands[2].name \"toxic\" See the note on POLLUTION.bands . POLLUTION.bands[2].from 60 See the note on POLLUTION.bands . POLLUTION.bands[2].sickens true See the note on POLLUTION.bands . POLLUTION.bands[3].name \"collapse\" See the note on POLLUTION.bands . POLLUTION.bands[3].from 80 See the note on POLLUTION.bands . POLLUTION.bands[3].sickens true See the note on POLLUTION.bands ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"map","title":"MAP","status":"In the game","text":"What every map must meet (the map rules), read by the checker, the generator and the map gallery. Size bands are sizeBand's. Counts are good plots per player. Setting Value What it does MAP.unknownMineral 0.6 The honest map-wide average Crystite and Rare earth rating, shown as the hint on every terrain: both exist only in random rings, so no terrain says where they are. Measured: the mean over maps of 2 to 6 players, each map counted once (about 0.82 small, 0.60 mid, 0.41 large). MAP.floor Every map has at least this many good plots per player. MAP.floor.food 10 See the note on MAP.floor . MAP.floor.water 10 See the note on MAP.floor . MAP.floor.energy 10 See the note on MAP.floor . MAP.floor.smithore 3 See the note on MAP.floor . MAP.normalLo Lower edge of the normal range: a good below it is the map's scarce good, and at most one may be. MAP.normalLo.food 12 See the note on MAP.normalLo . MAP.normalLo.water 13 See the note on MAP.normalLo . MAP.normalLo.energy 13 See the note on MAP.normalLo . MAP.normalLo.smithore 4 See the note on MAP.normalLo . MAP.bends Fewest river bends: rows holding two river plots. MAP.bends.small 2 See the note on MAP.bends . MAP.bends.mid 3 See the note on MAP.bends . MAP.bends.large 4 See the note on MAP.bends . MAP.jitter 1 / 3 ≈ 0.3333 A terrain's share may stray this fraction of its target, plus one plot. MAP.maxTerrainShare 1 / 3 ≈ 0.3333 No terrain may cover more than this share of non-river land. MAP.riverMargin 2 The river keeps at least this many columns from each side edge. MAP.minRegion 3 Smallest connected same-terrain region. MAP.maxRegionShare 0.12 Largest region, as a share of non-river land (rounded up to whole plots). MAP.seamShare 0.7 Share of good Smithore plots that touch another good Smithore plot. MAP.honesty 0.3 Largest gap between a terrain's mean rating over many maps and the hint players see. Read by the generator and the map-quality test, not the checker. MAP.placeHonesty 0.1 Largest gap, on one part of the map (each edge row or column, or the interior), between the mean of rating minus hint and zero. The hint is the same everywhere, so ground that is poorer by place misleads. MAP.traits Hidden hazard traits: a land plot draws its trait count evenly from this list (a repeat weights a count), then that many traits, a duplicate adding none. MAP.traits.counts [0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 2] See the note on MAP.traits . MAP.mountainPeaks Peaks drawn on a mountain plot, evenly from min to max. For the picture only: yield comes from the ground. MAP.mountainPeaks.min 1 See the note on MAP.mountainPeaks . MAP.mountainPeaks.max 3 See the note on MAP.mountainPeaks . MAP.scarceChance 0.25 Chance a map has one scarce good. Read by the generator and the map-quality test, not the checker. MAP.specials Special fields on each map. MAP.specials.small 3 See the note on MAP.specials . MAP.specials.mid 4 See the note on MAP.specials . MAP.specials.large 5 See the note on MAP.specials . MAP.meteorCrater A meteor crater, formed in play: its Crystite rating (the top of the mined scale), and no production bonus. MAP.meteorCrater.crystite 4 See the note on MAP.meteorCrater ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"map-good-at","title":"MAP.goodAt","status":"","text":"A plot is good for a raw good at this rating or more. Setting Value What it does MAP.goodAt.food 1 See the note on MAP.goodAt . MAP.goodAt.water 1 See the note on MAP.goodAt . MAP.goodAt.energy 1 See the note on MAP.goodAt . MAP.goodAt.smithore 3 See the note on MAP.goodAt . MAP.goodAt.crystite 3 See the note on MAP.goodAt . MAP.goodAt.rareEarth 3 See the note on MAP.goodAt ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"map-reach","title":"MAP.reach","status":"","text":"Most steps from any land plot to a good plot of each good. Setting Value What it does MAP.reach.small.food 3 See the note on MAP.reach . MAP.reach.small.water 3 See the note on MAP.reach . MAP.reach.small.energy 3 See the note on MAP.reach . MAP.reach.small.smithore 5 See the note on MAP.reach . MAP.reach.mid.food 3 See the note on MAP.reach . MAP.reach.mid.water 3 See the note on MAP.reach . MAP.reach.mid.energy 3 See the note on MAP.reach . MAP.reach.mid.smithore 5 See the note on MAP.reach . MAP.reach.large.food 4 See the note on MAP.reach . MAP.reach.large.water 4 See the note on MAP.reach . MAP.reach.large.energy 4 See the note on MAP.reach . MAP.reach.large.smithore 6 See the note on MAP.reach ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"map-shares","title":"MAP.shares","status":"","text":"Target share of non-river land for each terrain. Setting Value What it does MAP.shares.plains 0.24 See the note on MAP.shares . MAP.shares.forest 0.11 See the note on MAP.shares . MAP.shares.hills 0.1 See the note on MAP.shares . MAP.shares.desert 0.09 See the note on MAP.shares . MAP.shares.tundra 0.08 See the note on MAP.shares . MAP.shares.mountains 0.07 See the note on MAP.shares . MAP.shares.swamp 0.07 See the note on MAP.shares . MAP.shares.lake 0.06 See the note on MAP.shares . MAP.shares.volcanic 0.06 See the note on MAP.shares . MAP.shares.crater 0.06 See the note on MAP.shares . MAP.shares.ice 0.06 See the note on MAP.shares ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"map-peak","title":"MAP.peak","status":"","text":"Crystite and Rare earth come in rings: a peak plot rated high , the plots steps.medium steps from it medium , steps.low steps low , and 0 elsewhere. Setting Value What it does MAP.peak.high 4 See the note on MAP.peak . MAP.peak.medium 3 See the note on MAP.peak . MAP.peak.low.min 1 See the note on MAP.peak . MAP.peak.low.max 2 See the note on MAP.peak . MAP.peak.count.min 3 See the note on MAP.peak . MAP.peak.count.max 4 See the note on MAP.peak . MAP.peak.steps.medium 1 See the note on MAP.peak . MAP.peak.steps.low 2 See the note on MAP.peak ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"map-special","title":"MAP.special","status":"","text":"Where special fields may go (spec RULE-5), read by the generator and the checker. Setting Value What it does MAP.special.on Terrains each kind may sit on. Ruins and relics take any dry land: every land terrain but the river and lakes. MAP.special.on.ruins [\"plains\", \"forest\", \"hills\", \"desert\", \"tundra\", \"mountains\", \"swamp\", \"volcanic\", \"crater\", \"ice\"] See the note on MAP.special.on . MAP.special.on.crystalCavern [\"crater\", \"mountains\", \"hills\"] See the note on MAP.special.on . MAP.special.on.geyser [\"volcanic\", \"tundra\", \"ice\"] See the note on MAP.special.on . MAP.special.on.oasis [\"desert\"] See the note on MAP.special.on . MAP.special.on.relic [\"plains\", \"forest\", \"hills\", \"desert\", \"tundra\", \"mountains\", \"swamp\", \"volcanic\", \"crater\", \"ice\"] See the note on MAP.special.on . MAP.special.maxPerKind 2 See the note on MAP.special . MAP.special.minKinds 3 See the note on MAP.special . MAP.special.spacing 4 Fewest steps between two special fields. MAP.special.oasisDeserts 2 Fewest of an oasis's 4 neighbours that are desert. MAP.special.cavernCrystite 1 Least Crystite rating under a crystal cavern. MAP.special.placementTries 10000 Plots the generator's placement search tries for one map, backtracking included; real maps need 3 to 10. At the bound it throws rather than place the map short. Read by the generator, not the checker."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"map-special-effect","title":"MAP.specialEffect","status":"","text":"What special fields do (spec RULE-6). Output bonuses count before adjacency. Setting Value What it does MAP.specialEffect.crystalCavern 4 Extra Crystite from a crystal cavern mined for Crystite. MAP.specialEffect.geyser 2 Extra Water or Energy from a geyser outfitted for either. MAP.specialEffect.oasis 3 Extra Food from an oasis farm. MAP.specialEffect.relic 1 Extra output for every working plot, raw or factory, per relic among its 4 neighbours. MAP.specialEffect.salvage What ruins pay their first owner, once, by the salvage drawn with the map. MAP.specialEffect.salvage.alloy 2 See the note on MAP.specialEffect.salvage . MAP.specialEffect.salvage.electronics 1 See the note on MAP.specialEffect.salvage ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"map-layout","title":"MAP.layout","status":"","text":"Tuning for the layout stages (map-layout.ts): how the generator meets the rules above, not rules themselves. Setting Value What it does MAP.layout.shareCap 0.3 The budget never gives a terrain more than this share, so jitter can't take plains near maxTerrainShare. MAP.layout.avgRegion 6 Plots a region aims for: each terrain's count is split into regions about this size. MAP.layout.extraBends River bends beyond MAP.bends, drawn evenly from min to max. MAP.layout.extraBends.min 0 See the note on MAP.layout.extraBends . MAP.layout.extraBends.max 1 See the note on MAP.layout.extraBends . MAP.layout.twinSpacing 8 A region's seed scores its distance to seeds of its own terrain up to this many steps, and to any seed up to seedSpacing. MAP.layout.seedSpacing 4 Explained in the note on MAP.layout.twinSpacing . MAP.layout.crowdWeight 2 Distance to any seed scores this many times distance to a twin's seed: a crowded seed is the one that ends up boxed in. MAP.layout.compactPower 2 A growing region weights a free neighbour by how many of its plots touch it, to this power: higher grows rounder regions. MAP.layout.roomPower 2 A pocket's new region seeds on a plot weighted 1 plus its free neighbours to this power: higher seeds deeper in the pocket. MAP.layout.growPasses 4 Growth steps, per free plot, before the regions stop growing. MAP.layout.refillRounds 3 Rounds of planting new regions in pockets the first growth left. MAP.layout.leftoverPasses 10 Sweeps over the plots no region reached. MAP.layout.repairMoves 120 Most fixes the repair makes to one layout. MAP.layout.sidewaysMoves 30 Of those, how many may leave the layout no better, to step off a dead end. MAP.layout.worseMoves 10 Of those, how many may leave it worse, when nothing better or level is left; the best layout seen is kept. MAP.layout.tabu 6 Those level and worse moves skip plots the last this-many changed plots include, so they can't undo themselves. MAP.layout.lookahead 3 When no single move helps, runs of up to this many moves are searched, keeping the beamWidth least-bad at each step. MAP.layout.beamWidth 12 Explained in the note on MAP.layout.lookahead . MAP.layout.lookaheadEvaluations 20000 Moves that search may measure on one layout, in all, before it gives up. MAP.layout.carveStarts 3 Edge plots of each big patch tried as the start of a carved region. MAP.layout.carveFrom 2 A patch is big enough to carve from at this many times minRegion; under 2, what is left could be a speck."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"map-ground","title":"MAP.ground","status":"","text":"Tuning for the ground stages (map-ground.ts): how the generator meets the rules above, not rules themselves. Setting Value What it does MAP.ground.blobs The smooth fields that rank plots: one random blob per area plots of the box padded by blobPad (at least minBlobs), each a bump of this spread (variance, in plots squared). Smithore's are narrow, so its rich plots gather into seams. MAP.ground.blobs.grown.area 14 See the note on MAP.ground.blobs . MAP.ground.blobs.grown.spread 2.5 See the note on MAP.ground.blobs . MAP.ground.blobs.smithore.area 10 See the note on MAP.ground.blobs . MAP.ground.blobs.smithore.spread 1.3 See the note on MAP.ground.blobs . MAP.ground.minBlobs 3 Explained in the note on MAP.ground.blobs . MAP.ground.blobPad 3 Plots past each edge the blob centres may fall, so the field is as strong at the edges as inside. MAP.ground.fieldNoise 0.15 Random jitter on every plot's field, so a flat stretch still ranks by rng rather than by index. MAP.ground.reachPasses 200 Most repairs the reach stage makes for one good. MAP.ground.seamPasses 4 Sweeps the seam stage makes over the isolated good Smithore plots. MAP.ground.scarcityTries 4000 Most plots the scarcity stage tries, searching for a set to lower that keeps reach and the seams."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"game","title":"GAME","status":"In the game","text":"Setting Value What it does GAME.minPlayers 2 A game seats this many players, from the fewest to the most. GAME.maxPlayers 6 No note of its own; the note on GAME.minPlayers , just before it in the file, may cover it. GAME.maxMonths 12 No note of its own; the note on GAME.minPlayers , just before it in the file, may cover it. GAME.landRushMonths 3 Months the game continues after the last free plot is claimed. GAME.claimsPerGrant 1 Plots each seat may claim in a land grant: one (Heiko, 5 Nov). The corral is sized by MULE.corralPerPlayer, not this. GAME.grantSweeps 1 The land grant is one pass of the light over the free plots, or ends earlier once every claim is used. GAME.plotScore 300 What each plot a player owns adds to their score, before any MULE on it. GAME.exportShipEvery 3 The export ship calls every this many months. GAME.grantTickMs 650 How long each free plot stays lit in the land grant."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"export-ship","title":"EXPORT_SHIP","status":"In the game","text":"Setting Value What it does EXPORT_SHIP.wishlist 3 Goods on the ship's wishlist, announced one month before it calls. EXPORT_SHIP.stepDown 0.02 The ship pays the off-world price for its first unit of a good and this share less for each further unit."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"tax","title":"TAX","status":"In the game","text":"Setting Value What it does TAX.salesTax 0.05 Sales tax on every sale a player makes, into the colony fund. The Governor sets it later; this is the start."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"politics","title":"POLITICS","status":"In the game","text":"Colony politics: the election of the offices in POLITICS.offices, the vote weight, the Governor's sales tax and the offices' pay. No decision has a clock; only the reveal ceremony does, in milliseconds of game time, stepped like the land auction. Setting Value What it does POLITICS.electionMonths [1, 4, 7, 10] Months whose planning phase opens with an election; a term runs until the next one. POLITICS.offices [\"governor\"] The offices elected, in the order the ceremony reveals them; the Sheriff returns with slice 4's powers. POLITICS.maxGovernorTermsInRow 2 A Governor who has served this many terms in a row may not run for Governor again until someone else has held it. POLITICS.tax The sales tax the Governor may set, in whole percent, and how far it may move from the month's starting rate. POLITICS.tax.min 0 See the note on POLITICS.tax . POLITICS.tax.max 15 See the note on POLITICS.tax . POLITICS.tax.maxStep 5 See the note on POLITICS.tax . POLITICS.pay Paid from the colony fund to each officeholder at upkeep, as far as the fund reaches. POLITICS.pay.governor 50 See the note on POLITICS.pay . POLITICS.pay.sheriff 30 See the note on POLITICS.pay . POLITICS.termScore 1200 Worth per term served in either office: about one good month's income, the median monthly Worth gain of a top-half player with politics on (1,150-1,222 on the sim's three sets, 28 Oct). No AI choice and no sim check reads it; it moves only who wins. POLITICS.council The law ballot. Its only clock is the reveal's, a presentation; the ballot itself waits for every player. POLITICS.council.revealMs 4000 Ms the result is shown before a passed law takes effect. POLITICS.council.maxInForce 3 Laws in force at once; a fourth must name the one it replaces. POLITICS.council.retryMonths 2 Months a failed proposal waits before it may be put again."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"politics-ceremony","title":"POLITICS.ceremony","status":"","text":"The reveal ceremony's beats on its one show clock, from WS6's approved mock (ElectionReveal.tsx). Each office in turn: its title, its candidates, its vote blocks one at a time, a tie if one was broken, then its winner. Setting Value What it does POLITICS.ceremony.leadMs 300 Before the first office's title. POLITICS.ceremony.titleMs 1800 The office's title, before its candidates. POLITICS.ceremony.candidateMs 500 Each candidate popping in, in seat order. POLITICS.ceremony.barsLeadMs 500 After the last candidate, before the first vote block. POLITICS.ceremony.blockMs 550 Each vote block, one per weighted vote, interleaved across the candidates. POLITICS.ceremony.tieLeadMs 400 After the last block, before a tie is stamped. POLITICS.ceremony.tieMs 2600 The tie and the rule that broke it, before the winner. POLITICS.ceremony.winnerLeadMs 700 After the last block, before the winner, when there was no tie. POLITICS.ceremony.winnerMs 3800 The winner and the congratulations, before the next office. POLITICS.ceremony.summaryMs 1500 The whole result held after the last office, before the winners take office."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"politics-weight","title":"POLITICS.weight","status":"","text":"Setting Value What it does POLITICS.weight.base 1 Votes every player has, before the parts below. POLITICS.weight.plotsPerVote 10 One extra vote per this many plots owned. POLITICS.weight.repHigh 70 Reputation at or above this adds a vote. POLITICS.weight.repLow 30 Reputation below this takes a vote away. POLITICS.weight.repVotes 1 Votes a high reputation adds, and a low one takes away. POLITICS.weight.baronVotes 1 Extra votes for the month's Land Baron. POLITICS.weight.taxPerVote 300 One extra vote per this much sales tax paid over the game, up to taxCap votes. POLITICS.weight.taxCap 2 Explained in the note on POLITICS.weight.taxPerVote ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"politics-laws","title":"POLITICS.laws","status":"","text":"The colony's book of laws, each with its settings. Setting Value What it does POLITICS.laws.exportTax Per cent of each unit a player sells to the export ship. POLITICS.laws.exportTax.settings [10, 20, 30] See the note on POLITICS.laws.exportTax . POLITICS.laws.landTax Credits a plot owned, at each month end. POLITICS.laws.landTax.settings [5, 10, 20] See the note on POLITICS.laws.landTax . POLITICS.laws.pollutionTax Credits per point of colony pollution a player caused that month, at its end. POLITICS.laws.pollutionTax.settings [10, 20, 40] See the note on POLITICS.laws.pollutionTax . POLITICS.laws.emissionCap The air level above which no new mine or smelter of the listed outfits may be outfitted. POLITICS.laws.emissionCap.settings [40, 50, 60] See the note on POLITICS.laws.emissionCap . POLITICS.laws.emissionCap.blocks [\"rareEarth\", \"alloy\"] See the note on POLITICS.laws.emissionCap ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"politics-ai","title":"POLITICS.ai","status":"","text":"Setting Value What it does POLITICS.ai.actMs [800, 2500] Ms a seat waits before acting in announce or the ballot, drawn per stage: a human's pause. POLITICS.ai.declareLeadMonths 1 A seat decides whether to run in the first planning phase at most this many months before an election month; in month 1, at the last call. POLITICS.ai.runGovernor Chance per election that a persona runs for Governor. POLITICS.ai.runGovernor.trader 0.5 See the note on POLITICS.ai.runGovernor . POLITICS.ai.runGovernor.cautious 0.3 See the note on POLITICS.ai.runGovernor . POLITICS.ai.runGovernor.aggressive 0.8 See the note on POLITICS.ai.runGovernor . POLITICS.ai.runGovernor.saboteur 0.6 See the note on POLITICS.ai.runGovernor . POLITICS.ai.runSheriff Chance it runs for Sheriff when it does not run for Governor. POLITICS.ai.runSheriff.trader 0.3 See the note on POLITICS.ai.runSheriff . POLITICS.ai.runSheriff.cautious 0.4 See the note on POLITICS.ai.runSheriff . POLITICS.ai.runSheriff.aggressive 0.3 See the note on POLITICS.ai.runSheriff . POLITICS.ai.runSheriff.saboteur 0.7 See the note on POLITICS.ai.runSheriff . POLITICS.ai.taxTarget The sales tax in whole percent a persona steers toward as Governor. POLITICS.ai.taxTarget.trader 5 See the note on POLITICS.ai.taxTarget . POLITICS.ai.taxTarget.cautious 4 See the note on POLITICS.ai.taxTarget . POLITICS.ai.taxTarget.aggressive 8 See the note on POLITICS.ai.taxTarget . POLITICS.ai.taxTarget.saboteur 10 See the note on POLITICS.ai.taxTarget . POLITICS.ai.taxFundBump 2 Whole percent a Governor adds when the colony fund is low. POLITICS.ai.fundMonths 3 The fund is low under this many months of the elected offices' pay. POLITICS.ai.council The AI in the council. Placeholders until the sim tunes them. POLITICS.ai.council.minGap 20 Credits a month a law must cost the leader more than the AI Governor before it proposes it. POLITICS.ai.council.proposeChance Chance an AI Governor proposes its best law in a month it has one, per persona. POLITICS.ai.council.proposeChance.trader 0.5 See the note on POLITICS.ai.council.proposeChance . POLITICS.ai.council.proposeChance.cautious 0.3 See the note on POLITICS.ai.council.proposeChance . POLITICS.ai.council.proposeChance.aggressive 0.7 See the note on POLITICS.ai.council.proposeChance . POLITICS.ai.council.proposeChance.saboteur 0.6 See the note on POLITICS.ai.council.proposeChance . POLITICS.ai.council.capPlotCost 40 Credits a month an AI counts for each of its plots an emission cap would stop it re-outfitting. POLITICS.ai.council.rookieYes 0.5 A Rookie's chance of a yes on a law ballot: a coin from the council's own stream. POLITICS.ai.colony The AI Governor's colony acts and the mood-weighed vote (G-5 C25). Placeholders until the sim tunes them. POLITICS.ai.colony.commissions Whether an AI Governor of each persona commissions a prestige object the fund can pay for. POLITICS.ai.colony.commissions.trader true See the note on POLITICS.ai.colony.commissions . POLITICS.ai.colony.commissions.cautious true See the note on POLITICS.ai.colony.commissions . POLITICS.ai.colony.commissions.aggressive true See the note on POLITICS.ai.colony.commissions . POLITICS.ai.colony.commissions.saboteur false See the note on POLITICS.ai.colony.commissions . POLITICS.ai.colony.paysStrike Whether an AI Governor of each persona pays general strikers the fund can pay. POLITICS.ai.colony.paysStrike.trader true See the note on POLITICS.ai.colony.paysStrike . POLITICS.ai.colony.paysStrike.cautious true See the note on POLITICS.ai.colony.paysStrike . POLITICS.ai.colony.paysStrike.aggressive false See the note on POLITICS.ai.colony.paysStrike . POLITICS.ai.colony.paysStrike.saboteur false See the note on POLITICS.ai.colony.paysStrike ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"politics-ai-part-2","title":"POLITICS.ai , continued","status":"","text":"Setting Value What it does POLITICS.ai.colony.repairs Whether an AI Governor of each persona repairs a damaged object the fund can pay for (AM-10). POLITICS.ai.colony.repairs.trader true See the note on POLITICS.ai.colony.repairs . POLITICS.ai.colony.repairs.cautious true See the note on POLITICS.ai.colony.repairs . POLITICS.ai.colony.repairs.aggressive true See the note on POLITICS.ai.colony.repairs . POLITICS.ai.colony.repairs.saboteur false See the note on POLITICS.ai.colony.repairs . POLITICS.ai.colony.siteBid An AI Governor's top bid, in credits, for an object's site at the land auction (RULE-28, AM-10); null, it never bids, so it commissions only objects that need no plot. The engine caps the bid at the fund over its reserve. POLITICS.ai.colony.siteBid.trader 400 See the note on POLITICS.ai.colony.siteBid . POLITICS.ai.colony.siteBid.cautious 300 See the note on POLITICS.ai.colony.siteBid . POLITICS.ai.colony.siteBid.aggressive 500 See the note on POLITICS.ai.colony.siteBid . POLITICS.ai.colony.siteBid.saboteur null See the note on POLITICS.ai.colony.siteBid . POLITICS.ai.colony.leanIn [\"unhappy\", \"unrest\"] In these mood bands an AI voter will not vote for the sitting Governor if anyone else runs."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"prestige","title":"PRESTIGE","status":"In the game","text":"The colony as a shared project: its prestige objects, one table row each. Every number is a placeholder until the colony's sim tuning sets it and Heiko OKs it. An object's full cost is its credits plus its goods at their reference prices; upkeep and repair are in credits."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"prestige-park","title":"PRESTIGE.park","status":"","text":"Setting Value What it does PRESTIGE.park.name \"Park\" See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.park.credits 420 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.park.goods.alloy 2 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.park.buildMonths 1 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.park.needsPlot true See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.park.prestige 2 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.park.upkeep 30 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.park.repairCost 150 See the note on PRESTIGE ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"prestige-hospital","title":"PRESTIGE.hospital","status":"","text":"Setting Value What it does PRESTIGE.hospital.name \"Hospital\" See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.hospital.credits 680 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.hospital.goods.electronics 1 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.hospital.buildMonths 2 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.hospital.needsPlot false See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.hospital.prestige 3 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.hospital.upkeep 45 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.hospital.repairCost 225 See the note on PRESTIGE ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"prestige-observatory","title":"PRESTIGE.observatory","status":"","text":"Setting Value What it does PRESTIGE.observatory.name \"Observatory\" See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.observatory.credits 700 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.observatory.goods.crystite 3 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.observatory.buildMonths 2 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.observatory.needsPlot false See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.observatory.prestige 3 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.observatory.upkeep 50 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.observatory.repairCost 250 See the note on PRESTIGE ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"prestige-stadium","title":"PRESTIGE.stadium","status":"","text":"Setting Value What it does PRESTIGE.stadium.name \"Stadium\" See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.stadium.credits 1000 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.stadium.goods.alloy 2 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.stadium.goods.electronics 1 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.stadium.buildMonths 3 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.stadium.needsPlot true See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.stadium.prestige 4 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.stadium.upkeep 70 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.stadium.repairCost 350 See the note on PRESTIGE ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"prestige-spaceport","title":"PRESTIGE.spaceport","status":"","text":"Setting Value What it does PRESTIGE.spaceport.name \"Spaceport upgrade\" See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.spaceport.credits 1380 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.spaceport.goods.alloy 2 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.spaceport.goods.electronics 2 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.spaceport.buildMonths 3 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.spaceport.needsPlot true See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.spaceport.prestige 5 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.spaceport.upkeep 100 See the note on PRESTIGE . PRESTIGE.spaceport.repairCost 500 See the note on PRESTIGE ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"happiness","title":"HAPPINESS","status":"In the game","text":"The colony's mood (G-5 RULE-20). Every number is a placeholder, tuned at the end (HC-25). Each month the mood moves toward a target by at most maxStep ; an event's moodShift moves it at once. Setting Value What it does HAPPINESS.start 60 Where a new colony's mood starts, 0..100. HAPPINESS.base 60 The target of a month with nothing wrong and nothing standing. HAPPINESS.maxStep 5 The most the mood moves toward its target in one month. HAPPINESS.bands The least happiness of each band; below unhappy is Unrest. HAPPINESS.bands.thriving 80 See the note on HAPPINESS.bands . HAPPINESS.bands.content 60 See the note on HAPPINESS.bands . HAPPINESS.bands.restless 40 See the note on HAPPINESS.bands . HAPPINESS.bands.unhappy 20 See the note on HAPPINESS.bands ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"happiness-weights","title":"HAPPINESS.weights","status":"","text":"Setting Value What it does HAPPINESS.weights.shortage 8 Off the target for each good the store ran short of this upkeep. HAPPINESS.weights.air 0.4 Off the target for each point of air above airFree . HAPPINESS.weights.airFree 30 Explained in the note on HAPPINESS.weights.air . HAPPINESS.weights.tax 2 Off the target for each whole percent of sales tax above taxFree . HAPPINESS.weights.taxFree 5 Explained in the note on HAPPINESS.weights.tax . HAPPINESS.weights.broken 10 Off the target in a month the Governor broke their tax promise. HAPPINESS.weights.prestige 2 Onto the target for each point of prestige standing, up to prestigeCap points. HAPPINESS.weights.prestigeCap 15 Explained in the note on HAPPINESS.weights.prestige . HAPPINESS.weights.gap 2 Off the target for each gapUnit credits between the richest and poorest player, up to gapCap units. HAPPINESS.weights.gapUnit 1000 Explained in the note on HAPPINESS.weights.gap . HAPPINESS.weights.gapCap 10 Explained in the note on HAPPINESS.weights.gap ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"colony","title":"COLONY","status":"In the game","text":"The colony fund's own rules. Placeholders until the colony's sim tuning. Setting Value What it does COLONY.maxOpen 1 Projects commissioned or building at the same time. COLONY.reserve 100 Credits the fund keeps back from a project's funding, so next month's office pay is there. COLONY.fundBuysGoods true The fund buys an object's missing goods from the store at the store's price; off, only donors' goods fund it. COLONY.grant The Federation's grant into the fund at each upkeep, in credits; flat until a scaling rule is tuned. COLONY.grant.perMonth 150 See the note on COLONY.grant . COLONY.strike What the strikers of a general strike want from the fund to go back to work, in credits (G-5 RULE-26). COLONY.strike.cost 250 See the note on COLONY.strike ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"colony-score","title":"COLONY_SCORE","status":"In the game","text":"The colony score at game over (G-5 RULE-25): each term turned into credits, so they add. Placeholders, tuned at the end (HC-25). Setting Value What it does COLONY_SCORE.perCleanPoint 100 Credits for each point the air is cleaner than POLLUTION.airMax. COLONY_SCORE.perPrestige 1000 Credits for each point of the colony's prestige. COLONY_SCORE.factor The happiness factor: low at happiness 0, high at 100, straight between. COLONY_SCORE.factor.low 0.8 See the note on COLONY_SCORE.factor . COLONY_SCORE.factor.high 1.2 See the note on COLONY_SCORE.factor ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"walk","title":"WALK","status":"In the game","text":"The goods auction, played live as in the original: goods come up one at a time, each with a status screen and then a walk. Times are in milliseconds of game time; the server steps the auction on a fixed tick. Setting Value What it does WALK.order [\"food\", \"water\", \"energy\", \"smithore\", \"crystite\", \"rareEarth\", \"alloy\", \"electronics\", \"medicine\"] The order goods come up in: raw goods first, then processed ones. WALK.statusMs null The status screen before each good: what everyone had, used, made and is short of, and where each player picks a side for the walk. null: no clock (A-7, G-7), it waits for every player's Done or the host's Continue; a number restores a timer. WALK.goodMs 20000 The walk's clock for one good. WALK.closeMs 2500 The close stage after a good's walk: each phone shows its receipt for the good (UX audit G-5), as the land auction's result does. Display and game time only. WALK.tradeClockRate 0.25 While any unit is changing hands the clock runs at this share of its speed, as in the original. WALK.tradeStartMs 400 While a buyer and a seller (or a player and the store or the ship) stay matched, the first unit takes tradeStartMs, each further one tradeSpeedUp times as long as the one before, down to tradeFloorMs, so big lots don't take ages. The speed-up starts again when the pair separates. WALK.tradeSpeedUp 0.8 Explained in the note on WALK.tradeStartMs . WALK.tradeFloorMs 60 Explained in the note on WALK.tradeStartMs . WALK.crossSeconds 6 Walking speed in price units per second, set when a good comes up: its board's starting height divided by this, so crossing the board takes this many seconds whatever the good costs. Never under minSpeed. WALK.minSpeed 2 Explained in the note on WALK.crossSeconds . WALK.boardMargin 0.3 The board reaches this share beyond the outermost price line; sellers start at its top, buyers at its bottom. WALK.boardPostedMargin 0.1 And this share of the posted price more, so a board whose price lines sit close together still has room to walk. WALK.tickMs 100 How often the server steps the auction."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"lots","title":"LOTS","status":"In the game","text":"The land auction, after the land grant: plots and MULEs players listed in planning come up one at a time, each with an intro, a walk and a result. The walk reuses WALK's speed and tick; the highest bid at the bell wins. Setting Value What it does LOTS.perPlayer 2 Lots one player may list in a month. LOTS.maxLotsPerMonth 4 Lots the land auction takes in a month, from all sellers together. LOTS.introMs 3000 The intro: the lot lit on the map with its seller and minimum. LOTS.walkMs 12000 The walk's clock for one lot. LOTS.resultMs 2500 The result on screen before the next lot comes up. LOTS.boardLow 0.7 The board runs from this share of the minimum (where buyers start) to boardHigh's. LOTS.boardHigh 2 Explained in the note on LOTS.boardLow . LOTS.fastClimb 2 Past the board's top the walk speeds up by this much for the rest of the lot, so a contested lot can reach its runner-up's price before the bell. LOTS.lateMs 2000 A change of leader with less than this left on the walk clock sets it back to this, so the bell doesn't decide who wins. LOTS.lateCapMs 6000 The most those resets may add to one lot's walk. LOTS.storePlotShare 2 / 3 ≈ 0.6667 What the store pays for an unsold plot, as a share of GAME.plotScore, so it can't drift past the score. LOTS.storeMuleShare 0.5 What the store pays for an unsold MULE, as a share of this month's MULE price. LOTS.colonyPlotChance 0.1 The chance, each month, that the colony puts one free plot up after the land grant. Heiko's range: 1/10 to 1/8. LOTS.colonyPlotShare 2 / 3 ≈ 0.6667 The colony plot's minimum, as a share of GAME.plotScore: what the store would pay for land. LOTS.aiIntroMs [400, 1200] An AI seat presses Done on a lot's intro after this long. LOTS.aiStartMs [300, 1200] An AI seat first moves this long after a lot's walk starts, scaled by its patience. LOTS.aiOvershoot [0.1, 0.35] An AI's overtake goes past the leader by this share of the gap to its limit, drawn per overtake."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"phases","title":"PHASES","status":"In the game","text":"The longest each timed phase lasts, in milliseconds at phaseScale 1. Planning waits for every human's Ready, a dropped phone's too, unless the host continues. Other phases also end early: when every connected player is ready, the land grant after its one pass or once the claims are used, development when everyone is out of time or at the pub. The goods auction and the land auction have no fixed length; they run on WALK's and LOTS' clocks. Setting Value What it does PHASES.planningMs null Planning: the hub, listings, and in election months the election first. null: planning waits for every player's Ready or the host's Continue; a number restores a timer. PHASES.landGrantMs null The land grant: the light passes once over the free plots, GAME.grantTickMs a plot. null: no clock; it ends after the pass or once every claim is used. A number restores a timer. PHASES.grantFastForwardMs GAME.grantTickMs / 8 = 81.25 The land grant's fast-forward: once every human has used their claims or is done with land, the AI seats' presses resolve at once at each lit plot, and the light moves on after this display step, so it races across the TV. Like the grant's tick, phaseScale never changes it. PHASES.upkeepMs null Upkeep, the month end: colonists eat and goods spoil, and the table reads the league table. null: it waits for every player's Ready (\"On to month N\") or the host's Continue; a number restores a timer."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"event-show","title":"EVENT_SHOW","status":"In the game","text":"How long the production show plays each month event, and how far production stretches to fit them. Setting Value What it does EVENT_SHOW.playMs How long one headline or spotlight plays, in ms. EVENT_SHOW.playMs.headline 7000 See the note on EVENT_SHOW.playMs . EVENT_SHOW.playMs.spotlight 5200 See the note on EVENT_SHOW.playMs . EVENT_SHOW.introMs 1800 How long the intro (the phase's opening card of what fired) plays before the first event, in ms; none in a quiet month. EVENT_SHOW.rollCallMs 4000 How long the one roll call that every bulletin of a phase shares plays, in ms. EVENT_SHOW.capMs 30000 The most the events of one phase may play in all, in ms; past it each is cut in proportion."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"event-show-weight","title":"EVENT_SHOW.weight","status":"","text":"How big each event's story is on the TV; a key missing here plays as a headline. Setting Value What it does EVENT_SHOW.weight.dustStorm \"headline\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight . EVENT_SHOW.weight.drought \"headline\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight . EVENT_SHOW.weight.acidRain \"headline\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight . EVENT_SHOW.weight.solarFlare \"headline\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight . EVENT_SHOW.weight.cometPass \"headline\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight . EVENT_SHOW.weight.flood \"headline\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight . EVENT_SHOW.weight.volcanicAsh \"headline\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight . EVENT_SHOW.weight.pestSwarm \"spotlight\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight . EVENT_SHOW.weight.wildfire \"spotlight\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight . EVENT_SHOW.weight.bumperHarvest \"spotlight\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight . EVENT_SHOW.weight.cavernCollapse \"spotlight\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight . EVENT_SHOW.weight.federationTax \"headline\" Wave 2, as the approved gallery sizes them (design/mocks/ws8-events, catalogue.ts tier ). EVENT_SHOW.weight.federationGrant \"headline\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight . EVENT_SHOW.weight.immigrationWave \"headline\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight . EVENT_SHOW.weight.inheritance \"bulletin\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight . EVENT_SHOW.weight.muleTheft \"spotlight\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight . EVENT_SHOW.weight.epidemic \"headline\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight . EVENT_SHOW.weight.coldSnap \"headline\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight . EVENT_SHOW.weight.meteorStrike \"headline\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight . EVENT_SHOW.weight.pirateRaid \"headline\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight . EVENT_SHOW.weight.storeFire \"headline\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight . EVENT_SHOW.weight.ratsInSilo \"bulletin\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight . EVENT_SHOW.weight.crookedAccountant \"bulletin\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight . EVENT_SHOW.weight.packageFromHome \"bulletin\" See the note on EVENT_SHOW.weight ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"production-show","title":"PRODUCTION_SHOW","status":"In the game","text":"The production show (ws6-ux spec AM-19, RULE-3; Heiko's order C, 2026-11-05): good by good for every player at once, then every bonus at once, then every loss at once, then the month's events, then the goods fly into the silos. The timeline is packages/engine/src/production-show.ts; its length is production's length, so it grows with the goods made, never the plots. Named edit in WS1's config, values WS6's (approved in the mock, b65c3972). Setting Value What it does PRODUCTION_SHOW.order [\"food\", \"water\", \"energy\", \"smithore\", \"crystite\", \"rareEarth\", \"alloy\", \"electronics\", \"medicine\"] The order the goods harvest in: the crops, then the mines, then the factories. PRODUCTION_SHOW.introMs 1000 Before the first good lights. PRODUCTION_SHOW.goodBeatMs 1100 One good's turn: every plot of it lights and counts up together. PRODUCTION_SHOW.unitStepMs 140 One unit of a plot's base rising, so a 4-unit plot counts up as you watch. PRODUCTION_SHOW.allAtOnceMs 1600 Every bonus at once, then every loss at once: each held this long. PRODUCTION_SHOW.gapMs 500 Before the bonuses, before the events and before the flight. PRODUCTION_SHOW.flightsMs 2400 The plots take off for the silos over this long, the first gaps longest ( flightEase below 1). PRODUCTION_SHOW.flightEase 0.7 Explained in the note on PRODUCTION_SHOW.flightsMs . PRODUCTION_SHOW.flightMs 750 One plot's goods in the air. PRODUCTION_SHOW.holdMs 2000 The silos stand full to be read before the phase ends. PRODUCTION_SHOW.fast 3 Once every person at the table has asked, the rest of the show plays this many times faster."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"ai-personas","title":"AI_PERSONAS","status":"In the game","text":""},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"ai-personas-cautious","title":"AI_PERSONAS.cautious","status":"","text":"Setting Value What it does AI_PERSONAS.cautious.risk 1.6 Dislike of uncertain bets (unanalysed ground, thin markets). 1 is neutral; higher avoids risk. AI_PERSONAS.cautious.greed 0.8 Weight on money made. Higher chases bigger but less certain gains. AI_PERSONAS.cautious.reserve 1.6 Multiplies the cash and the Food and Energy it keeps back for next month. AI_PERSONAS.cautious.spite 0 Weight on hurting the leader: blocking their land, outbidding them for what they need. 0 never. AI_PERSONAS.cautious.speculate 0.3 Willingness to buy now to sell next month. 0 never. AI_PERSONAS.cautious.corner 0 Willingness to buy up a scarce good the others are short of, to sell it to them dear. 0 never. AI_PERSONAS.cautious.patience 1.4 Auction patience: higher waits longer for the other side to come to it."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"ai-personas-aggressive","title":"AI_PERSONAS.aggressive","status":"","text":"Setting Value What it does AI_PERSONAS.aggressive.risk 0.6 Dislike of uncertain bets (unanalysed ground, thin markets). 1 is neutral; higher avoids risk. AI_PERSONAS.aggressive.greed 1.4 Weight on money made. Higher chases bigger but less certain gains. AI_PERSONAS.aggressive.reserve 0.7 Multiplies the cash and the Food and Energy it keeps back for next month. AI_PERSONAS.aggressive.spite 0.3 Weight on hurting the leader: blocking their land, outbidding them for what they need. 0 never. AI_PERSONAS.aggressive.speculate 1 Willingness to buy now to sell next month. 0 never. AI_PERSONAS.aggressive.corner 1 Willingness to buy up a scarce good the others are short of, to sell it to them dear. 0 never. AI_PERSONAS.aggressive.patience 0.6 Auction patience: higher waits longer for the other side to come to it."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"ai-personas-trader","title":"AI_PERSONAS.trader","status":"","text":"Setting Value What it does AI_PERSONAS.trader.risk 1 Dislike of uncertain bets (unanalysed ground, thin markets). 1 is neutral; higher avoids risk. AI_PERSONAS.trader.greed 1.1 Weight on money made. Higher chases bigger but less certain gains. AI_PERSONAS.trader.reserve 1 Multiplies the cash and the Food and Energy it keeps back for next month. AI_PERSONAS.trader.spite 0 Weight on hurting the leader: blocking their land, outbidding them for what they need. 0 never. AI_PERSONAS.trader.speculate 1.4 Willingness to buy now to sell next month. 0 never. AI_PERSONAS.trader.corner 0.4 Willingness to buy up a scarce good the others are short of, to sell it to them dear. 0 never. AI_PERSONAS.trader.patience 1 Auction patience: higher waits longer for the other side to come to it."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"ai-personas-saboteur","title":"AI_PERSONAS.saboteur","status":"","text":"Setting Value What it does AI_PERSONAS.saboteur.risk 0.9 Dislike of uncertain bets (unanalysed ground, thin markets). 1 is neutral; higher avoids risk. AI_PERSONAS.saboteur.greed 0.9 Weight on money made. Higher chases bigger but less certain gains. AI_PERSONAS.saboteur.reserve 1 Multiplies the cash and the Food and Energy it keeps back for next month. AI_PERSONAS.saboteur.spite 1.5 Weight on hurting the leader: blocking their land, outbidding them for what they need. 0 never. AI_PERSONAS.saboteur.speculate 0.6 Willingness to buy now to sell next month. 0 never. AI_PERSONAS.saboteur.corner 0.6 Willingness to buy up a scarce good the others are short of, to sell it to them dear. 0 never. AI_PERSONAS.saboteur.patience 0.9 Auction patience: higher waits longer for the other side to come to it."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"ai-levels","title":"AI_LEVELS","status":"In the game","text":""},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"ai-levels-rookie","title":"AI_LEVELS.rookie","status":"","text":"Setting Value What it does AI_LEVELS.rookie.lookAhead false Prices from the engine's own look-ahead (next month's production, upkeep and spoilage); without it, today's posted prices. AI_LEVELS.rookie.fullScoring false Counts spoilage, the depot limit and the air in its values. AI_LEVELS.rookie.shortlist 3 Options handed to the decider, at most. AI_LEVELS.rookie.shortlistTolerance 0.35 An option reaches the shortlist only within this share of the best utility. AI_LEVELS.rookie.pickTolerance 0.25 The default decider picks among options within this share of the best, in proportion to utility. AI_LEVELS.rookie.reactMs [320, 550] Reaction time in ms, drawn per reaction. AI_LEVELS.rookie.devPace 1.4 Multiplies the hands' development step times. 1 is a quick human. AI_LEVELS.rookie.mulesPerMonth 1 MULEs it leads out of the store in one month, at most. AI_LEVELS.rookie.missChance 0.25 Chance it misses a plot it wanted in the land grant. AI_LEVELS.rookie.corners false Allowed to corner a good (a monopoly try). Rookie never does. AI_LEVELS.rookie.sabotage false Allowed to act on spite against the leader. Rookie never does."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"ai-levels-trader","title":"AI_LEVELS.trader","status":"","text":"Setting Value What it does AI_LEVELS.trader.lookAhead true Prices from the engine's own look-ahead (next month's production, upkeep and spoilage); without it, today's posted prices. AI_LEVELS.trader.fullScoring true Counts spoilage, the depot limit and the air in its values. AI_LEVELS.trader.shortlist 4 Options handed to the decider, at most. AI_LEVELS.trader.shortlistTolerance 0.2 An option reaches the shortlist only within this share of the best utility. AI_LEVELS.trader.pickTolerance 0.1 The default decider picks among options within this share of the best, in proportion to utility. AI_LEVELS.trader.reactMs [250, 400] Reaction time in ms, drawn per reaction. AI_LEVELS.trader.devPace 1.1 Multiplies the hands' development step times. 1 is a quick human. AI_LEVELS.trader.mulesPerMonth 2 MULEs it leads out of the store in one month, at most. AI_LEVELS.trader.missChance 0.05 Chance it misses a plot it wanted in the land grant. AI_LEVELS.trader.corners true Allowed to corner a good (a monopoly try). Rookie never does. AI_LEVELS.trader.sabotage true Allowed to act on spite against the leader. Rookie never does."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"ai-levels-tycoon","title":"AI_LEVELS.tycoon","status":"","text":"Setting Value What it does AI_LEVELS.tycoon.lookAhead true Prices from the engine's own look-ahead (next month's production, upkeep and spoilage); without it, today's posted prices. AI_LEVELS.tycoon.fullScoring true Counts spoilage, the depot limit and the air in its values. AI_LEVELS.tycoon.shortlist 5 Options handed to the decider, at most. AI_LEVELS.tycoon.shortlistTolerance 0.12 An option reaches the shortlist only within this share of the best utility. AI_LEVELS.tycoon.pickTolerance 0.04 The default decider picks among options within this share of the best, in proportion to utility. AI_LEVELS.tycoon.reactMs [200, 320] Reaction time in ms, drawn per reaction. AI_LEVELS.tycoon.devPace 1 Multiplies the hands' development step times. 1 is a quick human. AI_LEVELS.tycoon.mulesPerMonth 3 MULEs it leads out of the store in one month, at most. AI_LEVELS.tycoon.missChance 0 Chance it misses a plot it wanted in the land grant. AI_LEVELS.tycoon.corners true Allowed to corner a good (a monopoly try). Rookie never does. AI_LEVELS.tycoon.sabotage true Allowed to act on spite against the leader. Rookie never does."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"events","title":"EVENTS","status":"In the game","text":"Events: each row of the table below rolls on its own seeded dice, so several can fire in one phase and their effects stack on the same plots. Each event's effect numbers sit under its key. Setting Value What it does EVENTS.firstMonth 2 No event before this month: there is nothing developed to hit. EVENTS.dustStorm Share of an Energy plot's output a dust storm takes. EVENTS.dustStorm.cut 0.5 See the note on EVENTS.dustStorm . EVENTS.drought Food and Water each Food and Water plot loses in a drought. EVENTS.drought.loss 1 See the note on EVENTS.drought . EVENTS.acidRain Energy and Food each such plot loses to acid rain. EVENTS.acidRain.loss 1 See the note on EVENTS.acidRain . EVENTS.solarFlare Energy each Energy plot gains from a solar flare. EVENTS.solarFlare.gain 2 See the note on EVENTS.solarFlare . EVENTS.cometPass Water each Water plot gains from a comet pass. EVENTS.cometPass.gain 2 See the note on EVENTS.cometPass . EVENTS.volcanicAsh Plots within this many steps of the origin lose this much of their good to ash. EVENTS.volcanicAsh.radius 2 See the note on EVENTS.volcanicAsh . EVENTS.volcanicAsh.loss 1 See the note on EVENTS.volcanicAsh . EVENTS.pestSwarm Share of a Food plot's output pests eat, and the most plots a swarm reaches. EVENTS.pestSwarm.cut 0.5 See the note on EVENTS.pestSwarm . EVENTS.pestSwarm.maxPlots 4 See the note on EVENTS.pestSwarm . EVENTS.wildfire The burnt plot makes nothing; the rest of its owner's Food cluster loses this much. EVENTS.wildfire.loss 1 See the note on EVENTS.wildfire . EVENTS.bumperHarvest How many times its usual output the bumper plot makes. EVENTS.bumperHarvest.factor 2 See the note on EVENTS.bumperHarvest . EVENTS.excludes Pairs that never fire together in one phase: if both roll, the seeded dice keep one. EVENTS.excludes[0] [\"drought\", \"flood\"] See the note on EVENTS.excludes . EVENTS.excludes[1] [\"dustStorm\", \"solarFlare\"] See the note on EVENTS.excludes ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"events-colony","title":"EVENTS.colony","status":"","text":"The event table (Heiko's event model): one row per event, its fields an EventRow (events.ts), every number a share. A row rolls at the start of each phase it lists, or, with perPlot, once per plot its caller names after production's output: that phase's chance plus its raises, minus its lowers, never below 0 or above its cap. Colony rows roll once for the colony, first; player rows once a player. Setting Value What it does EVENTS.colony.dustStorm.phases.production 0.08 See the note on EVENTS.colony . EVENTS.colony.dustStorm.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS.colony . EVENTS.colony.drought.phases.production 0.08 See the note on EVENTS.colony . EVENTS.colony.drought.air By the colony air's band. EVENTS.colony.drought.air.toxic 0.02 See the note on EVENTS.colony.drought.air . EVENTS.colony.drought.air.collapse 0.04 See the note on EVENTS.colony.drought.air . EVENTS.colony.drought.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS.colony . EVENTS.colony.acidRain.phases.production 0.016 See the note on EVENTS.colony . EVENTS.colony.acidRain.air By the colony air's band. EVENTS.colony.acidRain.air.smoggy 0.04 See the note on EVENTS.colony.acidRain.air . EVENTS.colony.acidRain.air.toxic 0.08 See the note on EVENTS.colony.acidRain.air . EVENTS.colony.acidRain.air.collapse 0.12 See the note on EVENTS.colony.acidRain.air . EVENTS.colony.acidRain.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS.colony . EVENTS.colony.solarFlare.phases.production 0.08 See the note on EVENTS.colony . EVENTS.colony.solarFlare.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS.colony . EVENTS.colony.cometPass.phases.production 0.08 See the note on EVENTS.colony . EVENTS.colony.cometPass.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS.colony . EVENTS.colony.flood.phases.production 0.08 See the note on EVENTS.colony . EVENTS.colony.flood.follows By event key: points when that event fired in a window the row names; once, if it fired in both. EVENTS.colony.flood.follows.drought.points 0.1 See the note on EVENTS.colony.flood.follows . EVENTS.colony.flood.follows.drought.lastMonth true See the note on EVENTS.colony.flood.follows . EVENTS.colony.flood.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS.colony . EVENTS.colony.volcanicAsh.phases.production 0.064 See the note on EVENTS.colony . EVENTS.colony.volcanicAsh.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS.colony . EVENTS.colony.cavernCollapse Rolls once for each crystal cavern plot that produced Crystite this month, after production's output, never at the phase start; a collapse leaves a plain plot. EVENTS.colony.cavernCollapse.phases.production 0.06 See the note on EVENTS.colony.cavernCollapse . EVENTS.colony.cavernCollapse.perPlot true Rolls once per plot its caller names, after production's output; rollPhase never rolls it. EVENTS.colony.cavernCollapse.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS.colony.cavernCollapse . EVENTS.colony.federationTax Share of each player's cash the Federation takes into the colony fund (floored). EVENTS.colony.federationTax.phases.planning 0.02 See the note on EVENTS.colony.federationTax . EVENTS.colony.federationTax.share 0.1 See the note on EVENTS.colony.federationTax . EVENTS.colony.federationTax.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS.colony.federationTax . EVENTS.colony.federationGrant Credits the Federation pays each of the count poorest players, new money. EVENTS.colony.federationGrant.phases.planning 0.02 See the note on EVENTS.colony.federationGrant . EVENTS.colony.federationGrant.amount 150 See the note on EVENTS.colony.federationGrant . EVENTS.colony.federationGrant.count 2 See the note on EVENTS.colony.federationGrant . EVENTS.colony.federationGrant.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS.colony.federationGrant ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"events-colony-part-2","title":"EVENTS.colony , continued","status":"","text":"Setting Value What it does EVENTS.colony.immigrationWave The colonists grow by this share, for good (rounded). EVENTS.colony.immigrationWave.phases.planning 0.02 See the note on EVENTS.colony.immigrationWave . EVENTS.colony.immigrationWave.growth 0.1 See the note on EVENTS.colony.immigrationWave . EVENTS.colony.immigrationWave.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS.colony.immigrationWave . EVENTS.colony.epidemic Every player without a Medicine is sick this development and the next; one with a Medicine uses it. EVENTS.colony.epidemic.phases.development 0.005 See the note on EVENTS.colony.epidemic . EVENTS.colony.epidemic.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS.colony.epidemic . EVENTS.colony.coldSnap Every plot's Energy need is factor times this production. EVENTS.colony.coldSnap.phases.production 0.032 See the note on EVENTS.colony.coldSnap . EVENTS.colony.coldSnap.factor 2 See the note on EVENTS.colony.coldSnap . EVENTS.colony.coldSnap.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS.colony.coldSnap . EVENTS.colony.meteorStrike After production's output, one tile (not the store) becomes a meteor crater and loses its MULE. EVENTS.colony.meteorStrike.phases.production 0.032 See the note on EVENTS.colony.meteorStrike . EVENTS.colony.meteorStrike.afterOutput true Rolls once for the colony after production's output (rollAfterOutput); rollPhase never rolls it. EVENTS.colony.meteorStrike.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS.colony.meteorStrike . EVENTS.colony.pirateRaid Every player's and the store's Crystite goes to 0 before the auction opens. EVENTS.colony.pirateRaid.phases.auction 0.01 See the note on EVENTS.colony.pirateRaid . EVENTS.colony.pirateRaid.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS.colony.pirateRaid . EVENTS.colony.storeFire The store's stock of every good and its MULEs go to 0 before the auction opens. EVENTS.colony.storeFire.phases.auction 0.01 See the note on EVENTS.colony.storeFire . EVENTS.colony.storeFire.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS.colony.storeFire ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"events-player","title":"EVENTS.player","status":"","text":"Setting Value What it does EVENTS.player.sickness Sickness, at development start: the colonist walks at DEV_WALK.sickPace this turn unless it holds a Medicine, which it then takes. Likelier in dirty air (by band) and for a polluter: perPoint for each air point its plots caused last month (a filter halves them), at most max. Heiko's catalogue numbers. EVENTS.player.sickness.phases.development 0.03 See the note on EVENTS.player.sickness . EVENTS.player.sickness.air By the colony air's band. EVENTS.player.sickness.air.clean 0 See the note on EVENTS.player.sickness.air . EVENTS.player.sickness.air.smoggy 0.15 See the note on EVENTS.player.sickness.air . EVENTS.player.sickness.air.toxic 0.3 See the note on EVENTS.player.sickness.air . EVENTS.player.sickness.air.collapse 0.5 See the note on EVENTS.player.sickness.air . EVENTS.player.sickness.pollution perPoint for each air point the player's plots caused last month, at most max. EVENTS.player.sickness.pollution.perPoint 0.03 See the note on EVENTS.player.sickness.pollution . EVENTS.player.sickness.pollution.max 0.4 See the note on EVENTS.player.sickness.pollution . EVENTS.player.sickness.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS.player.sickness . EVENTS.player.pestSwarm.phases.production 0.024 See the note on EVENTS . EVENTS.player.pestSwarm.wealth Signed, for the richest and the poorest player by cash plus land at GAME.plotScore a plot; a tie goes to the first seat. EVENTS.player.pestSwarm.wealth.richest 0.01 See the note on EVENTS.player.pestSwarm.wealth . EVENTS.player.pestSwarm.wealth.poorest -0.01 See the note on EVENTS.player.pestSwarm.wealth . EVENTS.player.pestSwarm.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS . EVENTS.player.wildfire.phases.production 0.024 See the note on EVENTS . EVENTS.player.wildfire.wealth Signed, for the richest and the poorest player by cash plus land at GAME.plotScore a plot; a tie goes to the first seat. EVENTS.player.wildfire.wealth.richest 0.01 See the note on EVENTS.player.wildfire.wealth . EVENTS.player.wildfire.follows By event key: points when that event fired in a window the row names; once, if it fired in both. EVENTS.player.wildfire.follows.drought.points 0.03 See the note on EVENTS.player.wildfire.follows . EVENTS.player.wildfire.follows.drought.thisPhase true See the note on EVENTS.player.wildfire.follows . EVENTS.player.wildfire.follows.drought.lastMonth true See the note on EVENTS.player.wildfire.follows . EVENTS.player.wildfire.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS . EVENTS.player.bumperHarvest.phases.production 0.024 See the note on EVENTS . EVENTS.player.bumperHarvest.wealth Signed, for the richest and the poorest player by cash plus land at GAME.plotScore a plot; a tie goes to the first seat. EVENTS.player.bumperHarvest.wealth.poorest 0.015 See the note on EVENTS.player.bumperHarvest.wealth . EVENTS.player.bumperHarvest.wealth.richest -0.01 See the note on EVENTS.player.bumperHarvest.wealth . EVENTS.player.bumperHarvest.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS . EVENTS.player.inheritance Credits from min (the richest) to max (the poorest), linear in wealth rank (wave-2 AM-1 6a). EVENTS.player.inheritance.phases.planning 0.015 See the note on EVENTS.player.inheritance . EVENTS.player.inheritance.min 100 See the note on EVENTS.player.inheritance . EVENTS.player.inheritance.max 300 See the note on EVENTS.player.inheritance . EVENTS.player.inheritance.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS.player.inheritance ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"events-player-part-2","title":"EVENTS.player , continued","status":"","text":"Setting Value What it does EVENTS.player.muleTheft One installed MULE is taken off one of the player's plots; rolls in development and again at the month end. EVENTS.player.muleTheft.phases.development 0.01 See the note on EVENTS.player.muleTheft . EVENTS.player.muleTheft.phases.upkeep 0.01 See the note on EVENTS.player.muleTheft . EVENTS.player.muleTheft.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS.player.muleTheft . EVENTS.player.ratsInSilo Share of the player's Food the rats eat (floored); fires only when that is at least 1. EVENTS.player.ratsInSilo.phases.upkeep 0.015 See the note on EVENTS.player.ratsInSilo . EVENTS.player.ratsInSilo.share 0.5 See the note on EVENTS.player.ratsInSilo . EVENTS.player.ratsInSilo.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS.player.ratsInSilo . EVENTS.player.crookedAccountant Share of the player's cash the accountant takes (floored); fires only when that is at least 1. EVENTS.player.crookedAccountant.phases.upkeep 0.01 See the note on EVENTS.player.crookedAccountant . EVENTS.player.crookedAccountant.share 0.1 See the note on EVENTS.player.crookedAccountant . EVENTS.player.crookedAccountant.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS.player.crookedAccountant . EVENTS.player.packageFromHome Food the package brings. EVENTS.player.packageFromHome.phases.upkeep 0.015 See the note on EVENTS.player.packageFromHome . EVENTS.player.packageFromHome.amount 3 See the note on EVENTS.player.packageFromHome . EVENTS.player.packageFromHome.cap 0.9 See the note on EVENTS.player.packageFromHome ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"strategist","title":"STRATEGIST","status":"In the game","text":"Numbers the strategist's considerations use. Setting Value What it does STRATEGIST.horizon 6 Months of output it counts a MULE as earning, at most. STRATEGIST.discount 0.9 Each later month counts this share of the one before. STRATEGIST.gainScale Credits of gain that score 0.75 on the gain curve (0 scores 0.5). A plot's gain over the game runs to several thousand, so its scale is wide: at 400 every plot above about 1,200 scored the same. STRATEGIST.gainScale.plot 1200 See the note on STRATEGIST.gainScale . STRATEGIST.gainScale.develop 300 See the note on STRATEGIST.gainScale . STRATEGIST.gainScale.trade 80 See the note on STRATEGIST.gainScale . STRATEGIST.cashFloor 60 Cash it always keeps back, before the persona's reserve multiplier, plus the cost of next month's Food, Water and Energy shortfall. STRATEGIST.airCost 10 What one colony air point costs it, in credits, rising as the air gets dirtier (doubles by Smoggy). STRATEGIST.greenBeltCost 5 What it counts a green belt as costing a month, in credits (the engine charges none), set against the air it cleans; without full scoring, the cost alone. STRATEGIST.grantShare 0.75 The land grant is one pass, so a lit plot is weighed against the free plots still ahead of the light: its utility must be at least this share of the best of theirs to be pressed for. STRATEGIST.grantSpare 0 Beyond the claims it has left, how many more of its top plots still ahead of the light it presses for. 0: it presses only for plots it would claim with the claims it has left. STRATEGIST.factoryInputMonths 2 A factory needs this many months of its inputs on hand or from its own plots. STRATEGIST.keepSpare Spare Food, Water, Energy and Medicine it keeps over next month's need, before the reserve multiplier. STRATEGIST.keepSpare.food 1 See the note on STRATEGIST.keepSpare . STRATEGIST.keepSpare.water 1 See the note on STRATEGIST.keepSpare . STRATEGIST.keepSpare.energy 1 See the note on STRATEGIST.keepSpare . STRATEGIST.keepSpare.medicine 0 See the note on STRATEGIST.keepSpare . STRATEGIST.analyseWhenClose 0.25 It analyses an unknown plot of its own when the plot's best and second-best uses are this close, as a share. STRATEGIST.analysesPerMonth 2 Analyses per month, at most, and never with less than this many seconds left. STRATEGIST.analyseMinSeconds 20 No note of its own; the note on STRATEGIST.analysesPerMonth , just before it in the file, may cover it. STRATEGIST.rookieAnalyses 1 Analyses per month, at most, at a level without full scoring (Rookie), in place of analysesPerMonth. STRATEGIST.filterAirMargin 10 It fits filters once the air is within this many points of Smoggy, and only to polluters of more than filterOverAir air points a month. STRATEGIST.filterOverAir 1 Explained in the note on STRATEGIST.filterAirMargin . STRATEGIST.budgetShare 0.5 Share of its money it spends on one good at the auction, at most. STRATEGIST.residual 0.6 A developed plot counts in the final score at the MULE price; it counts this share of that as won back. A price in a sell-out doesn't last to the game's end, so counting most of it made a MULE bought while the corral was short look cheaper than it is."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"strategist-part-2","title":"STRATEGIST , continued","status":"","text":"Setting Value What it does STRATEGIST.secondValue What a second of development time is worth, with idle land to work and without. STRATEGIST.secondValue.busy 10 See the note on STRATEGIST.secondValue . STRATEGIST.secondValue.idle 3 See the note on STRATEGIST.secondValue . STRATEGIST.forecastHaircut 0.05 Share it takes off next month's price for a good it would hold, per point of risk weight, because the forecast may be wrong. STRATEGIST.resaleRisk 0.1 And this share more off a unit it would buy only to resell, per point of risk weight. STRATEGIST.cornerPremium 0.5 A corner pays this share over next month's price for the units the others are short of. STRATEGIST.cornerMaxSpoilage 0.5 It corners only a good that spoils less than this share a month, and at most this many units. STRATEGIST.cornerMaxUnits 6 No note of its own; the note on STRATEGIST.cornerMaxSpoilage , just before it in the file, may cover it. STRATEGIST.spiteShare 0.1 Spite: while the leader is short of the good, it bids this share of next month's price, times its spite, over its own value for at least the next spiteUnits units (all the cornered ones while it corners). STRATEGIST.spiteUnits 2 Explained in the note on STRATEGIST.spiteShare . STRATEGIST.outlookMonths 2 How many months past next the outlook plays the status quo forward (nobody builds or trades), to see a shortage coming before the price shows it: the corral's Smithore, Food or Water running out. STRATEGIST.reservePriceCap 3 When it sizes its cash reserve, a short unit counts at most this many times the reference price. STRATEGIST.selfSupply 3 A new farm's Food, Water or Energy that covers the seat's own shortfall is worth at least this many times the reference price: going short costs development time (Food), half of every farm (Water) or half of every plot (Energy), whatever the market price is today. STRATEGIST.cashLeftFloor 0.75 The \"cash left\" consideration: spending down to the reserve scores this much, and keeping cashLeftFull times the reserve scores 1. The reserve itself is a hard rule, so this is only a mild preference for cash in hand. STRATEGIST.cashLeftFull 2 Explained in the note on STRATEGIST.cashLeftFloor . STRATEGIST.develop The development decision's considerations (developDecision), besides gainScale.develop and the cash left. STRATEGIST.develop.unknownGround 0.75 \"Known ground\": what an idle plot it hasn't analysed scores; an analysed one scores 1. STRATEGIST.develop.feedsMe \"Feeds me\": what a MULE that doesn't cover its own Food or Energy scores (one that does scores 1); weighted this share of the persona's reserve. STRATEGIST.develop.feedsMe.other 0.8 See the note on STRATEGIST.develop.feedsMe . STRATEGIST.develop.feedsMe.reserveShare 0.5 See the note on STRATEGIST.develop.feedsMe ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"strategist-land","title":"STRATEGIST.land","status":"","text":"The land plan's considerations (landDecision), besides gainScale.plot. Setting Value What it does STRATEGIST.land.unknownGround 0.8 \"Known ground\": what a plot it hasn't analysed scores; an analysed one scores 1. STRATEGIST.land.nextToMine \"Next to my land\": full of its own plots beside a plot score 1, never under floor ; weighted clusterWeight in the cluster plan, else weight . STRATEGIST.land.nextToMine.full 2 See the note on STRATEGIST.land.nextToMine . STRATEGIST.land.nextToMine.floor 0.6 See the note on STRATEGIST.land.nextToMine . STRATEGIST.land.nextToMine.clusterWeight 2 See the note on STRATEGIST.land.nextToMine . STRATEGIST.land.nextToMine.weight 0.5 See the note on STRATEGIST.land.nextToMine . STRATEGIST.land.blocksLeader \"Blocks the leader\": full of the leader's plots beside a plot score 1, never under floor ; weighted by spite, at least minWeight. STRATEGIST.land.blocksLeader.full 2 See the note on STRATEGIST.land.blocksLeader . STRATEGIST.land.blocksLeader.floor 0.4 See the note on STRATEGIST.land.blocksLeader . STRATEGIST.land.blocksLeader.minWeight 0.5 See the note on STRATEGIST.land.blocksLeader . STRATEGIST.land.feedsMe \"Feeds me\": what a plan scores while its Food and Energy are covered, and, while they are short, any plan but farms first (which scores 1). STRATEGIST.land.feedsMe.covered 0.75 See the note on STRATEGIST.land.feedsMe . STRATEGIST.land.feedsMe.short 0.6 See the note on STRATEGIST.land.feedsMe . STRATEGIST.land.canDevelop \"Can develop it\": cash over the reserve scores 1 at mules MULE prices; weighted factoryWeight in the factory plan, else weight . STRATEGIST.land.canDevelop.mules 2 See the note on STRATEGIST.land.canDevelop . STRATEGIST.land.canDevelop.factoryWeight 1 See the note on STRATEGIST.land.canDevelop . STRATEGIST.land.canDevelop.weight 0.3 See the note on STRATEGIST.land.canDevelop . STRATEGIST.land.hurtsLeader 0.7 \"Hurts the leader\": what the plan that fences in the leader scores."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"strategist-trade","title":"STRATEGIST.trade","status":"","text":"The auction's trade decision (tradeDecision, expectedPrices), besides gainScale.trade and the cash left. Setting Value What it does STRATEGIST.trade.sure \"Sure thing\": what a sale scores, a buy of what it needs, and a buy partly to resell (a forecast); a pass scores 1. STRATEGIST.trade.sure.sell 0.9 See the note on STRATEGIST.trade.sure . STRATEGIST.trade.sure.need 0.9 See the note on STRATEGIST.trade.sure . STRATEGIST.trade.sure.resale 0.6 See the note on STRATEGIST.trade.sure . STRATEGIST.trade.bufferShare 0.5 Selling, it also offers to keep a buffer: this share of next month's need, at least 1 unit, over what it would keep. STRATEGIST.trade.sellFloor 0.85 It expects a sale to fetch at least this share of the posted price, whoever buys."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"ai-hands","title":"AI_HANDS","status":"In the game","text":"The AI's hands in the real-time phases. Times are in ms. Setting Value What it does AI_HANDS.devTickMs 100 How often AI hands act during development, in the server and the simulator alike. AI_HANDS.auction.startMs [300, 1500] Delay after the walk starts before it first moves, scaled by patience. AI_HANDS.auction.waitGap [0.03, 0.08] A gap to the other side's best price this small (share of the board) is left for them to close. AI_HANDS.auction.targetJitter 0.03 It wanders its target up to this share of the board inside its limit, so its stops are hard to read. AI_HANDS.auction.decideWaitMs 2500 How long it waits for the decider at the status screen before it plays its own top option. AI_HANDS.grant.choiceMs [150, 300] A go/no-go choice takes this much longer than a bare reaction."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"ai-hands-develop","title":"AI_HANDS.develop","status":"","text":"Development steps, as a quick human taps them: to the corral and buy, to an outfitter, out to the plot and install, analyse, the pub. Setting Value What it does AI_HANDS.develop.buyMs [1400, 2400] Buying and installing where the game has no walking: each includes a notional walk. AI_HANDS.develop.outfitMs [1000, 1900] See the note on AI_HANDS.develop . AI_HANDS.develop.installMs [1800, 3200] See the note on AI_HANDS.develop . AI_HANDS.develop.buyTapMs [600, 1000] The same two taps where the game walks (DEV_WALK charges the walk): the tap alone. AI_HANDS.develop.installTapMs [600, 1200] See the note on AI_HANDS.develop . AI_HANDS.develop.analyseTapMs [1200, 2000] See the note on AI_HANDS.develop . AI_HANDS.develop.filterMs [1200, 2000] See the note on AI_HANDS.develop . AI_HANDS.develop.pubMs [800, 1500] See the note on AI_HANDS.develop . AI_HANDS.develop.decideWaitMs 2500 The most it waits for its decider before it plays its own top option."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"ai-plans","title":"AI_PLANS","status":"In the game","text":"The AI's game plan (playbook.ts): which plan kind a seat drafts, the strategy fields it writes, and the bounds every playbook is held to, the engine's own draft and Claude's edits alike. Setting Value What it does AI_PLANS.on true Seats play their playbook; false: every seat plays the phase-by-phase asks, as before playbooks. AI_PLANS.ownPhases false A feature switch, not a balance value: a seat's own path plays its drafted development and auction parts; false, today's (AM-26). AI_PLANS.moneyMaker.plots 3 A good's score sums its install gain over this many of the seat's best plots for it (own idle and free). AI_PLANS.moneyMaker.rivalDiscount 0.15 Each rival plot already making the good cuts its score by this share. AI_PLANS.moneyMaker.weight 1 Weight of the \"My money-maker\" consideration in a development pick. AI_PLANS.moneyMaker.otherScore 0.8 That consideration's score, 0 to 1, for an outfit that makes another good (the money-maker scores 1). AI_PLANS.monopoly.goods [\"food\", \"energy\", \"smithore\"] The goods a seat may try to corner. AI_PLANS.monopoly.maxRivalPlots 1 A good is cornered only while at most this many rival plots make it. AI_PLANS.monopoly.minPlots 2 And only with at least this many of its own idle or free plots whose best outfit is the good. AI_PLANS.landBaron.minFreeShare 0.3 The land baron is a plan while at least this share of all plots is free. AI_PLANS.landTarget.months 3 The plot target is for this many months ahead, at most to the last month. AI_PLANS.landTarget.perMonth Plots a month the target adds, by plan kind; other for every kind not named. AI_PLANS.landTarget.perMonth.landBaron 2 See the note on AI_PLANS.landTarget.perMonth . AI_PLANS.landTarget.perMonth.other 1 See the note on AI_PLANS.landTarget.perMonth . AI_PLANS.sell A seat whose money is under its cash reserve lists a plot or a MULE for the land auction, unsold to the store. AI_PLANS.sell.perMonth 1 Lots a seat lists a month when short, at most. LOTS.perPlayer still caps every seller. AI_PLANS.sell.minimumShare 0.8 A lot's minimum, as a share of what it is worth to the seat; never under one credit over what the store pays."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"ai-plans-leaning","title":"AI_PLANS.leaning","status":"","text":"How much each persona leans to each plan kind, 0 to 1: the draft picks the eligible kind it leans to most. Setting Value What it does AI_PLANS.leaning.cautious.builder 1 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.cautious.specialist 0.5 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.cautious.monopolist 0.2 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.cautious.landBaron 0.3 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.cautious.politician 0.4 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.cautious.spoiler 0 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.aggressive.builder 0.3 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.aggressive.specialist 0.5 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.aggressive.monopolist 0.6 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.aggressive.landBaron 1 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.aggressive.politician 0.2 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.aggressive.spoiler 0.4 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.trader.builder 0.3 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.trader.specialist 1 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.trader.monopolist 1 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.trader.landBaron 0.3 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.trader.politician 0.2 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.trader.spoiler 0 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.saboteur.builder 0.2 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.saboteur.specialist 0.4 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.saboteur.monopolist 0.5 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.saboteur.landBaron 0.4 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.saboteur.politician 0.3 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning . AI_PLANS.leaning.saboteur.spoiler 1 See the note on AI_PLANS.leaning ."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"ai-plans-bounds","title":"AI_PLANS.bounds","status":"","text":"What a playbook may hold: a value outside is clamped or refused. Setting Value What it does AI_PLANS.bounds.cashFloorMax 2000 Credits a strategy's cash floor may ask, at most. AI_PLANS.bounds.rivalReadNotes 3 Notes on one rival, at most, and characters a note. AI_PLANS.bounds.rivalReadChars 140 See the note on AI_PLANS.bounds . AI_PLANS.bounds.lessons 5 Notes for later months, at most. AI_PLANS.bounds.routeSteps 24 Steps a development route may have, at most. AI_PLANS.bounds.concedeMaxCrPerS 50 Credits a second of the auction clock a seat may concede, at most."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"sim-targets","title":"SIM_TARGETS","status":"In the game","text":"The balance targets pnpm sim checks, from the design doc's Economy model tab, each named for its check, and the assumptions it measures under, which no check reads. Shares are of months or of games, as the check says; the report prints the targets from here. Setting Value What it does SIM_TARGETS.muleMedian MULE price median: from min to max credits. SIM_TARGETS.muleMedian.min 150 See the note on SIM_TARGETS.muleMedian . SIM_TARGETS.muleMedian.max 300 See the note on SIM_TARGETS.muleMedian . SIM_TARGETS.mule90th 500 MULE price 90th percentile: under this many credits. SIM_TARGETS.corralSoldOut 0.1 Corral sold out: in under this share of months. SIM_TARGETS.foodSpike Food above factor times its reference price: in this share of games, from min to max. SIM_TARGETS.foodSpike.factor 3 See the note on SIM_TARGETS.foodSpike . SIM_TARGETS.foodSpike.min 0.1 See the note on SIM_TARGETS.foodSpike . SIM_TARGETS.foodSpike.max 0.5 See the note on SIM_TARGETS.foodSpike . SIM_TARGETS.colonistsShort 0.15 Colonists short of Food or Water: in under this share of months. SIM_TARGETS.developed 0.7 Owned plots developed at the end: this share or more. SIM_TARGETS.broke No game has an AI broke, under below credits with idle land, for months months in a row. SIM_TARGETS.broke.below 100 See the note on SIM_TARGETS.broke . SIM_TARGETS.broke.months 3 See the note on SIM_TARGETS.broke . SIM_TARGETS.collapsed 0.1 Ended in Collapse: in this share of games or fewer. SIM_TARGETS.forcedAirPoints 5 --air <band> also counts each month's events as if every player's plots had caused this many air points last month (spec C5's measuring assumption). Measure only: no check reads it, and the games play on unforced."},{"page":"numbers.html","id":"brain","title":"BRAIN","status":"In the game","text":"The language model that decides for Smart AI seats, and its limits. Setting Value What it does BRAIN.model \"claude-haiku-5-5\" The model that decides and talks. BRAIN.seatBudgetUsd 0.06 A Smart seat's model budget per game, in US dollars; past it the seat plays Classic. BRAIN.spikeCapUsd 0.05 Hard ceiling, in US dollars, for the latency spike command. BRAIN.pricesPerMTok US dollars per million tokens. BRAIN.pricesPerMTok.input 0.1 See the note on BRAIN.pricesPerMTok . BRAIN.pricesPerMTok.cacheRead 0.01 See the note on BRAIN.pricesPerMTok . BRAIN.pricesPerMTok.cacheWrite 0.125 See the note on BRAIN.pricesPerMTok . BRAIN.pricesPerMTok.output 0.5 See the note on BRAIN.pricesPerMTok . BRAIN.maxTokens 200 Reply cap, in tokens. BRAIN.charsPerToken 2.5 Prompt characters per token, as the budget assumes. Haiku 5.5 measured 2.56 to 2.65 on real prompts; this sits below the lowest so the worst case never undercounts. BRAIN.maxLineChars 140 Longest chat line an AI says, in characters. BRAIN.linesPerMonth 3 Most chat lines per AI seat per game month: one each for land, development and the auction, since a seat says at most one a phase. BRAIN.maxTalkChars 200 Longest quoted table-talk line put in a prompt, in characters. BRAIN.talkLines 6 How many of the latest table-talk lines an AI keeps for its next prompt; each one costs prompt tokens. BRAIN.landTimeoutMs 5000 How long a land decision may wait for the model, in ms; planning lasts up to 45 s. In the code: packages/engine/src/config.ts · this page's builder: docs/rulebook/build-numbers.ts Kept current by: generated from config.ts; whoever changes a number reruns the builder in the same PR."},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"being-designed","title":"Being designed","status":"Being designed","text":"A design is under way or a question is open. Nothing is built yet."},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"smarter-computer-players","title":"Smarter buying and selling for computer players","status":"Being designed","text":"Computer players look at their whole need, the store&#x27;s prices and what is available, value having enough and a surplus when it is cheap, and later speculate on prices. Goals: stay supplied (short while the store had stock and they could pay in under 5% of months), value a surplus when cheap, and in a second step speculate under a price belief shaped by personality and settable by Claude. Who: AI Strategy team (WS9) · Status: Heiko chose option B, one value per unit (5 Nov). Design written (#385) and being checked by the owning teams; build plan due 6 Nov · Today's rule: computer players"},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"complete-store-pricing","title":"A complete design for how the store sets prices","status":"Being designed","text":"One design for the posted price, a wider gap between the store&#x27;s buying and selling prices (today 10% each way), restocking, Energy at the store, moving export prices, the MULE price, what happens after the store sells out and buyers bid higher, and Crystite&#x27;s off-world price (typically about 90, from 40 to 300, the higher the rarer; today it never moves from 100). Who: Maps team (WS5) · Status: Heiko chose option B, the store as a market maker, with a price ceiling of 12 times the usual price and Crystite as proposed (5 Nov). Design being written · Today's rule: store and prices"},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"auction-rules-b-to-e","title":"Auction rules: auto-stop, flip sides, rest zones, bidding above a sold-out store","status":"Being designed","text":"A seller stops automatically at their own balance point and moves up into the store lane; selling into a shortage becomes a conscious choice. You can change between buying and selling during the auction. Rest zones above and below the store&#x27;s lines stop you trading by accident. When the store has sold out, buyers can walk above it and push the price up. Who: Auctions team (WS3), screens by the UX team (WS6) · Status: Rules amendment and mock due 5 Nov 20:00 UTC · Today's rule: goods auction"},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"drone-and-geology","title":"The drone, assays and seeing your plots&#x27; resources","status":"Being designed","text":"Pick plots, press Scan and get a detailed report; always see your own plots&#x27; resources in planning and development; resources shown only after you paid for a drone or an assay; a clear marker on plots with Crystite or Rare earth; every resource rated 0 to 3 per plot. Who: UX & Gameplay team (WS6), with the Maps team (WS5) for geology · Status: How it works today and the goals due 5 Nov 23:00 UTC · Today's rule: land"},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"food-supply","title":"Is there enough Food in the economy?","status":"Being designed","text":"Once computer players buy the Food they need, they empty the same store shelf the colonists buy from. Colonists short of Food or Water rise from about 50% to 55-61% of games, and Food above three times its usual price from about 80% to about 90%. The levers are Heiko&#x27;s: start Food, the store&#x27;s Food per player, Food spoilage (50%), farm yield. Who: Heiko, at the balance pass at the end (C29) · Status: Recorded 5 Nov; measure now, tune at the end · Today's rule: production"},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"designed-and-building","title":"Designed or being built","status":"Being built","text":"Ruled or approved; a team is building it or it is in a pull request."},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"energy-turn-off","title":"Energy shortage turns plots off, biggest user first","status":"Being built","text":"Energy plots use 1 Energy each from their own output. When you are short, the plot that uses most is turned off first, then the next, until the rest fit; a turned-off plot makes 20% of its output. Today a short plot makes half, in map order. Still to design: the Energy use per plot and resource type, and what each factory needs (today&#x27;s numbers plus 1 for plants are the starting proposal). Who: Balance team (WS1), rule AM-17 · Status: Engine about 18:30 UTC, the computer players&#x27; side about 19:30 UTC, 5 Nov · Today's rule: production"},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"mule-swap","title":"Installing over a MULE gives you the old one","status":"Being built","text":"Installing a new outfit on a plot that has a MULE hands you the old MULE to lead (today it vanishes). Still leading it at turn end: it goes back to the corral, no refund. Installing the same outfit is refused: \"Same outfit: nothing to swap\". Who: Auctions team (WS3), rule AM-12 · Status: Being built, the failing test written first · Today's rule: development"},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"slower-auction-walk","title":"A slower auction walk and a slower trade start","status":"Being built","text":"Walking up and down the board is about 25% slower; trades start at about one unit a second and speed up; the land auction&#x27;s lot clock stretches from 12 to 15 seconds to match. Who: Auctions team (WS3) · Status: Pull request #376; one test to fix · Today's rule: goods auction"},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"auction-headline","title":"The auction headline names the good","status":"Being built","text":"Every goods-auction screen leads with the good&#x27;s symbol and name; the TV reads \"Food auction\" with the Food symbol. Who: UX team (WS6) · Status: Pull request #374; in the next batch once two teams sign off · Today's rule: table and phones"},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"tv-fit","title":"The TV fits every screen","status":"Being built","text":"On the auction board one chat line, none while a deal runs, so two deals fit; the gauges get their own row; the month-end title on one line. Who: Table team (WS4) · Status: Pull request #381, in review · Today's rule: table and phones"},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"sick-line","title":"The sideways phone says why you are slow","status":"Being built","text":"The arcade phone says you are sick, why, and your pace this turn. Who: UX team (WS6) · Status: Pull request #375; needs updating to the main build · Today's rule: table and phones"},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"last-walking-piece","title":"The old tap development screen is removed","status":"Being built","text":"Every game steers on the development floor; the old tap screen and its tests are gone. Who: Auctions team (WS3) · Status: Pull request #369; needs two sign-offs and updating to the main build · Today's rule: development"},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"ai-shortfall-stopgap","title":"Computer players buy what they are short of","status":"Being built","text":"A computer player short of Food, Energy or Water counts only what it holds, values each missing unit at least at the store&#x27;s price, does not sell below its need and does not let its cash reserve block the buy. Who: Balance team (WS1) · Status: Ruled to ship 5 Nov; pull request about 17:00 UTC · Today's rule: computer players"},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"plotless-grant","title":"Computer players no longer end the land grant with no plot","status":"Being built","text":"Computer players count rivals still claiming; a grab on a fading Crystite glint no longer starts a soil test. Who: Balance team (WS1) · Status: Pull request #366; after the stopgap and the Energy rule · Today's rule: land"},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"colony-social-events","title":"The colony&#x27;s social events, riots and the next colony steps","status":"Being built","text":"Planning social events (festival, strike, blame, immigration), then riots (per good the store loses a third of its stock), then the next colony piece. Who: Balance team (WS1) with the Politics team (WS7) · Status: First part in review (#370); riot part about 21:30 UTC; next part about 23:00 UTC, 5 Nov · Today's rule: colony and politics"},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"colony-event-visuals","title":"Strike, mood events and gauges on the TV and phones","status":"Being built","text":"The strike on the TV and phones with the Governor paying it, the colony&#x27;s mood events in the event show, the Happiness gauge counting at month end. Who: Events team (WS8) · Status: In the colony branch (#364, #365, #368) · Today's rule: colony and politics"},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"claude-as-player","title":"Claude plays a seat","status":"Being built","text":"Claude adjusts its auction plan just before the auction and its development plan as development starts; its own line gets the chat slot. The paid proof&#x27;s first step (at most about $1.10) is cleared; the full 20-game proof (about $20) needs Heiko. Who: Brain team (WS2) · Status: Two pull requests in the brain branch (#377, #378) · Today's rule: computer players"},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"backlog","title":"Backlog","status":"Backlog","text":"Wanted, nobody on it yet."},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"return-mule-refund","title":"Hand a MULE back to the store mid-turn for money","status":"Backlog","text":"From the MULE swap rule: today a swapped-out MULE goes back to the corral only at turn end, with no refund. Who: Not assigned · Status: Waiting for Heiko to want it · Today's rule: development"},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"strategy-engine-4","title":"Computer players keep a plan and re-plan on triggers","status":"Backlog","text":"The next step of the AI&#x27;s strategy engine. Who: AI Strategy team (WS9) · Status: Not started · Today's rule: computer players"},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"social-ai","title":"Social computer players","status":"Backlog","text":"Monthly social reasoning, replies to whispers, replies that can change a vote, a mute button, deal cards, politics from the reasoning, and a proof that social AIs win more. Who: Brain team (WS2) · Status: Not started · Today's rule: computer players"},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"politics-next","title":"Deal cards and reputation, sabotage and the Sheriff, budget and projects","status":"Backlog","text":"The colony politics still to come after the council and four laws. Who: Politics team (WS7) · Status: Not started · Today's rule: colony and politics"},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"air-gauge-bands","title":"The air gauge reads its bands from the game","status":"Backlog","text":"The TV&#x27;s air gauge keeps its own copy of the pollution bands; it should read them from the game so they cannot drift. Who: Events team (WS8) finding · Status: Backlog · Today's rule: colony and politics"},{"page":"open-designs.html","id":"retire-old-walk-settings","title":"Retire the old walk-time setting and the steering switch","status":"Backlog","text":"Once the last walking piece is in, the old development walk time and its switch go. Who: Balance team (WS1) with the Brain team (WS2) · Status: After the last walking piece · Today's rule: development"},{"page":"production.html","id":"production-order","title":"What happens in production, step by step","status":"In the game","text":"Production runs once a month, between development and the goods auction. Nobody acts during it: the whole table watches the production show while the game works through a fixed order. The order matters, because each step can only use what the steps before it left in your depot. Step What happens Why it comes here 1 The month's production events roll: dust storm, drought, acid rain, solar flare, comet pass, flood, volcanic ash, cold snap, and each player's pest swarm, wildfire and bumper harvest. Events work on the plots they reach, on top of everything else that plot made. 2 Every Energy plot produces, and its Energy goes straight into its owner's depot. So this month's Energy can power this month's other plots. 3 The game checks, for each player, whether they hold the Water their household and crew need this month. Decided before any well runs: a well harvested this month waters next month's crops, not this month's. 4 Every other farm, well and mine produces, in map order (row by row from the top left). Each pays its Energy from its owner's depot, or makes half without it; a Food farm whose owner is short of Water makes half again. Raw goods come before factories, because factories turn them. 5 Factories run: every Alloy smelter first, then every Electronics works, then every Medicine lab, each in map order. A factory takes all its inputs, Energy included, from its owner's depot, or makes nothing. Alloy first because Electronics needs it. In an Energy shortage the factories are served last. 6 Run counts move on: a plot that worked adds one month to its run, one that didn't starts again at 0. A green belt counts as working. Factories gain experience and thin veins run out by how long a plot has worked in a row. 7 Special fields settle: ruins pay their salvage to their owner, and a crystal cavern that was mined for Crystite this month may collapse. They pay or collapse after the month's output is made. 8 Pollution spreads around polluters and the colony's air gauge moves. It counts what ran this month, and changes what next month makes. 9 A meteor strike, if one rolls: the struck plot already made its output; then its MULE is lost and the plot becomes a crater. Heiko's ruling (4 Nov): the MULE produced this month, then is destroyed. Everything you made lands in your depot at once. There is no buying or selling during production: Energy or Water you are short of now can only be bought at the goods auction that follows, for next month. What this means at the table The goods auction comes after production. Energy you buy there is held over the month end, loses a quarter to spoilage, and powers next month's plots. So the Energy that matters for this month's production is what you held when the month started plus what your own Energy plots make now. In the code: packages/engine/src/production.ts::runProduction · packages/engine/src/game.ts::advancePhase (production branch) · packages/engine/src/specials.ts::settleSpecials · packages/engine/src/pollution.ts::runPollution Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1)."},{"page":"production.html","id":"raw-output","title":"What a farm, well, plant or mine makes","status":"In the game","text":"The six raw goods come out of the ground. Food, Water and Energy are grown ; Smithore, Crystite and Rare earth are mined . A plot's output is built up from a base and a list of pluses and minuses, always in the same order, and the game keeps every part so it can tell you why a plot made what it made."},{"page":"production.html","id":"raw-output-base","title":"The base: the ground","status":"","text":"Grown goods. A farm, well or Energy plot makes GROWN_BASE ( 2 ) plus the plot's rating for that good, but never less than GROWN_MIN ( 1 ). Ratings run from −2 to +3 , so the base runs from 1 to 5 . A poor plot still works, just badly. Mined goods. A mine makes exactly its deposit rating, 0 to 4 . A deposit of 0 means there is nothing to mine: the plot makes 0 and no bonus of any kind is added. The rating is rolled once per plot when the map is made, from odds set by the terrain, and stays hidden until you analyse it. The Land and geology chapter has the odds."},{"page":"production.html","id":"raw-output-parts","title":"The pluses and minuses, in order","status":"","text":"Order Part What it does Value 1 Thin vein (mines only) A thin vein's output drops by 1 for every THIN_VEIN_MONTHS months the mine has run in a row before this one. −1 per 3 months 2 Mollox (mines only) A Mollox owner's every working mine makes more. +1 2 Bramblet (Food only) A Bramblet owner's every farm makes more Food. +1 3 Hot spring (trait) Energy plot makes more; a well makes less. Energy +2, Water −1 3 Toxic ground (trait) A farm on toxic ground makes less Food. This is a plot trait, not the Toxic air band. Food −2 4 Crystal cavern (special field) Mined for Crystite. +4 4 Geyser (special field) Outfitted for Water or Energy. +2 4 Oasis (special field) Outfitted for Food. +3 4 Alien relic next door For each relic among the four plots touching this one, whoever owns them. A relic on the plot itself does not count. +1 each 5 Neighbour bonus Only if the plot makes something so far. At least one of your own plots touching it makes the same good: +1. Your connected group of plots of that good has 4 or more plots: +2 instead. +1 or +2 6 Polluted plot (Food, Water, Medicine) Loses 1 per pollution level of the plot (0 to 3). −0 to −3 6 Toxic air (Food and Water) From the Toxic band of the air gauge (60 and up), every farm and well loses 1 more. −1 7 Floor If the minuses took the total below 0, it is set back to 0. to 0 8 Short of Energy Half, rounded down (see Short of Energy ). ×0.5 9 Short of Water (Food only) Half again, rounded down (see Short of Water ). ×0.5 10 Events Each event that reaches the plot, in the order they fired, each working on what the ones before it left. Never below 0. see Events \"Touching\" always means the four plots that share a side: north, east, south and west, never diagonals. A \"connected group\" is every plot you can reach from this one by stepping through touching plots that you own and that make the same good. Groups count for Energy plots and mines as well as farms. Example: one Food farm, line by line Lena plays a Bramblet. Her farm is on a river plot with a Food rating of +2 . It touches one other farm of hers. A Rare earth mine next door has raised the plot's pollution to level 1 . Her household and crew need 2 Water this month, but she holds only 1 . She has the Energy. A drought hits the colony. Line Change Running total Ground: 2 + rating 2 4 4 Bramblet +1 5 Next to her own farm +1 6 Polluted plot, level 1 −1 5 Energy: 1 paid from her depot 0 5 Short of Water: half of 5, rounded down −3 2 Drought −1 1 The farm makes 1 Food. One missing unit of Water cost her three units of Food."},{"page":"production.html","id":"raw-output-traits","title":"Traits that change nothing yet","status":"","text":"A plot can carry other traits: bedrock, sheltered, unstable, infested and flood plain. The settings say these \"only change event risks\", but no event reads them today, so in the game they change nothing at all. What is changing Open: what bedrock, sheltered, unstable, infested and flood plain should do. The event model reads no plot trait ( events.ts has none of them), so the comment on TRAIT_EFFECT describes a plan, not the game. Owner of the answer: the Balance team (WS1), with the Maps team (WS5) for the traits themselves. Why it is like this Grown goods always make at least 1, so cheap land is never worthless; mined goods make exactly what is in the ground, so a 0 deposit is a real loss and assays matter. Both rules are in the design's starting numbers. The neighbour bonus rewards building clusters, and the minus for pollution makes industry next to farms a cost the farmer pays. In the code: packages/engine/src/production.ts::rawOutputParts · ::adjacencyBonus · ::pollutionLoss · packages/engine/src/specials.ts::specialBonus · config.ts::GROWN_BASE , GROWN_MIN , TRAIT_EFFECT , THIN_VEIN_MONTHS , SPECIES_PERK , ADJACENCY , MAP.specialEffect Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1); special fields with the Maps team (WS5)."},{"page":"production.html","id":"factories","title":"Factories and recipes","status":"In the game","text":"Three goods are made in factories from other goods. A factory takes its whole recipe from its owner's depot in one go. If any input is missing, Energy included, the factory makes nothing that month, takes nothing, and its run in a row starts again at 0. Factory Takes each month Makes Energy with a feeder next door Alloy smelter 2 Smithore, 2 Energy 2 Alloy 1 (next to your Smithore mine) Electronics works 1 Rare earth, 1 Alloy, 1 Energy 1 Electronics 0 (next to your Rare earth mine or Alloy smelter) Medicine lab 1 Food, 1 Water, 1 Energy 2 Medicine 0 (next to your farm or well) The inputs never change with the factory's output: a smelter that makes 3 Alloy still takes 2 Smithore. What changes is the output, from the base above: What helps or hurts Output When Good ground for this factory +1 See the terrain table below. Poor ground for this factory −1 See the terrain table below. Feeder plot saves 1 Energy One of your own plots touching it makes one of its inputs (not Energy). Only once, however many feeders. Industrial park +1 One of your own plots touching it is the same kind of factory. Experience +1 It has run 3 months in a row before this one, so from its 4th month running. Alien relic next door +1 each For each relic among the four touching plots, whoever owns them. Polluted plot (Medicine only) −1 per level The plot's pollution level, 0 to 3. Smelters and Electronics works don't mind. Output never goes below 0. Your phone lists every one of these lines, in words, before you install a factory, and the same numbers drive production."},{"page":"production.html","id":"factory-terrain","title":"Which ground suits which factory","status":"","text":"Every terrain not listed is neutral for that factory. Plains are neutral for a smelter and a lab (the phone says \"Plains suit any factory\") and good ground for an Electronics works. Factory Good ground (+1) Poor ground (−1) Alloy smelter Volcanic (\"Geothermal heat helps the furnace\"); hills and mountains (\"Firm rock under the furnace\") Swamp (\"Soft ground: the furnace sinks\"); ice (\"Too cold for the furnace\"); lake (\"Wet ground\") Electronics works Tundra (\"Cold air cools the works\"); desert (\"Dry air, no damp\"); plains (\"Flat and dry\") Swamp and river (\"Damp gets into the circuits\"); volcanic (\"Quakes and ash\") Medicine lab Forest and swamp (\"Herbs nearby\"); river (\"Clean water\") Desert (\"No herbs, little water\"); volcanic (\"Ash in the air\"); crater (\"Dust in the air\") Example: a Medicine lab that runs well Kai's Medicine lab stands on forest (+1, \"Herbs nearby\"). It touches his own well (a feeder: its Energy drops from 1 to 0) and another Medicine lab of his (industrial park, +1). It has run 4 months in a row (experience, +1). A neighbour's smelter has raised the plot's pollution to level 1 (−1). Output: 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 − 1 = 5 Medicine, for 1 Food and 1 Water and no Energy."},{"page":"production.html","id":"ruins-salvage","title":"Ruins pay once","status":"","text":"Ruins are a special field that makes nothing by itself. At the end of the first production after someone owns them, they pay their owner their salvage and become a plain plot: 2 Alloy or 1 Electronics, whichever the map drew. Why it is like this Factories are where the money is (Electronics' usual price is 220 ), so they cost: 2 crew instead of 1, inputs every month, and they run last in a shortage. The feeder, park and experience bonuses reward building an industrial estate in one place and keeping it running, which is also where pollution builds up. In the code: packages/engine/src/production.ts::factoryFit · packages/engine/src/specials.ts::relicFit , ::settleSpecials · config.ts::RECIPES , FACTORY_FIT , FACTORY , MAP.specialEffect.salvage Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1)."},{"page":"production.html","id":"energy-use","title":"Energy: what each plot uses, and what happens when you run short","status":"Being built","text":"Heiko set the Energy rules on 5 November. They replace today's rule (further down this page) as soon as the Balance team's change merges. In his words: \"if i am not having enugh energy i first turn off the plot that uses most until i end up with enoguh energy. enrgey prodcuing plots use energy as well ... A plot that is turned off only produces 20%\". The rules in one place Every plot uses Energy , by what it produces: the table below. Energy plants use Energy too. Energy plants produce first and pay their own use from their own output. They are never turned off, because that would only deepen the shortage. If your Energy can't cover all your other plots, the plot that uses most is turned off first , then the next biggest, until the plots still running fit what you have. A turned-off plot produces 20% of its normal output and uses no Energy. The plot's line says why: \"Turned off: not enough Energy (20%)\"."},{"page":"production.html","id":"energy-use-table","title":"Energy use per plot and resource type","status":"","text":"Each working plot uses this much Energy a month, paid during production. A cold snap (a colony event, 3.2% a month) doubles every plot's use for that month. Plot, by what it produces Energy a month Today Notes Food farm 1 1 Also needs Water if irrigated Water well 1 1 Energy plant 1 0 New. Paid from its own output, never from your stock Smithore mine 2 2 Crystite mine 2 2 Rare earth mine 2 2 Alloy smelter 2 2 1 next to your own Smithore mine (a feeder) Electronics works 1 1 0 next to your own Rare earth mine or Alloy smelter Medicine lab 1 1 0 next to your own farm or well Green belt 0 0 Grows nothing, uses nothing Still to design: are these the right numbers? Heiko asked for the Energy use per plot type and per factory to be designed. The table is today's set plus the new 1 for Energy plants. It is the starting proposal, not a final balance. Every number is a named setting ( UPKEEP.energyUse for farms, wells, plants and mines, and each factory's recipe), so it can change without touching the rules. First measurement with these numbers (40 simulated games, 5 Nov), before the computer players learned the new rule: Today's rule New rule New rule, plants use 0 Player months with a plot cut 42% 53% 50% Output lost in such a month (median) 41% 67% 67% Months colonists go short of Food or Water 53% 69% 65% The 20% output does most of the extra damage; the plants' use of 1 mainly raises Energy's price. The computer players still price a shortage at today's half, and they learn the 20% rule in the same change, so these numbers will move."},{"page":"production.html","id":"factory-energy","title":"What factories need","status":"","text":"A factory turns goods from your own stock into something worth more. Each run takes its inputs and its Energy together. Factory Takes per run Energy Makes Alloy smelter 2 Smithore 2 2 Alloy Electronics works 1 Rare earth, 1 Alloy 1 1 Electronics Medicine lab 1 Food, 1 Water 1 2 Medicine Ground, a neighbouring factory of the same kind, experience and relics can raise what a factory makes; see Factories and recipes . Under the new rule: A factory that holds its inputs is in the turn-off list like any other plot. A factory whose inputs only arrive this month (Smithore mined this month for your smelter) runs after your farms, wells and mines, on whatever Energy is left. It never causes another plot to be turned off. If too little Energy is left, it is turned off itself. A turned-off factory makes 20% of its output and uses 20% of each input, rounded up. If 20% of its output rounds to nothing, it takes no inputs. A factory missing its inputs doesn't run and doesn't count."},{"page":"production.html","id":"turn-off-order","title":"Which plot is turned off first","status":"","text":"The plot that uses the most Energy goes first. When two use the same, the one whose output is worth less at the store's current price goes first, so your most valuable plot keeps running. Still tied: map order. Your farms, wells, mines and factories are all in one list. Each player's plots are settled on their own. A turned-off plot gains no experience that month. 20% is rounded to the nearest whole unit, halves up: a plot making 3 or 4 still gives 1; a plot making 2 or less gives 0. Example Lena holds no Energy. She owns one Energy plant making 3, a Crystite mine (makes 3), a Smithore mine (makes 3), farm A (makes 3) and farm B (makes 4). Prices: Smithore 50, Crystite 100. The plant makes 3 and pays its own 1: 2 Energy go into her stock. Her other plots need 2 + 2 + 1 + 1 = 6. She has 2. Too little. The two mines use most, 2 each. The Smithore mine's output is worth 3 × 50 = 150, the Crystite mine's 3 × 100 = 300, so the Smithore mine is turned off first. The rest need 4: still too much. The Crystite mine is turned off next. The rest need 2: that fits. Both farms work in full and use her 2 Energy. Each mine makes 20% of 3 = 0.6, rounded to 1. Result: farms 3 and 4, mines 1 and 1. Under today's rule the farms' place on the map decided it, and farm B made only 2. Why it is like this Heiko, 5 Nov: the biggest users stop first, so a shortage is predictable and the cheap staples keep running, and a plant that pays its own Energy makes Energy a real cost of every estate. The PM settled the details on 5 Nov: plants pay from their own output and are never turned off; ties go by value; factories in the same list; inputs rounded up so nothing is made from nothing; no experience while off. What is changing Being built by the Balance team (WS1) as rule AM-17, with the computer players' side in the same change (they will price a missing unit of Energy at the 80% it saves). The phone and TV show the \"20%\" badge and the words \"Turned off: not enough Energy (20%)\". The auction's status screen will count each Energy plant's 1 in your need, so more players will show as short of Energy. In the code (branch ws1-energy-off ): packages/engine/src/production.ts::energyOffOrder , ::runProduction · config.ts::UPKEEP.energyUse , UPKEEP.offOutputShare , RECIPES , FACTORY.feederEnergySaving Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1)."},{"page":"production.html","id":"energy-turn-off","title":"How the new rule differs from today","status":"Being built","text":"Today New rule Which plot loses out Whichever comes later on the map The biggest Energy user, then the next What a short plot makes Half, rounded down 20%, rounded to the nearest unit Energy plants Use nothing Use 1 each, from their own output A factory without Energy Makes nothing Makes 20% from 20% of its inputs Cost of one missing unit Half of the plot it reaches 80% of the plot turned off Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1)."},{"page":"production.html","id":"energy-shortage","title":"Short of Energy today, until the new rule lands: half, in map order","status":"In the game","text":"Today a shortage is settled plot by plot, in map order, row by row from the top left of the map: Your Energy plots produce first. Their Energy joins what you already hold. Each farm, well and mine, in map order, asks for its Energy. If your depot holds enough, it pays and works in full. If not, it pays nothing and makes half its output, rounded down ( UPKEEP.shortOutputShare 0.5 ). Because a plot that can't pay takes nothing, a later plot that needs less may still be paid: a mine needing 2 can be halved while the farm after it, needing 1, works in full. Factories come after every raw plot of every player. A factory that cannot take its full Energy makes nothing at all. Rounding down bites at small numbers: a plot making 1 makes 0 without its Energy; a plot making 3 makes 1. Example: map order decides Lena holds no Energy. Her one Energy plot makes 3 . Her other plots, in map order: a Crystite mine (makes 3, needs 2), a Smithore mine (makes 3, needs 2), farm A (makes 3, needs 1), farm B (makes 4, needs 1). Plot Energy before Result Energy after Makes Crystite mine 3 pays 2 1 3 Smithore mine 1 can't pay 2: half 1 1 Farm A 1 pays 1 0 3 Farm B 0 can't pay 1: half 0 2 Had farm B stood further up the map than the Crystite mine, the farms would have worked in full and a mine would have been halved instead. Why it is like this The design: \"Without its Energy a farm or mine makes half and a factory makes nothing.\" Energy plots producing first means a player who makes their own Energy is never caught out by their own plants. Measured on 5 Nov (WS9, 40 games on each of 3 seeds): in 40 to 43% of seat-months a seat loses production for want of Energy, a median of 2 of 5 plots halved, 33 to 40% of the month's output value. That is why Heiko ruled the change below. In the code: packages/engine/src/production.ts::runProduction (the raw-plot loop and halve ) · config.ts::UPKEEP.shortOutputShare Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1)."},{"page":"production.html","id":"water-shortage","title":"Short of Water: farms make half","status":"In the game","text":"Food farms need irrigation. When production starts, the game compares the Water you hold with the Water your household and crew need this month (see Upkeep and crew ). If you hold less, every one of your Food farms makes half, rounded down. This comes on top of any Energy shortfall: a farm short of both makes a quarter, rounded down at each step. Only Food farms are irrigated. Wells, Energy plots and mines don't care about Water. The check is made once, before any plot runs. Water your wells make this month counts for next month. The Water is not taken at this point. You pay it at the month end, when your household and crew drink. Why it is like this Heiko's ruling of 21 October 2026 (economy design): \"Short of Water, your Food farms make half, because they can't irrigate.\" It makes Water a real need for a Food grower and gives wells a reason to exist beyond the colonists' demand. In the code: packages/engine/src/production.ts::runProduction (the dry set) · config.ts::UPKEEP.irrigated , UPKEEP.shortOutputShare Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1)."},{"page":"production.html","id":"production-events","title":"Events during production","status":"In the game","text":"Production is the busiest phase for events. Its events roll at the start of the phase and act at the end of each plot's line: after the ground, the bonuses, pollution and any shortage. Several can hit one plot; each works on what the ones before it left, and no plot goes below 0. Events only reach farms, wells, Energy plots and mines. Factories are never hit by a production event, except a meteor strike. Energy plots are credited first, events included: a dust storm leaves you less Energy for your other plots that same month, and a solar flare more. A cold snap does not touch output directly; it doubles every plot's Energy need, which can push you into a shortage. A crystal cavern that was mined for Crystite may collapse after production ( 6% each), and a meteor may strike after it ( 3.2% ). The Events chapter has every event, its chance and its numbers. What is changing A general strike, announced in planning when the colony is restless, will bite in production: every plot of the struck good makes 20% of its output, rounded down, unless the Governor pays the strikers. Being built in the colony branch (C26 PR-A, PR #370, the Balance team WS1). See the colony's social events . In the code: packages/engine/src/production.ts::runProduction ( credit ) · packages/engine/src/events.ts::eventOutput , ::rollPhase Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1)."},{"page":"production.html","id":"spoilage-and-storage","title":"Spoilage and storage","status":"In the game","text":"At the month end, after you have eaten and drunk, your goods spoil and anything above your home depot's space is lost. Each step rounds down. Each good loses its spoilage share: what is left is your stock × (1 − spoilage), rounded down. Your home depot holds UPKEEP.depotCapacity = 10 units of each good. Anything above that is gone. Good Lost each month (yours) Lost each month (the store's) Food 50% 12.5% Water 5% 1.25% Energy 25% 6.25% Medicine 20% 5% Smithore, Crystite, Rare earth, Alloy, Electronics 0% 0% The store has proper storage, so its goods spoil at PRICE.storeSpoilage = 0.25 of the normal rate, and it has no depot limit. Example: holding Food and Energy over the month end After eating, Kai holds 7 Food, 4 Energy and 14 Smithore. Food: 7 × 0.5 = 3.5, rounded down to 3 . Energy: 4 × 0.75 = 3. Smithore doesn't spoil, but the depot holds 10: 4 Smithore are lost. Selling the extra Smithore at the auction was his decision to make. Why it is like this The economy design: holding goods to sell later is allowed, but storing them costs space and spoilage, so every speculation is a real bet. Spoilage rates are the design's starting numbers. What is changing The storage buildings of the economy design (warehouse, silo, cold store, tank, battery bank, vault) are not in the game: the engine has none of them. Backlog, no team on it. In the code: packages/engine/src/upkeep.ts::runUpkeep · config.ts::GOOD_CONFIG ( spoilage ), UPKEEP.depotCapacity , PRICE.storeSpoilage Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1)."},{"page":"production.html","id":"upkeep-and-crew","title":"Upkeep and crew","status":"In the game","text":"Every plot you work has a crew, and your household and crew eat and drink at the month end. A bigger estate costs more to feed. Plot Crew Farm, well, Energy plot or mine 1 Factory 2 Green belt 0 Food a month: 2 for you, plus 1 for every 4 crew or part of 4. Water a month: 1 for you, plus 1 for every 6 crew or part of 6. At the month end both are taken from your depot; if you don't have enough, you go down to 0. Being short of Food shows up at the next development, where a hungry colonist walks slower (see Development ). Being short of Water shows up at the next production, where your farms make half. Energy is not part of upkeep: your plots paid it during production. Example: what an estate eats Five raw plots and one factory: a crew of 5 + 2 = 7. Food: 2 + 2 (7 crew is one full 4 and part of another) = 4 . Water: 1 + 2 = 3 . To keep your farms irrigated next month you need to hold 3 Water when production starts. In the code: packages/engine/src/production.ts::crewOf , ::needsOf · packages/engine/src/upkeep.ts::runUpkeep · config.ts::UPKEEP ( baseFood , baseWater , crewPerFood , crewPerWater , crew ) Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1)."},{"page":"production.html","id":"pollution","title":"Pollution and the air gauge","status":"In the game","text":"Industry fouls the land and the air. Pollution has two levels: each plot has its own level, and the colony shares one air gauge. Both move once a month, right after production."},{"page":"production.html","id":"pollution-sources","title":"Who pollutes","status":"","text":"Plot Air points a month With a filter Fouls the plots around it Rare earth mine 4 2 Yes (not with a filter) Alloy smelter 3 1.5 Yes (not with a filter) Electronics works 1 0.5 No Smithore mine 1 0.5 No Crystite mine 1 0.5 No Farm, well, Energy plot, Medicine lab 0 0 No A plot pollutes because a MULE is installed on it, whether or not it produced: the game counts every outfitted plot, so a smelter that stood idle for want of Smithore still adds its 3 points. A filter costs 1 Alloy and is fitted in development."},{"page":"production.html","id":"pollution-plots","title":"Pollution on a plot","status":"","text":"A polluter that fouls raises its own plot and the four touching it by 1 level each month, up to level 3 , whoever owns them. The store's plot never takes pollution. Every other plot recovers by 1 level a month. Untouched forest and swamp (no MULE on it) recovers by 2 . A green belt (a MULE outfit costing 20 cr) makes nothing, but cleans its own plot and the four touching it by 1 more each month, and takes 2 points off the air. Food, Water and Medicine made on a plot lose 1 per level. Mines, Energy plots, smelters and Electronics works don't mind."},{"page":"production.html","id":"air-gauge","title":"The colony's air gauge","status":"","text":"The gauge runs from 0 to 100 . Each month it adds every polluter's points, takes off 2 for each green belt, then nature cleans 2 points for each player in the game (a bigger colony has a bigger map). It never goes below 0 or above 100, and can hold half points because of filters. Band Gauge What it does Clean 0 to 29 The export ship pays 10% more. Smoggy 30 to 59 The air sickens: sickness is 15 points likelier for every player, the colonists' Medicine demand is × 1.5 , the auction's status screen counts 1 Medicine in your need, and acid rain is 4 points likelier. Toxic 60 to 79 As Smoggy, with sickness 30 points likelier; every farm and well makes 1 less; drought is 2 and acid rain 8 points likelier. Collapse 80 to 100 As Toxic, with sickness 50 points likelier, drought 4 and acid rain 12 points likelier; 5% of the colonists leave each month whether fed or not; every final score is multiplied by 0.8 . The air points each player caused this month are recorded. They make that player likelier to fall sick next month: 3 points of chance per air point, at most 40 (see Events ). Example: does the air move? Four players, so nature cleans 8 a month. On the map: one Rare earth mine (4), one Alloy smelter with a filter (1.5), two Smithore mines (2), one green belt (−2). The total is 4 + 1.5 + 2 − 2 − 8 = −2.5: the air gets cleaner. With three Rare earth mines and two unfiltered smelters instead (12 + 6 = 18), the gauge rises by 10 a month and reaches Smoggy in three months. Why it is like this From the pollution design: one player can get rich from a dirty mine while the whole table pays for it, which makes the colony's health something to deal, vote and argue about. The design said nature cleans 3 a month; the game cleans 2 per player, because a bigger colony has a bigger map. Measured on 28 October (WS1, 120 computer-player games): the gauge never reached Smoggy in any game; the highest month anywhere was 22. Computer players rarely build enough heavy industry to foul the air. In the code: packages/engine/src/pollution.ts::runPollution , ::airBand , ::airPoints · packages/engine/src/production.ts::pollutionLoss · config.ts::POLLUTION , MULE.outfitCost.greenBelt , DEVELOPMENT.filterAlloy Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1)."},{"page":"production.html","id":"production-show","title":"The production show on the TV and the phone","status":"In the game","text":"Production is played as a show, in the order Heiko chose on 5 November (\"order C\"): good by good for every player at once, then every bonus at once, then every loss at once, then the month's events, then the goods fly into the silos. The show's length is production's length: it grows with the number of goods made, never with the number of plots. Beat What you see How long Intro The map, before the first good lights. 1.0 s Harvest One good at a time (Food, Water, Energy, Smithore, Crystite, Rare earth, Alloy, Electronics, Medicine), only the goods made this month. Every plot of that good lights and counts up its base together, a unit every 0.14 s. 1.1 s a good Bonuses Every plus at once: neighbours, species, special fields. Green badges. 1.6 s Losses Every minus at once: pollution in amber, an Energy or Water shortage as a red \"½\" badge with the missing good. 1.6 s This month on Irata The month's production events play over the map, one after the other (see the event show ). Skipped in a quiet month. their own length Into the silos Every plot's goods fly to its owner's silo, the first gaps longest. 3.15 s Hold The silos stand full to be read. 2.0 s A short gap of 0.5 s sits before the bonuses, before the events and before the flight. Example: how long a month's show lasts A month where five goods were made and nothing fired: 1.0 + 5 × 1.1 + 0.5 + 1.6 + 1.6 + 0.5 + 3.15 + 2.0 = about 15.9 s. Add one drought (a headline: 1.8 s intro + 7.0 s) and it is about 25.2 s."},{"page":"production.html","id":"production-show-tv","title":"On the TV","status":"","text":"The whole map plays, every player's plots. Beside it a rail lists the beats (Harvest, Bonuses, Losses, This month on Irata, Into the silos) and ticks them off. During Bonuses and Losses it lists, in words, the reasons that appear this month, for example \"Next to your own plot of the same good\", \"Polluted ground\", \"A farm with no Water makes half\", \"No Energy for the plot: half\". During the events it keeps a log of what played. A silo bar along the bottom fills as the goods land."},{"page":"production.html","id":"production-show-phone","title":"On the phone","status":"","text":"Your phone plays the same map but fills only your plots, and your harvest flies into \"Your silo\", a strip showing everything you hold. The rail lists what you made, good by good (on a phone alone, once the show ends). With a TV in the room the phone shows each event as a light card and buzzes as it is announced. Alone, with no TV connected, the phone plays each event on its own map, and a tap on the map skips to the result. Ready waits for the end of the show, so the table always sees its month. With a TV in the room every person can press \"Speed up\"; once everyone has, the rest of the show plays 3 times faster. Computer players always agree. In the code: packages/engine/src/production-show.ts::productionShow · config.ts::PRODUCTION_SHOW · apps/web/src/table/TvProduction.tsx · apps/web/src/phone/Production.tsx · packages/ui/src/components/ProductionMap.tsx Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1) for the numbers; the UX team (WS6) for the phone and the Table team (WS4) for the TV."},{"page":"production.html","id":"month-report","title":"Why a plot made what it made","status":"In the game","text":"For every working plot the game files a line in the month's report: the plot, the good, what it made, and every part that made it up, in order. The parts always add up exactly to what the plot made. Part Meaning On screen ground The base from the ground rating or deposit. The base the plot counts up to in the harvest. factory A factory's whole run (its base and every fit line). The base the plot counts up to. trait Hot spring, toxic ground or a thin vein. A badge with the change. species The Bramblet's Food or the Mollox's mining perk. \"Your species is good at this\", +1. special A crystal cavern, geyser, oasis or touching relic. \"A special field\". cluster The neighbour bonus. \"Next to your own plot of the same good\". pollution The plot's pollution level, and Toxic air on farms and wells. \"Polluted ground\", amber. floor The total was set back up to 0. A badge with the change. noEnergy Half for want of Energy. \"No Energy for the plot: half\", a red ½ with the Energy icon. noWater Half for want of Water. \"A farm with no Water makes half\", a red ½ with the Water icon. event One event's change, naming the event. Played in the events beat, not as a badge. Every screen sees the same lines: what each plot made is public, like the auction's status table. The lines are also what the simulator and the computer players read. A factory's own reasons (terrain, feeder, park, experience, relic, pollution) are shown in words on the phone before you install it, not in the month's line. What is changing With the new Energy rule a new part, energyOff , reads \"Turned off: not enough Energy (20%)\" (being built, the Balance team WS1; the words on the phone and TV by WS6 and WS4). There is no written month report that lists each plot's parts in words outside the show; whether one is wanted is open (PM). In the code: packages/engine/src/types.ts::ProductionLine · packages/engine/src/production.ts::rawOutputParts · apps/server/src/mule-game.ts::productionView · packages/protocol/src/index.ts::ProductionLineView Kept current by: the Balance team (WS1)."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"overview","title":"The store in one minute","status":"In the game","text":"One price per good. Before every goods auction the store posts a price for each good. It sells at 10% above that price and buys at 10% below it. Prices follow cover. Cover is how many months the colony's whole supply of a good (the store's shelf plus every player's depot) would last at next month's demand. The store aims to see 2 months of cover. Less cover pushes the price up, more pushes it down. Prices move with momentum and have no ceiling. A posted price can rise by half a month and fall by 40% a month. In a real shortage it keeps climbing until supply comes back. The colony is self-contained. Nothing is imported. Every unit on the store's shelf was made by a player and sold to the store. The only way out is the export ship, which calls every third month. The store never runs out of money. It buys as much as it wants, limited only by shelf room. Crystite is for export. The colony has no use for it except one prestige building; its price is set by what the export ship pays. How you trade with the store at the auction is in The goods auction . In the code: packages/engine/src/economy.ts · packages/engine/src/config.ts::PRICE , GOOD_CONFIG Kept current by: WS1 (the store's code), WS5 (the store-pricing design)."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"store-stock","title":"The store's stock","status":"In the game","text":""},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"starting-stock","title":"What the store starts with","status":"","text":"Food and Energy are stocked per seat; everything else is the same for any table. Good 2 seats 4 seats 6 seats Setting Food 8 16 24 START.storeFoodPerPlayer 4 a seat Water 20 20 20 START.storeStock Energy 10 20 30 START.storeEnergyPerPlayer 5 a seat Smithore 20 20 20 START.storeStock Crystite 0 0 0 START.storeStock Rare earth 4 4 4 START.storeStock Alloy 8 8 8 START.storeStock Electronics 0 0 0 START.storeStock Medicine 4 4 4 START.storeStock MULEs in the corral 10 10 10 START.storeMules Store capital (credits) 4,000 4,000 4,000 START.storeCapital Each player starts with 1,000 credits, 2 Food, 6 Water and 2 Energy. The colony starts with 100 colonists. Why it is like this Water is kept short on purpose: Heiko ruled on 29 Oct that the colony should feel a coming Water shortage from the first month, so players build wells early. Food 4 a seat in the store, Energy 5 a seat, and each player's 2 Food and 2 Energy are Heiko's numbers of 5 Nov."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"colonists-reserve","title":"The colonists come first","status":"","text":"The colonists buy their Food and Water from the store at the end of every month, at its selling price, one unit of each per 20 colonists. The store keeps that much back: at the auction it sells players only what is above its colonists' reserve. In a shortage the shelf therefore runs empty for the players first. The status screen and the TV show the store's stock for sale , after the reserve."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"store-spoilage","title":"The store's goods spoil slowly","status":"","text":"The store has proper storage, so at month end its goods lose only a quarter of the usual spoilage ( PRICE.storeSpoilage = 0.25 ). The loss is rounded so that whole units are lost. Good In a player's depot In the store Example in the store Food 50% 12.5% 16 become 14 Energy 25% 6.25% 20 become 18 Medicine 20% 5% 4 become 3 Water 5% 1.25% 20 become 19 Smithore, Crystite, Rare earth, Alloy, Electronics 0% 0% Keep forever Because the result is rounded down, a small stock loses at least one unit a month of any good that spoils at all: 3 Water become 2."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"store-capital","title":"The store's money","status":"","text":"The store has its own working money, its capital, separate from the colony fund. It earns from MULEs, outfits and drone fees, from selling goods to players, colonists and the export ship, and from colony plots sold at the land auction; it pays for goods, unsold plots and MULEs it buys, and for wolf pelts. Its buying is never limited by its capital, and the capital may go below zero. It is never shown to players and does not count toward anyone's score. In the code: config.ts::START , storeStockFor , PRICE.storeSpoilage · economy.ts::storeForSale · upkeep.ts::runUpkeep Kept current by: WS1."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"posted-price","title":"How the posted price is set","status":"In the game","text":"The store posts its prices once a month, as the goods auction opens, after production and after any auction-phase event (a pirate raid or a store fire changes the stock first). The prices stay fixed for the whole auction. In month 1 the game also starts with each price where supply and demand put it."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"demand","title":"Step 1: next month's demand","status":"","text":"For each good the store estimates what the colony will need next month: Good Who counts as demand Food Every player's household and crew (2 each, plus 1 per 4 crew); colonists, 1 per 20, fewer if the price is above what their wages pay (150) Water Every player's household and crew (1 each, plus 1 per 6 crew); colonists, 1 per 20 (wages pay up to 120) Energy What every working plot and factory burns; colonists, half a unit per 20 (wages pay up to 100) Medicine Colonists, 1 per 50, half as much again while the air makes people sick (wages pay up to 400) Smithore 2 for every Alloy smelter; 1 for every MULE the colony will want (one per idle plot, plus a month's new land while free land is left); the export ship Alloy 1 for every Electronics works; the export ship Rare earth 1 for every Electronics works; the export ship Crystite Only the export ship Electronics Only the export ship The export ship's part: once its wishlist is announced (the month before it calls, and the month it is in port), each wishlist good counts a third of what the ship still wants, and goods not on the list count nothing. Before that, each good the ship might want counts its average share. See the export ship . Colonists' demand bends with price: they need a fixed amount, but when the store's selling price is above what their wages pay per unit, they buy only the share they can afford. That is the colony's demand curve."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"cover","title":"Step 2: cover","status":"","text":"Supply is everything the colony holds of the good: the store's shelf plus every player's depot. MULEs standing in the corral count as the Smithore they were built from. Cover is supply divided by next month's demand: how many months it would last. A good with no demand has endless cover."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"target","title":"Step 3: the target price","status":"","text":"The target price is the good's reference price times a scarcity factor: target = reference × (2 ÷ cover) ^ k 2 is the cover the store aims for, PRICE.targetCover . At exactly 2 months of cover the target is the reference price. cover never counts as less than PRICE.minCover = 0.25 month in this formula, so even an empty colony gives a finite target. k is the good's sensitivity ( GOOD_CONFIG ): how hard its price reacts. Essentials react strongly, because people can't stop eating. Cover k = 1.5 (Food, Water) k = 1.2 (Energy, Smithore) k = 0.5 (Crystite, Electronics) 0.25 month or less × 22.6 × 12.1 × 2.8 0.5 month × 8.0 × 5.3 × 2.0 1 month × 2.8 × 2.3 × 1.4 2 months × 1.0 × 1.0 × 1.0 4 months × 0.35 × 0.44 × 0.71"},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"momentum","title":"Step 4: momentum","status":"","text":"The posted price does not jump to the target. Each month it moves toward it, but: it rises by at most PRICE.maxRise = 50% of the current price, or of the reference price if the good has become cheaper than that, so a crashed good recovers in a couple of months; it falls by at most PRICE.maxFall = 40% ; it is a whole number of credits, and never below 1."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"short-goods","title":"Step 5: when a good is really short, there is no target","status":"","text":"In three cases the price ignores its target and climbs the full 50% step every month, until supply comes back: cover is below 0.25 month; the colonists went short of the good last month; for Food and Water only, the store's own shelf holds less than one month of its colonists' need ( PRICE.shelfCover = 1 ), so the price starts climbing before anyone goes hungry. There is no ceiling. A good that stays short reaches very high prices. WS5 measured, over 200 four-seat games on trunk, a Food peak of 1,985 and a Medicine peak of 10,389, with Food's median at 59 against its reference of 30."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"export-only-cap","title":"Goods only the ship wants","status":"","text":"For a good that nobody in the colony uses, only the export ship (Crystite and Electronics today), the target is never above the off-world price : the store will not aim higher than the ship pays. The momentum rule still applies, so a price that starts above it falls to it by at most 40% a month. Setting Value What it does PRICE.targetCover 2 Months of demand the store aims to see; at this cover the target is the reference price PRICE.minCover 0.25 Cover never counts as lower than this in the formula; below it the price climbs without a target PRICE.maxRise 0.5 Most a posted price rises in a month, as a share of the price (or of the reference, if higher) PRICE.maxFall 0.4 Most a posted price falls in a month PRICE.storeSpread 0.1 The store sells this much above and buys this much below its posted price PRICE.storeMaxCover 3 The store buys until its shelf holds this many months of demand PRICE.shelfCover 1 Food or Water counts as short when the store's shelf holds less than this many months of colonists' need PRICE.restockPremium 1.5 With that shelf short, the store bids up to this times the posted price PRICE.storeSpoilage 0.25 Share of normal spoilage the store suffers PRICE.refDrift 0.25 How fast the reference price follows what players pay each other PRICE.worldPull 0.1 How fast the reference price drifts back toward the off-world price Why it is like this The design doc's rule: prices are never set by hand; they come from supply and demand inside a self-contained colony (Heiko, 21 Oct: no imports). Supply counts the whole colony, as in the original, not only the store's shelf. Momentum makes a shortage build over two or three months, which is the speculator's window. The short-shelf rule and the restock bid (pricing B) were approved by Heiko on 22 Oct to get the price climbing before the colonists go hungry. Worth knowing (from reading the code) Colonists' Energy and Medicine count as demand in the price, but colonists only ever buy Food and Water from the store. Their Energy and Medicine raise those prices without taking any units off the shelf. A Medicine works' Food and Water inputs are not counted as demand for Food and Water (Smithore for smelters, and Alloy and Rare earth for Electronics works, are). Whether these are intended is for WS1 to say; WS5's store-pricing design may take them up. In the code: economy.ts::monthlyDemand , exportDemand , colonistDemand , colonySupply , cover , scarcity , targetPrice , nextPostedPrice , riseCap , postPrices · game.ts::createGame (month 1) · game.ts::advancePhase (when prices are posted) Kept current by: WS1."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"price-example","title":"Worked example: Food over three months","status":"In the game","text":"A four-seat game with 100 colonists. To keep the arithmetic simple, next month's Food demand stays at 13 throughout: 8 for the four households (2 each) and 5 for the colonists (1 per 20). Food's reference price is 30, the same as its off-world price, and players trade Food only with the store, so the reference does not move. Month Last price Supply Cover Short? Target Allowed range Posted Store buys / sells A 34 24 1.85 No 33.8 20.4 to 51 34 30 / 38 B 34 8 0.62 Yes: shelf 3, under 5 none 20.4 to 51 51 77 / 78 (restocking) C 51 20 1.54 Yes: colonists went short in B none 30.6 to 76.5 77 69 / 85 Month by month Month A, a normal month. The store holds 12 Food and the players 12: supply 24, cover 24 ÷ 13 = 1.85 months. Scarcity: (2 ÷ 1.85)^1.5 = 1.13, so the target is 30 × 1.13 = 33.8. The last price was 34, which may move between 34 × 0.6 = 20.4 and 34 + 34 × 0.5 = 51. The target is inside, so Food is posted at 34 . The store buys at 30 (34 less 10%, rounded down) and sells at 38 (34 plus 10%, rounded up). Month B, a drought. Harvests fail: the store holds 3 Food and the players 5, supply 8. Cover is 0.62, which alone would give a target of about 176. But the store's shelf (3) is under one month of its colonists' need (5), so Food counts as short: the price climbs the full step, 34 + 17 = 51 . To restock, the store bids 51 × 1.5 = 77 (well under the colonists' ceiling of 150 ÷ 1.1² = 123) and sells at 78, one above what it pays. Nobody sells it enough in time: at month end it has only 3 Food for the colonists' 5, and the colonists go short. Month C, the harvest is back. Supply is 20 again (store 8, players 12): cover 1.54, which would give a target of 44.5. But the colonists went short last month, so Food still counts as short and the price climbs the full step again: 51 + 25.5 = 76.5, posted 77 . The shelf now holds 8, more than a month of colonists' need, so there is no restock bid: the store buys at 69 and sells at 85. And after. In month D, with the same supply of 20 and nobody short, the target is 44.5. The price may fall only 40%, to 77 × 0.6 = 46.2, so Food is posted at 46 , and reaches its target the month after. The pattern is the design's: a shortage shows up as a rising price over two or three months, and the price stays high for a month after supply returns, which is when a player holding Food sells well. In the code: economy.ts::nextPostedPrice , shelfShort · auction.ts::comeUp (restock bid) Kept current by: WS1."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"reference-and-off-world","title":"Reference and off-world prices","status":"In the game","text":"Every good has two background prices: The reference price is what the good is worth at normal supply (2 months of cover). It is the base of the target price, and it learns from what players pay each other. The off-world price is what the export ship pays for its first unit. It is also where the reference price drifts back to. Good Reference and off-world price at the start Sensitivity k Spoilage a month (depot) Food 30 1.5 50% Water 25 1.5 5% Energy 25 1.2 25% Smithore 50 1.2 0% Crystite 100 0.5 0% Rare earth 60 0.8 0% Alloy 90 0.8 0% Electronics 220 0.5 0% Medicine 120 1.0 20% The off-world price never moves today. It is set from GOOD_CONFIG.refPrice when the game starts and stays there for the whole game, for every good."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"reference-learning","title":"How the reference price learns","status":"","text":"Once a month, when each good closes at the auction (or is skipped), its reference price moves: new reference = reference + (implied − reference) × 0.25 + (off-world − reference) × 0.1 implied is the average price players paid each other for the good this month, divided by the scarcity factor at that moment. A trade at a shortage price raises the reference only by as much as players paid above what the shortage alone explains. With no trades between players, implied is the reference itself and only the pull toward the off-world price is left. Trades with the store and the ship teach nothing: they happen at the store's and the ship's own prices. The result is rounded to whole credits, and is never below 1. There is no other bound. Example Paying above the shortage. Alloy, reference 90, cover 2 months (scarcity 1). Players pay each other 130 on average. Implied is 130, so the reference becomes 90 + 40 × 0.25 + 0 = 100 . Next month the pull alone takes it to 99. Paying less than the shortage explains. Food in month B above: cover 0.62, scarcity 5.86. Two players trade Food at 60. Implied is 60 ÷ 5.86 = 10.2, below the reference of 30, so the reference falls: 30 + (10.2 − 30) × 0.25 = 25 . The players paid twice the reference, but much less than the shortage would justify, and the market reads that as Food being worth less. Worth knowing (from reading the code) Because the reference is rounded every month, the pull toward the off-world price stops a few credits short: a gap of 5 credits or less above it (4 or less below it) moves by less than half a credit and rounds back. A reference that drifted to 34 for Food stays at 34 until players trade Food with each other again. WS1 owns the rule; the store-pricing design may change it. In the code: config.ts::GOOD_CONFIG · economy.ts::driftReference · auction.ts::learnFromGood , nextGood (skipped goods) · game.ts::createGame ( offWorld set once) Kept current by: WS1."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"spread","title":"Buying and selling at the store: the spread","status":"In the game","text":"The store sells at its posted price plus PRICE.storeSpread = 10% , rounded up, and buys at the posted price less 10%, rounded down (never below 1). The two together are about 20% wide. Its quotes at the reference prices: Good Posted Store buys Store sells Food 30 27 33 Water 25 22 28 Energy 25 22 28 Smithore 50 45 56 Crystite 100 90 111 Rare earth 60 54 66 Alloy 90 81 100 Electronics 220 198 243 Medicine 120 108 132 When a buyer and a seller meet between the store's lines, both do better than with the store. The store's lines are the fallback: a seller can always sell while the store wants the good, and a buyer can always buy while it has some for sale. With nothing for sale, the store shows no selling price (\"out\"). When it wants no more, it shows no buying price. While a Food or Water shelf is short it bids more and sells dearer, always at least one credit apart (see restocking ). Worth knowing (from reading the code) For Smithore, Crystite, Alloy and Electronics at their reference prices the selling price comes out one credit higher than 10% would give (56 rather than 55 for Smithore, 111 rather than 110 for Crystite). The computer's arithmetic makes 50 × 1.1 a hair over 55, and the store always rounds its selling price up. Small, but a player who checks will notice. WS1 owns it. Why it is like this The design doc: \"The store sells at 10% above the posted price and buys at 10% below it, so selling to another player is usually better than selling to the store.\" Heiko asked on 5 Nov for a wider spread; see the store-pricing design . In the code: economy.ts::storeQuotes , colonyPrice · auction.ts::comeUp (the auction's lines) Kept current by: WS1."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"restocking","title":"How the store restocks","status":"In the game","text":"Nothing is ever imported. The store's shelf fills in only two ways: players sell to it at the auction, and goods it already holds. It empties when players and the colony fund buy from it, when colonists buy Food and Water at month end, when goods spoil, when it sells its surplus to the export ship, when it builds MULEs from Smithore, and when an event strikes."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"how-much-it-buys","title":"How much it buys","status":"","text":"At each good's auction the store wants enough to fill its shelf to PRICE.storeMaxCover = 3 months of next month's demand, plus, for a good the export ship takes, a full ship's load to sell when the ship calls. Once it has bought that many its buying line goes. Its money never limits it. Example Food demand is 13 a month and the store holds 12. It will buy up to 13 × 3 − 12 = 27 Food at this auction. For Crystite in a four-seat game in a month when the ship has not announced its wishlist, demand is 4 (the ship's average) and a ship's load is 12, so the store wants up to 4 × 3 + 12 = 24 Crystite, less what it holds."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"short-shelf","title":"A short shelf of Food or Water","status":"","text":"When the store's Food or Water would not feed its colonists for a month (under PRICE.shelfCover = 1 month of their need): the posted price counts as short and climbs the full step (see step 5 ); at the auction the store bids up to PRICE.restockPremium = 1.5 times the posted price, but never more than its colonists could pay after the store's two margins: 150 ÷ 1.1² = 123 for Food and 120 ÷ 1.1² = 99 for Water; its selling price rises with it, to at least one credit above what it pays, so a player can't buy from it and sell straight back."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"colonists-buy","title":"The colonists buy at month end","status":"","text":"At upkeep the colonists buy 1 Food and 1 Water per 20 colonists at the store's selling price, paid from their off-world wages: at most 150 a unit for Food and 120 for Water. If the shelf is short, or the price is above their wages, they buy less and go hungry. A fed colony grows 4% a month; a hungry one shrinks 5%. A month the store couldn't feed its colonists is recorded as a shortage, which keeps that good's price climbing the next month."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"events-and-stock","title":"Events that empty the shelf","status":"","text":"Event Chance What it does Store fire 1 in 100 a month, as the auction opens The store's stock of every good and all its MULEs go to 0, before prices are posted Pirate raid 1 in 100 a month, as the auction opens All Crystite, the store's and every player's, goes to 0, before prices are posted See Events for the full list. Why it is like this Heiko, 21 Oct: no imports, the colony is self-contained; every unit of Food, Water and Energy has to be grown by a player, so a shortage is the players' own doing. Heiko, 22 Oct: \"the store should have unlimited amount of money\". In the code: economy.ts::storeWants , shelfShort · auction.ts::comeUp · upkeep.ts::runUpkeep · events.ts ( storeFire , pirateRaid ) · config.ts::UPKEEP.colonistBudget , UPKEEP.colonistGrowth , EVENTS.colony Kept current by: WS1."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"mule-price","title":"The MULE price and the corral","status":"In the game","text":"A MULE costs its labour plus the Smithore it is built from, times a scarcity factor: MULE price = (120 + 1 × Smithore's posted price) × scarcity factor , rounded. Labour is MULE.labour = 120 credits; each MULE takes MULE.smithorePerMule = 1 Smithore. So whoever mines Smithore sets the price of MULEs, as in the original. The scarcity factor starts at 1. Each month the corral sold out during development, it goes up by MULE.scarcityUp = 15% . Each month at least half the corral's MULEs were left over ( MULE.surplusShare ), it goes down by MULE.surplusDown = 10% , never below MULE.minFactor = 0.6 . It compounds, so a long MULE shortage gets steadily dearer. Gearlings pay 95% of the price for MULEs and outfits ( SPECIES_PERK.gearlingPrice ). Example Smithore is posted at 50: a MULE costs 120 + 50 = 170 (a Gearling pays 162). The corral sells out: next month it costs 170 × 1.15 = 195 . It sells out again: 170 × 1.15 × 1.15 = 225 . In a month with half the MULEs left over, the factor drops by 10% instead. In month 1 of a four-seat game, reading the code: the colony holds 20 Smithore in the store plus 10 MULEs in the corral (counted as Smithore), against a demand of 10 (8 MULEs for a month's new land and 2 for the export ship's average), so cover is 3 and Smithore starts at about 31. The first MULEs cost about 151 ."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"corral","title":"The corral","status":"","text":"At every month end the store builds MULEs from its own Smithore, one Smithore each, up to its corral target: 2 MULEs for every seat (a month's new land, by grant and land auction) plus 2 spare ( MULE.corralPerPlayer , MULE.spareInCorral ). That is 6 at 2 seats, 10 at 4 seats and 14 at 6 seats. Without enough Smithore it builds fewer, and the corral may sell out."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"outfits","title":"Outfitting","status":"","text":"Fitting a MULE for a job costs, at the store: Outfit Credits Green belt 20 Food, Water 25 Energy 50 Smithore 75 Crystite, Rare earth 100 Alloy, Medicine 150 Electronics 200 An unsold MULE listed at the land auction with \"the store buys it\" fetches half the month's MULE price ( LOTS.storeMuleShare ); see Land and geology . Buying and leading MULEs is in Development . Why it is like this The design doc, after the original: Smithore sets the MULE price, and starving the store of Smithore is a strategy with a cost for everyone. Heiko, 28 Oct: \"when the store runs out of mules and there is no smithore it needs to raise the prices\". Heiko, 5 Nov: one claim per land grant, with the corral kept at 2 per seat so it doesn't shrink. In the code: economy.ts::mulePrice , corralTarget , mulesWanted · game.ts::advancePhase (scarcity factor), mulePriceFor , outfitCostFor · upkeep.ts::runUpkeep (building) · config.ts::MULE Kept current by: WS1."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"export-ship","title":"The export ship","status":"In the game","text":"The colony's only outlet. The export ship calls every GAME.exportShipEvery = 3 months: in a 12-month game, in months 3, 6, 9 and 12."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"wishlist","title":"Its wishlist","status":"","text":"For each call the ship wants EXPORT_SHIP.wishlist = 3 goods, drawn from the game's seed from the six it ever buys. Food, Water and Energy don't travel, so the ship never wants them. The wishlist is announced the month before it calls (months 2, 5, 8, 11), and from then on what it wants counts as demand in the store's prices, so the wishlist goods' prices rise ahead of the ship. How many it wants of each is EXPORT_DEMAND × seats × 3 months, rounded up: Good EXPORT_DEMAND a seat a month 2 seats 4 seats 6 seats Smithore 1 6 12 18 Crystite 2 12 24 36 Rare earth 0.5 3 6 9 Alloy 1 6 12 18 Electronics 1 6 12 18 Medicine 0.5 3 6 9"},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"what-the-ship-pays","title":"What it pays","status":"","text":"The off-world price for its first unit of a good. EXPORT_SHIP.stepDown = 2% less for each further unit of that good, as its hold fills. 10% more from a colony whose air is clean ( POLLUTION.cleanExportBonus ). Rounded down to whole credits. Players sell to it first, at the auction in the month it is in port, where it stands on the board as an amber line. At that month's upkeep the store sells the ship its own surplus, everything above 2 months of demand, while the ship still has room. Then the ship leaves and the next call is planned. A player's sale to the ship pays the sales tax, and the export tax when that law is in force (10%, 20% or 30%), taken after the sales tax. The store's sales to the ship pay no tax; they go into its capital. Example: 24 Crystite A four-seat colony with clean air; the ship wants 24 Crystite at an off-world price of 100. The first unit fetches 110, the second 107, the third 105, … the 24th 59. All 24 fetch 2,023 credits before tax. With dirty air the same 24 fetch 100 down to 54, 1,847 in all. At the 5% sales tax a seller keeps about 95% of that. Why it is like this The design doc: the ship has limited cargo space and every extra unit of a good sells for a little less, so flooding the ship with one good lowers what each unit earns. The clean-air bonus rewards a colony that keeps its air clean. In the code: economy.ts::planShip , shipAnnounced , shipInPort , exportDemand , shipPrice · upkeep.ts::runUpkeep (the store's surplus) · politics.ts::chargeExportTax · config.ts::EXPORT_DEMAND , EXPORT_SHIP , GAME.exportShipEvery Kept current by: WS1."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"crystite","title":"Crystite","status":"In the game","text":"Crystite is a pure off-world luxury. Mining it costs a Crystite outfit (100 credits) and 2 Energy a plot a month, and it never spoils. No colony use in the price. Nobody in the colony consumes Crystite, so its colony demand is 0; only the export ship's demand counts. One building needs it. The Observatory, a colony prestige object, needs 3 Crystite and 700 credits. The colony fund buys missing goods for a project from the store at the store's selling price. This purchase is not counted in the store's demand. Its price is held to the off-world price. Because only the ship wants it, its target is never above the off-world price of 100. When the colony holds a lot of Crystite against the ship's demand, the target falls below 100: with 10 months of cover it is 100 × (2 ÷ 10)^0.5 = 45. The store buys it only for a ship. In a month when the ship has announced a wishlist without Crystite, and in the month it calls without wanting Crystite, the store wants none: nobody buys Crystite and the good is skipped. In the other months the store stocks up to a ship's load. Selling to the ship. In port with Crystite on its list, the ship pays 100 for the first unit (110 from a clean colony), 2% less for each after. Pirate raid. With a chance of 1 in 100 each month, as the auction opens, pirates take all the Crystite, the store's and every player's, before prices are posted. Nothing protects it today."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"crystite-month-one","title":"Crystite's first months","status":"","text":"Reading the code: in month 1 the colony holds no Crystite, so the starting price (which is set before the off-world limit applies) is 100 × (2 ÷ 0.25)^0.5 = 283 . From the first auction it falls by the most it may, 40% a month: 170, then 102, then 100. Electronics starts the same way at 622 and falls to 373, 224, then its off-world price of 220. WS5 measured a Crystite maximum of 170 and a 90th percentile of 102 at the auction, which agrees. Why it is like this Heiko, 5 Nov: \"Please confirm crystite is a pure luxury good, it has no purpose, correct? crystite is there for export only\". The PM confirmed from the code: colony demand 0, the price capped at the off-world price, the off-world price fixed at 100; the one colony use is the Observatory's 3 Crystite. What is changing Heiko wants Crystite's off-world price to vary from 40 to 300, usually about 90, with the higher prices more and more unlikely. Today it never moves from 100, so the store's price barely moves either and holding Crystite is not a bet. This is part of WS5's store-pricing design . In the code: economy.ts::monthlyDemand ( demand.crystite = 0 ), nextPostedPrice (the off-world limit), storeWants · game.ts::createGame · config.ts::PRESTIGE.observatory , COLONY.fundBuysGoods , EVENTS.colony.pirateRaid , MULE.outfitCost Kept current by: WS1, with WS5 for the design."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"colony-fund-and-taxes","title":"The colony fund and taxes","status":"In the game","text":"The colony has two pots of money, and neither can spend the other's: Store capital Colony fund What it is The merchant's working money The colony government's money Filled by Selling MULEs, outfits and goods to players, colonists, the fund and the ship Taxes, the Federation's grant, donations Spent on Buying goods, unsold plots and MULEs, and wolf pelts Office pay, prestige projects and their goods Starts with 4,000 0 Limits None: it may go below zero See Colony and politics"},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"taxes-on-trade","title":"Taxes on trade","status":"","text":"Tax Who pays Rate Set by Sales tax The seller, on every unit a player sells to another player, the store or the ship 5% at the start, 0 to 15% The Governor, at most 5 points up or down a month ( POLITICS.tax ) Export tax (a law) The seller, on units sold to the export ship, after the sales tax 10%, 20% or 30% A colony vote Each tax is worked out per unit and rounded to whole credits: at 5%, a sale at 26 pays 1 and a sale at 30 pays 2 (1.5 rounds up). Prices on the board are what the buyer pays; the seller's receipt shows the tax taken. Colonists and the store pay no tax. Taxes paid also earn extra votes; land and pollution taxes, fines and how the fund is spent are in Colony and politics . In the code: economy.ts::taxSale · politics.ts::chargeExportTax · colony.ts::fundIn , fund · config.ts::TAX.salesTax , POLITICS.tax , POLITICS.laws , COLONY Kept current by: WS7 (fund and laws), WS1 (sales tax in trades)."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"store-pricing-design","title":"The store-pricing design","status":"Being designed","text":"Heiko asked on 5 Nov for one complete design of how the store sets its prices. In his words: \"we need to think about how the store sets its selling and buying prices … also this needs to be a good clever algorithm\" (after his auction rules, especially a sold-out store being bid past; see the goods auction ). \"Also we need to think about the bid and ask spread of the store. i think the spread can be wider than what we are having currently. … I want to get a complete design there.\" Crystite: \"the prices should vary from 40 to 300 getting more and more unlikely the higher they get. so a normal price should be about 90 with extremes that can go up to 300 very rare and lower extremes of 40.\""},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"pricing-today","title":"What WS5 measured today","status":"","text":"On trunk, 200 four-seat games plus 100 each at 2 and 6 seats, no tuning: Prices lurch: the median month-on-month change for Food, Water and Energy is 41–50%, the full momentum step most months. No ceiling: Food peaks at 1,985 and Medicine at 10,389 (four seats); at two seats Food reaches 3,078. Food's median price is 59 against a reference of 30. The store is sold out when the good comes up in 30% of Food, 43% of Water and 26% of Energy auctions. Colonists go short of Water in 30% of months and of Food in 13%. One spread for every good, 20% wide in all. Players sell to the store far more than to each other: 21,734 Food units into the store against 2,041 traded between players. When the store is sold out, players pay each other far above its last price, but next month's store price learns from that only through the slow reference drift. Crystite's off-world price never moves; its posted price sits at 100."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"pricing-goals","title":"The goals put to Heiko","status":"","text":"Goal What it means at the table G-1: prices answer the table One rule a player can learn: a shortage shows as a rising line, a sold-out bid-up moves next month's price up to at least what was paid, a glut pulls it down, and Food, Water and Energy have a named ceiling. G-2: a wider spread The store buys clearly lower and sells clearly higher than today's 20%, so a fair deal between players beats the store, and more trade happens between players. Possibly per good, or widening with scarcity. G-3: Crystite 40 to 300 Crystite's off-world price moves month to month from the seed: median about 90, never below 40 or above 300, above 200 in under 5% of months. What is changing WS5 owns the design; WS1 will build it in the store's code. Goals and today's measurement were due at 19:00Z on 5 Nov (ready by 07:25Z), the options at 21:00–22:00Z. Nothing is decided yet. Questions for Heiko: is the MULE price part of the design? Should other goods' off-world prices move too, or only Crystite's? Is Energy supply at the store in scope? What number is the ceiling (to be set with the options)? PM lean for Crystite: an off-world price that wanders and is pulled back toward its normal level month by month, rather than a fresh draw each month, so that holding Crystite and selling when the ship pays well rewards a player who reads the trend. In the code (what it would change): economy.ts::storeQuotes , nextPostedPrice , driftReference · game.ts::createGame ( offWorld ) · config.ts::PRICE , GOOD_CONFIG Kept current by: WS5 (design), WS1 (code)."},{"page":"store-and-prices.html","id":"not-in-the-game","title":"In the economy design, not in the game","status":"Backlog","text":"The economy design ( docs/design/01-economy-model.md ) describes several things the code does not have. Some were replaced by later rulings. The design says The game today Off-world prices drift each month, with trends and events Fixed at the reference price for the whole game (see the store-pricing design ) Emergency purchase: the Governor orders goods at 3 times the off-world price Not built; nothing is ever imported Storage buildings: warehouse, silo, cold store, tank, battery bank, vault (safe from pirates) Not built; every player has only the home depot of 10 a good Forward contracts and short sales Not built Windfall tax law Not built; the laws are export, land and pollution tax and an emission cap A store low on capital posts no buying price Replaced: the store never runs out of money (Heiko, 22 Oct) The store starts with 30 Food Replaced: 4 Food a seat (Heiko, 5 Nov) What is changing Whether any of these is still wanted, and by whom, is not recorded in the rulings read for this chapter. The PM keeps Open designs and backlog . Kept current by: the PM, with WS1."},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"one-rule","title":"Public on the TV, private on the phone","status":"In the game","text":"The game is played with one shared big screen and a phone in every player's hand. The rule behind every screen: public things go on the big screen, private things stay on the phone. The TV never shows what a player could want to hide: the goods you hold, your ground surveys, your vote, your whispers, your Worth line by line. Money, plots and each player's Worth total are public, as in the original. The big screen is optional. The game runs on the server; the TV and every phone are just screens connected to it. The TV has no seat and no controls, and it never mirrors a phone. Without a TV, each phone shows everything it needs, and the game plays the same (see A phone alone ). Any screen with a browser can be the TV: a laptop, a PC on an HDMI cable, a smart TV's browser. The TV page is drawn at 1280 × 720 and scaled to fit the screen, so it reads the same on any size. The phone page also works on a laptop: from a window 1100 px wide the same screens grow a wider side rail and larger type, and development can be steered with the keyboard. Why it is like this From the design doc on big-screen mode: \"If a phone ever needs the big screen to make a private decision, or the big screen ever needs a phone to follow the game, the design is wrong.\" In the code: apps/web/src/main.tsx (the TV at /table , everything else the phone) · apps/web/src/table/TableApp.tsx · apps/web/src/phone/PhoneApp.tsx · docs/design/07-big-screen-mode.md Kept current by: the Table team (WS4) for the TV and the shell, the UX team (WS6) for the phone."},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"joining","title":"Joining, the lobby and starting","status":"In the game","text":""},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"joining-phone","title":"On a phone","status":"","text":"Open the game's address. The first screen, \"Pull up a chair\", asks for your name and either a room code to join or Start a new game. It also says: \"Got a TV or laptop? Open /table on it for the big screen.\" Whoever starts a new game gets a four-letter room code (no vowels, so codes never spell words) and becomes the host. In the lobby, step 1 of 2 is \"Make your colonist\": pick one of the six species and one of the six colours. You are drawn large on the stage, in your colour, with your species' perk. The side rail is the table: \"At the table · N of 6\", every seat with its creature, name and what it is (a computer player, the host, or AWAY when its phone has gone). A colour a person has taken is theirs; a computer player's colour can be taken, and the computer player moves to another. The host's rail also has Add AI, a ✕ to remove a seat, Close the table and Start. Removing a seat or closing the table asks for one confirming tap first. Start needs at least 2 seats. Anyone else can leave the table before the game starts."},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"joining-tv","title":"On the TV","status":"","text":"Open /table on the big screen. It asks for the room code shown on the host's phone (\"Show this table\"); a link with the code in it opens the table straight away. The TV lobby shows a large QR code to scan, the join address, the room code in big letters, every seat with its creature and whether it is a computer player, the host, Ready or Away, and \"Waiting for the host to start\". With a TV connected, the phone's QR card is small (the TV carries the big one). With no TV, the phone draws its QR big and puts it first, under the next-step card, because it is the only way in. Why it is like this Heiko ruled on 21 Oct to keep the compact lobby (version 1 of the approved mocks) and, when no TV is connected, to use the bigger room card with a larger QR in the same spot. In the code: apps/web/src/phone/Lobby.tsx::Entry, Lobby, JoinCard · apps/web/src/table/TableApp.tsx::PickRoom, TvLobby · packages/tabletop/src/registry.ts (codes) · packages/tabletop/src/room.ts Kept current by: the Table team (WS4)."},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"tv-screens","title":"The TV, phase by phase","status":"In the game","text":"Every TV screen has the same header: \"Month N / 12\" above the phase's title, a Day/Night switch, and at the right the colony's gauges (Wealth, Environment, Prestige, Happiness) when the council is on, or the air gauge alone when it is off, and the clock when the phase has one. The table chat's ticker runs under the header once someone has spoken. Phase Title on the TV What the TV shows Planning Planning The colony map; the Governor's tax promise and whether it was kept; how many are ready; who the table is waiting for (and that the host can continue without them); the market; the news; every player's place by Worth and whether they are still planning, ready or away Election day Election day, then \"· vote\", then \"· the result\" The candidates and their promises; who has voted, never how; then the reveal ceremony, vote block by vote block Law vote The law vote, then \"· the result\" The proposal and what it costs each player; who has voted, never how; then the result as one weighted bar, totals only, with the laws in force Land grant Land grant The map with the lit plot; \"Lit now: Plot …\" with its terrain; every player's plot count; how the grant works Land auction Land auction · 2 of 3 Intro: the map large with the plot lit, the lot's card, \"Bidding starts in … s\", who can bid. Walk and result: the bidding board large with every bidder walking live and the minimum line, the leader or the result, the lot's card and the map small Development Development The whole colony with every colonist walking live, named; the store's hall beside it with whoever is shopping; the shared clock; each colonist's pace and what they are doing; along the map's foot, the moments: the corral scramble, a glint, wolves Production Production The harvest show on the map; a rail naming each step (the good being harvested, Bonuses, Losses, the events, the flight); every player's silo along the foot, filling as their goods land; who has asked to speed up Goods auction Auction · Food Status: how many have picked, \"No clock\", the status table, the store's prices and stock, what the ship wants, each player's side or PICKING. Walk: the time bar, the whole walk board with every creature, the store's lines and the ship's; at the side the stage (Walk, Trading · clock slowed, Closed), units traded, the store's Sells, Buys and Stock, the running deals, the goods still to come Month end End of the month How many are on; the league table by Worth with the leader and each change since last month; the colony panel (air, fund, who went hungry) and the colony's buildings; a Collapse banner while the air is in Collapse; the Worth rule in one line Game over Game over The winner (several, if tied); the final table by Worth; with the council on, the colony's score and who gave most, otherwise the colony's verdict on its air"},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"tv-planning","title":"Planning on the TV","status":"","text":"The ready list shows each person's place by Worth (\"1st\", \"2nd\"), their creature and one word: PLANNING, READY, or AWAY · PLANNING for a dropped phone. Computer players, always ready, share one dimmed row. The map's caption reads the room code, the colonists, the MULEs in the corral and their price. The market tiles show six goods with their price and how many months of demand the store holds, amber under one month and red under half a month."},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"tv-auction","title":"The goods auction on the TV","status":"","text":"Each running deal is a row (\"Lena buys from the store at 34\", for example) with its speed: chevrons that fill as the pair stays matched, \"faster and faster\" until \"full speed\". When no deal runs, a help line explains the board: \"Sellers push the red line down, buyers push the green line up. When the two lines meet, the goods change hands.\" The TV plays the auction's sounds: a tick-tock clock that speeds up as time runs out and slows while trading, a click for every unit, a bell struck three times when a good's walk ends."},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"tv-events","title":"Events on the TV","status":"","text":"When events strike, the TV plays their show over the phase screen: the header with a pip for each event, the living map where each event lands on its plots, a log of what happened, and a colony bar with every player's slot glowing while their spotlight plays and a \"sick\" tag once their sickness lands. Production plays its events inside its own show. See Event shows . In the code: apps/web/src/table/TableApp.tsx::TvHeader, TvGame, TvAuction, TvMonthEnd, TvGameOver · apps/web/src/table/TvElection.tsx, TvCouncil.tsx, TvLandAuction.tsx, TvDevelop.tsx, TvProduction.tsx · apps/web/src/events/TvEventLayer.tsx · apps/web/src/game/sound.ts Kept current by: the Table team (WS4); WS7 for the election and law screens, WS8 for the event layer, WS3 for the auction's content."},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"tv-fit","title":"The TV fits a real table","status":"Being built","text":"Played at a real table, some TV screens ran out of room. The PM ruled the fixes on 5 Nov: The goods auction's walk shows one chat line, the newest, and none while a deal runs; during a trade, the speaker's bubble on the board carries what was said. The newest deal keeps its speed line; older deals take one line each, so two deals fit under the store's box. The status stage keeps three chat lines. The header : the ready count sits beside the title. When the colony's gauges also show, they move to a full-width row of their own under it whenever a ready chip or a status badge (such as a strike) is shown. The month end's title , \"End of the month\", stays on one line. Planning's side column scales to fit, so its last standings row never runs off the screen. What is changing PR #381 (the Table team, WS4), with a named edit in WS6's chat ticker. Being built; checked by screen rows in CI. The small captions on the development and month-end screens stay in the Table team's backlog, and the computer players' bubbles that can cover board labels were passed to the UX team (WS6). In the code: apps/web/src/table/TableApp.tsx::TvHeader, TvAuction · apps/web/src/chat/Chat.tsx::TvTicker Kept current by: the Table team (WS4)."},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"auction-headline","title":"The good leads every auction screen","status":"Being built","text":"Heiko at the table: \"we need to make it more prominent what the auction is currently about … in the headline together with the resource symbol\". So the good's glyph and name lead every goods-auction screen, large: Phone and laptop : a headline at the top of the main pane. On the status screen \"🌾 Food\" with the picked count; during the walk \"🌾 Food\" beside the clock; at the close \"🌾 Food closed\". The rail keeps its small line. TV : the title reads \"🌾 Food auction\" (today \"Auction · Food\", without the glyph). What is changing PR #374 (the UX team, WS6), reviewed. It goes into the same trunk batch as the TV fit, before it, since both touch the TV auction's title line. In the code: apps/web/src/phone/Auction.tsx · apps/web/src/table/TableApp.tsx::TvAuction Kept current by: the UX team (WS6), with the Table team (WS4) for the TV."},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"phone-shell","title":"The phone: always sideways","status":"In the game","text":"The phone is played held sideways for the whole game. Held upright it shows only \"turn your phone\"; the game stays loaded underneath, so nothing is lost while you turn it. The screens are drawn for a phone of 844 × 390 and still fit a small one of 667 × 375. Every phone screen has the same frame. The stage on the left holds the phase's content: the map, the board, the hall. The rail on the right holds, from the top: the month (\"Month 3 / 12\") and the phase's name, with the clock when the phase has one, or a ready count when it has none; your creature and name, the Day/Night switch, \"Move to my phone\", and your money; in planning, under your money, your place (\"2nd of 4\"), which opens the standings; Now : the one next step and why; the screen's own controls; the table chat: everyone's seat and the last two lines; at the bottom, within thumb reach, the main action. Why it is like this Heiko, 28 Oct: \"phone is always landscape btw and the steering control should be laying over the card\", then \"Whole game\" when asked whether that meant every screen. This replaced the design doc's earlier \"Phones stay portrait\". In the code: apps/web/src/phone/Shell.tsx::PhoneShell, NowCard, TurnSideways · apps/web/src/phone/PhoneApp.tsx Kept current by: the Table team (WS4) for the shell, the UX team (WS6) for its content."},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"phone-hub","title":"Planning on the phone: the hub","status":"In the game","text":"Planning opens the month's hub (the team calls its built parts \"Today\"). The stage holds: The month's roadmap : Plan, Land grant, Land auction (only in a month with a lot), Develop, Produce, Goods auction, this step lit, and one plain line on what to do now and what comes next. With the council on, the offices sit under it. What you need this month : Food, Energy and Water against what you hold, red where you are short. A tap opens the Colony desk. This month : everything the month allows, each a door into a desk or a map tool. It heads with the export ship (\"Export ship in month 6: it buys the colony's leftovers\", or \"in port\"). Its items: Next month's vote, Throw your hat in the ring (before an election), Geo drone, Sell a plot or MULE, and the Governor's desk for the Governor. The colony map with its tools: Look (tap a plot to see what it can make and whose it is), Geo drone (pick up to 2 free plots and send it) and Sell a plot (tap your plot or one with your MULE). Under the map: the store's prices and stock in one row of goods. The rail holds Now, the open tool's panel, and the desks: Colony (what you need, the news, the colony), Talk (the table chat, with an unread badge), Council (with the council on), Market (put a plot or MULE up; the month's line-up of lots) and, for the Governor, the Governor's desk (set the tax, propose a law). In an election month, election day takes the stage until the votes are over. At the foot, \"I'm ready\". Once you are, it shows who the table still waits for; the host can then continue without them after one confirming tap (\"End planning for everyone now? … stop where they are, and the land grant starts.\"). In the code: apps/web/src/phone/hub/today.tsx::HUB_SLOTS · apps/web/src/phone/hub/Hub.tsx · apps/web/src/phone/hub/Roadmap.tsx, NeedsStrip.tsx, desks.tsx, tools.tsx, ReadyBar.tsx, ready.ts · apps/web/src/phone/hub/politics/ Kept current by: the UX team (WS6); the Politics team (WS7) for the politics slots."},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"phone-phases","title":"The phone, phase by phase","status":"In the game","text":"Phase What your phone shows What you press Land grant The map with the lit plot; \"Your drone: 12·5 Desert, good for Energy\" while a plot your drone surveyed is lit; who is still choosing CLAIM (the big button) and \"I'm ready\" side by side; once you are done, \"You're done with land\" Land auction Intro: the plot lit on the map and what you will be doing on this lot. Walk: the board fills the stage. Result: sold to you, to someone else, or unsold, and what that means for you. The seller sees the same screens without Up and Down Hold Up to raise your bid Development The sideways arcade screen (see below) The stick and the action button Production The same show as the TV's, but only your own plots fill and your harvest flies into your private stock strip; the rail lists what each good made you (+3 Food, and so on) ⏩ Speed up (with a TV), then \"Ready for the auction\" once the show ends Goods auction Status: what everyone has, used, made and is short of, and your side, already picked from what you hold and need. Walk: the board with your creature. Close: your receipt for the good, with the tax spelled out on a sale and the month's running total Buy or Sell, then Ready; then hold Up or Down, or drag on the board Month end \"End of the month\": the league table by Worth with each change since last month; your own Worth line by line in the rail, marked as only yours; with no TV, the colony panel too \"On to month N\"; then \"Waiting for the others\" Game over Your place and your Worth beside your own lines, the colony's verdict, the final standings in the rail; with no TV, the colony's report Nothing The goods auction's side pick has no clock. If the host moves on for players still picking, the host's confirm says what each of them will do (\"Kai buys Food: 1 of 3, short 2.\"; a dropped phone sits the good out), and the host taps once more to go on. Phones play the auction's sounds too. In the code: apps/web/src/phone/LandGrant.tsx, LandAuction.tsx, Production.tsx, Auction.tsx, Results.tsx, GameOver.tsx · apps/web/src/components/MonthEnd.tsx Kept current by: the UX team (WS6), with the Auctions team (WS3) for the auction screens and the Table team (WS4) for the month end and game over."},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"arcade-phone","title":"Development on the sideways phone","status":"In the game","text":"In development the phone becomes an arcade controller. The whole colony map fills the screen , with every colonist walking live. A translucent stick lies over the bottom-left corner and the action button over the bottom-right. When you walk into the store, the stage becomes the store's hall: six shopfronts for the raw goods along the top, the works for the factories, the corral and the pub. The button says what it will do where you stand: BUY MULE, ANALYSE, FIT FILTER, TAKE MULE, CATCH MULE, FIGHT, GRAB THE GLINT, PUB · END TURN and so on. It is dim when there is nothing to do. The server decides; a press is a request. A ring shows your next step : the store and its corral when you have no MULE, the outfitters you can pay when it is empty, then the best of your free plots for its good. When you cannot afford a MULE, it points to what you can do instead: analyse a plot, or the pub. The side rooms beside the map hold the clock, your money and your pace line, for example \"Your pace 47%: you ate 1 of the 3 Food you need\", with a tap for what to do about it next month. On a small phone the status lies in a bar over the map's top edge. When your turn ends , a card says \"In the pub: your turn is over\" or \"Development over\", with how many MULEs you installed and how long the others still walk. If no update has arrived for 1 s, the phone shows \"reconnecting\" and stops guessing where you are. On a laptop, the arrow keys steer and SPACE presses the button; you can also hold the mouse on a spot to walk there and click the lit thing to act. Why it is like this Heiko, 28 Oct, on the walking phone: the whole map in full on the phone, \"the steering control should be laying over the card\". He passed the arcade design gate the same day (\"Yes, build this\") and approved the walking build at his table on 5 Nov (\"Walking is good\"). In the code: apps/web/src/phone/arcade/Develop.tsx, stages.tsx, guide.ts, parts.tsx::paceWords, layout.ts, controls.ts · packages/engine/src/floor/reach.ts::reachWords · packages/engine/src/config.ts::DEV_STEER Kept current by: the UX team (WS6). Rules in Development ."},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"sick-line","title":"The sick line on the sideways phone","status":"Being built","text":"Today a sick player's pace line only says \"Pace 50%: sick\". The new line says that you are sick, why, and your pace this turn: the sickness and its cause, with a cross in the Medicine colour: \"✚ Sick: the air is toxic.\", \"… your own pollution fouled the air.\", or \"Sick this month.\"; then \"50% pace this turn\", adding \"· you ate 2 of 5 Food\" when you are hungry too; a tap adds that it struck as your turn began with no Medicine to cure it, and that a Medicine in hand when a turn begins stops it (it is used up); on a small phone the bar reads \"✚ Sick · 50% pace\". What is changing PR #375 (the UX team, WS6), reviewed. It carries the tap walk's removal (PR #369, the Auctions team) until that merges, because the sick line belongs to the arcade screen only. In the code: apps/web/src/phone/arcade/parts.tsx::paceWords, PaceLine Kept current by: the UX team (WS6)."},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"chat","title":"Chat, whispers, emotes and bubbles","status":"In the game","text":"You type in the Talk desk in planning: to the whole table, or whispered to one player (\"WHISPER · ONLY YOU AND KAI SEE THIS\"). Five quick emotes sit above the box: Deal?, Nice!, Traitor!, Ha! and Help!. A line holds up to 280 characters. A whisper reaches only its two ends, never the TV; a whisper between two people stays hidden from the computer players too. Every screen, the TV included, shows what was said: A speech bubble pops at the speaker's creature wherever the screen draws it (on the map, on the auction board, in the store's hall). Where the screen does not draw the speaker, the bubble opens at their place in the seat row. One bubble shows at a time on each screen, so they never cover each other: each shows for at least 2 s and at most 4.5 s, and a line that would wait more than 4 s for its turn gets no bubble. A new phase ends every bubble. The ticker keeps the last lines, newest first: two on a phone (in the rail, under its content), three on the TV (under the header). A whisper is marked 🔒. Emotes float at the top of every phone's stage for 3.5 s. Computer players talk too: each says at most 3 lines a month and one a phase, up to 140 characters, about what it just decided. A public line from a person reaches every computer player, a whisper only the one it was for. Why it is like this Heiko, 22 Oct: live table chat \"Live on every screen\", a speech bubble at the speaker and a short ticker on the phones and the TV in every phase. He approved the design the same day (ticker 2 lines on a phone, 3 on the TV; whispers only on the two screens involved, with a lock, never on the TV), with the PM's condition that bubbles never cover each other or the ticker. What is changing Only the planning hub has a place to type. In every other phase you can read the chat and see bubbles but not write, and no ruling says whether that is intended. Owner: the UX team (WS6). In the code: apps/web/src/chat/Chat.tsx, store.ts, queue.ts · apps/web/src/phone/parts.tsx (the Talk desk's chat, EmoteBubbles ) · packages/protocol/src/index.ts::EMOTES · packages/tabletop/src/room.ts · apps/server/src/brain/chat.ts · packages/engine/src/config.ts::BRAIN Kept current by: the UX team (WS6); the Brain team (WS2) for the computer players' lines."},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"event-shows","title":"Event shows on the TV and on the phone","status":"In the game","text":"An event's story is sized as a headline ( 7 s), a spotlight ( 5.2 s) or a bulletin ; all of a phase's bulletins share one roll call of 4 s. A 1.8 s intro opens the show, and one phase's events never play longer than 30 s in all; past that each is cut in proportion. While the show plays at the start of development or the goods auction, that phase's clocks wait. Where With a TV at the table A phone alone TV The full show over the phase screen: pips, the living map, the log, the colony bar Not there Phone, a held phase Covers the screen until the show ends: your own plots marked on the map and a light card of what happened to you; the phone vibrates once as each event is announced Plays the whole show itself on its own map; a tap skips ahead to the result Phone, planning The light card floats over the hub, which stays usable Plays the show; the first tap folds it to the light card Production Plays inside the production show; the phone vibrates per event and carries its light card The phone plays each event on its own map; a tap on the map skips this phone's show to the result A skip only moves your own phone's show ahead; it never moves the table's clock. In the code: apps/web/src/events/PhoneEventOverlay.tsx, TvEventLayer.tsx · apps/web/src/phone/Production.tsx · packages/engine/src/config.ts::EVENT_SHOW Kept current by: the Events team (WS8). Every event is in Events ."},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"solo-play","title":"A phone alone","status":"In the game","text":"The game plays without a TV. A phone on its own (one person against computer players, or several people each on their own phone with no big screen) notices that no TV is connected and takes over what the TV would show: The lobby draws the room's QR big, first on the screen. Every phase already shows the whole map: the hub's colony map, the land grant's lit plot, the arcade's whole colony, the auction board. Production and events play their full show on the phone, and a tap skips ahead, instead of the light card and the buzz. The month end fits the league table and the colony panel on one stage. Game over shows the colony's report under your result. This follows the TV live: if the TV disconnects mid-game, the phones switch to these screens; when it comes back, they switch back. What is changing From the design doc, not built: a remote player in a big-screen game seeing both the board and their controller on one device. Owner: the Table team (WS4). In the code: table.boards read in apps/web/src/phone/Lobby.tsx, Production.tsx, Results.tsx, GameOver.tsx and apps/web/src/events/PhoneEventOverlay.tsx Kept current by: the Table team (WS4), with the UX team (WS6)."},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"reconnect","title":"Dropping out and getting back in","status":"In the game","text":"A dropped connection : the phone shows \"CONNECTION LOST · REJOINING…\" and tries again by itself, waiting a little longer each time, up to 5 s between tries. It takes its seat back with the seat's own key. A reload : the phone remembers its seat and takes it back by itself, unless you opened a link to another table. A new device : \"Move to my phone\" (in every game screen's rail, and in the lobby) shows a QR and a one-time code. Opened on the other device within 2 minutes, it moves your seat there, with your money, goods and plots, and the old screen steps aside (\"You're playing on another device now\"). The game does not pause meanwhile. Nobody new can join a game that has started. A seat whose device is lost and has no saved key cannot be taken back. The host passes to the next connected person while the host's phone is gone, and back when it returns. A room that nobody has been connected to for 30 minutes is closed. While you are away, the table treats you by phase: planning, the votes, the goods auction's side pick and the month end wait for you (the host can go on without you); in the land grant you just do not claim; your colonist stands still in development; in the walks you stop where you stand; production does not wait for you. The TV and the other phones mark you AWAY. In the code: apps/web/src/net/client.ts (reconnect, saved seat, REMOVED messages) · apps/web/src/components/Toasts.tsx::ConnectionBar · apps/web/src/phone/MoveToPhone.tsx · packages/tabletop/src/room.ts ( handoffCodeMs , hostId ) · apps/server/src/server.ts ( IDLE_ROOM_MS ) · apps/server/src/mule-game.ts::connectionChanged, checkAllReady Kept current by: the Table team (WS4)."},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"accessibility","title":"Reduced motion and accessibility","status":"In the game","text":"Reduced motion : on a device set to reduce motion, the event shows' looping effects hold still and their entrances become a plain fade; the store and the MULEs stop animating; creatures stop their walking cycle and hold a still lean; the show's vibrate icon stops shaking. The production show, the election reveal and the event shows all follow the setting live. Telling players apart : every player has both a colour and a species shape, so colour is never the only clue. Changes in the league table carry an arrow and a sign (↑ +120, ↓ −40), never colour alone. Day and Night : every screen has a Day/Night switch; the phone remembers its choice. Screen readers : the chat ticker is announced as it changes; private lines carry an \"only you see this\" label; the creature in your header is named (\"You, Bramblet\"). Keyboard : on a laptop, development is played with the arrow keys and SPACE. Readable from the couch : the TV is drawn for 3 metres: large type, few words. What is changing The design doc's host Pause button (for dinner and phone calls, shown on the TV) is not built: the game never pauses, and a move to another phone says so. Owner: the Table team (WS4). In the code: packages/ui/src/theme.css, events/events.css, events/motion.ts ( prefers-reduced-motion ) · apps/web/src/components/ThemeToggle.tsx · apps/web/src/components/MonthEnd.tsx::ChangeTag Kept current by: the Table team (WS4), with the UX team (WS6)."},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"colony-on-the-tv","title":"The colony on the TV: mood drift and strikes","status":"Being built","text":"Two colony shows are built in the colony branch and are not on trunk yet."},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"mood-drift","title":"The month end's mood drift","status":"","text":"At the month end, the TV's Happiness gauge opens on last month's value and, about 1.2 s later, counts to this month's. When the count crosses into another band (Restless into Unhappy, for example), the band's name flips and the gauge pulses. It shows no reasons, only \"↓ n this month\". It plays once, as the TV sees the month end arrive; a TV that reloads into the month end, or a device set to reduce motion, shows the end state at once. Nothing plays in month 1, with an unchanged mood, or with the council off."},{"page":"table-and-phones.html","id":"strikes","title":"Strikes","status":"","text":"During a general strike, the TV header carries a strike badge (\"STRIKE · Food 20%\", then \"PAID · −250 cr\" once paid), the TV shows whose choice the strike waits on (\"Waiting for Kai, the Governor\"), and strikers with placards stand over every plot of the struck good on the map. The Governor's phone shows the strike in its Now card, with \"Pay the strikers\" and \"Let them strike\"; everyone else's phone shows a line saying the strike is on and who decides. What is changing Mood drift: PR #365. Strikes: PR #368. Both by the Events team (WS8) into the colony branch, with the Table team's OK on the TV header. When the colony branch reaches trunk, the TV fit's header row (PR #381) keeps the drift. The strike badge is one of the status badges that move the gauges to their own row. In the code: apps/web/src/table/TableApp.tsx::TvHeader, TvMonthEnd · apps/web/src/components/Colony.tsx::ColonyGauges Kept current by: the Events team (WS8), with the Table team (WS4)."}];
